    {
      "id": "pico-deploy",
      "engine": "picoruby",
      "live": false,
      "pico": {
        "board": "pico2w"
      },
      "de": {
        "title": "Auf einen echten Pico: R2P2 und prremote",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Auf einen echten Pico</h2><p>Alles in den letzten Lektionen lief auf einem simulierten Board. Geschrieben war der Code für ein echtes – so kommt er dorthin. Zuerst: welches Board? Raspberry Pi baut vier Picos (Stand Oktober 2026):</p><table class='cheat boards'><tr><th>Board</th><th>Chip, RAM, Flash</th><th>Funk</th><th>LED auf dem Board</th></tr><tr><td>Pico</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td>Pico W</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>CYW43439: WLAN, Bluetooth</td><td>am Funkchip</td></tr><tr><td>Pico 2</td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pico 2 W</strong></td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>CYW43439</td><td>am Funkchip</td></tr></table><p>Die PicoRuby-Firmware R2P2 gibt es als zwei Dateien. Welche auf welches Board gehört und was damit geht:</p><dl class='files'><dt><code>R2P2-FEMTORUBY-…-PICO</code></dt><dd>Pico: Pins, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI. Pico W: dieselbe Datei, ohne Funk – Pins; WLAN nur selbst gebaut, BLE nie.</dd><dt><code>R2P2-PICORUBY-…-PICO2_W</code></dt><dd><strong>Pico 2 W</strong>: alles – WLAN, HTTPS, BLE, dRuby. Pico 2: Pins, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI.</dd></dl><p>FemtoRuby ist PicoRuby auf der kleineren VM von mruby/c, PicoRuby selbst läuft auf der VM von mruby – derselben, die dieser Kurs im Browser laufen lässt. Der Pico 2 W ist das einzige Board mit einer offiziellen Datei, die alles hat, was diese Lektionen benutzt haben; nimm also den.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Eine Einkaufsliste</h3><table class='cheat'><tr><th>Teil</th><th>Wofür</th></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html' target='_blank'>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 WH</a> (das H: Stiftleisten schon angelötet)</td><td>das Board; passt ins Steckbrett</td></tr><tr><td>ein Micro-USB-Kabel, das <strong>Daten</strong> überträgt</td><td>Strom, das Terminal, das Kopieren der Firmware; mit einem reinen Ladekabel erscheint kein Laufwerk</td></tr><tr><td>ein Steckbrett und Steckkabel</td><td>verdrahten ohne Löten</td></tr><tr><td>LEDs, Widerstände mit 330 Ω, ein Taster</td><td>Blinken, der Knopf</td></tr><tr><td>ein Fotowiderstand (LDR) und ein Widerstand mit 10 kΩ, ein passiver Summer</td><td>der Lichtsensor an GP26, Töne</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-Environment-Sensor' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-Environment-Sensor</a></td><td>Temperatur, Luftfeuchtigkeit und Luftdruck (BME280) über I2C</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-ePaper-2.13' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.13</a></td><td>das E-Paper-Display, 250 × 122 Pixel</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PicoGo' target='_blank'>Waveshare PicoGo</a></td><td>der Roboter: Motoren, Infrarotsensoren, Ultraschallsensor</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-BLE' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-BLE</a> (wenn du willst)</td><td>Bluetooth für einen Pico ohne Funk</td></tr></table><p>Die Waveshare-Boards werden auf die Pins des Picos gesteckt; sie benutzen die Pins, die die Werkbank gezeigt hat. Nicht jedes Paar passt zusammen – der PicoGo und der Umweltsensor wollen beide GP20 und GP21.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>R2P2 Schritt für Schritt</h3><ol><li><strong>Die Firmware holen.</strong> <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code> lädt die neueste R2P2-Datei (eine <code>.uf2</code>) herunter, oder du nimmst sie aus den Releases auf GitHub.</li><li><strong>Aufspielen.</strong> Halt den Knopf BOOTSEL gedrückt, während du den Pico einsteckst: Ein Laufwerk erscheint (<code>RP2350</code> bei einem Pico 2 W). Zieh die <code>.uf2</code> darauf; der Pico startet neu, als kleiner Ruby-Computer.</li><li><strong>Mit ihm reden.</strong> Öffne das R2P2 Web Terminal auf <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> in Chrome oder Edge – es benutzt Web Serial, installieren musst du nichts. Du bekommst die Shell von R2P2, mit <code>ls</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>vim</code> und <code>irb</code>: das IRB aus Lektion {{nr:pico-blink}}, in echt.</li><li><strong>Dateien hinbringen.</strong> <code>.rb</code>-Dateien aufs Laufwerk ziehen geht seit PicoRuby 3.4 nicht mehr. Stattdessen: das Hochladen im Web Terminal, das <code>vim</code> auf dem Board oder PicoModem, das eigene Übertragungsprotokoll von R2P2 mit CRC-Prüfung: <code>picoruby tools/picomodem.rb put app.rb /home/app.rb</code>.</li><li><strong>Starten.</strong> Beim Einschalten wartet R2P2 2 Sekunden (<kbd>s</kbd> überspringt den Start), verbindet sich mit dem WLAN, wenn es <code>/etc/network/wifi.yml</code> gibt, und führt <strong><code>/home/app.rb</code></strong> aus. Dorthin gehört dein Programm.</li><li><strong>Bibliotheken</strong> kommen nach <code>/lib</code>: <code>/lib/epd2in13.rb</code>, geladen mit <code>require \"epd2in13\"</code>, weil R2P2 <code>$LOAD_PATH = [\"/lib\"]</code> setzt. Die Tabs der Zellen hier tun dasselbe – jeder Tab wird vor dem Lauf nach <code>/lib</code> geschrieben.</li></ol>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>prremote: auf dem Computer übersetzen, auf dem Chip ausführen</h3><p>R2P2 ist ein ganzes Ruby auf dem Board, mit Dateisystem und Shell. <strong>prremote</strong> geht den anderen Weg. Es ist ein Kommandozeilen-Werkzeug von ITO Yosei von LumberMill, Inc. in Takachiho (Miyazaki, Japan), ein Gem unter der MIT-Lizenz seit Mai 2026; Version 0.4.2 kam am 16. Oktober 2026 heraus, und sein README warnt, dass es noch früh in der Entwicklung ist und sich Befehle ändern können. Es nennt sich «inspired by mpremote for MicroPython» – ein mpremote für Ruby.</p><p>prremote spielt eine winzige Laufzeit-Firmware mit <strong>mruby/c</strong> aufs Board. Übersetzt wird das Ruby auf deinem Computer, von deinem eigenen <code>mrbc</code> (es muss mruby 4.x sein), und der Bytecode geht über die serielle USB-Leitung aufs Board, mit 115200 Baud, wenn du nichts anderes einstellst. Die beiden sprechen ein kurzes Protokoll:</p><ul><li>Der Computer schickt jede Sekunde <code>Ctrl-C</code> (das Byte 3), bis das Board <code>READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow</code> antwortet.</li><li>Er schickt den Bytecode. Eine übersetzte mruby-Datei beginnt mit den Buchstaben <code>RITE</code>, und daran erkennt das Board, dass ein Programm kommt. (Es achtet auf drei solche Wörter aus vier Buchstaben: <code>RITE</code>, <code>DPLY</code>, <code>BORD</code>.) Der Computer schreibt in Stücken von 256 Bytes, mit 4 ms Pause dazwischen.</li><li>Das Board antwortet <code>RUNNING</code>, dann, was das Programm ausgibt, dann <code>DONE</code> – oder <code>ERROR</code> und den Grund.</li><li>Für <code>prremote deploy</code> schickt der Computer <code>DPLY</code> vor dem Bytecode. Das Board behält das Programm im Flash, führt es bei jedem Start aus und antwortet <code>DEPLOYED</code>.</li></ul><figure><img src='assets/pico/prremote-sequence.svg' alt='Sequenzdiagramm: Der Computer schickt jede Sekunde Ctrl-C, bis das Gerät READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow antwortet; dann schickt er den Bytecode, der mit RITE beginnt; das Gerät antwortet RUNNING, die Ausgabe des Programms und DONE, oder ERROR und einen Grund. Für prremote deploy schickt der Computer DPLY und den Bytecode, und das Gerät antwortet DEPLOYED.' width='640'><figcaption>Der Handschlag von prremote über die serielle USB-Leitung</figcaption></figure><p>Hier dieses Gespräch mit einem Schein-Gerät – reines Ruby, ohne Board:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "# Ein Schein-Gerät, das antwortet wie die Laufzeit-Firmware von prremote.\n# Beim ersten Ctrl-C ist es noch beschäftigt, das zweite bekommt READY.\nstupser = 0\ngeraet = lambda do |bytes|\n  if bytes == \"\\x03\"\n    stupser += 1\n    stupser < 2 ? [] : [\"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"RITE\")\n    [\"RUNNING\", \"Hello from Pico W!\", \"DONE\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"DPLY\")\n    [\"DEPLOYED\"]\n  else\n    []\n  end\nend\n\n# die Seite des Computers: etwas schicken, die Antwort ausgeben\nreden = lambda do |bytes, was|\n  puts \"Computer -> #{was}\"\n  geraet.call(bytes).each { |zeile| puts \"  <- #{zeile}\" }\nend\n\nbytecode = \"RITE...\" # was mrbc aus app.rb gemacht hat\nreden.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C\")\nreden.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C (eine Sekunde später)\")\nreden.call(bytecode, \"der Bytecode (prremote run)\")\nreden.call(\"DPLY\" + bytecode, \"DPLY + Bytecode (prremote deploy)\")\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><strong>Mehrere Dateien</strong>: <code>prremote run lib.rb main.rb</code> übersetzt die Dateien in dieser Reihenfolge zu einer einzigen Bytecode-Datei, sodass Klassen und Methoden aus früheren Dateien in späteren da sind. mruby/c hat kein <code>require</code>, und das README empfiehlt das als Ersatz. Nennt ein Skript die Konstante eines Boards wie <code>M5StickCPlus</code>, stellt prremote sogar die Bibliothek dieses Boards von selbst davor. Die Tabs dieses Kurses ahmen stattdessen den Weg von R2P2 nach – Dateien in <code>/lib</code> und <code>require</code> –, aber die Idee ist dieselbe: die Bibliothek im Hintergrund, dein Programm vorne.</p><p><strong>Die Befehle</strong>: <code>install -b picow</code> spielt die Firmware auf, <code>run</code> führt Dateien einmal aus, <code>watch</code> bei jedem Speichern wieder, <code>deploy</code> und <code>undeploy</code> legen ein Programm ins Flash oder entfernen es, <code>eval</code> führt eine Zeile aus, <code>reset</code> schickt Ctrl-C, <code>list</code> zeigt die seriellen Schnittstellen und <code>version</code> die Versionen.</p><p><strong>Seine Grenzen</strong>: prremote kennt den Pico, den Pico W, den Pico 2 (das Aufspielen ist als noch nicht auf echter Hardware getestet markiert) und ESP32-Boards wie den M5Stack; der Pico 2 W steht nicht auf seiner Liste. Auf einem Pico W hat es Pins, ADC, PWM, I2C, SPI, WLAN, NTP und TCP – aber kein TLS (also kein HTTPS) und kein BLE. Auf dem Board gibt es keine Shell, kein Dateisystem und kein IRB. Und es hat seine eigene Ruby-API, mit Absicht – es folgt nicht den Namen von PicoRuby:</p><table class='cheat'><tr><th></th><th>prremote</th><th>PicoRuby (R2P2)</th></tr><tr><td>LED auf dem Board</td><td><code>GPIO.led</code></td><td><code>CYW43::GPIO.new(CYW43::GPIO::LED_PIN)</code></td></tr><tr><td>ein Ausgangs-Pin</td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, :output)</code></td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, GPIO::OUT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>WLAN</td><td><code>WiFi.connect(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td><td><code>CYW43.connect_timeout(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>wie das Programm hinkommt</td><td>Bytecode in den RAM, oder mit <code>deploy</code> ins Flash</td><td>Quelldateien im Dateisystem</td></tr></table><p>Der Code dieser Lektionen läuft also unverändert auf R2P2 und muss für prremote angepasst werden.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><p><strong>R2P2</strong>: die Schritte oben – <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>, BOOTSEL, die Datei hinziehen, dann das Web Terminal.</p><p><strong>prremote</strong>: <code>gem install prremote</code> (Ruby 3.4 oder neuer) und ein <code>mrbc</code> von mruby 4: <code>brew install mruby</code> auf dem Mac; unter Linux mruby 4 aus den Quellen bauen oder mit <code>MRBC=/path/to/mrbc</code> auf eines zeigen – das mruby-Paket von Debian ist 3.x, was prremote nicht kompatibel nennt. Dann einmal <code>prremote install -b picow</code> (BOOTSEL halten, einstecken) und von da an <code>prremote watch app.rb</code> – jedes Speichern führt die Datei wieder aus. <code>prremote deploy lib.rb main.rb</code> lässt sie auf dem Board. In WSL2 braucht das USB-Gerät zuerst <code>usbipd attach --wsl</code>.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://github.com/lumbermill/prremote#readme' target='_blank'>das README von prremote</a> · <a href='https://picoruby.org/setup' target='_blank'>R2P2 einrichten</a> · <a href='https://github.com/picoruby/picoruby/blob/master/mrbgems/picoruby-r2p2/README.md' target='_blank'>das README von R2P2</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreib <code>lauf_status(zeilen)</code>, so wie die Computer-Seite von prremote die Zeilen des Boards liest: <code>:done</code>, sobald <code>DONE</code> kam, <code>\"error: &lt;grund&gt;\"</code> für <code>ERROR &lt;grund&gt;</code>, <code>:running</code> nach <code>RUNNING</code>, solange noch kein Ende da ist, und sonst <code>:waiting</code>. Die letzten Zeilen der Zelle probieren vier Gespräche aus.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def lauf_status(zeilen)\n  # DONE -> :done, \"ERROR x\" -> \"error: x\", RUNNING -> :running\n  :waiting\nend\n\nbereit = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit])]\n",
            "hints": [
              "Merk dir den Stand in einer Variablen, die mit <code>:waiting</code> beginnt. Geh die Zeilen mit <code>each</code> durch und setz den Stand um, wenn eine Zeile <code>DONE</code>, <code>RUNNING</code> oder <code>ERROR …</code> ist."
            ],
            "check": "result == [:done, \"error: NoMethodError\", :running, :waiting] && code.include?(\"def lauf_status\")",
            "hint": "Geh mit <code>each</code> durch die Zeilen und merk dir den Stand: <code>zeile == \"DONE\"</code>, <code>zeile.start_with?(\"ERROR \")</code> (der Grund ist <code>zeile[6..-1]</code>), <code>zeile == \"RUNNING\"</code>. Am Ende gibst du den Stand zurück.",
            "solution": "def lauf_status(zeilen)\n  stand = :waiting\n  zeilen.each do |zeile|\n    if zeile == \"DONE\"\n      stand = :done\n    elsif zeile.start_with?(\"ERROR \")\n      stand = \"error: #{zeile[6..-1]}\"\n    elsif zeile == \"RUNNING\"\n      stand = :running\n    end\n  end\n  stand\nend\n\nbereit = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n lauf_status([bereit])]\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Direkt von dieser Seite</h3><p>Unter jeder Werkbank dieser Lektionen gibt es ein zugeklapptes Feld <strong>🔌 Ein echter Pico (USB)</strong>. Mit einem Pico mit R2P2 am USB-Kabel macht es, was das Web Terminal macht – direkt aus der Zelle:</p><ol><li><strong>Echten Pico verbinden …</strong> öffnet die Liste der seriellen Ports, die der Browser selbst zeigt. Sie zeigt die Ports von R2P2 (USB-Hersteller 16C0); R2P2 hat drei, seine Shell ist normalerweise auf dem ersten (der tiefsten COM- oder ttyACM-Nummer) – kommt keine Eingabeaufforderung <code>$&gt;</code>, versuch den nächsten. Die Seite bekommt genau den Port, den du wählst – sonst nichts von deinem Computer. Zum Senden braucht R2P2 PicoModem, wie es R2P2 4.0.3 hat.</li><li>Der schwarze Kasten ist das Terminal von R2P2: was der Pico ausgibt, und darunter eine Zeile zum Eintippen, für <code>ls</code> oder <code>irb</code>. <strong>Ctrl-C</strong> hält ein laufendes Programm an.</li><li><strong>Diese Zelle auf den Pico senden und starten</strong> drückt Ctrl-C (und Ctrl-D, das ein <code>irb</code> verlässt), schreibt jeden Tab der Zelle nach <code>/lib</code> und den Code nach <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> – mit PicoModem, wie der Upload des Web Terminals, jede Datei mit CRC-32 geprüft – und tippt dann <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> bei der Eingabeaufforderung <code>$&gt;</code> ein: Das startet es. Mit dem Häkchen wird daraus <code>/home/app.rb</code>, das Programm, das R2P2 beim Einschalten startet. Eine erste Zeile in der Datei sorgt dafür, dass <code>require</code> die Tabs neu liest; <code>show_irb</code>, das es nur auf dieser Seite gibt, wird zu einem Kommentar – tipp stattdessen <code>irb</code> bei der Eingabeaufforderung.</li></ol><p><strong>Welche Browser</strong>: Web Serial gibt es in Chrome, Edge und Opera auf einem Computer (Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS), nicht in Firefox oder Safari. Unter Linux muss dein Benutzer eventuell in der Gruppe <code>dialout</code> sein, um <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code> zu öffnen. Und die Seite muss sicher sein: <a href='https://letsruby.com' target='_blank'>letsruby.com</a> über https, oder localhost. Auf einer Adresse mit einfachem http sagt das Feld nur, warum es nicht geht.</p><p><strong>Bluetooth</strong>: In Lektion {{nr:pico-ble}} haben die Werkbänke mit dem Laptop zusätzlich <strong>📶 Ein echter Pico 2 W (Bluetooth)</strong>: Der Laptop dort spricht dann mit einem echten Pico 2 W, auf dem ein BLE::UART-Programm läuft. Web Bluetooth gibt es in Chrome und Edge unter Windows, macOS, ChromeOS und Android; unter Linux muss es eventuell in <code>chrome://flags</code> eingeschaltet werden.</p><p><strong>Sicher gebaut</strong>: Die Seite erreicht nur ein Gerät, das du in der Auswahl des Browsers gewählt hast, und nur solange dieser Tab offen ist; «Trennen» oder das Schliessen des Tabs beendet es. Nichts geht anderswohin: Der Code geht aus diesem Tab über das Kabel oder den Funk auf deinen Pico. Was dort läuft, ist der Code der Zelle, den du lesen kannst – und wie alles auf einem Pico schaltet er die Pins, die du verkabelt hast. Prüf also zuerst die Verkabelung.</p><p><strong>Ehrlich gesagt</strong>: Diese Verbindung ist noch mit keinem echten Pico ausprobiert. Sie folgt den Quellen von R2P2 4.0.3 – der Eingabeaufforderung, Ctrl-B für PicoModem, den Frames und ihren Prüfsummen – und ist gegen ein nachgebautes R2P2 und gegen den Gerätecode von PicoModem selbst getestet. Wenn es mit deinem Board nicht klappt, macht das R2P2 Web Terminal auf <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> dasselbe von Hand.</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Onto a real Pico: R2P2 and prremote",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Onto a real Pico</h2><p>Everything in the last lessons ran on a simulated board. The code was written for a real one, so here is how it gets there. First: which board? Raspberry Pi makes four Picos (as of October 2026):</p><table class='cheat boards'><tr><th>Board</th><th>Chip, RAM, flash</th><th>Radio</th><th>Onboard LED</th></tr><tr><td>Pico</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td>Pico W</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>CYW43439: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth</td><td>on the radio chip</td></tr><tr><td>Pico 2</td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pico 2 W</strong></td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>CYW43439</td><td>on the radio chip</td></tr></table><p>PicoRuby's firmware R2P2 comes as two files. Which one goes on which board, and what works with it:</p><dl class='files'><dt><code>R2P2-FEMTORUBY-…-PICO</code></dt><dd>Pico: pins, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI. Pico W: the same file, without the radio – pins; Wi-Fi only in a build of your own, never BLE.</dd><dt><code>R2P2-PICORUBY-…-PICO2_W</code></dt><dd><strong>Pico 2 W</strong>: everything – Wi-Fi, HTTPS, BLE, dRuby. Pico 2: pins, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI.</dd></dl><p>FemtoRuby is PicoRuby built on the smaller VM of mruby/c, PicoRuby proper is built on mruby's VM – the one this course runs in the browser. The Pico 2 W is the only board with an official file that has everything these lessons used, so get that one.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>A shopping list</h3><table class='cheat'><tr><th>Part</th><th>What for</th></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html' target='_blank'>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 WH</a> (the H: pin headers already soldered on)</td><td>the board; fits into a breadboard</td></tr><tr><td>a micro-USB cable that carries <strong>data</strong></td><td>power, the terminal, copying the firmware; a charging-only cable shows no drive</td></tr><tr><td>a breadboard and jumper wires</td><td>wiring without solder</td></tr><tr><td>LEDs, 330 Ω resistors, a push button</td><td>blinking, the button</td></tr><tr><td>a light-dependent resistor (LDR) and a 10 kΩ resistor, a passive buzzer</td><td>the light sensor on GP26, tones</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-Environment-Sensor' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-Environment-Sensor</a></td><td>temperature, humidity and air pressure (BME280) over I2C</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-ePaper-2.13' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.13</a></td><td>the e-paper display, 250 × 122 pixels</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PicoGo' target='_blank'>Waveshare PicoGo</a></td><td>the robot: motors, infrared sensors, ultrasonic sensor</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-BLE' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-BLE</a> (optional)</td><td>Bluetooth for a Pico without radio</td></tr></table><p>The Waveshare boards plug onto the Pico's pins; they use the pins the bench showed. Not every pair fits together – the PicoGo and the environment sensor both want GP20 and GP21.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>R2P2 step by step</h3><ol><li><strong>Get the firmware.</strong> <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code> downloads the newest R2P2 file (a <code>.uf2</code>), or take it from the releases on GitHub.</li><li><strong>Flash it.</strong> Hold the BOOTSEL button while you plug the Pico in: a drive appears (<code>RP2350</code> on a Pico 2 W). Drag the <code>.uf2</code> onto it; the Pico restarts as a small Ruby computer.</li><li><strong>Talk to it.</strong> Open the R2P2 Web Terminal at <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> in Chrome or Edge – it uses Web Serial, nothing to install. You get R2P2's shell, with <code>ls</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>vim</code> and <code>irb</code>: the IRB of lesson {{nr:pico-blink}}, for real.</li><li><strong>Put files on it.</strong> Dragging <code>.rb</code> files onto the drive is gone since PicoRuby 3.4. Instead: the Web Terminal's upload, the <code>vim</code> on the board, or PicoModem, R2P2's own transfer protocol with a CRC check: <code>picoruby tools/picomodem.rb put app.rb /home/app.rb</code>.</li><li><strong>Start it.</strong> At boot R2P2 waits 2 seconds (press <kbd>s</kbd> to skip), connects to Wi-Fi if <code>/etc/network/wifi.yml</code> exists, and runs <strong><code>/home/app.rb</code></strong>. That is where your program goes.</li><li><strong>Libraries</strong> go into <code>/lib</code>: <code>/lib/epd2in13.rb</code>, loaded with <code>require \"epd2in13\"</code>, because R2P2 sets <code>$LOAD_PATH = [\"/lib\"]</code>. The tabs of the cells here do the same – each tab is written to <code>/lib</code> before the run.</li></ol>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>prremote: compile on the computer, run on the chip</h3><p>R2P2 is a whole Ruby on the board, with a file system and a shell. <strong>prremote</strong> goes the other way. It is a command-line tool by ITO Yosei of LumberMill, Inc. in Takachiho (Miyazaki, Japan), a gem under the MIT licence since May 2026; version 0.4.2 came out on 16 October 2026, and its README warns that it is in early development and that commands may change. It calls itself \"inspired by mpremote for MicroPython\" – mpremote for Ruby.</p><p>prremote puts a tiny <strong>mruby/c</strong> runtime firmware on the board. The Ruby is compiled on your computer, by your own <code>mrbc</code> (it must be mruby 4.x), and the bytecode goes to the board over the USB serial line, at 115200 baud unless you choose otherwise. The two speak a short protocol:</p><ul><li>The computer sends <code>Ctrl-C</code> (the byte 3) every second, until the board answers <code>READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow</code>.</li><li>It sends the bytecode. A compiled mruby file begins with the letters <code>RITE</code>, and that is how the board knows a program is coming. (It watches for three such four-letter words: <code>RITE</code>, <code>DPLY</code>, <code>BORD</code>.) The computer writes in pieces of 256 bytes with 4 ms between them.</li><li>The board answers <code>RUNNING</code>, then whatever the program prints, then <code>DONE</code> – or <code>ERROR</code> and the reason.</li><li>For <code>prremote deploy</code> the computer sends <code>DPLY</code> before the bytecode. The board keeps the program in flash, runs it at every boot, and answers <code>DEPLOYED</code>.</li></ul><figure><img src='assets/pico/prremote-sequence.svg' alt='Sequence diagram: the computer sends Ctrl-C every second until the device answers READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow; then it sends the bytecode, which begins with RITE; the device answers RUNNING, the program's output and DONE, or ERROR and a reason. For prremote deploy the computer sends DPLY and the bytecode, and the device answers DEPLOYED.' width='640'><figcaption>prremote's handshake over the USB serial line</figcaption></figure><p>Here is that dialogue with a pretend device – plain Ruby, no board needed:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "# A pretend device that answers like prremote's runtime firmware.\n# It is still busy at the first Ctrl-C; the second one gets READY.\nkicks = 0\ndevice = lambda do |bytes|\n  if bytes == \"\\x03\"\n    kicks += 1\n    kicks < 2 ? [] : [\"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"RITE\")\n    [\"RUNNING\", \"Hello from Pico W!\", \"DONE\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"DPLY\")\n    [\"DEPLOYED\"]\n  else\n    []\n  end\nend\n\n# the host's side: send something, print what comes back\ntalk = lambda do |bytes, label|\n  puts \"host -> #{label}\"\n  device.call(bytes).each { |line| puts \"  <- #{line}\" }\nend\n\nbytecode = \"RITE...\" # what mrbc made of app.rb\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C\")\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C (one second later)\")\ntalk.call(bytecode, \"the bytecode (prremote run)\")\ntalk.call(\"DPLY\" + bytecode, \"DPLY + bytecode (prremote deploy)\")\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><strong>Several files</strong>: <code>prremote run lib.rb main.rb</code> compiles the files in that order into one bytecode file, so classes and methods from earlier files are there in later ones. mruby/c has no <code>require</code>, and the README recommends this as its replacement. When a script names a board's constant such as <code>M5StickCPlus</code>, prremote even puts that board's library in front by itself. The tabs of this course imitate R2P2's way instead – files in <code>/lib</code> and <code>require</code> – but the idea is the same: the library in the background, your program in front.</p><p><strong>The commands</strong>: <code>install -b picow</code> puts the firmware on the board, <code>run</code> runs files once, <code>watch</code> runs them again on every save, <code>deploy</code> and <code>undeploy</code> keep a program in flash or remove it, <code>eval</code> runs one line, <code>reset</code> sends Ctrl-C, <code>list</code> shows the serial ports and <code>version</code> the versions.</p><p><strong>Its limits</strong>: prremote knows the Pico, the Pico W, the Pico 2 (flashing marked as not yet tested on hardware) and ESP32 boards such as the M5Stack; the Pico 2 W is not on its list. On a Pico W it has pins, ADC, PWM, I2C, SPI, Wi-Fi, NTP and TCP – but no TLS (so no HTTPS) and no BLE. The board has no shell, no file system and no IRB. And it has its own Ruby API, on purpose – it does not follow PicoRuby's names:</p><table class='cheat'><tr><th></th><th>prremote</th><th>PicoRuby (R2P2)</th></tr><tr><td>onboard LED</td><td><code>GPIO.led</code></td><td><code>CYW43::GPIO.new(CYW43::GPIO::LED_PIN)</code></td></tr><tr><td>an output pin</td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, :output)</code></td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, GPIO::OUT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td><code>WiFi.connect(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td><td><code>CYW43.connect_timeout(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>getting the program there</td><td>bytecode into RAM, or flash with <code>deploy</code></td><td>source files in the file system</td></tr></table><p>So the code of these lessons runs on R2P2 as it is, and needs changes for prremote.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><p><strong>R2P2</strong>: the steps above – <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>, BOOTSEL, drag the file, then the Web Terminal.</p><p><strong>prremote</strong>: <code>gem install prremote</code> (Ruby 3.4 or newer) and an <code>mrbc</code> of mruby 4: <code>brew install mruby</code> on a Mac; on Linux build mruby 4 from source, or point <code>MRBC=/path/to/mrbc</code> at one – Debian's mruby package is 3.x, which prremote calls not compatible. Then once <code>prremote install -b picow</code> (hold BOOTSEL, plug in), and from then on <code>prremote watch app.rb</code> – every save runs the file again. <code>prremote deploy lib.rb main.rb</code> keeps it on the board. In WSL2 the USB device needs <code>usbipd attach --wsl</code> first.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/lumbermill/prremote#readme' target='_blank'>the prremote README</a> · <a href='https://picoruby.org/setup' target='_blank'>setting up R2P2</a> · <a href='https://github.com/picoruby/picoruby/blob/master/mrbgems/picoruby-r2p2/README.md' target='_blank'>the R2P2 README</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>run_status(lines)</code>, the way prremote's computer side reads the board's lines: <code>:done</code> once <code>DONE</code> came, <code>\"error: &lt;reason&gt;\"</code> for <code>ERROR &lt;reason&gt;</code>, <code>:running</code> after <code>RUNNING</code> without an end yet, and <code>:waiting</code> otherwise. The last lines of the cell try four dialogues.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def run_status(lines)\n  # DONE -> :done, \"ERROR x\" -> \"error: x\", RUNNING -> :running\n  :waiting\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n",
            "hints": [
              "Keep the state in a variable that starts as <code>:waiting</code>. Walk the lines with <code>each</code> and change the state when a line is <code>DONE</code>, <code>RUNNING</code> or <code>ERROR …</code>."
            ],
            "check": "result == [:done, \"error: NoMethodError\", :running, :waiting] && code.include?(\"def run_status\")",
            "hint": "Go through the lines with <code>each</code> and remember the state: <code>line == \"DONE\"</code>, <code>line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")</code> (the reason is <code>line[6..-1]</code>), <code>line == \"RUNNING\"</code>. Return the state at the end.",
            "solution": "def run_status(lines)\n  status = :waiting\n  lines.each do |line|\n    if line == \"DONE\"\n      status = :done\n    elsif line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")\n      status = \"error: #{line[6..-1]}\"\n    elsif line == \"RUNNING\"\n      status = :running\n    end\n  end\n  status\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Straight from this page</h3><p>Under every bench of these lessons there is a folded panel <strong>🔌 A real Pico (USB)</strong>. With a Pico running R2P2 on a USB cable, it does what the Web Terminal does, from the cell itself:</p><ol><li><strong>Connect a real Pico…</strong> opens the browser's own list of serial ports. It shows R2P2's ports (USB vendor 16C0); R2P2 has three; its shell is normally on the first (the lowest COM or ttyACM number) – if no <code>$&gt;</code> prompt comes, try the next. The page gets exactly the port you choose – nothing else on your computer. Sending needs an R2P2 with PicoModem, as R2P2 4.0.3 has.</li><li>The black box is R2P2's terminal: what the Pico prints, and below it a line to type into, for <code>ls</code> or <code>irb</code>. <strong>Ctrl-C</strong> stops a running program.</li><li><strong>Send this cell to the Pico and run it</strong> presses Ctrl-C (and Ctrl-D, which leaves an <code>irb</code>), writes each tab of the cell to <code>/lib</code> and the code to <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> – with PicoModem, like the Web Terminal's upload, each file checked with CRC-32 – and then types <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> at the prompt <code>$&gt;</code>, which runs it. With the box ticked it becomes <code>/home/app.rb</code>, the program R2P2 starts at power-up. A first line in the file makes <code>require</code> read the tabs anew; <code>show_irb</code>, which only this page has, becomes a comment – type <code>irb</code> at the prompt instead.</li></ol><p><strong>Which browsers</strong>: Web Serial is in Chrome, Edge and Opera on a computer (Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS), not in Firefox or Safari. On Linux your user may have to be in the group <code>dialout</code> to open <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code>. And the page has to be secure: <a href='https://letsruby.com' target='_blank'>letsruby.com</a> over https, or localhost. On an address with plain http the panel only says why it cannot.</p><p><strong>Bluetooth</strong>: in lesson {{nr:pico-ble}} the benches with the laptop also have <strong>📶 A real Pico 2 W (Bluetooth)</strong>: the laptop there then talks to a real Pico 2 W running a BLE::UART program. Web Bluetooth is in Chrome and Edge on Windows, macOS, ChromeOS and Android; on Linux it may have to be switched on in <code>chrome://flags</code>.</p><p><strong>Safe by design</strong>: the page can only reach a device you picked in the browser's own chooser, and only while this tab is open; Disconnect, or closing the tab, ends it. Nothing goes anywhere else: the code goes from this tab over the cable or the radio to your Pico. What runs there is the cell's code, which you can read – and like everything on a Pico it switches the pins you wired, so check the wiring first.</p><p><strong>To be honest</strong>: this connection has not been tried on a real Pico yet. It follows R2P2 4.0.3's sources – the prompt, Ctrl-B for PicoModem, the frames and their checksums – and is tested against a pretend R2P2 and against PicoModem's own device code. If it does not work with your board, the R2P2 Web Terminal at <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> does the same by hand.</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "本物のPicoへ：R2P2とprremote",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>本物のPicoへ</h2><p>これまでのレッスンはすべてシミュレーションのボードで動いていました。でもコードは本物のボードのために書かれています。ここではそこへの載せ方を説明します。まず、どのボードを選ぶのでしょうか。Raspberry PiはPicoを4種類作っています（2026年10月時点）。</p><table class='cheat boards'><tr><th>ボード</th><th>チップ、RAM、フラッシュ</th><th>無線</th><th>ボード上のLED</th></tr><tr><td>Pico</td><td>RP2040、264 kB、2 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td>Pico W</td><td>RP2040、264 kB、2 MB</td><td>CYW43439：Wi-Fi、Bluetooth</td><td>無線チップにつながっている</td></tr><tr><td>Pico 2</td><td>RP2350、520 kB、4 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pico 2 W</strong></td><td>RP2350、520 kB、4 MB</td><td>CYW43439</td><td>無線チップにつながっている</td></tr></table><p>PicoRubyのファームウェアR2P2は2つのファイルで配布されています。どのボードにどちらを入れるか、そして何ができるかは次のとおりです。</p><dl class='files'><dt><code>R2P2-FEMTORUBY-…-PICO</code></dt><dd>Pico：ピン、PWM、ADC、I2C、SPI。Pico W：同じファイル（無線なし）で、ピンが使えます。Wi-Fiは自分でビルドしたときだけ、BLEは使えません。</dd><dt><code>R2P2-PICORUBY-…-PICO2_W</code></dt><dd><strong>Pico 2 W</strong>：すべて（Wi-Fi、HTTPS、BLE、dRuby）。Pico 2：ピン、PWM、ADC、I2C、SPI。</dd></dl><p>FemtoRubyはmruby/cの小さなVMの上に作ったPicoRubyで、PicoRuby本体はmrubyのVMの上で動きます。このコースがブラウザーで動かしているのと同じVMです。これらのレッスンで使ったものがすべてそろった公式ファイルがあるのはPico 2 Wだけなので、それを選びましょう。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>買い物リスト</h3><table class='cheat'><tr><th>部品</th><th>用途</th></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html' target='_blank'>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 WH</a>（Hはピンヘッダーがはんだ付け済みという意味）</td><td>ボード本体。ブレッドボードに挿せる</td></tr><tr><td><strong>データ</strong>を通すmicro-USBケーブル</td><td>電源、ターミナル、ファームウェアのコピー。充電専用のケーブルではドライブが出てこない</td></tr><tr><td>ブレッドボードとジャンパーワイヤー</td><td>はんだ付けなしで配線する</td></tr><tr><td>LED、330 Ωの抵抗、押しボタン</td><td>点滅、ボタン</td></tr><tr><td>光センサー（LDR）と10 kΩの抵抗、パッシブブザー</td><td>GP26の光センサー、音</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-Environment-Sensor' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-Environment-Sensor</a></td><td>I2Cで温度・湿度・気圧（BME280）</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-ePaper-2.13' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.13</a></td><td>電子ペーパーディスプレイ、250 × 122ピクセル</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PicoGo' target='_blank'>Waveshare PicoGo</a></td><td>ロボット：モーター、赤外線センサー、超音波センサー</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-BLE' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-BLE</a>（なくてもよい）</td><td>無線のないPicoにBluetoothを</td></tr></table><p>WaveshareのボードはPicoのピンに挿して使います。使うピンは作業台で見たとおりです。どれでも組み合わせられるわけではありません。PicoGoと環境センサーはどちらもGP20とGP21を使います。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>R2P2をひとつずつ</h3><ol><li><strong>ファームウェアを入手します。</strong><code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>で最新のR2P2ファイル（<code>.uf2</code>）をダウンロードできます。GitHubのリリースから取ってきてもかまいません。</li><li><strong>書き込みます。</strong>BOOTSELボタンを押したままPicoをつなぐと、ドライブが現れます（Pico 2 Wでは<code>RP2350</code>）。そこへ<code>.uf2</code>をドラッグすると、Picoは小さなRubyコンピューターとして再起動します。</li><li><strong>話しかけます。</strong>ChromeかEdgeで<a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a>のR2P2 Web Terminalを開きます。Web Serialを使うので、インストールは不要です。R2P2のシェルが使えて、<code>ls</code>、<code>cat</code>、<code>vim</code>、<code>irb</code>があります。レッスン{{nr:pico-blink}}のIRBの本物です。</li><li><strong>ファイルを載せます。</strong><code>.rb</code>ファイルをドライブにドラッグする方法は、PicoRuby 3.4からなくなりました。代わりにWeb Terminalのアップロード、ボード上の<code>vim</code>、またはR2P2独自のCRCチェック付き転送プロトコルPicoModemを使います：<code>picoruby tools/picomodem.rb put app.rb /home/app.rb</code></li><li><strong>起動します。</strong>電源を入れるとR2P2は2秒待ち（<kbd>s</kbd>で飛ばせます）、<code>/etc/network/wifi.yml</code>があればWi-Fiにつなぎ、<strong><code>/home/app.rb</code></strong>を実行します。あなたのプログラムはそこに置きます。</li><li><strong>ライブラリ</strong>は<code>/lib</code>に置きます。<code>/lib/epd2in13.rb</code>なら<code>require \"epd2in13\"</code>で読み込めます。R2P2が<code>$LOAD_PATH = [\"/lib\"]</code>を設定するからです。ここのセルのタブも同じことをしています。実行の前に、タブがひとつずつ<code>/lib</code>に書き込まれます。</li></ol>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>prremote：コンピューターでコンパイルして、チップで実行する</h3><p>R2P2はファイルシステムとシェルを備えた、ボード上の完全なRubyです。<strong>prremote</strong>は逆の道を行きます。宮崎県高千穂町のLumberMill, Inc.のITO Yoseiさんが作ったコマンドラインツールで、2026年5月からMITライセンスのgemとして公開されています。バージョン0.4.2は2026年10月16日に出ました。READMEには、まだ開発の初期段階でコマンドが変わるかもしれないと書かれています。自身を「inspired by mpremote for MicroPython」と説明していて、いわばRuby版のmpremoteです。</p><p>prremoteはボードに小さな<strong>mruby/c</strong>のランタイムファームウェアを書き込みます。Rubyはあなたのコンピューターで、あなたの<code>mrbc</code>（mruby 4.xが必要です）がコンパイルし、バイトコードはUSBシリアル回線でボードへ送られます（速度は標準で115200ボーです）。両者は短いプロトコルで話します。</p><ul><li>コンピューターは、ボードが<code>READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow</code>と答えるまで、1秒ごとに<code>Ctrl-C</code>（バイト値3）を送ります。</li><li>次にバイトコードを送ります。コンパイルしたmrubyのファイルは<code>RITE</code>という文字で始まり、ボードはそれでプログラムが来たとわかります（ボードは<code>RITE</code>、<code>DPLY</code>、<code>BORD</code>という4文字の合図を見張っています）。コンピューターは256バイトずつ、間に4ミリ秒空けて書き込みます。</li><li>ボードは<code>RUNNING</code>、プログラムの出力、そして<code>DONE</code>を返します。失敗したときは<code>ERROR</code>と理由を返します。</li><li><code>prremote deploy</code>では、コンピューターはバイトコードの前に<code>DPLY</code>を送ります。ボードはプログラムをフラッシュに保存して起動のたびに実行し、<code>DEPLOYED</code>と答えます。</li></ul><figure><img src='assets/pico/prremote-sequence.svg' alt='シーケンス図：コンピューターはデバイスがREADY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picowと答えるまで、1秒ごとにCtrl-Cを送ります。次にRITEで始まるバイトコードを送ります。デバイスはRUNNING、プログラムの出力、DONEを返すか、ERRORと理由を返します。prremote deployではコンピューターがDPLYとバイトコードを送り、デバイスはDEPLOYEDと答えます。' width='640'><figcaption>USBシリアル回線の上のprremoteのハンドシェイク</figcaption></figure><p>そのやりとりを、見せかけのデバイスで再現してみましょう。ボードのいらない、ただのRubyです。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "# prremoteのランタイムファームウェアのように答える、見せかけのデバイス\n# 最初のCtrl-Cではまだ忙しく、2回目でREADYを返す\nkicks = 0\ndevice = lambda do |bytes|\n  if bytes == \"\\x03\"\n    kicks += 1\n    kicks < 2 ? [] : [\"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"RITE\")\n    [\"RUNNING\", \"Hello from Pico W!\", \"DONE\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"DPLY\")\n    [\"DEPLOYED\"]\n  else\n    []\n  end\nend\n\n# ホスト側：何かを送り、返ってきたものを表示する\ntalk = lambda do |bytes, label|\n  puts \"host -> #{label}\"\n  device.call(bytes).each { |line| puts \"  <- #{line}\" }\nend\n\nbytecode = \"RITE...\" # mrbcがapp.rbから作ったもの\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C\")\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C (one second later)\")\ntalk.call(bytecode, \"the bytecode (prremote run)\")\ntalk.call(\"DPLY\" + bytecode, \"DPLY + bytecode (prremote deploy)\")\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><strong>複数のファイル</strong>：<code>prremote run lib.rb main.rb</code>は、ファイルをこの順に1つのバイトコードファイルへコンパイルします。そのため、前のファイルのクラスやメソッドを後のファイルで使えます。mruby/cには<code>require</code>がなく、READMEはこれをその代わりとして勧めています。スクリプトが<code>M5StickCPlus</code>のようなボードの定数を使うと、prremoteはそのボードのライブラリを自動で前に付けてくれます。このコースのタブは代わりにR2P2のやり方（<code>/lib</code>のファイルと<code>require</code>）をまねていますが、考え方は同じです。ライブラリは裏に、あなたのプログラムは表に置きます。</p><p><strong>コマンド</strong>：<code>install -b picow</code>はファームウェアを書き込み、<code>run</code>はファイルを1回実行し、<code>watch</code>は保存するたびに実行し直します。<code>deploy</code>と<code>undeploy</code>はプログラムをフラッシュに保存したり消したりします。<code>eval</code>は1行を実行し、<code>reset</code>はCtrl-Cを送り、<code>list</code>はシリアルポートを、<code>version</code>はバージョンを表示します。</p><p><strong>限界</strong>：prremoteが対応しているのはPico、Pico W、Pico 2（書き込みは実機で未確認と書かれています）と、M5StackなどのESP32ボードです。Pico 2 Wはリストにありません。Pico Wではピン、ADC、PWM、I2C、SPI、Wi-Fi、NTP、TCPが使えますが、TLS（つまりHTTPS）とBLEはありません。ボードにはシェルもファイルシステムもIRBもありません。そして、わざと独自のRuby APIを持っていて、PicoRubyの名前には合わせていません。</p><table class='cheat'><tr><th></th><th>prremote</th><th>PicoRuby（R2P2）</th></tr><tr><td>ボード上のLED</td><td><code>GPIO.led</code></td><td><code>CYW43::GPIO.new(CYW43::GPIO::LED_PIN)</code></td></tr><tr><td>出力ピン</td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, :output)</code></td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, GPIO::OUT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td><code>WiFi.connect(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td><td><code>CYW43.connect_timeout(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>プログラムの載せ方</td><td>バイトコードをRAMへ、または<code>deploy</code>でフラッシュへ</td><td>ファイルシステムのソースファイル</td></tr></table><p>つまり、これらのレッスンのコードはR2P2ではそのまま動き、prremoteでは書き直しが必要です。</p><div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><p><strong>R2P2</strong>：上の手順のとおりです。<code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>、BOOTSEL、ファイルのドラッグ、そしてWeb Terminalです。</p><p><strong>prremote</strong>：<code>gem install prremote</code>（Ruby 3.4以上）と、mruby 4の<code>mrbc</code>が必要です。Macなら<code>brew install mruby</code>です。Linuxではmruby 4をソースからビルドするか、<code>MRBC=/path/to/mrbc</code>で指定します。Debianのmrubyパッケージは3.xで、prremoteは互換性がないとしています。最初に一度<code>prremote install -b picow</code>（BOOTSELを押したままつなぐ）を実行し、あとは<code>prremote watch app.rb</code>を使います。保存するたびにファイルが実行し直されます。<code>prremote deploy lib.rb main.rb</code>でボードに残せます。WSL2では、先に<code>usbipd attach --wsl</code>でUSBデバイスをつなぐ必要があります。</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://github.com/lumbermill/prremote#readme' target='_blank'>prremoteのREADME（英語）</a>・<a href='https://picoruby.org/setup' target='_blank'>R2P2のセットアップ（英語）</a>・<a href='https://github.com/picoruby/picoruby/blob/master/mrbgems/picoruby-r2p2/README.md' target='_blank'>R2P2のREADME（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong> prremoteのコンピューター側がボードの行を読むように、<code>run_status(lines)</code>を書きましょう。<code>DONE</code>が来たら<code>:done</code>、<code>ERROR &lt;reason&gt;</code>なら<code>\"error: &lt;reason&gt;\"</code>、<code>RUNNING</code>のあとまだ終わっていなければ<code>:running</code>、それ以外は<code>:waiting</code>を返します。セルの最後の数行で4つのやりとりを試します。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def run_status(lines)\n  # DONEなら:done、\"ERROR x\"なら\"error: x\"、RUNNINGなら:running\n  :waiting\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n",
            "hints": [
              "状態を変数に持っておいて、最初は<code>:waiting</code>にしてね。<code>each</code>で行をたどって、<code>DONE</code>、<code>RUNNING</code>、<code>ERROR …</code>の行が来たら状態を変えるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "result == [:done, \"error: NoMethodError\", :running, :waiting] && code.include?(\"def run_status\")",
            "hint": "<code>each</code>で行をたどって、状態を覚えておこう：<code>line == \"DONE\"</code>、<code>line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")</code>（理由は<code>line[6..-1]</code>）、<code>line == \"RUNNING\"</code>。最後にその状態を返せばいいよ。",
            "solution": "def run_status(lines)\n  status = :waiting\n  lines.each do |line|\n    if line == \"DONE\"\n      status = :done\n    elsif line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")\n      status = \"error: #{line[6..-1]}\"\n    elsif line == \"RUNNING\"\n      status = :running\n    end\n  end\n  status\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>このページから直接</h3><p>これらのレッスンの作業台の下には、折りたたまれた<strong>🔌 本物のPico（USB）</strong>の欄があります。R2P2が入ったPicoをUSBケーブルでつなぐと、Web Terminalと同じことをセルから直接できます。</p><ol><li><strong>本物のPicoに接続…</strong>を押すと、ブラウザー自身のシリアルポート一覧が開きます。一覧に出るのはR2P2のポート（USBベンダー16C0）です。R2P2のポートは3つあり、シェルはふつう最初のポート（COMやttyACMの番号がいちばん小さいもの）にあります。<code>$&gt;</code>プロンプトが出ないときは、次のポートを試してください。ページが使えるのは選んだポートだけで、コンピューターのほかのものには触れません。送るには、R2P2 4.0.3のようにPicoModemを持つR2P2が必要です。</li><li>黒い枠はR2P2の端末です。Picoが出力したものが表示され、その下の行に<code>ls</code>や<code>irb</code>を入力できます。<strong>Ctrl-C</strong>で動いているプログラムが止まります。</li><li><strong>このセルをPicoに送って実行</strong>は、Ctrl-C（と、<code>irb</code>から抜けるCtrl-D）を送り、セルの各タブを<code>/lib</code>に、コードを<code>/home/chunky.rb</code>に書き込みます。書き込みにはWeb Terminalのアップロードと同じPicoModemを使い、ファイルごとにCRC-32で確かめます。そのあとプロンプト<code>$&gt;</code>で<code>/home/chunky.rb</code>と入力するので、プログラムが動きます。チェックを入れると<code>/home/app.rb</code>になり、R2P2が電源を入れたときに起動するプログラムになります。ファイルの最初の1行で、<code>require</code>がタブを読み直すようにしています。このページにしかない<code>show_irb</code>はコメントになるので、代わりにプロンプトで<code>irb</code>と入力してください。</li></ol><p><strong>使えるブラウザー</strong>：Web Serialは、パソコン（Windows、macOS、Linux、ChromeOS）のChrome、Edge、Operaにあります。FirefoxとSafariにはありません。Linuxでは、<code>/dev/ttyACM0</code>を開くためにユーザーを<code>dialout</code>グループに入れる必要があるかもしれません。また、ページが安全である必要があります。httpsの<a href='https://letsruby.com' target='_blank'>letsruby.com</a>か、localhostです。ただのhttpのアドレスでは、欄は使えない理由を表示するだけです。</p><p><strong>Bluetooth</strong>：レッスン{{nr:pico-ble}}では、ノートPCのある作業台に<strong>📶 本物のPico 2 W（Bluetooth）</strong>の欄もあります。そこのノートPCが、BLE::UARTのプログラムが動いている本物のPico 2 Wと話します。Web Bluetoothは、Windows、macOS、ChromeOS、AndroidのChromeとEdgeにあります。Linuxでは<code>chrome://flags</code>で有効にする必要があるかもしれません。</p><p><strong>安全のしくみ</strong>：ページが使えるのは、ブラウザーの選択画面であなたが選んだデバイスだけで、このタブが開いている間だけです。「切断」を押すかタブを閉じると終わります。ほかのどこかへ送られるものはありません。コードはこのタブから、ケーブルか電波であなたのPicoへ行くだけです。Picoで動くのはセルのコードで、中身を読むことができます。Picoで動くものはどれも、配線したピンを切り替えます。先に配線を確かめてください。</p><p><strong>正直に言うと</strong>：この接続は、まだ本物のPicoで試していません。R2P2 4.0.3のソース（プロンプト、PicoModemのためのCtrl-B、フレームとそのチェックサム）に従って作り、まねしたR2P2と、PicoModem自身のデバイス側のコードに対してテストしています。手元のボードでうまく動かないときは、<a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a>のR2P2 Web Terminalで同じことを手でできます。</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Sur un vrai Pico : R2P2 et prremote",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Sur un vrai Pico</h2><p>Tout ce qui a été fait dans les dernières leçons a tourné sur une carte simulée. Le code a été écrit pour une vraie carte ; voici donc comment l'y installer. D'abord : quelle carte ? Raspberry Pi fabrique quatre Pico (en octobre 2026) :</p><table class='cheat boards'><tr><th>Carte</th><th>Puce, RAM, flash</th><th>Radio</th><th>LED intégrée</th></tr><tr><td>Pico</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td>Pico W</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>CYW43439 : Wi-Fi, Bluetooth</td><td>sur la puce radio</td></tr><tr><td>Pico 2</td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pico 2 W</strong></td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>CYW43439</td><td>sur la puce radio</td></tr></table><p>Le firmware R2P2 de PicoRuby se présente sous la forme de deux fichiers. Lequel va sur quelle carte, et ce qui fonctionne avec :</p><dl class='files'><dt><code>R2P2-FEMTORUBY-…-PICO</code></dt><dd>Pico : broches, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI. Pico W : le même fichier, sans la radio – les broches ; le Wi-Fi seulement avec un build personnel, jamais BLE.</dd><dt><code>R2P2-PICORUBY-…-PICO2_W</code></dt><dd><strong>Pico 2 W</strong> : tout – Wi-Fi, HTTPS, BLE, dRuby. Pico 2 : broches, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI.</dd></dl><p>FemtoRuby est PicoRuby construit sur la VM plus petite de mruby/c ; PicoRuby proprement dit est construit sur la VM de mruby – celle que ce cours fait tourner dans le navigateur. Le Pico 2 W est la seule carte disposant d'un fichier officiel qui contient tout ce que ces leçons ont utilisé : c'est donc celle-là qu'il vous faut.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Une liste d'achats</h3><table class='cheat'><tr><th>Composant</th><th>Pour quoi faire</th></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html' target='_blank'>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 WH</a> (le H : connecteurs à broches déjà soudés)</td><td>la carte ; s'enfiche dans une plaque d'essai</td></tr><tr><td>un câble micro-USB qui transmet les <strong>données</strong></td><td>l'alimentation, le terminal, la copie du firmware ; un câble de charge seule ne fait apparaître aucun lecteur</td></tr><tr><td>une plaque d'essai (breadboard) et des fils de connexion</td><td>câbler sans soudure</td></tr><tr><td>des LED, des résistances de 330 Ω, un bouton-poussoir</td><td>le clignotement, le bouton</td></tr><tr><td>une photorésistance (LDR) et une résistance de 10 kΩ, un buzzer passif</td><td>le capteur de lumière sur GP26, les sons</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-Environment-Sensor' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-Environment-Sensor</a></td><td>température, humidité et pression atmosphérique (BME280) via I2C</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-ePaper-2.13' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.13</a></td><td>l'écran à papier électronique, 250 × 122 pixels</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PicoGo' target='_blank'>Waveshare PicoGo</a></td><td>le robot : moteurs, capteurs infrarouges, capteur à ultrasons</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-BLE' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-BLE</a> (facultatif)</td><td>le Bluetooth pour un Pico sans radio</td></tr></table><p>Les cartes Waveshare s'enfichent sur les broches du Pico ; elles utilisent les broches que montrait le banc d'essai. Toutes les combinaisons ne sont pas compatibles – le PicoGo et le capteur d'environnement veulent tous deux GP20 et GP21.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>R2P2 pas à pas</h3><ol><li><strong>Récupérer le firmware.</strong> <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code> télécharge le fichier R2P2 le plus récent (un <code>.uf2</code>), ou prenez-le dans les releases sur GitHub.</li><li><strong>Le flasher.</strong> Maintenez le bouton BOOTSEL enfoncé pendant que vous branchez le Pico : un lecteur apparaît (<code>RP2350</code> sur un Pico 2 W). Faites-y glisser le <code>.uf2</code> ; le Pico redémarre en petit ordinateur Ruby.</li><li><strong>Dialoguer avec lui.</strong> Ouvrez le Web Terminal R2P2 à l'adresse <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> dans Chrome ou Edge – il utilise Web Serial, rien à installer. Vous obtenez le shell de R2P2, avec <code>ls</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>vim</code> et <code>irb</code> : l'IRB de la leçon {{nr:pico-blink}}, pour de vrai.</li><li><strong>Y copier des fichiers.</strong> Le glisser-déposer de fichiers <code>.rb</code> sur le lecteur n'existe plus depuis PicoRuby 3.4. À la place : le téléversement du Web Terminal, le <code>vim</code> de la carte, ou PicoModem, le protocole de transfert propre à R2P2 avec contrôle CRC : <code>picoruby tools/picomodem.rb put app.rb /home/app.rb</code>.</li><li><strong>Le lancer.</strong> Au démarrage, R2P2 attend 2 secondes (appuyez sur <kbd>s</kbd> pour passer), se connecte au Wi-Fi si <code>/etc/network/wifi.yml</code> existe, et exécute <strong><code>/home/app.rb</code></strong>. C'est là que va votre programme.</li><li><strong>Les bibliothèques</strong> vont dans <code>/lib</code> : <code>/lib/epd2in13.rb</code>, chargée avec <code>require \"epd2in13\"</code>, car R2P2 définit <code>$LOAD_PATH = [\"/lib\"]</code>. Les onglets des cellules ici font de même – chaque onglet est écrit dans <code>/lib</code> avant l'exécution.</li></ol>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>prremote : compiler sur l'ordinateur, exécuter sur la puce</h3><p>R2P2, c'est un Ruby complet sur la carte, avec un système de fichiers et un shell. <strong>prremote</strong> prend le chemin inverse. C'est un outil en ligne de commande d'ITO Yosei, de LumberMill, Inc. à Takachiho (Miyazaki, Japon), une gem sous licence MIT depuis mai 2026 ; la version 0.4.2 est sortie le 16 octobre 2026, et son README avertit qu'il est en début de développement et que les commandes peuvent changer. Il se présente comme « inspired by mpremote for MicroPython » – un mpremote pour Ruby.</p><p>prremote installe sur la carte un minuscule firmware d'exécution <strong>mruby/c</strong>. Le Ruby est compilé sur votre ordinateur, par votre propre <code>mrbc</code> (ce doit être mruby 4.x), et le bytecode est envoyé à la carte via la liaison série USB, à 115200 bauds sauf indication contraire. Les deux parlent un court protocole :</p><ul><li>L'ordinateur envoie <code>Ctrl-C</code> (l'octet 3) toutes les secondes, jusqu'à ce que la carte réponde <code>READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow</code>.</li><li>Il envoie le bytecode. Un fichier mruby compilé commence par les lettres <code>RITE</code>, et c'est ainsi que la carte sait qu'un programme arrive. (Elle guette trois mots de quatre lettres de ce genre : <code>RITE</code>, <code>DPLY</code>, <code>BORD</code>.) L'ordinateur écrit par morceaux de 256 octets, avec 4 ms d'intervalle.</li><li>La carte répond <code>RUNNING</code>, puis tout ce que le programme affiche, puis <code>DONE</code> – ou <code>ERROR</code> et la raison.</li><li>Pour <code>prremote deploy</code>, l'ordinateur envoie <code>DPLY</code> avant le bytecode. La carte garde le programme en mémoire flash, l'exécute à chaque démarrage, et répond <code>DEPLOYED</code>.</li></ul><figure><img src='assets/pico/prremote-sequence.svg' alt=\"Diagramme de séquence : l'ordinateur envoie Ctrl-C toutes les secondes jusqu'à ce que l'appareil réponde READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow ; il envoie ensuite le bytecode, qui commence par RITE ; l'appareil répond RUNNING, la sortie du programme et DONE, ou ERROR et une raison. Pour prremote deploy, l'ordinateur envoie DPLY et le bytecode, et l'appareil répond DEPLOYED.\" width='640'><figcaption>La poignée de main de prremote sur la liaison série USB</figcaption></figure><p>Voici ce dialogue avec un appareil factice – du Ruby ordinaire, sans carte :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "# Un appareil factice qui répond comme le firmware d'exécution de prremote.\n# Il est encore occupé au premier Ctrl-C ; le deuxième obtient READY.\nkicks = 0\ndevice = lambda do |bytes|\n  if bytes == \"\\x03\"\n    kicks += 1\n    kicks < 2 ? [] : [\"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"RITE\")\n    [\"RUNNING\", \"Hello from Pico W!\", \"DONE\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"DPLY\")\n    [\"DEPLOYED\"]\n  else\n    []\n  end\nend\n\n# le côté hôte : envoyer quelque chose, afficher ce qui revient\ntalk = lambda do |bytes, label|\n  puts \"host -> #{label}\"\n  device.call(bytes).each { |line| puts \"  <- #{line}\" }\nend\n\nbytecode = \"RITE...\" # ce que mrbc a fait de app.rb\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C\")\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C (one second later)\")\ntalk.call(bytecode, \"the bytecode (prremote run)\")\ntalk.call(\"DPLY\" + bytecode, \"DPLY + bytecode (prremote deploy)\")\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><strong>Plusieurs fichiers</strong> : <code>prremote run lib.rb main.rb</code> compile les fichiers dans cet ordre en un seul fichier de bytecode ; les classes et méthodes des premiers fichiers sont donc disponibles dans les suivants. mruby/c n'a pas de <code>require</code>, et le README recommande ceci pour le remplacer. Quand un script mentionne la constante d'une carte, comme <code>M5StickCPlus</code>, prremote place même tout seul la bibliothèque de cette carte en tête. Les onglets de ce cours imitent plutôt la méthode de R2P2 – des fichiers dans <code>/lib</code> et <code>require</code> – mais l'idée est la même : la bibliothèque en arrière-plan, votre programme au premier plan.</p><p><strong>Les commandes</strong> : <code>install -b picow</code> installe le firmware sur la carte, <code>run</code> exécute des fichiers une fois, <code>watch</code> les réexécute à chaque enregistrement, <code>deploy</code> et <code>undeploy</code> conservent un programme en flash ou l'en retirent, <code>eval</code> exécute une ligne, <code>reset</code> envoie Ctrl-C, <code>list</code> affiche les ports série et <code>version</code> les versions.</p><p><strong>Ses limites</strong> : prremote connaît le Pico, le Pico W, le Pico 2 (le flashage étant signalé comme pas encore testé sur du matériel) et des cartes ESP32 comme le M5Stack ; le Pico 2 W ne figure pas sur sa liste. Sur un Pico W, il gère les broches, l'ADC, la PWM, l'I2C, le SPI, le Wi-Fi, NTP et TCP – mais pas TLS (donc pas de HTTPS) ni BLE. La carte n'a ni shell, ni système de fichiers, ni IRB. Et il a sa propre API Ruby, volontairement – il ne suit pas les noms de PicoRuby :</p><table class='cheat'><tr><th></th><th>prremote</th><th>PicoRuby (R2P2)</th></tr><tr><td>LED intégrée</td><td><code>GPIO.led</code></td><td><code>CYW43::GPIO.new(CYW43::GPIO::LED_PIN)</code></td></tr><tr><td>une broche de sortie</td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, :output)</code></td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, GPIO::OUT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td><code>WiFi.connect(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td><td><code>CYW43.connect_timeout(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>installer le programme</td><td>bytecode en RAM, ou en flash avec <code>deploy</code></td><td>fichiers source dans le système de fichiers</td></tr></table><p>Le code de ces leçons tourne donc tel quel sur R2P2, et doit être adapté pour prremote.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><p><strong>R2P2</strong> : les étapes ci-dessus – <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>, BOOTSEL, glisser le fichier, puis le Web Terminal.</p><p><strong>prremote</strong> : <code>gem install prremote</code> (Ruby 3.4 ou plus récent) et un <code>mrbc</code> de mruby 4 : <code>brew install mruby</code> sur Mac ; sous Linux, compilez mruby 4 depuis les sources, ou faites pointer <code>MRBC=/path/to/mrbc</code> vers un exécutable existant – le paquet mruby de Debian est en 3.x, que prremote déclare incompatible. Ensuite, une seule fois, <code>prremote install -b picow</code> (maintenez BOOTSEL, branchez), puis à partir de là <code>prremote watch app.rb</code> – chaque enregistrement réexécute le fichier. <code>prremote deploy lib.rb main.rb</code> le conserve sur la carte. Sous WSL2, le périphérique USB nécessite d'abord <code>usbipd attach --wsl</code>.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://github.com/lumbermill/prremote#readme' target='_blank'>le README de prremote</a> · <a href='https://picoruby.org/setup' target='_blank'>installer R2P2</a> · <a href='https://github.com/picoruby/picoruby/blob/master/mrbgems/picoruby-r2p2/README.md' target='_blank'>le README de R2P2</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> écrivez <code>run_status(lines)</code>, à la manière dont le côté ordinateur de prremote lit les lignes de la carte : <code>:done</code> dès que <code>DONE</code> est arrivé, <code>\"error: &lt;reason&gt;\"</code> pour <code>ERROR &lt;reason&gt;</code>, <code>:running</code> après <code>RUNNING</code> sans fin pour l'instant, et <code>:waiting</code> sinon. Les dernières lignes de la cellule essaient quatre dialogues.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def run_status(lines)\n  # DONE -> :done, \"ERROR x\" -> \"error: x\", RUNNING -> :running\n  :waiting\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n",
            "hints": [
              "Gardez l'état dans une variable qui commence à <code>:waiting</code>. Parcourez les lignes avec <code>each</code> et changez l'état quand une ligne vaut <code>DONE</code>, <code>RUNNING</code> ou <code>ERROR …</code>."
            ],
            "check": "result == [:done, \"error: NoMethodError\", :running, :waiting] && code.include?(\"def run_status\")",
            "hint": "Parcourez les lignes avec <code>each</code> et retenez l'état : <code>line == \"DONE\"</code>, <code>line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")</code> (la raison est <code>line[6..-1]</code>), <code>line == \"RUNNING\"</code>. Renvoyez l'état à la fin.",
            "solution": "def run_status(lines)\n  status = :waiting\n  lines.each do |line|\n    if line == \"DONE\"\n      status = :done\n    elsif line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")\n      status = \"error: #{line[6..-1]}\"\n    elsif line == \"RUNNING\"\n      status = :running\n    end\n  end\n  status\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Directement depuis cette page</h3><p>Sous chaque banc d'essai de ces leçons se trouve un panneau replié <strong>🔌 Un vrai Pico (USB)</strong>. Avec un Pico sous R2P2 relié par un câble USB, il fait ce que fait le Web Terminal, depuis la cellule elle-même :</p><ol><li><strong>Connecter un vrai Pico…</strong> ouvre la liste des ports série propre au navigateur. Elle affiche les ports de R2P2 (fabricant USB 16C0) ; R2P2 en a trois ; son shell est normalement sur le premier (le numéro COM ou ttyACM le plus bas) – si aucune invite <code>$&gt;</code> n'apparaît, essayez le suivant. La page obtient exactement le port que vous choisissez – rien d'autre sur votre ordinateur. L'envoi nécessite un R2P2 avec PicoModem, comme R2P2 4.0.3.</li><li>Le cadre noir est le terminal de R2P2 : ce que le Pico affiche, et en dessous une ligne de saisie, pour <code>ls</code> ou <code>irb</code>. <strong>Ctrl-C</strong> arrête un programme en cours.</li><li><strong>Envoyer cette cellule au Pico et l'exécuter</strong> appuie sur Ctrl-C (et Ctrl-D, qui quitte un <code>irb</code>), écrit chaque onglet de la cellule dans <code>/lib</code> et le code dans <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> – avec PicoModem, comme le téléversement du Web Terminal, chaque fichier étant vérifié par CRC-32 – puis tape <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> à l'invite <code>$&gt;</code>, ce qui l'exécute. Avec la case cochée, cela devient <code>/home/app.rb</code>, le programme que R2P2 lance à la mise sous tension. Une première ligne dans le fichier oblige <code>require</code> à relire les onglets ; <code>show_irb</code>, qui n'existe que sur cette page, devient un commentaire – tapez plutôt <code>irb</code> à l'invite.</li></ol><p><strong>Quels navigateurs</strong> : Web Serial est disponible dans Chrome, Edge et Opera sur ordinateur (Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS), pas dans Firefox ni Safari. Sous Linux, votre utilisateur doit parfois faire partie du groupe <code>dialout</code> pour ouvrir <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code>. Et la page doit être sécurisée : <a href='https://letsruby.com' target='_blank'>letsruby.com</a> en https, ou localhost. Sur une adresse en http simple, le panneau se contente d'expliquer pourquoi il ne peut pas fonctionner.</p><p><strong>Bluetooth</strong> : dans la leçon {{nr:pico-ble}}, les bancs d'essai avec l'ordinateur portable proposent aussi <strong>📶 Un vrai Pico 2 W (Bluetooth)</strong> : l'ordinateur portable y dialogue alors avec un vrai Pico 2 W qui exécute un programme BLE::UART. Web Bluetooth est disponible dans Chrome et Edge sous Windows, macOS, ChromeOS et Android ; sous Linux, il faut parfois l'activer dans <code>chrome://flags</code>.</p><p><strong>Sûr par conception</strong> : la page ne peut atteindre qu'un appareil que vous avez choisi dans le sélecteur propre au navigateur, et seulement tant que cet onglet est ouvert ; Déconnecter, ou fermer l'onglet, y met fin. Rien ne part ailleurs : le code va de cet onglet à votre Pico, par le câble ou par la radio. Ce qui s'y exécute, c'est le code de la cellule, que vous pouvez lire – et comme tout ce qui tourne sur un Pico, il commute les broches que vous avez câblées ; vérifiez donc d'abord le câblage.</p><p><strong>Soyons honnêtes</strong> : cette connexion n'a pas encore été essayée sur un vrai Pico. Elle suit les sources de R2P2 4.0.3 – l'invite, Ctrl-B pour PicoModem, les trames et leurs sommes de contrôle – et elle est testée avec un R2P2 factice et avec le code côté appareil de PicoModem lui-même. Si elle ne fonctionne pas avec votre carte, le Web Terminal R2P2 à l'adresse <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> fait la même chose à la main.</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Su un Pico reale: R2P2 e prremote",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Su un Pico reale</h2><p>Tutto nelle ultime lezioni girava su una scheda simulata. Il codice però è stato scritto per una scheda reale, ed ecco come arrivarci. Prima di tutto: quale scheda? Raspberry Pi produce quattro Pico (a ottobre 2026):</p><table class='cheat boards'><tr><th>Scheda</th><th>Chip, RAM, flash</th><th>Radio</th><th>LED integrato</th></tr><tr><td>Pico</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td>Pico W</td><td>RP2040, 264 kB, 2 MB</td><td>CYW43439: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth</td><td>sul chip radio</td></tr><tr><td>Pico 2</td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>–</td><td>GP25</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pico 2 W</strong></td><td>RP2350, 520 kB, 4 MB</td><td>CYW43439</td><td>sul chip radio</td></tr></table><p>Il firmware R2P2 di PicoRuby è disponibile in due file. Quale va su quale scheda, e che cosa funziona con esso:</p><dl class='files'><dt><code>R2P2-FEMTORUBY-…-PICO</code></dt><dd>Pico: pin, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI. Pico W: lo stesso file, senza la radio – pin; Wi-Fi solo in una build compilata da te, mai il BLE.</dd><dt><code>R2P2-PICORUBY-…-PICO2_W</code></dt><dd><strong>Pico 2 W</strong>: tutto – Wi-Fi, HTTPS, BLE, dRuby. Pico 2: pin, PWM, ADC, I2C, SPI.</dd></dl><p>FemtoRuby è PicoRuby costruito sulla VM più piccola di mruby/c, mentre PicoRuby vero e proprio è costruito sulla VM di mruby – quella che questo corso esegue nel browser. Il Pico 2 W è l'unica scheda con un file ufficiale che offre tutto ciò che queste lezioni hanno usato, quindi prendi quello.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Una lista della spesa</h3><table class='cheat'><tr><th>Componente</th><th>A che cosa serve</th></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html' target='_blank'>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 WH</a> (la H: header dei pin già saldati)</td><td>la scheda; si inserisce in una breadboard</td></tr><tr><td>un cavo micro-USB che trasmetta <strong>dati</strong></td><td>alimentazione, il terminale, la copia del firmware; con un cavo di sola ricarica non appare alcuna unità</td></tr><tr><td>una breadboard e cavetti jumper</td><td>collegamenti senza saldature</td></tr><tr><td>LED, resistenze da 330 Ω, un pulsante</td><td>il lampeggio, il pulsante</td></tr><tr><td>una fotoresistenza (LDR) e una resistenza da 10 kΩ, un cicalino passivo</td><td>il sensore di luce su GP26, i suoni</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-Environment-Sensor' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-Environment-Sensor</a></td><td>temperatura, umidità e pressione atmosferica (BME280) via I2C</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-ePaper-2.13' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.13</a></td><td>il display e-paper, 250 × 122 pixel</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/PicoGo' target='_blank'>Waveshare PicoGo</a></td><td>il robot: motori, sensori a infrarossi, sensore a ultrasuoni</td></tr><tr><td><a href='https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/Pico-BLE' target='_blank'>Waveshare Pico-BLE</a> (facoltativo)</td><td>Bluetooth per un Pico senza radio</td></tr></table><p>Le schede Waveshare si innestano sui pin del Pico; usano i pin mostrati dal banco di prova. Non tutte le coppie sono compatibili – il PicoGo e il sensore ambientale vogliono entrambi GP20 e GP21.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>R2P2 passo per passo</h3><ol><li><strong>Scarica il firmware.</strong> <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code> scarica il file R2P2 più recente (un <code>.uf2</code>), oppure prendilo dalle release su GitHub.</li><li><strong>Installalo.</strong> Tieni premuto il pulsante BOOTSEL mentre colleghi il Pico: compare un'unità (<code>RP2350</code> su un Pico 2 W). Trascinaci sopra il file <code>.uf2</code>; il Pico si riavvia come un piccolo computer Ruby.</li><li><strong>Parlaci.</strong> Apri il Web Terminal di R2P2 su <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> in Chrome o Edge – usa Web Serial, non c'è nulla da installare. Ottieni la shell di R2P2, con <code>ls</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>vim</code> e <code>irb</code>: l'IRB della lezione {{nr:pico-blink}}, per davvero.</li><li><strong>Copiaci i file.</strong> Da PicoRuby 3.4 non è più possibile trascinare i file <code>.rb</code> sull'unità. Al loro posto: il caricamento del Web Terminal, <code>vim</code> sulla scheda, oppure PicoModem, il protocollo di trasferimento di R2P2 con controllo CRC: <code>picoruby tools/picomodem.rb put app.rb /home/app.rb</code>.</li><li><strong>Avvialo.</strong> All'avvio R2P2 attende 2 secondi (premi <kbd>s</kbd> per saltare), si connette al Wi-Fi se esiste <code>/etc/network/wifi.yml</code> ed esegue <strong><code>/home/app.rb</code></strong>. È lì che va il tuo programma.</li><li><strong>Le librerie</strong> vanno in <code>/lib</code>: <code>/lib/epd2in13.rb</code>, caricato con <code>require \"epd2in13\"</code>, perché R2P2 imposta <code>$LOAD_PATH = [\"/lib\"]</code>. Le tab delle celle qui fanno lo stesso – ogni tab viene scritta in <code>/lib</code> prima dell'esecuzione.</li></ol>"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>prremote: compilare sul computer, eseguire sul chip</h3><p>R2P2 è un Ruby completo sulla scheda, con un file system e una shell. <strong>prremote</strong> segue la strada opposta. È uno strumento a riga di comando di ITO Yosei di LumberMill, Inc. a Takachiho (Miyazaki, Giappone), una gem con licenza MIT da maggio 2026; la versione 0.4.2 è uscita il 16 ottobre 2026, e il suo README avverte che è in una fase iniziale di sviluppo e che i comandi potrebbero cambiare. Si definisce «inspired by mpremote for MicroPython»: un mpremote per Ruby.</p><p>prremote installa sulla scheda un minuscolo firmware di runtime <strong>mruby/c</strong>. Il Ruby viene compilato sul tuo computer, dal tuo <code>mrbc</code> (deve essere mruby 4.x), e il bytecode arriva alla scheda attraverso la linea seriale USB, a 115200 baud se non scegli diversamente. I due parlano un breve protocollo:</p><ul><li>Il computer invia <code>Ctrl-C</code> (il byte 3) ogni secondo, finché la scheda risponde <code>READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow</code>.</li><li>Invia il bytecode. Un file mruby compilato comincia con le lettere <code>RITE</code>, ed è così che la scheda sa che sta arrivando un programma. (Tiene d'occhio tre parole di quattro lettere di questo tipo: <code>RITE</code>, <code>DPLY</code>, <code>BORD</code>.) Il computer scrive a pezzi di 256 byte con 4 ms di pausa tra l'uno e l'altro.</li><li>La scheda risponde <code>RUNNING</code>, poi tutto ciò che il programma stampa, poi <code>DONE</code> – oppure <code>ERROR</code> e il motivo.</li><li>Per <code>prremote deploy</code> il computer invia <code>DPLY</code> prima del bytecode. La scheda conserva il programma nella flash, lo esegue a ogni avvio e risponde <code>DEPLOYED</code>.</li></ul><figure><img src='assets/pico/prremote-sequence.svg' alt='Diagramma di sequenza: il computer invia Ctrl-C ogni secondo finché il dispositivo risponde READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow; poi invia il bytecode, che comincia con RITE; il dispositivo risponde RUNNING, l’output del programma e DONE, oppure ERROR e un motivo. Per prremote deploy il computer invia DPLY e il bytecode, e il dispositivo risponde DEPLOYED.' width='640'><figcaption>L'handshake di prremote sulla linea seriale USB</figcaption></figure><p>Ecco quel dialogo con un dispositivo finto – semplice Ruby, non serve alcuna scheda:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "# Un dispositivo finto che risponde come il firmware di runtime di prremote.\n# Al primo Ctrl-C è ancora occupato; il secondo ottiene READY.\nkicks = 0\ndevice = lambda do |bytes|\n  if bytes == \"\\x03\"\n    kicks += 1\n    kicks < 2 ? [] : [\"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"RITE\")\n    [\"RUNNING\", \"Hello from Pico W!\", \"DONE\"]\n  elsif bytes.start_with?(\"DPLY\")\n    [\"DEPLOYED\"]\n  else\n    []\n  end\nend\n\n# il lato host: invia qualcosa, stampa ciò che torna indietro\ntalk = lambda do |bytes, label|\n  puts \"host -> #{label}\"\n  device.call(bytes).each { |line| puts \"  <- #{line}\" }\nend\n\nbytecode = \"RITE...\" # ciò che mrbc ha ricavato da app.rb\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C\")\ntalk.call(\"\\x03\", \"Ctrl-C (one second later)\")\ntalk.call(bytecode, \"the bytecode (prremote run)\")\ntalk.call(\"DPLY\" + bytecode, \"DPLY + bytecode (prremote deploy)\")\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><strong>Più file</strong>: <code>prremote run lib.rb main.rb</code> compila i file in quest'ordine in un unico file di bytecode, così le classi e i metodi dei file precedenti sono disponibili in quelli successivi. mruby/c non ha <code>require</code>, e il README raccomanda questo metodo come sostituto. Quando uno script nomina la costante di una scheda come <code>M5StickCPlus</code>, prremote mette persino da solo la libreria di quella scheda in testa. Le tab di questo corso imitano invece il modo di R2P2 – file in <code>/lib</code> e <code>require</code> – ma l'idea è la stessa: la libreria sullo sfondo, il tuo programma in primo piano.</p><p><strong>I comandi</strong>: <code>install -b picow</code> installa il firmware sulla scheda, <code>run</code> esegue i file una volta, <code>watch</code> li riesegue a ogni salvataggio, <code>deploy</code> e <code>undeploy</code> conservano un programma nella flash o lo rimuovono, <code>eval</code> esegue una riga, <code>reset</code> invia Ctrl-C, <code>list</code> mostra le porte seriali e <code>version</code> le versioni.</p><p><strong>I suoi limiti</strong>: prremote conosce il Pico, il Pico W, il Pico 2 (con il flashing segnalato come non ancora provato su hardware) e schede ESP32 come l'M5Stack; il Pico 2 W non è nel suo elenco. Su un Pico W offre pin, ADC, PWM, I2C, SPI, Wi-Fi, NTP e TCP – ma niente TLS (quindi niente HTTPS) e niente BLE. La scheda non ha shell, né file system, né IRB. E ha una propria API Ruby, di proposito – non segue i nomi di PicoRuby:</p><table class='cheat'><tr><th></th><th>prremote</th><th>PicoRuby (R2P2)</th></tr><tr><td>LED integrato</td><td><code>GPIO.led</code></td><td><code>CYW43::GPIO.new(CYW43::GPIO::LED_PIN)</code></td></tr><tr><td>un pin di uscita</td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, :output)</code></td><td><code>GPIO.new(15, GPIO::OUT)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi</td><td><code>WiFi.connect(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td><td><code>CYW43.connect_timeout(ssid, pw, auth, secs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>come arriva il programma</td><td>bytecode nella RAM, o nella flash con <code>deploy</code></td><td>file sorgente nel file system</td></tr></table><p>Il codice di queste lezioni gira quindi su R2P2 così com'è, mentre per prremote richiede modifiche.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><p><strong>R2P2</strong>: i passi qui sopra – <code>curl -fsSL https://picoruby.org/r2p2-latest.sh | sh</code>, BOOTSEL, trascina il file, poi il Web Terminal.</p><p><strong>prremote</strong>: <code>gem install prremote</code> (Ruby 3.4 o più recente) e un <code>mrbc</code> di mruby 4: <code>brew install mruby</code> su un Mac; su Linux compila mruby 4 dal sorgente, oppure indicane uno con <code>MRBC=/path/to/mrbc</code> – il pacchetto mruby di Debian è 3.x, che prremote considera non compatibile. Poi, una volta sola, <code>prremote install -b picow</code> (tieni premuto BOOTSEL, collega), e da quel momento <code>prremote watch app.rb</code> – ogni salvataggio riesegue il file. <code>prremote deploy lib.rb main.rb</code> lo conserva sulla scheda. In WSL2 il dispositivo USB richiede prima <code>usbipd attach --wsl</code>.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/lumbermill/prremote#readme' target='_blank'>il README di prremote</a> · <a href='https://picoruby.org/setup' target='_blank'>configurare R2P2</a> · <a href='https://github.com/picoruby/picoruby/blob/master/mrbgems/picoruby-r2p2/README.md' target='_blank'>il README di R2P2</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>run_status(lines)</code>, nel modo in cui il lato computer di prremote legge le righe della scheda: <code>:done</code> quando è arrivato <code>DONE</code>, <code>\"error: &lt;reason&gt;\"</code> per <code>ERROR &lt;reason&gt;</code>, <code>:running</code> dopo <code>RUNNING</code> se non è ancora arrivata una fine, e <code>:waiting</code> altrimenti. Le ultime righe della cella provano quattro dialoghi.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def run_status(lines)\n  # DONE -> :done, \"ERROR x\" -> \"error: x\", RUNNING -> :running\n  :waiting\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n",
            "hints": [
              "Tieni lo stato in una variabile che parte da <code>:waiting</code>. Scorri le righe con <code>each</code> e cambia lo stato quando una riga è <code>DONE</code>, <code>RUNNING</code> o <code>ERROR …</code>."
            ],
            "check": "result == [:done, \"error: NoMethodError\", :running, :waiting] && code.include?(\"def run_status\")",
            "hint": "Scorri le righe con <code>each</code> e ricorda lo stato: <code>line == \"DONE\"</code>, <code>line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")</code> (il motivo è <code>line[6..-1]</code>), <code>line == \"RUNNING\"</code>. Alla fine restituisci lo stato.",
            "solution": "def run_status(lines)\n  status = :waiting\n  lines.each do |line|\n    if line == \"DONE\"\n      status = :done\n    elsif line.start_with?(\"ERROR \")\n      status = \"error: #{line[6..-1]}\"\n    elsif line == \"RUNNING\"\n      status = :running\n    end\n  end\n  status\nend\n\nready = \"READY prremote-runtime/0.4.2 board=picow\"\n[run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"hello\", \"DONE\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"ERROR NoMethodError\"]),\n run_status([ready, \"RUNNING\", \"tick\"]),\n run_status([ready])]\n"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h3>Direttamente da questa pagina</h3><p>Sotto ogni banco di prova di queste lezioni c'è un pannello richiuso <strong>🔌 Un vero Pico (USB)</strong>. Con un Pico con R2P2 collegato a un cavo USB, fa ciò che fa il Web Terminal, direttamente dalla cella:</p><ol><li><strong>Collega un vero Pico…</strong> apre l'elenco delle porte seriali del browser stesso. Mostra le porte di R2P2 (vendor USB 16C0); R2P2 ne ha tre; la sua shell di solito è sulla prima (il numero COM o ttyACM più basso) – se non compare il prompt <code>$&gt;</code>, prova la successiva. La pagina ottiene esattamente la porta che scegli – nient'altro del tuo computer. L'invio richiede un R2P2 con PicoModem, come R2P2 4.0.3.</li><li>Il riquadro nero è il terminale di R2P2: ciò che il Pico stampa e, sotto, una riga in cui scrivere, per <code>ls</code> o <code>irb</code>. <strong>Ctrl-C</strong> interrompe un programma in esecuzione.</li><li><strong>Invia questa cella al Pico ed eseguila</strong> preme Ctrl-C (e Ctrl-D, che esce da un <code>irb</code>), scrive ogni tab della cella in <code>/lib</code> e il codice in <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> – con PicoModem, come il caricamento del Web Terminal, ogni file verificato con CRC-32 – e poi digita <code>/home/chunky.rb</code> al prompt <code>$&gt;</code>, il che lo esegue. Con la casella spuntata diventa <code>/home/app.rb</code>, il programma che R2P2 avvia all'accensione. Una prima riga nel file fa sì che <code>require</code> rilegga le tab; <code>show_irb</code>, che esiste solo in questa pagina, diventa un commento – digita invece <code>irb</code> al prompt.</li></ol><p><strong>Quali browser</strong>: Web Serial è disponibile in Chrome, Edge e Opera su computer (Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS), non in Firefox o Safari. Su Linux il tuo utente potrebbe dover appartenere al gruppo <code>dialout</code> per aprire <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code>. E la pagina deve essere sicura: <a href='https://letsruby.com' target='_blank'>letsruby.com</a> su https, oppure localhost. Su un indirizzo con semplice http il pannello si limita a spiegare perché non può funzionare.</p><p><strong>Bluetooth</strong>: nella lezione {{nr:pico-ble}} i banchi di prova con il laptop hanno anche <strong>📶 Un vero Pico 2 W (Bluetooth)</strong>: il laptop parla allora con un vero Pico 2 W su cui gira un programma BLE::UART. Web Bluetooth è disponibile in Chrome ed Edge su Windows, macOS, ChromeOS e Android; su Linux potrebbe essere necessario attivarlo in <code>chrome://flags</code>.</p><p><strong>Sicuro per progettazione</strong>: la pagina può raggiungere solo un dispositivo che hai scelto nella finestra di selezione del browser stesso, e solo finché questa scheda del browser è aperta; Disconnetti, o la chiusura della scheda, termina la connessione. Nulla va da nessun'altra parte: il codice va da questa scheda, attraverso il cavo o la radio, al tuo Pico. Ciò che gira lì è il codice della cella, che puoi leggere – e come tutto su un Pico commuta i pin che hai collegato, quindi controlla prima i collegamenti.</p><p><strong>A essere onesti</strong>: questa connessione non è ancora stata provata su un Pico reale. Segue i sorgenti di R2P2 4.0.3 – il prompt, Ctrl-B per PicoModem, i frame e i loro checksum – ed è testata con un R2P2 finto e con il codice lato dispositivo di PicoModem stesso. Se non funziona con la tua scheda, il Web Terminal di R2P2 su <a href='https://picoruby.org/terminal' target='_blank'>picoruby.org/terminal</a> fa lo stesso a mano.</p>"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "qr-rechnung",
      "de": {
        "title": "QR-Rechnungen schreiben und lesen",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Die Schweizer QR-Rechnung</h2><p>In der Schweiz liegt einer Rechnung ein Einzahlungsschein bei, unten auf der Seite – und seit 2020 ist das die <strong>QR-Rechnung</strong> (die alten orangen und roten Scheine gibt es seit Herbst 2022 nicht mehr). Ihr QR-Code enthält alles, was eine Bank braucht: auf welches Konto, wie viel, in welcher Währung, von wem, und eine Referenz, an der die Firma erkennt, welche Rechnung bezahlt wurde. Die Banking-App scannt ihn, und niemand tippt mehr eine 27-stellige Nummer ab.</p><p>Zwei Gems, beide reines Ruby: <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> schreibt QR-Rechnungen, <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> liest sie wieder und prüft jede Regel des Standards; den Code in einem Bild oder PDF findet es mit <a href='https://github.com/largo/qr_reader' target='_blank'>qr_reader</a>. Die Velowerkstatt und ihre Kundin sind erfunden, und die Kontonummern sind die Testnummern aus der Spezifikation der QR-Rechnung – hier fliesst kein Geld.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\n\nangaben = QRBills.get_qr_params\nangaben[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_with_qr_reference_type\nangaben[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"   # nur der Code, kein HTML\nangaben[:qrcode_format] = \"svg\"\n\nrechnung = angaben[:bill_params]\nrechnung[:amount] = 84.50\nrechnung[:currency] = \"CHF\"\nrechnung[:reference_type] = \"QRR\"\nrechnung[:reference] = \"21 00000 00003 13947 14300 09017\"\nrechnung[:additionally_information] = \"Rechnung 2026-042, Kette und Bremsbeläge\"\nrechnung[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH44 3199 9123 0008 8901 2\"\nrechnung[:creditor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Velo Speck GmbH\", street_name: \"Musterstrasse\", building_number: \"1\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\nrechnung[:debtor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Pia Muster\", street_name: \"Beispielweg\", building_number: \"7\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\n\nsvg = QRBills.generate(angaben)[:output]\nshow_image svg, alt: \"Der QR-Code der Rechnung, mit dem Schweizerkreuz in der Mitte\"\ndownload_file svg, \"qr-code.svg\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Was ist passiert?</p><ul><li><code>QRBills.get_qr_params</code> gibt einen Hash mit allen Feldern, die meisten leer; wir füllen sie aus. <code>rechnung</code> ist der innere Teil, <code>angaben[:bill_params]</code> – wer ihn ändert, ändert auch <code>angaben</code>, es ist derselbe Hash.</li><li>Der <strong>Rechnungstyp</strong>: mit QR-Referenz (<code>QRR</code>), mit Creditor Reference (<code>SCOR</code>, beginnt mit RF) oder ohne Referenz (<code>NON</code>).</li><li>Eine <strong>QR-IBAN</strong> – die 5. bis 9. Stelle liegt zwischen 30000 und 31999 – ist das Konto für Rechnungen mit QR-Referenz. Die Referenz hat 27 Ziffern, die letzte ist eine Prüfziffer, aus den anderen berechnet. Daran erkennt die Firma, welche Rechnung bezahlt wurde.</li><li>Die IBAN gehört in <code>rechnung[:creditor][:iban]</code>: Die Adresse hat zwar auch ein Feld dafür, aber das Gem liest sie hier.</li><li><code>QRBills.generate</code> gibt einen Hash zurück; unter <code>:output</code> steht das SVG als String. <code>show_image</code> zeigt es, <code>download_file</code> bietet es als Datei an.</li></ul><p>Und was steht im Code? Gewöhnlicher Text, ein Feld pro Zeile:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "inhalt = QRGenerator.build_payload(rechnung)\nputs inhalt"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Das ist das ganze Geheimnis: <code>SPC</code> (Swiss Payments Code), Version <code>0200</code>, Zeichensatz 1 (UTF-8), dann das Konto, die Empfängerin mit Adresstyp <code>S</code> (strukturiert: Strasse und Hausnummer in eigenen Feldern), sieben leere Zeilen (reserviert für einen «endgültigen Zahlungsempfänger», der noch nicht erlaubt ist), Betrag, Währung, die Zahlerin, Referenztyp und Referenz, die Mitteilung und <code>EPD</code> – End Payment Data. Die Zeilen trennt CR LF, wie in E-Mails.</p><p>Auf Papier steht der QR-Code in einem Zahlteil fester Grösse: die untersten 105 mm einer A4-Seite, links der <em>Empfangsschein</em>, den die Zahlerin behält, rechts der <em>Zahlteil</em> mit dem 46 mm grossen Code. Prawn (Lektion {{nr:pdf}}) zeichnet ihn:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"prawn\"\nrequire \"prawn\"\n\nangaben[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nFile.binwrite(\"qr.png\", QRBills.generate(angaben)[:output].to_blob)\n\nmm = 72 / 25.4   # PDF misst in Punkten: 1 mm sind 2,83 Punkte\nan = rechnung[:creditor][:address]\nvon = rechnung[:debtor][:address]\nzahlbar_an = [rechnung[:creditor][:iban], an[:name], \"#{an[:street_name]} #{an[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{an[:postal_code]} #{an[:town]}\"]\nzahlbar_durch = [von[:name], \"#{von[:street_name]} #{von[:building_number]}\",\n                 \"#{von[:postal_code]} #{von[:town]}\"]\nbetrag = format(\"%.2f\", rechnung[:amount])\n\nPrawn::Document.generate(\"rechnung.pdf\", page_size: \"A4\", margin: 0) do\n  text_box \"Velo Speck GmbH\\nRechnung 2026-042\", at: [20 * mm, 277 * mm], size: 14\n  dash(3)\n  stroke_horizontal_line 0, bounds.width, at: 105 * mm   # der Zahlteil: die untersten 105 mm\n  stroke_vertical_line 0, 105 * mm, at: 62 * mm          # Empfangsschein | Zahlteil\n  undash\n\n  feld = lambda do |x, y, titel, zeilen|\n    text_box titel, at: [x * mm, y * mm], size: 6, style: :bold\n    text_box zeilen.join(\"\\n\"), at: [x * mm, (y - 3) * mm], size: 8\n  end\n\n  text_box \"Empfangsschein\", at: [5 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  feld.(5, 93, \"Konto / Zahlbar an\", zahlbar_an)\n  feld.(5, 70, \"Referenz\", [rechnung[:reference]])\n  feld.(5, 60, \"Zahlbar durch\", zahlbar_durch)\n\n  text_box \"Zahlteil\", at: [67 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  image \"qr.png\", at: [67 * mm, 88 * mm], width: 46 * mm\n  feld.(118, 100, \"Konto / Zahlbar an\", zahlbar_an)\n  feld.(118, 77, \"Referenz\", [rechnung[:reference]])\n  feld.(118, 67, \"Zusätzliche Informationen\", [rechnung[:additionally_information]])\n  feld.(118, 55, \"Zahlbar durch\", zahlbar_durch)\n\n  [5, 67].each do |x|   # Währung und Betrag stehen auf beiden Teilen\n    feld.(x, 37, \"Währung\", [rechnung[:currency]])\n    feld.(x + 15, 37, \"Betrag\", [betrag])\n  end\nend\nshow_pdf \"rechnung.pdf\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Die Koordinaten sind Millimeter mal <code>mm</code>; y zählt in PDF von der unteren Kante aufwärts, <code>105 * mm</code> ist also die Linie zum Abtrennen. <code>feld</code> ist ein kleines Lambda: ein Titel fett in 6 Punkt, darunter die Zeilen in 8 Punkt – die Grössen, die der Standard verlangt. Diesmal liefert qr-bills den Code als PNG, denn Prawn setzt PNG- und JPEG-Bilder. Es ist ein <code>ChunkyPNG::Image</code> wie in Lektion {{nr:gems}}. Der echte Standard hat noch mehr Regeln – Schriften, Eckmarken für einen leeren Betrag, das Scherensymbol –, und qr-bills kann die ganze Rechnung auch als HTML schreiben (<code>output_params[:format] = \"html\"</code>).</p><p>Jetzt andersherum: Jemand bekommt <code>rechnung.pdf</code> und will wissen, was zu zahlen ist. <strong>swiss_qr</strong> findet den Code mit qr_reader, entziffert ihn und zerlegt den Text:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"   # findet den QR-Code und entziffert ihn\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\ngelesen = SwissQr.read(\"rechnung.pdf\")\nputs gelesen.creditor.lines\nputs gelesen.formatted_iban, gelesen.qr_iban?\nputs \"#{gelesen.currency} #{gelesen.formatted_amount}\"\nputs gelesen.formatted_reference\nputs gelesen.unstructured_message\ngelesen.debtor.name"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>SwissQr.read</code> nimmt einen Dateinamen, ein IO wie <code>StringIO.new(bytes)</code> oder ein Bild wie das von ChunkyPNG und gibt die erste QR-Rechnung, die es findet, als <code>SwissQr::Bill</code> zurück: ein unveränderliches Objekt (ein <code>Data</code>, Lektion {{nr:tl-mixins}}) mit dem Betrag als <code>BigDecimal</code> (Lektion {{nr:bigdecimal}}) und den Adressen als eigene Objekte. Andere QR-Codes auf der Seite – etwa ein Link zum Webshop – überspringt es.</p><p>swiss_qr liest nicht nur, es prüft auch jede Regel: die Prüfziffern der IBAN und der Referenz, die Längen, den Zeichensatz. Ein Tippfehler in der Referenz fällt auf:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "tippfehler = inhalt.sub(\"09017\", \"09071\")   # zwei Ziffern vertauscht\nbegin\n  SwissQr.parse(tippfehler)\nrescue SwissQr::InvalidPayload => e\n  puts e.message\nend\nSwissQr.valid?(inhalt)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><p><code>gem install qr-bills swiss_qr qr_reader</code>. swiss_qr bringt auch einen Befehl mit: <code>swiss_qr rechnung.pdf</code> gibt die Rechnung als JSON aus. Für echte Rechnungen brauchst du eine eigene (QR-)IBAN von deiner Bank; die QR-Referenz baut die Buchhaltung meist aus Kunden- und Rechnungsnummer, <code>QRBills.create_creditor_reference</code> macht aus einer eigenen Nummer eine RF-Referenz.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills#readme' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> · <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr#readme' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> · <a href='https://www.six-group.com/en/products-services/banking-services/payment-standardization/standards/qr-bill.html' target='_blank'>SIX: Standards zur QR-Rechnung</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Glace Fuchs, ein (erfundener) Glacestand am Seeweg 3, 8000 Seldwyla, hat ein gewöhnliches Konto: IBAN <code>CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2</code>. Zu einer gewöhnlichen IBAN gehört keine QR-Referenz, also nimm den Rechnungstyp ohne Referenz (<code>QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type</code> <code>\"NON\"</code>, die Referenz bleibt leer). Erstelle eine QR-Rechnung über <strong>CHF 12.30</strong> an Kaz Muster, Bahnhofstrasse 2, 8000 Seldwyla, zeig den QR-Code als PNG mit <code>show_image</code> und lies ihn mit <code>SwissQr.read</code> wieder ein. Lass die gelesene Rechnung als letzten Wert stehen oder gib ihre IBAN und ihren Betrag aus.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Glace Fuchs: QR-Rechnung ohne Referenz über CHF 12.30,\n# als PNG zeigen und mit SwissQr.read wieder einlesen\n",
            "hints": [
              "Kopier dir die erste Zelle als Vorlage und ändere drei Dinge: den Rechnungstyp ohne Referenz, den Referenztyp <code>\"NON\"</code> und das Bildformat <code>\"png\"</code>. Das Bild zeigst du dann und liest es wieder ein."
            ],
            "check": "code.include?(\"SwissQr.read\") && images.any? && ->(bill, text) { bill.respond_to?(:iban) ? bill.iban == \"CH5800791123000889012\" && bill.formatted_amount == \"12.30\" && bill.reference_type == :non : text.delete(\" \").include?(\"CH5800791123000889012\") && text.match?(/12\\.30?(?!\\d)|0\\.123e2/) }.(result, output)",
            "hint": "Kopier die erste Zelle und ändere sie: <code>get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type = \"NON\"</code>, <code>qrcode_format = \"png\"</code>. <code>QRBills.generate(…)[:output]</code> ist dann ein ChunkyPNG-Bild, das du direkt an <code>show_image</code> und <code>SwissQr.read</code> geben kannst. Meldet SwissQr, der Name der Zahlerin fehle: Füll auch <code>[:debtor][:address]</code> aus.",
            "solution": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\ninstall_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\nfuchs = QRBills.get_qr_params\nfuchs[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type\nfuchs[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"\nfuchs[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nglace = fuchs[:bill_params]\nglace[:amount] = 12.30\nglace[:reference_type] = \"NON\"\nglace[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2\"\nglace[:creditor][:address].merge!(name: \"Glace Fuchs\", street_name: \"Seeweg\", building_number: \"3\",\n                                  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nglace[:debtor][:address].merge!(name: \"Kaz Muster\", street_name: \"Bahnhofstrasse\", building_number: \"2\",\n                                postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nbild = QRBills.generate(fuchs)[:output]\nshow_image bild\nSwissQr.read(bild)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Writing and reading QR-bills",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>The Swiss QR-bill</h2><p>In Switzerland a bill comes with a payment slip at the bottom of the page – and since 2020 that slip is the <strong>QR-bill</strong> (the old orange and red slips have been gone since autumn 2022). Its QR code holds everything a bank needs: which account, how much, in which currency, from whom, and a reference that tells the business which invoice was paid. A banking app scans it, and nobody types a 27-digit number any more.</p><p>Two gems, both pure Ruby: <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> writes QR-bills, <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> reads them back and checks every rule of the standard; it finds the code in an image or a PDF with <a href='https://github.com/largo/qr_reader' target='_blank'>qr_reader</a>. The bike shop and its customer are made up, and the account numbers are the test numbers from the QR-bill's specification – no money moves here.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\n\nparams = QRBills.get_qr_params\nparams[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_with_qr_reference_type\nparams[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"   # just the code, no HTML\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"svg\"\n\nbill = params[:bill_params]\nbill[:amount] = 84.50\nbill[:currency] = \"CHF\"\nbill[:reference_type] = \"QRR\"\nbill[:reference] = \"21 00000 00003 13947 14300 09017\"\nbill[:additionally_information] = \"Invoice 2026-042, chain and brake pads\"\nbill[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH44 3199 9123 0008 8901 2\"\nbill[:creditor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Velo Speck GmbH\", street_name: \"Musterstrasse\", building_number: \"1\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\nbill[:debtor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Pia Muster\", street_name: \"Beispielweg\", building_number: \"7\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\n\nsvg = QRBills.generate(params)[:output]\nshow_image svg, alt: \"The bill's QR code, with a Swiss cross in the middle\"\ndownload_file svg, \"qr-code.svg\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>What happened?</p><ul><li><code>QRBills.get_qr_params</code> hands out a Hash with every field, most of them empty; we fill them in. <code>bill</code> is the part inside, <code>params[:bill_params]</code> – changing it changes <code>params</code> too, it is the same Hash.</li><li>The <strong>bill type</strong>: with a QR reference (<code>QRR</code>), with a creditor reference (<code>SCOR</code>, starts with RF) or without a reference (<code>NON</code>).</li><li>A <strong>QR-IBAN</strong> – its 5th to 9th digits are between 30000 and 31999 – is the account for bills with a QR reference. The reference has 27 digits, the last one a check digit worked out from the others. It tells the business which invoice was paid.</li><li>The IBAN goes in <code>bill[:creditor][:iban]</code>: the address has a field for it as well, but the gem reads it from here.</li><li><code>QRBills.generate</code> returns a Hash; its <code>:output</code> is the SVG as a String. <code>show_image</code> shows it, <code>download_file</code> offers it as a file.</li></ul><p>And what is in the code? Plain text, one field per line:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "payload = QRGenerator.build_payload(bill)\nputs payload"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>That is the whole secret: <code>SPC</code> (Swiss Payments Code), version <code>0200</code>, character set 1 (UTF-8), then the account, the creditor with address type <code>S</code> (structured: street and number in fields of their own), seven empty lines (kept for an \"ultimate creditor\" that is not allowed yet), amount, currency, the debtor, reference type and reference, the message and <code>EPD</code> – End Payment Data. The lines are separated by CR LF, as in e-mails.</p><p>On paper the QR code sits in a payment slip of a fixed size: the bottom 105 mm of an A4 page, on the left the <em>receipt</em> that the payer keeps, on the right the <em>payment part</em> with the code, 46 mm square. Prawn (lesson {{nr:pdf}}) draws it:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"prawn\"\nrequire \"prawn\"\n\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nFile.binwrite(\"qr.png\", QRBills.generate(params)[:output].to_blob)\n\nmm = 72 / 25.4   # PDF counts in points: 1 mm is 2.83 points\nto = bill[:creditor][:address]\nby = bill[:debtor][:address]\npayable_to = [bill[:creditor][:iban], to[:name], \"#{to[:street_name]} #{to[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{to[:postal_code]} #{to[:town]}\"]\npayable_by = [by[:name], \"#{by[:street_name]} #{by[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{by[:postal_code]} #{by[:town]}\"]\namount = format(\"%.2f\", bill[:amount])\n\nPrawn::Document.generate(\"invoice.pdf\", page_size: \"A4\", margin: 0) do\n  text_box \"Velo Speck GmbH\\nInvoice 2026-042\", at: [20 * mm, 277 * mm], size: 14\n  dash(3)\n  stroke_horizontal_line 0, bounds.width, at: 105 * mm   # the bill: the bottom 105 mm\n  stroke_vertical_line 0, 105 * mm, at: 62 * mm          # receipt | payment part\n  undash\n\n  field = lambda do |x, y, heading, lines|\n    text_box heading, at: [x * mm, y * mm], size: 6, style: :bold\n    text_box lines.join(\"\\n\"), at: [x * mm, (y - 3) * mm], size: 8\n  end\n\n  text_box \"Receipt\", at: [5 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  field.(5, 93, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(5, 70, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(5, 60, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  text_box \"Payment part\", at: [67 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  image \"qr.png\", at: [67 * mm, 88 * mm], width: 46 * mm\n  field.(118, 100, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(118, 77, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(118, 67, \"Additional information\", [bill[:additionally_information]])\n  field.(118, 55, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  [5, 67].each do |x|   # currency and amount are on both parts\n    field.(x, 37, \"Currency\", [bill[:currency]])\n    field.(x + 15, 37, \"Amount\", [amount])\n  end\nend\nshow_pdf \"invoice.pdf\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The coordinates are millimetres times <code>mm</code>; in PDF, y counts up from the bottom edge, so <code>105 * mm</code> is the line to tear along. <code>field</code> is a small lambda: a heading in bold 6 point, the lines in 8 point below it – the sizes the standard asks for. This time qr-bills hands out the code as a PNG, because Prawn places PNG and JPEG images. It is a <code>ChunkyPNG::Image</code> as in lesson {{nr:gems}}. The real standard has more rules – fonts, corner marks for an empty amount, the scissors symbol – and qr-bills can also write the whole bill as HTML (<code>output_params[:format] = \"html\"</code>).</p><p>Now the other way round: someone receives <code>invoice.pdf</code> and wants to know what to pay. <strong>swiss_qr</strong> finds the code with qr_reader, decodes it and takes the text apart:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"   # finds and decodes the QR code\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\npaid = SwissQr.read(\"invoice.pdf\")\nputs paid.creditor.lines\nputs paid.formatted_iban, paid.qr_iban?\nputs \"#{paid.currency} #{paid.formatted_amount}\"\nputs paid.formatted_reference\nputs paid.unstructured_message\npaid.debtor.name"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>SwissQr.read</code> takes a file name, an IO such as <code>StringIO.new(bytes)</code> or an image like ChunkyPNG's, and returns the first QR-bill it finds as a <code>SwissQr::Bill</code>: an immutable object (a <code>Data</code>, lesson {{nr:tl-mixins}}) with the amount as a <code>BigDecimal</code> (lesson {{nr:bigdecimal}}) and the addresses as objects of their own. Other QR codes on the page – a link to a web shop, say – are skipped.</p><p>swiss_qr does not just read, it checks every rule too: the check digits of the IBAN and of the reference, the lengths, the character set. A typo in the reference is caught:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "typo = payload.sub(\"09017\", \"09071\")   # two digits swapped\nbegin\n  SwissQr.parse(typo)\nrescue SwissQr::InvalidPayload => e\n  puts e.message\nend\nSwissQr.valid?(payload)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><p><code>gem install qr-bills swiss_qr qr_reader</code>. swiss_qr comes with a command as well: <code>swiss_qr invoice.pdf</code> prints the bill as JSON. For real bills you need an IBAN or QR-IBAN of your own from your bank; accounting software usually builds the QR reference from a customer and an invoice number, and <code>QRBills.create_creditor_reference</code> turns a number of your own into an RF reference.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills#readme' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> · <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr#readme' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> · <a href='https://www.six-group.com/en/products-services/banking-services/payment-standardization/standards/qr-bill.html' target='_blank'>SIX: the QR-bill standards</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Glace Fuchs, a (made-up) ice-cream stand at Seeweg 3, 8000 Seldwyla, has an ordinary account: IBAN <code>CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2</code>. An ordinary IBAN goes without a QR reference, so take the bill type without a reference (<code>QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type</code> <code>\"NON\"</code>, the reference stays empty). Make a QR-bill for <strong>CHF 12.30</strong> to Kaz Muster, Bahnhofstrasse 2, 8000 Seldwyla, show its QR code as a PNG with <code>show_image</code> and read it back with <code>SwissQr.read</code>. Leave the bill you read as the last value, or print its IBAN and amount.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Glace Fuchs: a QR-bill without a reference for CHF 12.30,\n# shown as a PNG and read back with SwissQr.read\n",
            "hints": [
              "Copy the first cell as your template and change three things: the bill type without a reference, the reference type <code>\"NON\"</code> and the picture format <code>\"png\"</code>. Then show the picture and read it back in."
            ],
            "check": "code.include?(\"SwissQr.read\") && images.any? && ->(bill, text) { bill.respond_to?(:iban) ? bill.iban == \"CH5800791123000889012\" && bill.formatted_amount == \"12.30\" && bill.reference_type == :non : text.delete(\" \").include?(\"CH5800791123000889012\") && text.match?(/12\\.30?(?!\\d)|0\\.123e2/) }.(result, output)",
            "hint": "Copy the first cell and change it: <code>get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type = \"NON\"</code>, <code>qrcode_format = \"png\"</code>. <code>QRBills.generate(…)[:output]</code> is then a ChunkyPNG image that you can hand straight to <code>show_image</code> and <code>SwissQr.read</code>. If SwissQr says the debtor's name is missing, fill in <code>[:debtor][:address]</code> too.",
            "solution": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\ninstall_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\nfox = QRBills.get_qr_params\nfox[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type\nfox[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"\nfox[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nice = fox[:bill_params]\nice[:amount] = 12.30\nice[:reference_type] = \"NON\"\nice[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2\"\nice[:creditor][:address].merge!(name: \"Glace Fuchs\", street_name: \"Seeweg\", building_number: \"3\",\n                                postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nice[:debtor][:address].merge!(name: \"Kaz Muster\", street_name: \"Bahnhofstrasse\", building_number: \"2\",\n                              postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\npng = QRBills.generate(fox)[:output]\nshow_image png\nread = SwissQr.read(StringIO.new(png.to_blob))\nputs read.formatted_iban\nputs read.amount"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "QR請求書を書いて読む",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>スイスのQR請求書</h2><p>スイスでは、請求書のページの下に払込票が付いています。2020年からは、それが<strong>QR請求書</strong>（QR-bill）です（昔のオレンジと赤の払込票は2022年の秋になくなりました）。そのQRコードには、銀行が必要なものが全部入っています。どの口座に、いくら、どの通貨で、だれから。そして、どの請求書が支払われたかを会社が知るための参照番号です。バンキングアプリがそれをスキャンするので、27桁の番号を打ち込む人はもういません。</p><p>gemは2つで、どちらもピュアRubyです。<a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a>はQR請求書を書き、<a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a>はそれを読み戻して、規格のすべてのルールをチェックします。画像やPDFの中のコードは<a href='https://github.com/largo/qr_reader' target='_blank'>qr_reader</a>で見つけます。自転車屋さんとお客さんは架空のもので、口座番号はQR請求書の仕様書にあるテスト用の番号です。ここでお金は動きません。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\n\nparams = QRBills.get_qr_params\nparams[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_with_qr_reference_type\nparams[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"   # HTMLではなく、コードだけ\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"svg\"\n\nbill = params[:bill_params]\nbill[:amount] = 84.50\nbill[:currency] = \"CHF\"\nbill[:reference_type] = \"QRR\"\nbill[:reference] = \"21 00000 00003 13947 14300 09017\"\nbill[:additionally_information] = \"Invoice 2026-042, chain and brake pads\"\nbill[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH44 3199 9123 0008 8901 2\"\nbill[:creditor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Velo Speck GmbH\", street_name: \"Musterstrasse\", building_number: \"1\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\nbill[:debtor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Pia Muster\", street_name: \"Beispielweg\", building_number: \"7\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\n\nsvg = QRBills.generate(params)[:output]\nshow_image svg, alt: \"The bill's QR code, with a Swiss cross in the middle\"\ndownload_file svg, \"qr-code.svg\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>何が起きたのでしょう？</p><ul><li><code>QRBills.get_qr_params</code>は、すべての項目が入ったハッシュを返します。ほとんどは空なので、それを埋めていきます。<code>bill</code>は内側の部分<code>params[:bill_params]</code>です。これを変えると<code>params</code>も変わります。同じハッシュだからです。</li><li><strong>請求書の種類</strong>：QR参照番号つき（<code>QRR</code>）、クレジター参照番号つき（<code>SCOR</code>、RFで始まる）、参照番号なし（<code>NON</code>）。</li><li><strong>QR-IBAN</strong>（5桁目から9桁目が30000から31999のあいだ）は、QR参照番号つきの請求書のための口座です。参照番号は27桁で、最後の1桁はほかの桁から計算したチェックディジットです。これで会社は、どの請求書が支払われたかがわかります。</li><li>IBANは<code>bill[:creditor][:iban]</code>に入れます。住所にもIBANの項目がありますが、gemが読むのはこちらです。</li><li><code>QRBills.generate</code>はハッシュを返し、その<code>:output</code>がSVGの文字列です。<code>show_image</code>で表示し、<code>download_file</code>でファイルとしてダウンロードできるようにします。</li></ul><p>では、コードの中には何が入っているのでしょう？　ふつうのテキストで、1行に1項目です：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "payload = QRGenerator.build_payload(bill)\nputs payload"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>秘密はこれで全部です。<code>SPC</code>（Swiss Payments Code）、バージョン<code>0200</code>、文字セット1（UTF-8）。そのあとに口座、住所の種類<code>S</code>（構造化：通りと番地が別々の項目）の受取人、7つの空行（まだ使えない「最終受取人」のために取ってある）、金額、通貨、支払人、参照番号の種類と参照番号、メッセージ、そして<code>EPD</code>（End Payment Data）。行と行はメールと同じようにCR LFで区切られています。</p><p>紙の上では、QRコードは決まった大きさの払込票の中にあります。A4のページの下の105 mmで、左は支払った人が取っておく<em>受領証</em>、右は46 mm四方のコードがある<em>支払部分</em>です。Prawn（レッスン{{nr:pdf}}）で描いてみましょう：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"prawn\"\nrequire \"prawn\"\n\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nFile.binwrite(\"qr.png\", QRBills.generate(params)[:output].to_blob)\n\nmm = 72 / 25.4   # PDFの単位はポイント：1 mmは2.83ポイント\nto = bill[:creditor][:address]\nby = bill[:debtor][:address]\npayable_to = [bill[:creditor][:iban], to[:name], \"#{to[:street_name]} #{to[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{to[:postal_code]} #{to[:town]}\"]\npayable_by = [by[:name], \"#{by[:street_name]} #{by[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{by[:postal_code]} #{by[:town]}\"]\namount = format(\"%.2f\", bill[:amount])\n\nPrawn::Document.generate(\"invoice.pdf\", page_size: \"A4\", margin: 0) do\n  text_box \"Velo Speck GmbH\\nInvoice 2026-042\", at: [20 * mm, 277 * mm], size: 14\n  dash(3)\n  stroke_horizontal_line 0, bounds.width, at: 105 * mm   # 払込票：下の105 mm\n  stroke_vertical_line 0, 105 * mm, at: 62 * mm          # 受領証 | 支払部分\n  undash\n\n  field = lambda do |x, y, heading, lines|\n    text_box heading, at: [x * mm, y * mm], size: 6, style: :bold\n    text_box lines.join(\"\\n\"), at: [x * mm, (y - 3) * mm], size: 8\n  end\n\n  text_box \"Receipt\", at: [5 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  field.(5, 93, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(5, 70, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(5, 60, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  text_box \"Payment part\", at: [67 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  image \"qr.png\", at: [67 * mm, 88 * mm], width: 46 * mm\n  field.(118, 100, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(118, 77, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(118, 67, \"Additional information\", [bill[:additionally_information]])\n  field.(118, 55, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  [5, 67].each do |x|   # 通貨と金額は両方に入る\n    field.(x, 37, \"Currency\", [bill[:currency]])\n    field.(x + 15, 37, \"Amount\", [amount])\n  end\nend\nshow_pdf \"invoice.pdf\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>座標はミリメートルに<code>mm</code>を掛けたものです。PDFではyが下の端から上に数えるので、<code>105 * mm</code>は切り取り線です。<code>field</code>は小さなラムダで、太字6ポイントの見出しと、その下に8ポイントの行を書きます。規格が求める大きさです。今回はqr-billsが、レッスン{{nr:gems}}で使った<code>ChunkyPNG::Image</code>としてコードを返します。PNG画像なら、Prawnが配置できるからです。本物の規格にはもっとルールがあります（フォント、金額が空のときのコーナーマーク、はさみの記号など）。qr-billsは請求書全体をHTMLで書くこともできます（<code>output_params[:format] = \"html\"</code>）。</p><p>今度は逆です。だれかが<code>invoice.pdf</code>を受け取って、いくら払えばいいか知りたいとします。<strong>swiss_qr</strong>はqr_readerでコードを見つけ、読み取って、テキストを分解します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"   # QRコードを見つけて読み取る\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\npaid = SwissQr.read(\"invoice.pdf\")\nputs paid.creditor.lines\nputs paid.formatted_iban, paid.qr_iban?\nputs \"#{paid.currency} #{paid.formatted_amount}\"\nputs paid.formatted_reference\nputs paid.unstructured_message\npaid.debtor.name"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>SwissQr.read</code>は、ファイル名、<code>StringIO.new(bytes)</code>のようなIO、またはChunkyPNGのような画像を受け取り、最初に見つけたQR請求書を<code>SwissQr::Bill</code>として返します。金額は<code>BigDecimal</code>（レッスン{{nr:bigdecimal}}）で、住所はそれぞれ独自のオブジェクトです。全体は変更できないオブジェクト、つまり<code>Data</code>（レッスン{{nr:tl-mixins}}）です。ページの上のほかのQRコード（たとえばウェブショップへのリンク）は飛ばします。</p><p>swiss_qrは読むだけでなく、すべてのルールをチェックします。IBANと参照番号のチェックディジット、長さ、文字セット。参照番号の打ち間違いも見つかります：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "typo = payload.sub(\"09017\", \"09071\")   # 2つの数字が入れ替わった\nbegin\n  SwissQr.parse(typo)\nrescue SwissQr::InvalidPayload => e\n  puts e.message\nend\nSwissQr.valid?(payload)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><p><code>gem install qr-bills swiss_qr qr_reader</code>。swiss_qrにはコマンドも付いています。<code>swiss_qr invoice.pdf</code>で請求書をJSONとして表示します。本物の請求書には、銀行からもらう自分のIBANかQR-IBANが必要です。QR参照番号はふつう会計ソフトが顧客番号と請求書番号からつくります。<code>QRBills.create_creditor_reference</code>は、自分の番号をRF参照番号にします。</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills#readme' target='_blank'>qr-bills（英語）</a>・<a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr#readme' target='_blank'>swiss_qr（英語）</a>・<a href='https://www.six-group.com/en/products-services/banking-services/payment-standardization/standards/qr-bill.html' target='_blank'>SIX：QR請求書の規格（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>Glace Fuchsは、Seeweg 3, 8000 Seldwylaにある（架空の）アイスクリーム屋さんで、ふつうの口座を持っています。IBANは<code>CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2</code>です。ふつうのIBANにはQR参照番号を付けないので、参照番号なしの種類にします（<code>QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>、<code>reference_type</code>は<code>\"NON\"</code>、参照番号は空のまま）。Kaz Muster, Bahnhofstrasse 2, 8000 Seldwyla宛てに<strong>CHF 12.30</strong>のQR請求書をつくり、そのQRコードをPNGとして<code>show_image</code>で表示して、<code>SwissQr.read</code>で読み戻してください。読んだ請求書を最後の値として残すか、そのIBANと金額を出力します。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Glace Fuchs：参照番号なしのQR請求書（CHF 12.30）を\n# PNGで表示して、SwissQr.readで読み戻す\n",
            "hints": [
              "最初のセルをコピーしてひな形にして、3つだけ変えてみて。参照なしの請求書タイプ、参照タイプの<code>\"NON\"</code>、画像形式の<code>\"png\"</code>だよ。それから画像を表示して、読み戻すんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "code.include?(\"SwissQr.read\") && images.any? && ->(bill, text) { bill.respond_to?(:iban) ? bill.iban == \"CH5800791123000889012\" && bill.formatted_amount == \"12.30\" && bill.reference_type == :non : text.delete(\" \").include?(\"CH5800791123000889012\") && text.match?(/12\\.30?(?!\\d)|0\\.123e2/) }.(result, output)",
            "hint": "最初のセルをコピーして変えてみて：<code>get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>、<code>reference_type = \"NON\"</code>、<code>qrcode_format = \"png\"</code>。そうすると<code>QRBills.generate(…)[:output]</code>はChunkyPNGの画像になるから、そのまま<code>show_image</code>と<code>SwissQr.read</code>に渡せるよ。支払人の名前がないってSwissQrに言われたら、<code>[:debtor][:address]</code>も埋めてね。",
            "solution": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\ninstall_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\nfox = QRBills.get_qr_params\nfox[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type\nfox[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"\nfox[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nice = fox[:bill_params]\nice[:amount] = 12.30\nice[:reference_type] = \"NON\"\nice[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2\"\nice[:creditor][:address].merge!(name: \"Glace Fuchs\", street_name: \"Seeweg\", building_number: \"3\",\n                                postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nice[:debtor][:address].merge!(name: \"Kaz Muster\", street_name: \"Bahnhofstrasse\", building_number: \"2\",\n                              postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\npng = QRBills.generate(fox)[:output]\nshow_image png\nread = SwissQr.read(StringIO.new(png.to_blob))\nputs read.formatted_iban\nputs read.amount"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Créer et lire des QR-factures",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>La QR-facture suisse</h2><p>En Suisse, une facture est accompagnée d'un bulletin de versement en bas de page – et depuis 2020, ce bulletin est la <strong>QR-facture</strong> (les anciens bulletins orange et rouges ont disparu depuis l'automne 2022). Son code QR contient tout ce dont une banque a besoin : quel compte, quel montant, dans quelle monnaie, de la part de qui, et une référence qui indique à l'entreprise quelle facture a été payée. Une application bancaire le scanne, et plus personne ne tape de numéro à 27 chiffres.</p><p>Deux gems, toutes deux en Ruby pur : <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> crée des QR-factures, <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> les relit et vérifie chaque règle de la norme ; elle trouve le code dans une image ou un PDF grâce à <a href='https://github.com/largo/qr_reader' target='_blank'>qr_reader</a>. Le magasin de vélos et sa cliente sont inventés, et les numéros de compte sont les numéros de test de la spécification de la QR-facture – aucun argent ne circule ici.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\n\nparams = QRBills.get_qr_params\nparams[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_with_qr_reference_type\nparams[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"   # seulement le code, sans HTML\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"svg\"\n\nbill = params[:bill_params]\nbill[:amount] = 84.50\nbill[:currency] = \"CHF\"\nbill[:reference_type] = \"QRR\"\nbill[:reference] = \"21 00000 00003 13947 14300 09017\"\nbill[:additionally_information] = \"Invoice 2026-042, chain and brake pads\"\nbill[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH44 3199 9123 0008 8901 2\"\nbill[:creditor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Velo Speck GmbH\", street_name: \"Musterstrasse\", building_number: \"1\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\nbill[:debtor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Pia Muster\", street_name: \"Beispielweg\", building_number: \"7\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\n\nsvg = QRBills.generate(params)[:output]\nshow_image svg, alt: \"The bill's QR code, with a Swiss cross in the middle\"\ndownload_file svg, \"qr-code.svg\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Que s'est-il passé ?</p><ul><li><code>QRBills.get_qr_params</code> fournit un Hash contenant tous les champs, la plupart vides ; nous les remplissons. <code>bill</code> est la partie intérieure, <code>params[:bill_params]</code> – la modifier modifie aussi <code>params</code>, c'est le même Hash.</li><li>Le <strong>type de facture</strong> : avec une référence QR (<code>QRR</code>), avec une référence créancier (<code>SCOR</code>, commence par RF) ou sans référence (<code>NON</code>).</li><li>Un <strong>QR-IBAN</strong> – ses 5<sup>e</sup> à 9<sup>e</sup> chiffres sont compris entre 30000 et 31999 – est le compte des factures avec référence QR. La référence compte 27 chiffres, le dernier étant un chiffre de contrôle calculé à partir des autres. Elle indique à l'entreprise quelle facture a été payée.</li><li>L'IBAN va dans <code>bill[:creditor][:iban]</code> : l'adresse possède aussi un champ pour lui, mais la gem le lit ici.</li><li><code>QRBills.generate</code> renvoie un Hash ; son <code>:output</code> est le SVG sous forme de String. <code>show_image</code> l'affiche, <code>download_file</code> le propose en téléchargement.</li></ul><p>Et que contient le code ? Du texte brut, un champ par ligne :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "payload = QRGenerator.build_payload(bill)\nputs payload"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Voilà tout le secret : <code>SPC</code> (Swiss Payments Code), version <code>0200</code>, jeu de caractères 1 (UTF-8), puis le compte, le créancier avec le type d'adresse <code>S</code> (structurée : rue et numéro dans des champs séparés), sept lignes vides (réservées à un « créancier final » qui n'est pas encore autorisé), le montant, la monnaie, le débiteur, le type de référence et la référence, la communication et <code>EPD</code> – End Payment Data. Les lignes sont séparées par CR LF, comme dans les e-mails.</p><p>Sur papier, le code QR se trouve dans un bulletin de taille fixe : les 105 mm du bas d'une page A4, à gauche le <em>récépissé</em> que le payeur conserve, à droite la <em>section paiement</em> avec le code, un carré de 46 mm. Prawn (leçon {{nr:pdf}}) le dessine :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"prawn\"\nrequire \"prawn\"\n\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nFile.binwrite(\"qr.png\", QRBills.generate(params)[:output].to_blob)\n\nmm = 72 / 25.4   # le PDF compte en points : 1 mm vaut 2,83 points\nto = bill[:creditor][:address]\nby = bill[:debtor][:address]\npayable_to = [bill[:creditor][:iban], to[:name], \"#{to[:street_name]} #{to[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{to[:postal_code]} #{to[:town]}\"]\npayable_by = [by[:name], \"#{by[:street_name]} #{by[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{by[:postal_code]} #{by[:town]}\"]\namount = format(\"%.2f\", bill[:amount])\n\nPrawn::Document.generate(\"invoice.pdf\", page_size: \"A4\", margin: 0) do\n  text_box \"Velo Speck GmbH\\nInvoice 2026-042\", at: [20 * mm, 277 * mm], size: 14\n  dash(3)\n  stroke_horizontal_line 0, bounds.width, at: 105 * mm   # la facture : les 105 mm du bas\n  stroke_vertical_line 0, 105 * mm, at: 62 * mm          # récépissé | section paiement\n  undash\n\n  field = lambda do |x, y, heading, lines|\n    text_box heading, at: [x * mm, y * mm], size: 6, style: :bold\n    text_box lines.join(\"\\n\"), at: [x * mm, (y - 3) * mm], size: 8\n  end\n\n  text_box \"Receipt\", at: [5 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  field.(5, 93, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(5, 70, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(5, 60, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  text_box \"Payment part\", at: [67 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  image \"qr.png\", at: [67 * mm, 88 * mm], width: 46 * mm\n  field.(118, 100, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(118, 77, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(118, 67, \"Additional information\", [bill[:additionally_information]])\n  field.(118, 55, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  [5, 67].each do |x|   # la monnaie et le montant figurent sur les deux parties\n    field.(x, 37, \"Currency\", [bill[:currency]])\n    field.(x + 15, 37, \"Amount\", [amount])\n  end\nend\nshow_pdf \"invoice.pdf\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Les coordonnées sont des millimètres multipliés par <code>mm</code> ; en PDF, y compte à partir du bord inférieur, donc <code>105 * mm</code> est la ligne de découpe. <code>field</code> est une petite lambda : un titre en gras de 6 points, les lignes en 8 points en dessous – les tailles exigées par la norme. Cette fois, qr-bills fournit le code en PNG, car Prawn place des images PNG et JPEG. C'est une <code>ChunkyPNG::Image</code>, comme dans la leçon {{nr:gems}}. La vraie norme comporte d'autres règles – polices, coins de marquage pour un montant vide, symbole des ciseaux – et qr-bills peut aussi produire la facture complète en HTML (<code>output_params[:format] = \"html\"</code>).</p><p>Maintenant, dans l'autre sens : quelqu'un reçoit <code>invoice.pdf</code> et veut savoir quoi payer. <strong>swiss_qr</strong> trouve le code avec qr_reader, le décode et analyse le texte :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"   # trouve et décode le code QR\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\npaid = SwissQr.read(\"invoice.pdf\")\nputs paid.creditor.lines\nputs paid.formatted_iban, paid.qr_iban?\nputs \"#{paid.currency} #{paid.formatted_amount}\"\nputs paid.formatted_reference\nputs paid.unstructured_message\npaid.debtor.name"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>SwissQr.read</code> accepte un nom de fichier, un IO comme <code>StringIO.new(bytes)</code> ou une image comme celles de ChunkyPNG, et renvoie la première QR-facture trouvée sous forme de <code>SwissQr::Bill</code> : un objet immuable (un <code>Data</code>, leçon {{nr:tl-mixins}}) avec le montant en <code>BigDecimal</code> (leçon {{nr:bigdecimal}}) et les adresses sous forme d'objets distincts. Les autres codes QR de la page – un lien vers une boutique en ligne, par exemple – sont ignorés.</p><p>swiss_qr ne se contente pas de lire, il vérifie aussi chaque règle : les chiffres de contrôle de l'IBAN et de la référence, les longueurs, le jeu de caractères. Une faute de frappe dans la référence est détectée :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "typo = payload.sub(\"09017\", \"09071\")   # deux chiffres inversés\nbegin\n  SwissQr.parse(typo)\nrescue SwissQr::InvalidPayload => e\n  puts e.message\nend\nSwissQr.valid?(payload)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><p><code>gem install qr-bills swiss_qr qr_reader</code>. swiss_qr fournit aussi une commande : <code>swiss_qr invoice.pdf</code> affiche la facture en JSON. Pour de vraies factures, il vous faut votre propre IBAN ou QR-IBAN, fourni par votre banque ; les logiciels de comptabilité construisent généralement la référence QR à partir d'un numéro de client et d'un numéro de facture, et <code>QRBills.create_creditor_reference</code> transforme un numéro de votre choix en référence RF.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills#readme' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> · <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr#readme' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> · <a href='https://www.six-group.com/en/products-services/banking-services/payment-standardization/standards/qr-bill.html' target='_blank'>SIX : les normes de la QR-facture</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Glace Fuchs, un stand de glaces (fictif) situé Seeweg 3, 8000 Seldwyla, possède un compte ordinaire : IBAN <code>CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2</code>. Un IBAN ordinaire s'utilise sans référence QR, prenez donc le type de facture sans référence (<code>QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type</code> <code>\"NON\"</code>, la référence reste vide). Créez une QR-facture de <strong>CHF 12.30</strong> adressée à Kaz Muster, Bahnhofstrasse 2, 8000 Seldwyla, affichez son code QR en PNG avec <code>show_image</code> et relisez-la avec <code>SwissQr.read</code>. Laissez la facture relue comme dernière valeur, ou affichez son IBAN et son montant.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Glace Fuchs : une QR-facture sans référence de CHF 12.30,\n# affichée en PNG et relue avec SwissQr.read\n",
            "hints": [
              "Copiez la première cellule comme modèle et changez trois choses : le type de facture sans référence, le type de référence <code>\"NON\"</code> et le format d'image <code>\"png\"</code>. Ensuite, affichez l'image et relisez-la."
            ],
            "check": "code.include?(\"SwissQr.read\") && images.any? && ->(bill, text) { bill.respond_to?(:iban) ? bill.iban == \"CH5800791123000889012\" && bill.formatted_amount == \"12.30\" && bill.reference_type == :non : text.delete(\" \").include?(\"CH5800791123000889012\") && text.match?(/12\\.30?(?!\\d)|0\\.123e2/) }.(result, output)",
            "hint": "Copiez la première cellule et modifiez-la : <code>get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type = \"NON\"</code>, <code>qrcode_format = \"png\"</code>. <code>QRBills.generate(…)[:output]</code> est alors une image ChunkyPNG que vous pouvez passer directement à <code>show_image</code> et à <code>SwissQr.read</code>. Si SwissQr signale que le nom du débiteur manque, remplissez aussi <code>[:debtor][:address]</code>.",
            "solution": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\ninstall_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\nfox = QRBills.get_qr_params\nfox[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type\nfox[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"\nfox[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nice = fox[:bill_params]\nice[:amount] = 12.30\nice[:reference_type] = \"NON\"\nice[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2\"\nice[:creditor][:address].merge!(name: \"Glace Fuchs\", street_name: \"Seeweg\", building_number: \"3\",\n                                postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nice[:debtor][:address].merge!(name: \"Kaz Muster\", street_name: \"Bahnhofstrasse\", building_number: \"2\",\n                              postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\npng = QRBills.generate(fox)[:output]\nshow_image png\nread = SwissQr.read(StringIO.new(png.to_blob))\nputs read.formatted_iban\nputs read.amount"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Creare e leggere QR-fatture",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>La QR-fattura svizzera</h2><p>In Svizzera una fattura arriva con una polizza di versamento in fondo alla pagina – e dal 2020 quella polizza è la <strong>QR-fattura</strong> (le vecchie polizze arancioni e rosse sono scomparse dall'autunno 2022). Il suo codice QR contiene tutto ciò che serve a una banca: quale conto, quanto, in quale valuta, da parte di chi, e un riferimento che dice all'azienda quale fattura è stata pagata. Un'app bancaria lo scansiona, e nessuno deve più digitare un numero di 27 cifre.</p><p>Due gem, entrambe in Ruby puro: <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> crea le QR-fatture, <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> le rilegge e verifica ogni regola dello standard; trova il codice in un'immagine o in un PDF con <a href='https://github.com/largo/qr_reader' target='_blank'>qr_reader</a>. Il negozio di bici e il suo cliente sono inventati, e i numeri di conto sono i numeri di prova della specifica della QR-fattura – qui non si muove denaro.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\n\nparams = QRBills.get_qr_params\nparams[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_with_qr_reference_type\nparams[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"   # solo il codice, senza HTML\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"svg\"\n\nbill = params[:bill_params]\nbill[:amount] = 84.50\nbill[:currency] = \"CHF\"\nbill[:reference_type] = \"QRR\"\nbill[:reference] = \"21 00000 00003 13947 14300 09017\"\nbill[:additionally_information] = \"Invoice 2026-042, chain and brake pads\"\nbill[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH44 3199 9123 0008 8901 2\"\nbill[:creditor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Velo Speck GmbH\", street_name: \"Musterstrasse\", building_number: \"1\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\nbill[:debtor][:address].merge!(\n  name: \"Pia Muster\", street_name: \"Beispielweg\", building_number: \"7\",\n  postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\"\n)\n\nsvg = QRBills.generate(params)[:output]\nshow_image svg, alt: \"The bill's QR code, with a Swiss cross in the middle\"\ndownload_file svg, \"qr-code.svg\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Che cosa è successo?</p><ul><li><code>QRBills.get_qr_params</code> fornisce un hash con tutti i campi, quasi tutti vuoti; noi li compiliamo. <code>bill</code> è la parte interna, <code>params[:bill_params]</code> – modificarla modifica anche <code>params</code>, perché è lo stesso hash.</li><li>Il <strong>tipo di fattura</strong>: con riferimento QR (<code>QRR</code>), con riferimento del creditore (<code>SCOR</code>, inizia con RF) oppure senza riferimento (<code>NON</code>).</li><li>Un <strong>QR-IBAN</strong> – le sue cifre dalla 5ª alla 9ª sono comprese tra 30000 e 31999 – è il conto per le fatture con riferimento QR. Il riferimento ha 27 cifre, l'ultima è una cifra di controllo calcolata dalle altre. Dice all'azienda quale fattura è stata pagata.</li><li>L'IBAN va in <code>bill[:creditor][:iban]</code>: anche l'indirizzo ha un campo apposito, ma la gem lo legge da qui.</li><li><code>QRBills.generate</code> restituisce un hash; il suo <code>:output</code> è l'SVG sotto forma di stringa. <code>show_image</code> lo mostra, <code>download_file</code> lo offre come file da scaricare.</li></ul><p>E che cosa c'è nel codice? Testo semplice, un campo per riga:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "payload = QRGenerator.build_payload(bill)\nputs payload"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Ecco tutto il segreto: <code>SPC</code> (Swiss Payments Code), versione <code>0200</code>, set di caratteri 1 (UTF-8), poi il conto, il creditore con tipo di indirizzo <code>S</code> (strutturato: via e numero civico in campi separati), sette righe vuote (riservate a un \"creditore finale\" non ancora ammesso), importo, valuta, il debitore, tipo di riferimento e riferimento, la comunicazione e <code>EPD</code> – End Payment Data. Le righe sono separate da CR LF, come nelle e-mail.</p><p>Sulla carta il codice QR si trova in una sezione di dimensioni fisse: i 105 mm inferiori di una pagina A4, a sinistra la <em>ricevuta</em> che il pagatore conserva, a destra la <em>sezione pagamento</em> con il codice, un quadrato di 46 mm. Prawn (lezione {{nr:pdf}}) la disegna:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"prawn\"\nrequire \"prawn\"\n\nparams[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nFile.binwrite(\"qr.png\", QRBills.generate(params)[:output].to_blob)\n\nmm = 72 / 25.4   # il PDF misura in punti: 1 mm corrisponde a 2,83 punti\nto = bill[:creditor][:address]\nby = bill[:debtor][:address]\npayable_to = [bill[:creditor][:iban], to[:name], \"#{to[:street_name]} #{to[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{to[:postal_code]} #{to[:town]}\"]\npayable_by = [by[:name], \"#{by[:street_name]} #{by[:building_number]}\",\n              \"#{by[:postal_code]} #{by[:town]}\"]\namount = format(\"%.2f\", bill[:amount])\n\nPrawn::Document.generate(\"invoice.pdf\", page_size: \"A4\", margin: 0) do\n  text_box \"Velo Speck GmbH\\nInvoice 2026-042\", at: [20 * mm, 277 * mm], size: 14\n  dash(3)\n  stroke_horizontal_line 0, bounds.width, at: 105 * mm   # la QR-fattura: i 105 mm in basso\n  stroke_vertical_line 0, 105 * mm, at: 62 * mm          # ricevuta | sezione pagamento\n  undash\n\n  field = lambda do |x, y, heading, lines|\n    text_box heading, at: [x * mm, y * mm], size: 6, style: :bold\n    text_box lines.join(\"\\n\"), at: [x * mm, (y - 3) * mm], size: 8\n  end\n\n  text_box \"Receipt\", at: [5 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  field.(5, 93, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(5, 70, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(5, 60, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  text_box \"Payment part\", at: [67 * mm, 100 * mm], size: 11, style: :bold\n  image \"qr.png\", at: [67 * mm, 88 * mm], width: 46 * mm\n  field.(118, 100, \"Account / Payable to\", payable_to)\n  field.(118, 77, \"Reference\", [bill[:reference]])\n  field.(118, 67, \"Additional information\", [bill[:additionally_information]])\n  field.(118, 55, \"Payable by\", payable_by)\n\n  [5, 67].each do |x|   # valuta e importo compaiono su entrambe le parti\n    field.(x, 37, \"Currency\", [bill[:currency]])\n    field.(x + 15, 37, \"Amount\", [amount])\n  end\nend\nshow_pdf \"invoice.pdf\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le coordinate sono millimetri moltiplicati per <code>mm</code>; nel PDF la y cresce a partire dal bordo inferiore, quindi <code>105 * mm</code> è la linea lungo cui staccare. <code>field</code> è una piccola lambda: un'intestazione in grassetto a 6 punti, sotto le righe a 8 punti – le dimensioni richieste dallo standard. Questa volta qr-bills fornisce il codice come PNG, perché Prawn inserisce immagini PNG e JPEG. È un <code>ChunkyPNG::Image</code> come nella lezione {{nr:gems}}. Lo standard vero ha altre regole – caratteri, angoli di marcatura per un importo vuoto, il simbolo delle forbici – e qr-bills sa anche scrivere l'intera fattura in HTML (<code>output_params[:format] = \"html\"</code>).</p><p>Ora il percorso inverso: qualcuno riceve <code>invoice.pdf</code> e vuole sapere che cosa pagare. <strong>swiss_qr</strong> trova il codice con qr_reader, lo decodifica e scompone il testo:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"   # trova e decodifica il codice QR\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\npaid = SwissQr.read(\"invoice.pdf\")\nputs paid.creditor.lines\nputs paid.formatted_iban, paid.qr_iban?\nputs \"#{paid.currency} #{paid.formatted_amount}\"\nputs paid.formatted_reference\nputs paid.unstructured_message\npaid.debtor.name"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>SwissQr.read</code> accetta un nome di file, un IO come <code>StringIO.new(bytes)</code> o un'immagine come quella di ChunkyPNG, e restituisce la prima QR-fattura che trova come <code>SwissQr::Bill</code>: un oggetto immutabile (un <code>Data</code>, lezione {{nr:tl-mixins}}) con l'importo come <code>BigDecimal</code> (lezione {{nr:bigdecimal}}) e gli indirizzi come oggetti a sé. Gli altri codici QR sulla pagina – per esempio un link a un negozio online – vengono ignorati.</p><p>swiss_qr non si limita a leggere, verifica anche ogni regola: le cifre di controllo dell'IBAN e del riferimento, le lunghezze, il set di caratteri. Un errore di battitura nel riferimento viene scoperto:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "typo = payload.sub(\"09017\", \"09071\")   # due cifre scambiate\nbegin\n  SwissQr.parse(typo)\nrescue SwissQr::InvalidPayload => e\n  puts e.message\nend\nSwissQr.valid?(payload)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><p><code>gem install qr-bills swiss_qr qr_reader</code>. swiss_qr include anche un comando: <code>swiss_qr invoice.pdf</code> stampa la fattura in formato JSON. Per fatture reali ti serve un IBAN o un QR-IBAN tuo, fornito dalla tua banca; di solito il software di contabilità costruisce il riferimento QR a partire da un numero cliente e da un numero di fattura, e <code>QRBills.create_creditor_reference</code> trasforma un tuo numero in un riferimento RF.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/damoiser/qr-bills#readme' target='_blank'>qr-bills</a> · <a href='https://github.com/largo/swiss_qr#readme' target='_blank'>swiss_qr</a> · <a href='https://www.six-group.com/en/products-services/banking-services/payment-standardization/standards/qr-bill.html' target='_blank'>SIX: gli standard della QR-fattura</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Glace Fuchs, una gelateria (inventata) in Seeweg 3, 8000 Seldwyla, ha un conto normale: IBAN <code>CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2</code>. Un IBAN normale non prevede il riferimento QR, quindi scegli il tipo di fattura senza riferimento (<code>QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type</code> <code>\"NON\"</code>, il riferimento resta vuoto). Crea una QR-fattura di <strong>CHF 12.30</strong> intestata a Kaz Muster, Bahnhofstrasse 2, 8000 Seldwyla, mostra il suo codice QR come PNG con <code>show_image</code> e rileggila con <code>SwissQr.read</code>. Lascia la fattura letta come ultimo valore, oppure stampane IBAN e importo.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Glace Fuchs: una QR-fattura senza riferimento di CHF 12.30,\n# mostrata come PNG e riletta con SwissQr.read\n",
            "hints": [
              "Copia la prima cella come modello e cambia tre cose: il tipo di fattura senza riferimento, il tipo di riferimento <code>\"NON\"</code> e il formato dell'immagine <code>\"png\"</code>. Poi mostra l'immagine e rileggila."
            ],
            "check": "code.include?(\"SwissQr.read\") && images.any? && ->(bill, text) { bill.respond_to?(:iban) ? bill.iban == \"CH5800791123000889012\" && bill.formatted_amount == \"12.30\" && bill.reference_type == :non : text.delete(\" \").include?(\"CH5800791123000889012\") && text.match?(/12\\.30?(?!\\d)|0\\.123e2/) }.(result, output)",
            "hint": "Copia la prima cella e modificala: <code>get_qrbill_without_reference_type</code>, <code>reference_type = \"NON\"</code>, <code>qrcode_format = \"png\"</code>. <code>QRBills.generate(…)[:output]</code> è allora un'immagine ChunkyPNG che puoi passare direttamente a <code>show_image</code> e a <code>SwissQr.read</code>. Se SwissQr segnala che manca il nome del debitore, compila anche <code>[:debtor][:address]</code>.",
            "solution": "install_gem \"qr-bills\"\ninstall_gem \"swiss_qr\"\ninstall_gem \"qr_reader\"\nrequire \"qr-bills\"\nrequire \"swiss_qr\"\n\nfox = QRBills.get_qr_params\nfox[:bill_type] = QRBills.get_qrbill_without_reference_type\nfox[:output_params][:format] = \"qrcode\"\nfox[:qrcode_format] = \"png\"\nice = fox[:bill_params]\nice[:amount] = 12.30\nice[:reference_type] = \"NON\"\nice[:creditor][:iban] = \"CH58 0079 1123 0008 8901 2\"\nice[:creditor][:address].merge!(name: \"Glace Fuchs\", street_name: \"Seeweg\", building_number: \"3\",\n                                postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\nice[:debtor][:address].merge!(name: \"Kaz Muster\", street_name: \"Bahnhofstrasse\", building_number: \"2\",\n                              postal_code: \"8000\", town: \"Seldwyla\", country: \"CH\")\npng = QRBills.generate(fox)[:output]\nshow_image png\nread = SwissQr.read(StringIO.new(png.to_blob))\nputs read.formatted_iban\nputs read.amount"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-collections",
      "section": {
        "de": "Aufbaukurs: timelog",
        "en": "Advanced: timelog",
        "ja": "応用コース：timelog",
        "fr": "Cours avancé : timelog",
        "it": "Corso avanzato: timelog"
      },
      "de": {
        "title": "Projekt timelog: Collections",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Projekt timelog – los geht's!</h2><p>Ab hier bauen wir gemeinsam ein richtiges Programm: <strong>timelog</strong>, eine Zeiterfassung. In jeder Lektion wächst sie ein Stück – bis sie am Ende Einträge parst, Berichte rechnet, getestet ist und sogar eine Weboberfläche hat.</p><p>Wir starten mit der Datenform. Ein Zeiteintrag hat ein Projekt und Stunden – als Hash. Viele Einträge – als Array von Hashes:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintraege = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }\n]\neintraege.length"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Jetzt zeigen die Collection-Methoden ihre Kraft. <code>map</code> zieht Werte heraus, <code>select</code> filtert, <code>sum</code> summiert – und alles lässt sich verketten:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintraege.select { |e| e[:projekt] == \"ProjectX\" }\n         .sum { |e| e[:stunden] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Der Star für Berichte ist <code>group_by</code>: Es sortiert Elemente in einen Hash von Gruppen. Zusammen mit <code>transform_values</code> wird daraus in zwei Zeilen ein kompletter Bericht:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Auch nützlich: <code>tally</code> zählt Vorkommen, <code>sort_by</code> sortiert, <code>each_with_object</code> baut beliebige Strukturen auf.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-group_by' target='_blank'>group_by</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#method-i-transform_values' target='_blank'>transform_values</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-tally' target='_blank'>tally</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Baue aus <code>eintraege</code> einen Hash <code>stunden</code>, der jedem Projekt die <strong>Gesamtstunden</strong> zuordnet: <code>{\"ProjectX\"=>6.5, \"Intern\"=>2.0}</code>. Tipp: <code>group_by</code> + <code>transform_values</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "eintraege = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }\n]\n\n# stunden = ...\n",
            "hints": [
              "Erst <code>group_by</code> nach dem Projekt – das gibt dir pro Projekt eine Liste von Einträgen. Dann verwandelst du jede Liste mit <code>transform_values</code> in ihre Stundensumme."
            ],
            "check": "stunden == { \"ProjectX\" => 6.5, \"Intern\" => 2.0 }",
            "hint": "<code>stunden = eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.transform_values { |liste| liste.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } }</code>",
            "solution": "eintraege = [{ projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 }, { projekt: \"Intern\", stunden: 2.0 }, { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }]\nstunden = eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Project timelog: collections",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Project timelog – here we go!</h2><p>From here on we build a real program together: <strong>timelog</strong>, a time tracker. It grows a little in every lesson – until it parses entries, computes reports, is fully tested and even has a web interface.</p><p>We start with the data shape. A time entry has a project and hours – as a hash. Many entries – as an array of hashes:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\nentries.length"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Now the collection methods show their power. <code>map</code> extracts values, <code>select</code> filters, <code>sum</code> adds up – and everything chains:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.select { |e| e[:project] == \"ProjectX\" }\n       .sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The star for reports is <code>group_by</code>: it sorts elements into a hash of groups. Together with <code>transform_values</code> that becomes a complete report in two lines:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Also useful: <code>tally</code> counts occurrences, <code>sort_by</code> sorts, <code>each_with_object</code> builds arbitrary structures.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-group_by' target='_blank'>group_by</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#method-i-transform_values' target='_blank'>transform_values</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-tally' target='_blank'>tally</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Build a hash <code>hours</code> from <code>entries</code> that maps each project to its <strong>total hours</strong>: <code>{\"ProjectX\"=>6.5, \"Intern\"=>2.0}</code>. Hint: <code>group_by</code> + <code>transform_values</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\n# hours = ...\n",
            "hints": [
              "First <code>group_by</code> the project – that gives you a list of entries per project. Then turn each list into its sum of hours with <code>transform_values</code>."
            ],
            "check": "hours == { \"ProjectX\" => 6.5, \"Intern\" => 2.0 }",
            "hint": "<code>hours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |list| list.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code>",
            "solution": "entries = [{ project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }, { project: \"Intern\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }]\nhours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "timelogプロジェクト：コレクション",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelogプロジェクト、始動！</h2><p>ここからは、本格的なプログラムをいっしょに作っていきます。作るのは、作業時間を記録するツール<strong>timelog</strong>です。レッスンごとに少しずつ育てていき、最後にはエントリをパースし、レポートを計算し、テストもしっかりそろい、Webインターフェースまで備えたプログラムになります。</p><p>まずはデータの形から始めましょう。作業時間のエントリ1件には、プロジェクトと時間があります。これをハッシュで表します。エントリがたくさんあるなら、ハッシュの配列にします：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\nentries.length"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ここでコレクションのメソッドが本領を発揮します。<code>map</code>は値を取り出し、<code>select</code>は絞り込み、<code>sum</code>は合計します。しかも、どれもつなげて書けます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.select { |e| e[:project] == \"ProjectX\" }\n       .sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>レポート作りの主役は<code>group_by</code>です。要素をグループに振り分けて、グループのハッシュにしてくれます。<code>transform_values</code>と組み合わせれば、たった2行で立派なレポートになります：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ほかにも便利なメソッドがあります。<code>tally</code>は出現回数を数え、<code>sort_by</code>は並べ替え、<code>each_with_object</code>は好きな構造を組み立てます。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Enumerable/i/group_by.html' target='_blank'>group_by</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Hash/i/transform_values.html' target='_blank'>transform_values</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Enumerable/i/tally.html' target='_blank'>tally</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>entries</code>から、各プロジェクトにその<strong>合計時間</strong>を対応させたハッシュ<code>hours</code>を作ってください：<code>{\"ProjectX\"=>6.5, \"Intern\"=>2.0}</code>。ヒント：<code>group_by</code>と<code>transform_values</code>を組み合わせます。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\n# hours = ...\n",
            "hints": [
              "まずプロジェクトで<code>group_by</code>してみて。プロジェクトごとにエントリのリストができるよ。それから<code>transform_values</code>で、それぞれのリストを時間の合計に変えるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "hours == { \"ProjectX\" => 6.5, \"Intern\" => 2.0 }",
            "hint": "こう書けるよ：<code>hours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |list| list.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code>",
            "solution": "entries = [{ project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }, { project: \"Intern\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }]\nhours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Projet timelog : les collections",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Projet timelog – c'est parti !</h2><p>À partir d'ici, nous construisons ensemble un vrai programme : <strong>timelog</strong>, un outil de suivi du temps. Il s'enrichit un peu à chaque leçon – jusqu'à analyser des saisies, calculer des rapports, être entièrement testé et même disposer d'une interface web.</p><p>Commençons par la forme des données. Une saisie de temps comporte un projet et des heures – sous forme de hash. Plusieurs saisies – sous forme d'array de hashes :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\nentries.length"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>C'est maintenant que les méthodes de collection montrent leur puissance. <code>map</code> extrait des valeurs, <code>select</code> filtre, <code>sum</code> additionne – et tout s'enchaîne :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.select { |e| e[:project] == \"ProjectX\" }\n       .sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>La vedette des rapports, c'est <code>group_by</code> : elle répartit les éléments dans un hash de groupes. Avec <code>transform_values</code>, on obtient un rapport complet en deux lignes :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Également utiles : <code>tally</code> compte les occurrences, <code>sort_by</code> trie, <code>each_with_object</code> construit des structures quelconques.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-group_by' target='_blank'>group_by</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#method-i-transform_values' target='_blank'>transform_values</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-tally' target='_blank'>tally</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Construisez un hash <code>hours</code> à partir de <code>entries</code>, qui associe à chaque projet son <strong>total d'heures</strong> : <code>{\"ProjectX\"=>6.5, \"Intern\"=>2.0}</code>. Indice : <code>group_by</code> + <code>transform_values</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\n# hours = ...\n",
            "hints": [
              "Commencez par un <code>group_by</code> sur le projet – vous obtenez une liste de saisies par projet. Puis transformez chaque liste en sa somme d'heures avec <code>transform_values</code>."
            ],
            "check": "hours == { \"ProjectX\" => 6.5, \"Intern\" => 2.0 }",
            "hint": "<code>hours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |list| list.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code>",
            "solution": "entries = [{ project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }, { project: \"Intern\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }]\nhours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Progetto timelog: collezioni",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Progetto timelog – si parte!</h2><p>Da qui in poi costruiamo insieme un programma vero: <strong>timelog</strong>, un registro delle ore. Cresce un po' in ogni lezione – finché interpreta le voci, calcola report, è completamente testato e ha persino un'interfaccia web.</p><p>Cominciamo dalla forma dei dati. Una voce di tempo ha un progetto e delle ore – come hash. Tante voci – come array di hash:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\nentries.length"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Ora i metodi delle collezioni mostrano la loro forza. <code>map</code> estrae valori, <code>select</code> filtra, <code>sum</code> somma – e tutto si può concatenare:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.select { |e| e[:project] == \"ProjectX\" }\n       .sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>La star dei report è <code>group_by</code>: smista gli elementi in un hash di gruppi. Insieme a <code>transform_values</code> diventa un report completo in due righe:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Utili anche: <code>tally</code> conta le occorrenze, <code>sort_by</code> ordina, <code>each_with_object</code> costruisce strutture di ogni tipo.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-group_by' target='_blank'>group_by</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#method-i-transform_values' target='_blank'>transform_values</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html#method-i-tally' target='_blank'>tally</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Costruisci un hash <code>hours</code> a partire da <code>entries</code>, che associ a ogni progetto il suo <strong>totale di ore</strong>: <code>{\"ProjectX\"=>6.5, \"Intern\"=>2.0}</code>. Suggerimento: <code>group_by</code> + <code>transform_values</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\n# hours = ...\n",
            "hints": [
              "Prima raggruppa per progetto con <code>group_by</code> – ottieni così una lista di voci per ogni progetto. Poi trasforma ogni lista nella sua somma di ore con <code>transform_values</code>."
            ],
            "check": "hours == { \"ProjectX\" => 6.5, \"Intern\" => 2.0 }",
            "hint": "<code>hours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |list| list.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code>",
            "solution": "entries = [{ project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }, { project: \"Intern\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }]\nhours = entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-parsing",
      "de": {
        "title": "Text parsen: Regex",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Einträge parsen – reguläre Ausdrücke</h2><p>timelog soll Zeilen wie diese verstehen:</p><pre><code>2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX Planungsmeeting</code></pre><p>Dafür gibt es <strong>reguläre Ausdrücke</strong> (Regex): Muster, die Text beschreiben. <code>\\d</code> ist eine Ziffer, <code>{2}</code> heisst „genau zwei davon“, und mit <code>(?&lt;name&gt;…)</code> gibst du einer Fundstelle einen Namen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "zeile = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX Planungsmeeting\"\n\nmuster = /(?<datum>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<von>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<bis>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<projekt>\\S+)/\ntreffer = zeile.match(muster)\ntreffer[:projekt]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>match</code> liefert ein <code>MatchData</code>-Objekt – benannte Gruppen holst du mit <code>treffer[:von]</code> heraus. Passt nichts, kommt <code>nil</code> zurück (perfekt für <code>if</code>). Schnelltest ohne Daten: <code>zeile.match?(muster)</code>.</p><p>Für Fallunterscheidungen hat Ruby das elegante <code>case/when</code> – es versteht Ranges, Klassen und sogar Regexe:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def einordnen(stunden)\n  case stunden\n  when 0...4 then \"Halbtag\"\n  when 4...9 then \"Ganztag\"\n  else            \"Ueberstunden!\"\n  end\nend\n\neinordnen(7.5)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Regexp.html' target='_blank'>Regexp: Muster</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/MatchData.html' target='_blank'>MatchData</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/control_expressions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>case/when</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>parse_zeile(zeile)</code>: Sie zerlegt eine timelog-Zeile mit benannten Gruppen und gibt <code>{ projekt:, von:, bis: }</code> zurück – bei unpassenden Zeilen <code>nil</code>. Teste mit der Beispielzeile als letzter Zeile: <code>parse_zeile(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX Meeting\")</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def parse_zeile(zeile)\n#   muster = /.../  # benannte Gruppen: datum, von, bis, projekt\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Nimm das Muster aus der Demo-Zelle als Vorlage. Dann <code>match</code> auf die Zeile anwenden, bei <code>nil</code> sofort <code>nil</code> zurückgeben, und sonst die drei benannten Gruppen in einen Hash packen."
            ],
            "check": "parse_zeile(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX Meeting\") == { projekt: \"ProjectX\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"12:00\" } && parse_zeile(\"Kaffeepause\").nil? && code.include?(\"(?<\")",
            "hint": "Muster wie in der Demo. Danach: <code>treffer = zeile.match(muster)</code>, <code>return nil unless treffer</code>, dann <code>{ projekt: treffer[:projekt], von: treffer[:von], bis: treffer[:bis] }</code>.",
            "solution": "def parse_zeile(zeile)\n  muster = /(?<datum>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<von>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<bis>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<projekt>\\S+)/\n  treffer = zeile.match(muster)\n  return nil unless treffer\n  { projekt: treffer[:projekt], von: treffer[:von], bis: treffer[:bis] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Parsing text: regex",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Parsing entries – regular expressions</h2><p>timelog should understand lines like this:</p><pre><code>2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting</code></pre><p>That's what <strong>regular expressions</strong> (regex) are for: patterns that describe text. <code>\\d</code> is a digit, <code>{2}</code> means “exactly two of them”, and <code>(?&lt;name&gt;…)</code> gives a match a name:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "line = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting\"\n\npattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\nhit = line.match(pattern)\nhit[:project]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>match</code> returns a <code>MatchData</code> object – you fetch named groups with <code>hit[:from]</code>. If nothing matches you get <code>nil</code> (perfect for <code>if</code>). Quick test without data: <code>line.match?(pattern)</code>.</p><p>For branching, Ruby has the elegant <code>case/when</code> – it understands ranges, classes and even regexes:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def classify(hours)\n  case hours\n  when 0...4 then \"half day\"\n  when 4...9 then \"full day\"\n  else            \"overtime!\"\n  end\nend\n\nclassify(7.5)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Regexp.html' target='_blank'>Regexp: patterns</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/MatchData.html' target='_blank'>MatchData</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/control_expressions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>case/when</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>parse_line(line)</code>: it splits a timelog line using named groups and returns <code>{ project:, from:, to: }</code> – or <code>nil</code> for lines that don't match. Test it as the last line: <code>parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\")</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def parse_line(line)\n#   pattern = /.../  # named groups: date, from, to, project\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Take the pattern from the demo cell as your template. Then apply <code>match</code> to the line, return <code>nil</code> at once when it is <code>nil</code>, and otherwise pack the three named groups into a hash."
            ],
            "check": "parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\") == { project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\" } && parse_line(\"coffee break\").nil? && code.include?(\"(?<\")",
            "hint": "Pattern like in the demo. Then: <code>hit = line.match(pattern)</code>, <code>return nil unless hit</code>, then <code>{ project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }</code>.",
            "solution": "def parse_line(line)\n  pattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\n  hit = line.match(pattern)\n  return nil unless hit\n  { project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "テキストのパース：正規表現",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>エントリをパースする：正規表現</h2><p>timelogには、次のような行を読み取れるようになってもらいます：</p><pre><code>2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting</code></pre><p>そのためにあるのが<strong>正規表現</strong>（regex）です。正規表現は、テキストの形を表すパターンです。<code>\\d</code>は数字1文字、<code>{2}</code>は「ちょうど2つ」という意味です。そして<code>(?&lt;name&gt;…)</code>を使うと、マッチした部分に名前を付けられます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "line = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting\"\n\npattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\nhit = line.match(pattern)\nhit[:project]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>match</code>は<code>MatchData</code>オブジェクトを返します。名前付きグループは<code>hit[:from]</code>のように取り出せます。何もマッチしなければ<code>nil</code>が返ってきます（<code>if</code>にぴったりです）。データはいらず、マッチするかどうかだけを手早く調べたいなら<code>line.match?(pattern)</code>を使います。</p><p>条件分岐には、Rubyのエレガントな<code>case/when</code>があります。範囲やクラス、さらには正規表現まで理解してくれます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def classify(hours)\n  case hours\n  when 0...4 then \"half day\"\n  when 4...9 then \"full day\"\n  else            \"overtime!\"\n  end\nend\n\nclassify(7.5)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fregexp.html' target='_blank'>正規表現</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/MatchData.html' target='_blank'>MatchData</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fcontrol.html' target='_blank'>case/when</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>parse_line(line)</code>を書いてください。このメソッドは、名前付きグループを使ってtimelogの行を分解し、<code>{ project:, from:, to: }</code>を返します。マッチしない行には<code>nil</code>を返します。最後の行に<code>parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\")</code>と書いて試してみましょう。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def parse_line(line)\n#   pattern = /.../  # 名前付きグループ：date、from、to、project\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "デモのセルのパターンをひな形にしてね。行に<code>match</code>をかけて、<code>nil</code>ならすぐ<code>nil</code>を返す。そうでなければ、名前つきグループ3つをハッシュに詰めるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\") == { project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\" } && parse_line(\"coffee break\").nil? && code.include?(\"(?<\")",
            "hint": "パターンはデモと同じでいいよ。そのあとは<code>hit = line.match(pattern)</code>、<code>return nil unless hit</code>、そして<code>{ project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }</code>を返そう。",
            "solution": "def parse_line(line)\n  pattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\n  hit = line.match(pattern)\n  return nil unless hit\n  { project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Analyser du texte : les regex",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Analyser les saisies – les expressions régulières</h2><p>timelog doit comprendre des lignes comme celle-ci :</p><pre><code>2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting</code></pre><p>C'est à cela que servent les <strong>expressions régulières</strong> (regex) : des motifs qui décrivent du texte. <code>\\d</code> est un chiffre, <code>{2}</code> signifie « exactement deux », et <code>(?&lt;name&gt;…)</code> donne un nom à une correspondance :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "line = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting\"\n\npattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\nhit = line.match(pattern)\nhit[:project]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>match</code> renvoie un objet <code>MatchData</code> – vous récupérez les groupes nommés avec <code>hit[:from]</code>. Si rien ne correspond, vous obtenez <code>nil</code> (parfait pour un <code>if</code>). Test rapide sans données : <code>line.match?(pattern)</code>.</p><p>Pour les branchements, Ruby propose l'élégant <code>case/when</code> – il comprend les intervalles, les classes et même les regex :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def classify(hours)\n  case hours\n  when 0...4 then \"half day\"\n  when 4...9 then \"full day\"\n  else            \"overtime!\"\n  end\nend\n\nclassify(7.5)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Regexp.html' target='_blank'>Regexp : les motifs</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/MatchData.html' target='_blank'>MatchData</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/control_expressions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>case/when</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>parse_line(line)</code> : elle découpe une ligne de timelog à l'aide de groupes nommés et renvoie <code>{ project:, from:, to: }</code> – ou <code>nil</code> pour les lignes qui ne correspondent pas. Testez-la en dernière ligne : <code>parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\")</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def parse_line(line)\n#   pattern = /.../  # groupes nommés : date, from, to, project\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Prenez le motif de la cellule de démonstration comme modèle. Appliquez ensuite <code>match</code> à la ligne, renvoyez immédiatement <code>nil</code> si le résultat est <code>nil</code>, et sinon rangez les trois groupes nommés dans un hash."
            ],
            "check": "parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\") == { project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\" } && parse_line(\"coffee break\").nil? && code.include?(\"(?<\")",
            "hint": "Un motif comme dans la démo. Ensuite : <code>hit = line.match(pattern)</code>, <code>return nil unless hit</code>, puis <code>{ project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }</code>.",
            "solution": "def parse_line(line)\n  pattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\n  hit = line.match(pattern)\n  return nil unless hit\n  { project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Interpretare testo: regex",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Interpretare le voci – espressioni regolari</h2><p>timelog deve capire righe come questa:</p><pre><code>2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting</code></pre><p>A questo servono le <strong>espressioni regolari</strong> (regex): schemi che descrivono del testo. <code>\\d</code> è una cifra, <code>{2}</code> significa “esattamente due”, e <code>(?&lt;name&gt;…)</code> dà un nome a una corrispondenza:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "line = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX planning-meeting\"\n\npattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\nhit = line.match(pattern)\nhit[:project]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>match</code> restituisce un oggetto <code>MatchData</code> – i gruppi con nome si leggono con <code>hit[:from]</code>. Se non c'è corrispondenza ottieni <code>nil</code> (perfetto per <code>if</code>). Verifica rapida senza dati: <code>line.match?(pattern)</code>.</p><p>Per le diramazioni Ruby ha l'elegante <code>case/when</code> – capisce intervalli, classi e persino regex:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def classify(hours)\n  case hours\n  when 0...4 then \"half day\"\n  when 4...9 then \"full day\"\n  else            \"overtime!\"\n  end\nend\n\nclassify(7.5)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Regexp.html' target='_blank'>Regexp: schemi</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/MatchData.html' target='_blank'>MatchData</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/control_expressions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>case/when</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>parse_line(line)</code>: scompone una riga di timelog con gruppi con nome e restituisce <code>{ project:, from:, to: }</code> – oppure <code>nil</code> per le righe che non corrispondono. Provala come ultima riga: <code>parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\")</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def parse_line(line)\n#   pattern = /.../  # gruppi con nome: date, from, to, project\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Prendi come modello lo schema della cella dimostrativa. Poi applica <code>match</code> alla riga, restituisci subito <code>nil</code> quando il risultato è <code>nil</code>, e altrimenti metti i tre gruppi con nome in un hash."
            ],
            "check": "parse_line(\"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX meeting\") == { project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\" } && parse_line(\"coffee break\").nil? && code.include?(\"(?<\")",
            "hint": "Schema come nella dimostrazione. Poi: <code>hit = line.match(pattern)</code>, <code>return nil unless hit</code>, quindi <code>{ project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }</code>.",
            "solution": "def parse_line(line)\n  pattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<from>\\d{2}:\\d{2})-(?<to>\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<project>\\S+)/\n  hit = line.match(pattern)\n  return nil unless hit\n  { project: hit[:project], from: hit[:from], to: hit[:to] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-methods",
      "de": {
        "title": "Methoden richtig bauen",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Methoden mit Stil</h2><p>Du kennst <code>def</code> – jetzt kommen die Feinheiten, die Ruby-Code lesbar machen. <strong>Keyword-Argumente</strong> machen Aufrufe selbsterklärend, Defaults machen Argumente optional:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def gruss(name:, laut: false)\n  text = \"Hallo, #{name}\"\n  laut ? text.upcase + \"!\" : text\nend\n\ngruss(name: \"Kaz\", laut: true)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Vergleiche <code>gruss(name: \"Kaz\")</code> mit einem anonymen <code>gruss(\"Kaz\", true)</code> – bei mehreren Argumenten gewinnt die Keyword-Variante klar an Lesbarkeit. Weitere Konventionen: <code>*rest</code> sammelt beliebig viele Argumente ein, Methoden mit <code>?</code> geben wahr/falsch zurück, Methoden mit <code>!</code> sind die „gefährliche“ Variante. Und: Der Wert der letzten Zeile ist automatisch der Rückgabewert.</p><p>Zur Erinnerung aus Lektion {{nr:methoden}}: Klammern sind optional. Zusammen mit Keyword-Argumenten entsteht so der deklarative Ruby-Stil, den du aus <code>attr_reader :name</code> kennst – <code>add_entry projekt: \"X\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"10:00\"</code> liest sich fast wie Konfiguration. In verschachtelten Ausdrücken gehören die Klammern aber wieder hin.</p><p>Für timelog brauchen wir Zeitrechnung – <code>\"08:30\"</code> in Stunden seit Mitternacht:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def als_stunden(uhrzeit)\n  h, m = uhrzeit.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\nals_stunden(\"08:30\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Keyword+Arguments' target='_blank'>Keyword-Argumente</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/calling_methods_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Methoden aufrufen</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>add_entry(projekt:, von:, bis:, notiz: nil)</code>. Sie gibt einen Hash zurück: <code>{ projekt:, von:, bis:, notiz:, stunden: }</code>, wobei <code>stunden</code> die Differenz aus <code>als_stunden(bis)</code> und <code>als_stunden(von)</code> ist. <code>add_entry(projekt: \"X\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"10:00\")</code> soll also <code>stunden: 1.5</code> enthalten.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def als_stunden(uhrzeit)\n  h, m = uhrzeit.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\n# def add_entry(projekt:, von:, bis:, notiz: nil)\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Entferne die <code>#</code> vor dem Methodengerüst. Die letzte Zeile der Methode ist ein Hash-Literal mit den fünf Schlüsseln aus der Aufgabe – <code>stunden</code> rechnest du mit <code>als_stunden</code> aus."
            ],
            "check": "e = add_entry(projekt: \"X\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"10:00\"); e[:stunden] == 1.5 && e[:projekt] == \"X\" && e[:notiz].nil? && add_entry(projekt: \"Y\", von: \"09:00\", bis: \"17:00\", notiz: \"Doku\")[:notiz] == \"Doku\" && code.include?(\"projekt:\")",
            "hint": "<code>{ projekt: projekt, von: von, bis: bis, notiz: notiz, stunden: als_stunden(bis) - als_stunden(von) }</code> – als letzte Zeile der Methode.",
            "solution": "def als_stunden(uhrzeit)\n  h, m = uhrzeit.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\ndef add_entry(projekt:, von:, bis:, notiz: nil)\n  { projekt: projekt, von: von, bis: bis, notiz: notiz, stunden: als_stunden(bis) - als_stunden(von) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Building methods properly",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Methods with style</h2><p>You know <code>def</code> – now come the finer points that make Ruby code readable. <strong>Keyword arguments</strong> make calls self-explaining, defaults make arguments optional:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def greet(name:, loud: false)\n  text = \"Hello, #{name}\"\n  loud ? text.upcase + \"!\" : text\nend\n\ngreet(name: \"Kaz\", loud: true)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Compare <code>greet(name: \"Kaz\")</code> with an anonymous <code>greet(\"Kaz\", true)</code> – with several arguments the keyword variant clearly wins on readability. More conventions: <code>*rest</code> collects any number of arguments, methods ending in <code>?</code> return true/false, methods ending in <code>!</code> are the “dangerous” variant. And: the value of the last line is automatically the return value.</p><p>Remember lesson {{nr:methoden}}: parentheses are optional. Combined with keyword arguments this creates the declarative Ruby style you know from <code>attr_reader :name</code> – <code>add_entry project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"</code> reads almost like configuration. In nested expressions, though, the parentheses go back in.</p><p>timelog needs time math – <code>\"08:30\"</code> as hours since midnight:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\nas_hours(\"08:30\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Keyword+Arguments' target='_blank'>Keyword arguments</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/calling_methods_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Calling methods</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)</code>. It returns a hash: <code>{ project:, from:, to:, note:, hours: }</code>, where <code>hours</code> is the difference of <code>as_hours(to)</code> and <code>as_hours(from)</code>. So <code>add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\")</code> should contain <code>hours: 1.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\n# def add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Remove the <code>#</code> in front of the method skeleton. The method's last line is a hash literal with the five keys from the task – you work out <code>hours</code> with <code>as_hours</code>."
            ],
            "check": "e = add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"); e[:hours] == 1.5 && e[:project] == \"X\" && e[:note].nil? && add_entry(project: \"Y\", from: \"09:00\", to: \"17:00\", note: \"docs\")[:note] == \"docs\" && code.include?(\"project:\")",
            "hint": "<code>{ project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }</code> – as the method's last line.",
            "solution": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\ndef add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n  { project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "メソッドをきちんと作る",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>メソッドをスマートに</h2><p><code>def</code>はもう知っていますね。ここでは、Rubyのコードを読みやすくするための細かな工夫を紹介します。<strong>キーワード引数</strong>を使うと、呼び出しを見ただけで意味がわかるようになります。デフォルト値を決めておけば、その引数は省略できるようになります：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def greet(name:, loud: false)\n  text = \"Hello, #{name}\"\n  loud ? text.upcase + \"!\" : text\nend\n\ngreet(name: \"Kaz\", loud: true)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>greet(name: \"Kaz\")</code>と、何を渡しているのかわからない<code>greet(\"Kaz\", true)</code>を比べてみてください。引数がいくつもあるときは、読みやすさの点でキーワード引数のほうがはっきり有利です。ほかにも決まりごとがあります。<code>*rest</code>は任意の数の引数をまとめて受け取ります。<code>?</code>で終わるメソッドはtrueかfalseを返し、<code>!</code>で終わるメソッドは「危険な」バージョンです。そして、最後の行の値が自動的に戻り値になります。</p><p>レッスン{{nr:methoden}}を思い出してください。メソッド呼び出しのかっこは省略できるのでした。これをキーワード引数と組み合わせると、<code>attr_reader :name</code>でおなじみの、宣言的なRubyのスタイルになります。<code>add_entry project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"</code>は、まるで設定ファイルのように読めます。ただし、式が入れ子になるときは、かっこを付けましょう。</p><p>timelogには時刻の計算が必要です。<code>\"08:30\"</code>を、午前0時から数えて何時間かという数値に直します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\nas_hours(\"08:30\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fdef.html' target='_blank'>キーワード引数</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fcall.html' target='_blank'>メソッド呼び出し</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)</code>を書いてください。戻り値はハッシュ<code>{ project:, from:, to:, note:, hours: }</code>で、<code>hours</code>は<code>as_hours(to)</code>と<code>as_hours(from)</code>の差です。つまり<code>add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\")</code>の結果には、<code>hours: 1.5</code>が含まれるはずです。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\n# def add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "メソッドの骨組みの<code>#</code>を外してね。メソッドの最後の行は、課題にある5つのキーを持つハッシュのリテラルだよ。<code>hours</code>は<code>as_hours</code>で計算するんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "e = add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"); e[:hours] == 1.5 && e[:project] == \"X\" && e[:note].nil? && add_entry(project: \"Y\", from: \"09:00\", to: \"17:00\", note: \"docs\")[:note] == \"docs\" && code.include?(\"project:\")",
            "hint": "メソッドの最後の行に<code>{ project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }</code>と書けばOKだよ。",
            "solution": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\ndef add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n  { project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Bien construire ses méthodes",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Des méthodes avec style</h2><p>Vous connaissez <code>def</code> – voici maintenant les subtilités qui rendent le code Ruby lisible. Les <strong>arguments nommés</strong> (keyword arguments) rendent les appels explicites, les valeurs par défaut rendent les arguments facultatifs :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def greet(name:, loud: false)\n  text = \"Hello, #{name}\"\n  loud ? text.upcase + \"!\" : text\nend\n\ngreet(name: \"Kaz\", loud: true)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Comparez <code>greet(name: \"Kaz\")</code> à un anonyme <code>greet(\"Kaz\", true)</code> – dès qu'il y a plusieurs arguments, la variante nommée l'emporte nettement en lisibilité. Autres conventions : <code>*rest</code> recueille un nombre quelconque d'arguments, les méthodes terminées par <code>?</code> renvoient true/false, celles terminées par <code>!</code> sont la variante « dangereuse ». Et : la valeur de la dernière ligne est automatiquement la valeur de retour.</p><p>Souvenez-vous de la leçon {{nr:methoden}} : les parenthèses sont facultatives. Combiné aux arguments nommés, cela donne le style déclaratif de Ruby que vous connaissez avec <code>attr_reader :name</code> – <code>add_entry project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"</code> se lit presque comme de la configuration. Dans les expressions imbriquées, en revanche, on remet les parenthèses.</p><p>timelog a besoin de calculs horaires – <code>\"08:30\"</code> en heures depuis minuit :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\nas_hours(\"08:30\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Keyword+Arguments' target='_blank'>Arguments nommés</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/calling_methods_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Appeler des méthodes</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)</code>. Elle renvoie un hash : <code>{ project:, from:, to:, note:, hours: }</code>, où <code>hours</code> est la différence entre <code>as_hours(to)</code> et <code>as_hours(from)</code>. Ainsi, <code>add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\")</code> doit contenir <code>hours: 1.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\n# def add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Supprimez le <code>#</code> devant le squelette de la méthode. La dernière ligne de la méthode est un hash littéral avec les cinq clés de l'exercice – vous calculez <code>hours</code> avec <code>as_hours</code>."
            ],
            "check": "e = add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"); e[:hours] == 1.5 && e[:project] == \"X\" && e[:note].nil? && add_entry(project: \"Y\", from: \"09:00\", to: \"17:00\", note: \"docs\")[:note] == \"docs\" && code.include?(\"project:\")",
            "hint": "<code>{ project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }</code> – comme dernière ligne de la méthode.",
            "solution": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\ndef add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n  { project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Costruire bene i metodi",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Metodi con stile</h2><p>Conosci <code>def</code> – ora arrivano le finezze che rendono leggibile il codice Ruby. Gli <strong>argomenti keyword</strong> rendono le chiamate autoesplicative, i valori predefiniti rendono gli argomenti facoltativi:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def greet(name:, loud: false)\n  text = \"Hello, #{name}\"\n  loud ? text.upcase + \"!\" : text\nend\n\ngreet(name: \"Kaz\", loud: true)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Confronta <code>greet(name: \"Kaz\")</code> con un anonimo <code>greet(\"Kaz\", true)</code> – con più argomenti la variante keyword vince nettamente in leggibilità. Altre convenzioni: <code>*rest</code> raccoglie un numero qualsiasi di argomenti, i metodi che terminano con <code>?</code> restituiscono true/false, quelli che terminano con <code>!</code> sono la variante “pericolosa”. E ancora: il valore dell'ultima riga è automaticamente il valore di ritorno.</p><p>Ricorda la lezione {{nr:methoden}}: le parentesi sono facoltative. Insieme agli argomenti keyword nasce così lo stile dichiarativo di Ruby che conosci da <code>attr_reader :name</code> – <code>add_entry project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"</code> si legge quasi come una configurazione. Nelle espressioni annidate, però, le parentesi tornano.</p><p>timelog ha bisogno di calcoli con gli orari – <code>\"08:30\"</code> come ore dalla mezzanotte:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\nas_hours(\"08:30\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Keyword+Arguments' target='_blank'>Argomenti keyword</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/calling_methods_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Chiamare i metodi</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)</code>. Restituisce un hash: <code>{ project:, from:, to:, note:, hours: }</code>, dove <code>hours</code> è la differenza tra <code>as_hours(to)</code> e <code>as_hours(from)</code>. Quindi <code>add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\")</code> deve contenere <code>hours: 1.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\n# def add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Togli il <code>#</code> davanti allo scheletro del metodo. L'ultima riga del metodo è un letterale hash con le cinque chiavi dell'esercizio – <code>hours</code> lo calcoli con <code>as_hours</code>."
            ],
            "check": "e = add_entry(project: \"X\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"10:00\"); e[:hours] == 1.5 && e[:project] == \"X\" && e[:note].nil? && add_entry(project: \"Y\", from: \"09:00\", to: \"17:00\", note: \"docs\")[:note] == \"docs\" && code.include?(\"project:\")",
            "hint": "<code>{ project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }</code> – come ultima riga del metodo.",
            "solution": "def as_hours(time)\n  h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n  h + m / 60.0\nend\n\ndef add_entry(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n  { project: project, from: from, to: to, note: note, hours: as_hours(to) - as_hours(from) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-classes",
      "de": {
        "title": "Entry & Timesheet",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Vom Hash zur Klasse</h2><p>Hashes sind super zum Anfangen – aber sobald Daten <em>Verhalten</em> brauchen (Stunden ausrechnen, sich schön ausgeben), ist eine Klasse der richtige Ort. Wir bauen timelog um zwei Klassen: <code>Entry</code> (ein Eintrag) und <code>Timesheet</code> (die Sammlung).</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :projekt, :von, :bis, :notiz\n\n  def initialize(projekt:, von:, bis:, notiz: nil)\n    @projekt = projekt\n    @von = von\n    @bis = bis\n    @notiz = notiz\n  end\n\n  def stunden\n    als_stunden(@bis) - als_stunden(@von)\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{@projekt}: #{@von}-#{@bis} (#{stunden}h)\"\n  end\n\n  private\n\n  def als_stunden(uhrzeit)\n    h, m = uhrzeit.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n    h + m / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nEntry.new(projekt: \"ProjectX\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"12:00\").to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Beachte: <code>als_stunden</code> ist <code>private</code> – ein internes Detail, das von aussen niemand braucht. <code>to_s</code> bestimmt, wie sich das Objekt als Text ausgibt. Instanzvariablen (<code>@projekt</code>) gehören zum Objekt; Konstanten (<code>GROSS</code>) zur Klasse. Von Klassenvariablen (<code>@@…</code>) und globalen Variablen (<code>$…</code>) lässt man besser die Finger – sie sind geteilter Zustand, der schwer zu verfolgen ist.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @eintraege = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @eintraege << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def anzahl\n    @eintraege.length\n  end\nend\n\nblatt = Timesheet.new\nblatt.add(Entry.new(projekt: \"ProjectX\", von: \"08:30\", bis: \"12:00\"))\nblatt.anzahl"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Das <code>self</code> am Ende von <code>add</code> erlaubt Verkettung: <code>blatt.add(a).add(b)</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/modules_and_classes_rdoc.html#label-Visibility' target='_blank'>Sichtbarkeit: private</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-to_s' target='_blank'>to_s</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Ergänze <code>Timesheet</code> um <code>total_for(projekt)</code>: die Gesamtstunden aller Einträge dieses Projekts. Nutze <code>select</code> und <code>sum</code> auf <code>@eintraege</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Entry aus der Demo-Zelle oben wird hier weiterverwendet -\n# fuehre sie zuerst aus!\n\nclass Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @eintraege = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @eintraege << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  # def total_for(projekt)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Führ zuerst die Demo-Zelle mit <code>Entry</code> aus. Dann aktivierst du <code>total_for</code>: Aus <code>@eintraege</code> die Einträge dieses Projekts mit <code>select</code> herausfiltern und ihre Stunden mit <code>sum</code> zusammenzählen."
            ],
            "check": "ts = Timesheet.new.add(Entry.new(projekt: \"A\", von: \"08:00\", bis: \"10:30\")).add(Entry.new(projekt: \"B\", von: \"10:30\", bis: \"11:30\")).add(Entry.new(projekt: \"A\", von: \"13:00\", bis: \"14:00\")); ts.total_for(\"A\") == 3.5 && ts.total_for(\"B\") == 1.0 && ts.total_for(\"C\") == 0",
            "hint": "<code>def total_for(projekt); @eintraege.select { |e| e.projekt == projekt }.sum(&:stunden); end</code> – und vorher die Entry-Zelle oben ausführen.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @eintraege = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @eintraege << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def total_for(projekt)\n    @eintraege.select { |e| e.projekt == projekt }.sum(&:stunden)\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Entry & Timesheet",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>From hash to class</h2><p>Hashes are great to start with – but as soon as data needs <em>behavior</em> (computing hours, printing itself nicely), a class is the right home. We build timelog around two classes: <code>Entry</code> (one entry) and <code>Timesheet</code> (the collection).</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :from, :to, :note\n\n  def initialize(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n    @project = project\n    @from = from\n    @to = to\n    @note = note\n  end\n\n  def hours\n    as_hours(@to) - as_hours(@from)\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{@project}: #{@from}-#{@to} (#{hours}h)\"\n  end\n\n  private\n\n  def as_hours(time)\n    h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n    h + m / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nEntry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\").to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Note: <code>as_hours</code> is <code>private</code> – an internal detail nobody outside needs. <code>to_s</code> decides how the object prints as text. Instance variables (<code>@project</code>) belong to the object; constants (<code>BIG</code>) to the class. Class variables (<code>@@…</code>) and globals (<code>$…</code>) are best avoided – shared state that's hard to trace.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def count\n    @entries.length\n  end\nend\n\nsheet = Timesheet.new\nsheet.add(Entry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\"))\nsheet.count"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The <code>self</code> at the end of <code>add</code> enables chaining: <code>sheet.add(a).add(b)</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/modules_and_classes_rdoc.html#label-Visibility' target='_blank'>Visibility: private</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-to_s' target='_blank'>to_s</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Extend <code>Timesheet</code> with <code>total_for(project)</code>: the total hours of all entries for that project. Use <code>select</code> and <code>sum</code> on <code>@entries</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Entry from the demo cell above is reused here -\n# run it first!\n\nclass Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  # def total_for(project)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Run the demo cell with <code>Entry</code> first. Then enable <code>total_for</code>: filter this project's entries out of <code>@entries</code> with <code>select</code> and add up their hours with <code>sum</code>."
            ],
            "check": "ts = Timesheet.new.add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"08:00\", to: \"10:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"B\", from: \"10:30\", to: \"11:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"13:00\", to: \"14:00\")); ts.total_for(\"A\") == 3.5 && ts.total_for(\"B\") == 1.0 && ts.total_for(\"C\") == 0",
            "hint": "<code>def total_for(project); @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours); end</code> – and run the Entry cell above first.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def total_for(project)\n    @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours)\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "EntryとTimesheet",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>ハッシュからクラスへ</h2><p>最初のうちはハッシュで十分です。でも、データに<em>ふるまい</em>（時間を計算する、自分をきれいに表示する、など）が必要になったら、クラスの出番です。timelogは2つのクラスを中心に組み立てていきます。1件のエントリを表す<code>Entry</code>と、エントリをまとめる<code>Timesheet</code>です。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :from, :to, :note\n\n  def initialize(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n    @project = project\n    @from = from\n    @to = to\n    @note = note\n  end\n\n  def hours\n    as_hours(@to) - as_hours(@from)\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{@project}: #{@from}-#{@to} (#{hours}h)\"\n  end\n\n  private\n\n  def as_hours(time)\n    h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n    h + m / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nEntry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\").to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ポイント：<code>as_hours</code>は<code>private</code>にしてあります。外から使う人のいない、内部の細かい処理だからです。<code>to_s</code>は、オブジェクトを文字列にしたときの見た目を決めます。インスタンス変数（<code>@project</code>）はオブジェクトのもの、定数（<code>BIG</code>）はクラスのものです。クラス変数（<code>@@…</code>）やグローバル変数（<code>$…</code>）は、なるべく使わないようにしましょう。あちこちで共有される状態になり、どこで変わったのかを追いかけにくくなるからです。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def count\n    @entries.length\n  end\nend\n\nsheet = Timesheet.new\nsheet.add(Entry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\"))\nsheet.count"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>add</code>の最後で<code>self</code>を返しているので、<code>sheet.add(a).add(b)</code>のように呼び出しをつなげられます（メソッドチェーン）。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fdef.html' target='_blank'>可視性：private</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Object/i/to_s.html' target='_blank'>to_s</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>Timesheet</code>に<code>total_for(project)</code>を追加しましょう。指定したプロジェクトのエントリをすべて集めて、時間を合計するメソッドです。<code>@entries</code>に<code>select</code>と<code>sum</code>を使います。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# 上のデモセルのEntryをここでも使う。\n# 先にそのセルを実行しておこう！\n\nclass Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  # def total_for(project)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "まず<code>Entry</code>のデモのセルを実行してね。それから<code>total_for</code>を有効にして、<code>@entries</code>からそのプロジェクトのエントリを<code>select</code>で選び、<code>sum</code>で時間をたすんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "ts = Timesheet.new.add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"08:00\", to: \"10:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"B\", from: \"10:30\", to: \"11:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"13:00\", to: \"14:00\")); ts.total_for(\"A\") == 3.5 && ts.total_for(\"B\") == 1.0 && ts.total_for(\"C\") == 0",
            "hint": "こう書けるよ：<code>def total_for(project); @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours); end</code>。それと、上のEntryのセルを先に実行しておいてね。",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def total_for(project)\n    @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours)\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Entry et Timesheet",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Du hash à la classe</h2><p>Les hashes sont parfaits pour commencer – mais dès que des données ont besoin d'un <em>comportement</em> (calculer des heures, s'afficher proprement), une classe est le bon endroit pour les accueillir. Nous construisons timelog autour de deux classes : <code>Entry</code> (une saisie) et <code>Timesheet</code> (la collection).</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :from, :to, :note\n\n  def initialize(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n    @project = project\n    @from = from\n    @to = to\n    @note = note\n  end\n\n  def hours\n    as_hours(@to) - as_hours(@from)\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{@project}: #{@from}-#{@to} (#{hours}h)\"\n  end\n\n  private\n\n  def as_hours(time)\n    h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n    h + m / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nEntry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\").to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Remarque : <code>as_hours</code> est <code>private</code> – un détail interne dont personne n'a besoin à l'extérieur. <code>to_s</code> décide de la façon dont l'objet s'affiche sous forme de texte. Les variables d'instance (<code>@project</code>) appartiennent à l'objet ; les constantes (<code>BIG</code>) à la classe. Mieux vaut éviter les variables de classe (<code>@@…</code>) et les variables globales (<code>$…</code>) – un état partagé difficile à suivre.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def count\n    @entries.length\n  end\nend\n\nsheet = Timesheet.new\nsheet.add(Entry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\"))\nsheet.count"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le <code>self</code> à la fin de <code>add</code> permet d'enchaîner les appels : <code>sheet.add(a).add(b)</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/modules_and_classes_rdoc.html#label-Visibility' target='_blank'>Visibilité : private</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-to_s' target='_blank'>to_s</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Ajoutez à <code>Timesheet</code> une méthode <code>total_for(project)</code> : le total des heures de toutes les saisies de ce projet. Utilisez <code>select</code> et <code>sum</code> sur <code>@entries</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Entry, de la cellule de démonstration ci-dessus, est réutilisée ici -\n# exécutez-la d'abord !\n\nclass Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  # def total_for(project)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Exécutez d'abord la cellule de démonstration contenant <code>Entry</code>. Activez ensuite <code>total_for</code> : filtrez les saisies de ce projet dans <code>@entries</code> avec <code>select</code> et additionnez leurs heures avec <code>sum</code>."
            ],
            "check": "ts = Timesheet.new.add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"08:00\", to: \"10:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"B\", from: \"10:30\", to: \"11:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"13:00\", to: \"14:00\")); ts.total_for(\"A\") == 3.5 && ts.total_for(\"B\") == 1.0 && ts.total_for(\"C\") == 0",
            "hint": "<code>def total_for(project); @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours); end</code> – et exécutez d'abord la cellule Entry ci-dessus.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def total_for(project)\n    @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours)\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Entry e Timesheet",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Dall'hash alla classe</h2><p>Gli hash sono ottimi per cominciare – ma appena i dati hanno bisogno di un <em>comportamento</em> (calcolare le ore, stamparsi in modo ordinato), la casa giusta è una classe. Costruiamo timelog attorno a due classi: <code>Entry</code> (una voce) e <code>Timesheet</code> (la raccolta).</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :from, :to, :note\n\n  def initialize(project:, from:, to:, note: nil)\n    @project = project\n    @from = from\n    @to = to\n    @note = note\n  end\n\n  def hours\n    as_hours(@to) - as_hours(@from)\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{@project}: #{@from}-#{@to} (#{hours}h)\"\n  end\n\n  private\n\n  def as_hours(time)\n    h, m = time.split(\":\").map(&:to_i)\n    h + m / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nEntry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\").to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Nota: <code>as_hours</code> è <code>private</code> – un dettaglio interno che a nessuno fuori serve. <code>to_s</code> decide come l'oggetto viene stampato come testo. Le variabili d'istanza (<code>@project</code>) appartengono all'oggetto; le costanti (<code>BIG</code>) alla classe. Variabili di classe (<code>@@…</code>) e globali (<code>$…</code>) è meglio evitarle – stato condiviso difficile da seguire.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def count\n    @entries.length\n  end\nend\n\nsheet = Timesheet.new\nsheet.add(Entry.new(project: \"ProjectX\", from: \"08:30\", to: \"12:00\"))\nsheet.count"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Il <code>self</code> alla fine di <code>add</code> permette di concatenare: <code>sheet.add(a).add(b)</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/modules_and_classes_rdoc.html#label-Visibility' target='_blank'>Visibilità: private</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-to_s' target='_blank'>to_s</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Estendi <code>Timesheet</code> con <code>total_for(project)</code>: il totale delle ore di tutte le voci di quel progetto. Usa <code>select</code> e <code>sum</code> su <code>@entries</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Qui si riusa Entry della cella dimostrativa qui sopra -\n# eseguila prima!\n\nclass Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  # def total_for(project)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Esegui prima la cella dimostrativa con <code>Entry</code>. Poi attiva <code>total_for</code>: filtra da <code>@entries</code> con <code>select</code> le voci di questo progetto e somma le loro ore con <code>sum</code>."
            ],
            "check": "ts = Timesheet.new.add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"08:00\", to: \"10:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"B\", from: \"10:30\", to: \"11:30\")).add(Entry.new(project: \"A\", from: \"13:00\", to: \"14:00\")); ts.total_for(\"A\") == 3.5 && ts.total_for(\"B\") == 1.0 && ts.total_for(\"C\") == 0",
            "hint": "<code>def total_for(project); @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours); end</code> – e prima esegui la cella con Entry qui sopra.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(entry)\n    @entries << entry\n    self\n  end\n\n  def total_for(project)\n    @entries.select { |e| e.project == project }.sum(&:hours)\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-time",
      "de": {
        "title": "Rechnen mit Zeit",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Uhrzeiten richtig: Time und Date</h2><p>Bisher rechnet timelog mit Strings: <code>\"08:30\"</code> wird von Hand zerlegt, und <code>als_stunden</code> macht eine Zahl daraus. Das geht gut – bis ein Eintrag über Mitternacht geht oder du wissen willst, auf welchen Wochentag der 15. September fällt. Ruby hat dafür zwei Klassen: <code>Time</code> für einen <strong>Zeitpunkt</strong> (Datum und Uhrzeit) und <code>Date</code> für einen <strong>Kalendertag</strong>.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "anfang = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30)\nende = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 12, 0)\nsekunden = ende - anfang\nsekunden / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Zieht man zwei Zeitpunkte voneinander ab, kommen die Sekunden dazwischen heraus, als <code>Float</code>; durch 3600 geteilt sind es Stunden. Umgekehrt zählst du Sekunden zu einem Zeitpunkt dazu – zum Beispiel eine Mittagspause von 45 Minuten:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "weiter = ende + 45 * 60\nweiter.strftime(\"%H:%M\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>strftime</code> („string format time“) macht aus einem Zeitpunkt Text, mit Platzhaltern: <code>%Y</code> Jahr, <code>%m</code> Monat, <code>%d</code> Tag, <code>%H</code> Stunde, <code>%M</code> Minute, <code>%A</code> Wochentag (auf Englisch). Was nicht mit <code>%</code> anfängt, bleibt stehen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts anfang.strftime(\"%d.%m.%Y\")\nputs anfang.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nputs anfang.strftime(\"%A\")\nanfang.wday   # Wochentag als Zahl: Sonntag = 0, Montag = 1 ..."
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Den umgekehrten Weg, von Text zum Zeitpunkt, geht <code>Time.strptime</code> („string parse time“) – mit denselben Platzhaltern. Es kommt aus der Bibliothek <code>time</code>. So wird aus einer timelog-Zeile eine echte Dauer:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\nzeile = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX\"\ndatum, zeiten = zeile.split(\" \")\nvon, bis = zeiten.split(\"-\")\nstart = Time.strptime(\"#{datum} #{von}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nschluss = Time.strptime(\"#{datum} #{bis}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n(schluss - start) / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Für ganze Tage ohne Uhrzeit gibt es <code>Date</code> aus der Bibliothek <code>date</code>. <code>Date</code> rechnet direkt in Tagen: <code>+ 1</code> ist morgen, und zwei Daten voneinander abgezogen ergeben die Tage dazwischen (als Bruch – <code>to_i</code> macht eine ganze Zahl daraus). Und einen Bereich von Daten kannst du durchlaufen wie einen Zahlenbereich:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"date\"\n\ntag = Date.new(2026, 9, 15)\nputs tag + 1\nbis_weihnachten = (Date.new(2026, 12, 24) - tag).to_i\nputs \"Noch #{bis_weihnachten} Tage bis Weihnachten\"\n\nwoche = tag..(tag + 6)\nwoche.count { |d| d.saturday? || d.sunday? }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Weitere Helfer für Berichte: <code>Date.parse(\"2026-09-15\")</code> liest ein Datum aus Text, <code>tag.cweek</code> nennt die Kalenderwoche, <code>Date.today</code> und <code>Time.now</code> den heutigen Tag und den Moment jetzt. Die beiden letzten sind in Tests allerdings unpraktisch: Sie liefern bei jedem Lauf etwas anderes. Gib einer Methode darum lieber die Zeit als Argument, statt sie drinnen nach <code>Time.now</code> fragen zu lassen.</p><p>Und ein Wort zu Zeitzonen: <code>Time.new</code> ohne Angabe nimmt die Zeitzone des Rechners – hier im Browser ist das immer UTC. Läuft ein Programm auf Servern in verschiedenen Ländern, gibt man die Zone ausdrücklich an: <code>Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30, 0, \"+02:00\")</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Time.html' target='_blank'>Time</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Date.html' target='_blank'>Date</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/language/strftime_formatting_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>alle Platzhalter von strftime</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>dauer_in_stunden(datum, von, bis)</code>: Die Methode macht aus Datum und den beiden Uhrzeiten zwei <code>Time</code>-Objekte und gibt die Stunden dazwischen zurück. Geht ein Eintrag über Mitternacht (<code>bis</code> liegt früher als <code>von</code>), endet er am nächsten Tag – dann zählst du einen Tag dazu, also <code>24 * 3600</code> Sekunden. <code>dauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")</code> ergibt <code>3.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\n# def dauer_in_stunden(datum, von, bis)\n#   anfang = Time.strptime(...)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# dauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")\n",
            "hints": [
              "Baue aus Datum und Uhrzeit einen String und lass <code>Time.strptime</code> mit dem passenden Format ein Time-Objekt daraus machen – für Anfang und Ende. Die Differenz zweier Zeiten sind Sekunden."
            ],
            "check": "dauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"08:30\", \"12:00\") == 3.5 && dauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\") == 3.5 && dauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"09:15\", \"10:00\") == 0.75 && code.include?(\"Time\")",
            "hint": "<code>anfang = Time.strptime(\"#{datum} #{von}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")</code>, genauso <code>ende</code>. Dann <code>ende += 24 * 3600 if ende &lt; anfang</code> und zum Schluss <code>(ende - anfang) / 3600</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"time\"\n\ndef dauer_in_stunden(datum, von, bis)\n  anfang = Time.strptime(\"#{datum} #{von}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ende = Time.strptime(\"#{datum} #{bis}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ende += 24 * 3600 if ende < anfang\n  (ende - anfang) / 3600\nend\n\ndauer_in_stunden(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Calculating with time",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Times done right: Time and Date</h2><p>So far timelog computes with strings: <code>\"08:30\"</code> is taken apart by hand, and <code>as_hours</code> turns it into a number. That works well – until an entry runs past midnight or you want to know which weekday September 15 falls on. Ruby has two classes for this: <code>Time</code> for a <strong>point in time</strong> (date and time of day) and <code>Date</code> for a <strong>calendar day</strong>.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "start = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30)\nfinish = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 12, 0)\nseconds = finish - start\nseconds / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Subtracting two points in time gives the seconds between them, as a <code>Float</code>; divided by 3600 that's hours. The other way round, you add seconds to a point in time – a 45-minute lunch break, for example:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "back = finish + 45 * 60\nback.strftime(\"%H:%M\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>strftime</code> (“string format time”) turns a point in time into text, using placeholders: <code>%Y</code> year, <code>%m</code> month, <code>%d</code> day, <code>%H</code> hour, <code>%M</code> minute, <code>%A</code> weekday. Anything that doesn't start with <code>%</code> stays as it is:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts start.strftime(\"%d.%m.%Y\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%A\")\nstart.wday   # the weekday as a number: Sunday = 0, Monday = 1 ..."
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The opposite way, from text to a point in time, is <code>Time.strptime</code> (“string parse time”) – with the same placeholders. It comes from the library <code>time</code>. This is how a timelog line becomes a real duration:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\nline = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX\"\ndate, times = line.split(\" \")\nfrom, to = times.split(\"-\")\nbegins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n(ends - begins) / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>For whole days without a time of day there's <code>Date</code> from the library <code>date</code>. <code>Date</code> computes in days directly: <code>+ 1</code> is tomorrow, and subtracting two dates gives the days between them (as a fraction – <code>to_i</code> makes a whole number of it). And you can walk through a range of dates just like a range of numbers:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"date\"\n\nday = Date.new(2026, 9, 15)\nputs day + 1\nuntil_christmas = (Date.new(2026, 12, 24) - day).to_i\nputs \"#{until_christmas} days until Christmas\"\n\nweek = day..(day + 6)\nweek.count { |d| d.saturday? || d.sunday? }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>More helpers for reports: <code>Date.parse(\"2026-09-15\")</code> reads a date from text, <code>day.cweek</code> gives the calendar week, <code>Date.today</code> and <code>Time.now</code> today's date and this very moment. Those last two are awkward in tests, though: they return something different on every run. So rather hand a method the time as an argument than let it ask <code>Time.now</code> inside.</p><p>And a word on time zones: <code>Time.new</code> without one takes the computer's time zone – here in the browser that's always UTC. If a program runs on servers in different countries, you state the zone explicitly: <code>Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30, 0, \"+02:00\")</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Time.html' target='_blank'>Time</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Date.html' target='_blank'>Date</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/language/strftime_formatting_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>all strftime placeholders</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>duration_in_hours(date, from, to)</code>: the method turns the date and the two times of day into two <code>Time</code> objects and returns the hours between them. If an entry runs past midnight (<code>to</code> is earlier than <code>from</code>), it ends on the next day – then you add a day, that is <code>24 * 3600</code> seconds. <code>duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")</code> gives <code>3.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\n# def duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n#   begins = Time.strptime(...)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")\n",
            "hints": [
              "Build a string from the date and the time of day and let <code>Time.strptime</code> with a matching format make a Time object of it – for the beginning and the end. The difference of two times is in seconds."
            ],
            "check": "duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"08:30\", \"12:00\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"09:15\", \"10:00\") == 0.75 && code.include?(\"Time\")",
            "hint": "<code>begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")</code>, the same for <code>ends</code>. Then <code>ends += 24 * 3600 if ends &lt; begins</code> and finally <code>(ends - begins) / 3600</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"time\"\n\ndef duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n  begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends += 24 * 3600 if ends < begins\n  (ends - begins) / 3600\nend\n\nduration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "時間を計算する",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>時刻を正しく扱う：TimeとDate</h2><p>これまでtimelogは文字列で計算してきました。<code>\"08:30\"</code>を手作業で分解し、<code>as_hours</code>で数に変えていたのです。それでもうまくいきます。ただし、記録が真夜中をまたいだり、9月15日が何曜日かを知りたくなったりするまでは。Rubyにはそのためのクラスが2つあります。<strong>時点</strong>（日付と時刻）を表す<code>Time</code>と、<strong>暦の上の1日</strong>を表す<code>Date</code>です。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "start = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30)\nfinish = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 12, 0)\nseconds = finish - start\nseconds / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>2つの時点を引き算すると、そのあいだの秒数が<code>Float</code>で得られます。3600でわれば時間です。逆に、時点に秒数をたすこともできます。たとえば45分の昼休み：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "back = finish + 45 * 60\nback.strftime(\"%H:%M\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>strftime</code>（「string format time」）は、時点を文字列にします。プレースホルダーは<code>%Y</code>年、<code>%m</code>月、<code>%d</code>日、<code>%H</code>時、<code>%M</code>分、<code>%A</code>曜日（英語）です。<code>%</code>で始まらない部分は、そのまま残ります：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts start.strftime(\"%d.%m.%Y\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%A\")\nstart.wday   # 曜日を数で：日曜 = 0、月曜 = 1 ..."
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>反対に、文字列から時点を作るのが<code>Time.strptime</code>（「string parse time」）で、同じプレースホルダーを使います。ライブラリ<code>time</code>に入っています。こうすれば、timelogの行から本物の長さが求められます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\nline = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX\"\ndate, times = line.split(\" \")\nfrom, to = times.split(\"-\")\nbegins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n(ends - begins) / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>時刻のない、まる1日を扱うには、ライブラリ<code>date</code>の<code>Date</code>を使います。<code>Date</code>は日単位で計算します。<code>+ 1</code>は明日で、2つの日付を引き算すると、そのあいだの日数になります（分数で返るので、<code>to_i</code>で整数にします）。日付の範囲も、数の範囲と同じようにたどれます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"date\"\n\nday = Date.new(2026, 9, 15)\nputs day + 1\nuntil_christmas = (Date.new(2026, 12, 24) - day).to_i\nputs \"#{until_christmas} days until Christmas\"\n\nweek = day..(day + 6)\nweek.count { |d| d.saturday? || d.sunday? }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>レポートに役立つメソッドはほかにもあります。<code>Date.parse(\"2026-09-15\")</code>は文字列から日付を読み取り、<code>day.cweek</code>は暦の上の週番号を、<code>Date.today</code>と<code>Time.now</code>は今日の日付と現在の時点を返します。ただし最後の2つは、テストでは扱いにくいものです。実行するたびに違う値を返すからです。メソッドの中で<code>Time.now</code>をたずねるより、時刻を引数として渡すほうがよいでしょう。</p><p>タイムゾーンについても一言。タイムゾーンを指定しない<code>Time.new</code>は、コンピューターのタイムゾーンを使います。このブラウザの中では、いつもUTCです。いろいろな国のサーバーで動くプログラムなら、<code>Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30, 0, \"+09:00\")</code>のようにゾーンをはっきり書きましょう。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Time.html' target='_blank'>Time</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Date.html' target='_blank'>Date</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Time/i/strftime.html' target='_blank'>strftimeのプレースホルダー</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>duration_in_hours(date, from, to)</code>を書きましょう。このメソッドは、日付と2つの時刻から<code>Time</code>オブジェクトを2つ作り、そのあいだの時間数を返します。記録が真夜中をまたぐ場合（<code>to</code>が<code>from</code>より早い場合）は、次の日に終わったということなので、1日分、つまり<code>24 * 3600</code>秒をたします。<code>duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")</code>は<code>3.5</code>になります。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\n# def duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n#   begins = Time.strptime(...)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")\n",
            "hints": [
              "日付と時刻をつなげた文字列を作って、<code>Time.strptime</code>に合う書式を渡してTimeオブジェクトにしてみて。はじまりと終わりの両方だよ。2つの時刻の差は秒になるからね。"
            ],
            "check": "duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"08:30\", \"12:00\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"09:15\", \"10:00\") == 0.75 && code.include?(\"Time\")",
            "hint": "<code>begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")</code>、<code>ends</code>も同じように作ろう。それから<code>ends += 24 * 3600 if ends &lt; begins</code>、最後に<code>(ends - begins) / 3600</code>だよ。",
            "solution": "require \"time\"\n\ndef duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n  begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends += 24 * 3600 if ends < begins\n  (ends - begins) / 3600\nend\n\nduration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Calculer avec le temps",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Bien gérer le temps : Time et Date</h2><p>Jusqu'ici, timelog calcule avec des chaînes : <code>\"08:30\"</code> est découpée à la main, et <code>as_hours</code> la transforme en nombre. Cela marche bien – jusqu'à ce qu'une saisie dépasse minuit ou que vous vouliez savoir quel jour de la semaine tombe le 15 septembre. Ruby possède deux classes pour cela : <code>Time</code> pour un <strong>instant</strong> (date et heure) et <code>Date</code> pour un <strong>jour du calendrier</strong>.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "start = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30)\nfinish = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 12, 0)\nseconds = finish - start\nseconds / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Soustraire deux instants donne les secondes qui les séparent, sous forme de <code>Float</code> ; divisé par 3600, cela fait des heures. Dans l'autre sens, on ajoute des secondes à un instant – une pause déjeuner de 45 minutes, par exemple :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "back = finish + 45 * 60\nback.strftime(\"%H:%M\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>strftime</code> (« string format time ») transforme un instant en texte, à l'aide de marqueurs : <code>%Y</code> l'année, <code>%m</code> le mois, <code>%d</code> le jour, <code>%H</code> l'heure, <code>%M</code> la minute, <code>%A</code> le jour de la semaine. Tout ce qui ne commence pas par <code>%</code> reste tel quel :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts start.strftime(\"%d.%m.%Y\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%A\")\nstart.wday   # le jour de la semaine sous forme de nombre : dimanche = 0, lundi = 1 ..."
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le chemin inverse, du texte vers un instant, c'est <code>Time.strptime</code> (« string parse time ») – avec les mêmes marqueurs. Elle provient de la bibliothèque <code>time</code>. Voici comment une ligne de timelog devient une vraie durée :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\nline = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX\"\ndate, times = line.split(\" \")\nfrom, to = times.split(\"-\")\nbegins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n(ends - begins) / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Pour des jours entiers sans heure, il existe <code>Date</code>, de la bibliothèque <code>date</code>. <code>Date</code> calcule directement en jours : <code>+ 1</code>, c'est demain, et soustraire deux dates donne le nombre de jours qui les séparent (sous forme de fraction – <code>to_i</code> en fait un nombre entier). Et vous pouvez parcourir un intervalle de dates exactement comme un intervalle de nombres :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"date\"\n\nday = Date.new(2026, 9, 15)\nputs day + 1\nuntil_christmas = (Date.new(2026, 12, 24) - day).to_i\nputs \"#{until_christmas} days until Christmas\"\n\nweek = day..(day + 6)\nweek.count { |d| d.saturday? || d.sunday? }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>D'autres outils pour les rapports : <code>Date.parse(\"2026-09-15\")</code> lit une date dans un texte, <code>day.cweek</code> donne le numéro de semaine, <code>Date.today</code> et <code>Time.now</code> la date du jour et l'instant présent. Ces deux dernières sont toutefois gênantes dans les tests : elles renvoient une valeur différente à chaque exécution. Mieux vaut donc passer l'heure à une méthode en argument que de la laisser interroger <code>Time.now</code> en interne.</p><p>Un mot sur les fuseaux horaires : <code>Time.new</code> sans fuseau prend celui de l'ordinateur – ici, dans le navigateur, c'est toujours UTC. Si un programme tourne sur des serveurs dans différents pays, indiquez le fuseau explicitement : <code>Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30, 0, \"+02:00\")</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Time.html' target='_blank'>Time</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Date.html' target='_blank'>Date</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/language/strftime_formatting_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>tous les marqueurs de strftime</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>duration_in_hours(date, from, to)</code> : la méthode transforme la date et les deux heures en deux objets <code>Time</code> et renvoie le nombre d'heures qui les séparent. Si une saisie dépasse minuit (<code>to</code> est antérieur à <code>from</code>), elle se termine le jour suivant – il faut alors ajouter un jour, soit <code>24 * 3600</code> secondes. <code>duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")</code> donne <code>3.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\n# def duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n#   begins = Time.strptime(...)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")\n",
            "hints": [
              "Construisez une chaîne à partir de la date et de l'heure, et laissez <code>Time.strptime</code>, avec un format adapté, en faire un objet Time – pour le début et pour la fin. La différence entre deux instants est exprimée en secondes."
            ],
            "check": "duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"08:30\", \"12:00\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"09:15\", \"10:00\") == 0.75 && code.include?(\"Time\")",
            "hint": "<code>begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")</code>, de même pour <code>ends</code>. Puis <code>ends += 24 * 3600 if ends &lt; begins</code> et enfin <code>(ends - begins) / 3600</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"time\"\n\ndef duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n  begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends += 24 * 3600 if ends < begins\n  (ends - begins) / 3600\nend\n\nduration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Calcolare con il tempo",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Orari fatti bene: Time e Date</h2><p>Finora timelog calcola con le stringhe: <code>\"08:30\"</code> viene scomposto a mano e <code>as_hours</code> lo trasforma in un numero. Funziona bene – finché una voce non supera la mezzanotte o non vuoi sapere in che giorno della settimana cade il 15 settembre. Ruby ha due classi per questo: <code>Time</code> per un <strong>istante</strong> (data e ora del giorno) e <code>Date</code> per un <strong>giorno di calendario</strong>.</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "start = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30)\nfinish = Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 12, 0)\nseconds = finish - start\nseconds / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Sottraendo due istanti si ottengono i secondi che li separano, come <code>Float</code>; divisi per 3600 sono ore. Al contrario, puoi aggiungere secondi a un istante – per esempio una pausa pranzo di 45 minuti:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "back = finish + 45 * 60\nback.strftime(\"%H:%M\")"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>strftime</code> (“string format time”) trasforma un istante in testo, usando dei segnaposto: <code>%Y</code> anno, <code>%m</code> mese, <code>%d</code> giorno, <code>%H</code> ora, <code>%M</code> minuto, <code>%A</code> giorno della settimana. Tutto ciò che non inizia con <code>%</code> resta com'è:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts start.strftime(\"%d.%m.%Y\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nputs start.strftime(\"%A\")\nstart.wday   # il giorno della settimana come numero: domenica = 0, lunedì = 1 ..."
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>La direzione opposta, dal testo a un istante, è <code>Time.strptime</code> (“string parse time”) – con gli stessi segnaposto. Viene dalla libreria <code>time</code>. Ecco come una riga di timelog diventa una vera durata:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\nline = \"2026-09-15 08:30-12:00 ProjectX\"\ndate, times = line.split(\" \")\nfrom, to = times.split(\"-\")\nbegins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\nends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n(ends - begins) / 3600"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Per giorni interi senza ora c'è <code>Date</code> della libreria <code>date</code>. <code>Date</code> calcola direttamente in giorni: <code>+ 1</code> è domani, e sottraendo due date ottieni i giorni che le separano (come frazione – <code>to_i</code> ne fa un numero intero). E puoi percorrere un intervallo di date proprio come un intervallo di numeri:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"date\"\n\nday = Date.new(2026, 9, 15)\nputs day + 1\nuntil_christmas = (Date.new(2026, 12, 24) - day).to_i\nputs \"#{until_christmas} days until Christmas\"\n\nweek = day..(day + 6)\nweek.count { |d| d.saturday? || d.sunday? }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Altri aiuti per i report: <code>Date.parse(\"2026-09-15\")</code> legge una data da un testo, <code>day.cweek</code> dà la settimana di calendario, <code>Date.today</code> e <code>Time.now</code> la data di oggi e l'istante attuale. Questi ultimi due però sono scomodi nei test: a ogni esecuzione restituiscono qualcosa di diverso. Meglio quindi passare l'orario a un metodo come argomento, piuttosto che fargli chiedere <code>Time.now</code> al suo interno.</p><p>E una parola sui fusi orari: <code>Time.new</code> senza fuso usa quello del computer – qui nel browser è sempre UTC. Se un programma gira su server in paesi diversi, indichi il fuso esplicitamente: <code>Time.new(2026, 9, 15, 8, 30, 0, \"+02:00\")</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Time.html' target='_blank'>Time</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Date.html' target='_blank'>Date</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/language/strftime_formatting_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>tutti i segnaposto di strftime</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>duration_in_hours(date, from, to)</code>: il metodo trasforma la data e i due orari in due oggetti <code>Time</code> e restituisce le ore che li separano. Se una voce supera la mezzanotte (<code>to</code> è prima di <code>from</code>), finisce il giorno dopo – allora aggiungi un giorno, cioè <code>24 * 3600</code> secondi. <code>duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")</code> dà <code>3.5</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"time\"\n\n# def duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n#   begins = Time.strptime(...)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")\n",
            "hints": [
              "Componi una stringa con la data e l'ora e lascia che <code>Time.strptime</code>, con un formato adatto, ne faccia un oggetto Time – per l'inizio e per la fine. La differenza tra due istanti è in secondi."
            ],
            "check": "duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"08:30\", \"12:00\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\") == 3.5 && duration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"09:15\", \"10:00\") == 0.75 && code.include?(\"Time\")",
            "hint": "<code>begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")</code>, lo stesso per <code>ends</code>. Poi <code>ends += 24 * 3600 if ends &lt; begins</code> e infine <code>(ends - begins) / 3600</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"time\"\n\ndef duration_in_hours(date, from, to)\n  begins = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{from}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends = Time.strptime(\"#{date} #{to}\", \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")\n  ends += 24 * 3600 if ends < begins\n  (ends - begins) / 3600\nend\n\nduration_in_hours(\"2026-09-15\", \"22:00\", \"01:30\")"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-objects",
      "de": {
        "title": "Objekte teilen: dup & freeze",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Wer zeigt worauf?</h2><p>Eine Variable enthält in Ruby nicht das Objekt selbst – sie <strong>zeigt</strong> nur darauf, wie ein Namensschild an einer Kiste. Weist du sie einer zweiten Variable zu, bekommt dieselbe Kiste ein zweites Schild. Das überrascht fast alle irgendwann:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "montag = [\"ProjectX\"]\ndienstag = montag\ndienstag << \"Intern\"\nmontag"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>montag</code> hat sich verändert, obwohl wir nur an <code>dienstag</code> gearbeitet haben – es ist ein und dasselbe Array. <code>equal?</code> prüft, ob zwei Namen auf <em>dasselbe</em> Objekt zeigen; <code>==</code> prüft nur, ob die Inhalte gleich sind. Und <code>object_id</code> ist sozusagen die Nummer der Kiste:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "p dienstag.equal?(montag)\np dienstag == [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"]\np dienstag.equal?([\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"])\nmontag.object_id == dienstag.object_id"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Willst du eine echte Kopie, sag es: <code>dup</code> baut ein neues Objekt mit demselben Inhalt. Danach sind die beiden unabhängig voneinander:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "mittwoch = montag.dup\nmittwoch << \"Support\"\n[montag, mittwoch]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Aber Achtung: <code>dup</code> kopiert nur die äusserste Schicht – eine <em>flache</em> Kopie. Stecken im Array Hashes, teilen sich Original und Kopie dieselben Hashes:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintraege = [{ projekt: \"A\", stunden: 2.0 }]\nkopie = eintraege.dup\nkopie.first[:stunden] = 99\neintraege.first[:stunden]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Für eine tiefe Kopie kopierst du jede Ebene selbst, etwa mit <code>eintraege.map(&amp;:dup)</code>.</p><p>Dieselbe Falle steckt in einem Klassiker: <code>Hash.new</code> mit einem Array als Standardwert. Alle fehlenden Schlüssel bekommen <em>dasselbe</em> Array:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "nach_projekt = Hash.new([])\nnach_projekt[\"A\"] << 2.0\nnach_projekt[\"B\"] << 1.5\n[nach_projekt[\"C\"], nach_projekt.keys]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Gleich zwei Überraschungen: Der „leere“ Standardwert ist längst nicht mehr leer, und der Hash hat überhaupt keine Schlüssel – <code>&lt;&lt;</code> hat ja nur das gemeinsame Standard-Array verändert, nie etwas im Hash gespeichert. Die Lösung ist ein Block: Er läuft für jeden fehlenden Schlüssel neu, legt ein eigenes Array an und speichert es:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "nach_projekt = Hash.new { |hash, schluessel| hash[schluessel] = [] }\nnach_projekt[\"A\"] << 2.0\nnach_projekt[\"B\"] << 1.5\nnach_projekt"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Der sicherste Schutz gegen ungewollte Änderungen: Objekte <strong>einfrieren</strong>. <code>freeze</code> macht ein Objekt unveränderlich. Wer es trotzdem versucht, bekommt einen <code>FrozenError</code> – sofort und genau dort, wo es passiert, statt irgendwann später als rätselhaft falsche Daten:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "projekte = [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"].freeze\np projekte.frozen?\nprojekte << \"Geheim\"",
            "raises": "FrozenError"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Eingefrorene Objekte lassen sich gefahrlos teilen. Zahlen und Symbole sind darum immer eingefroren, und <code>Data</code> (Lektion {{nr:tl-mixins}}) friert seine Objekte von selbst ein. Viele Ruby-Dateien beginnen mit dem magischen Kommentar <code># frozen_string_literal: true</code>: Dann ist jeder String, der im Code in Anführungszeichen steht, eingefroren.</p><p>Drei Faustregeln zum Mitnehmen: Methoden mit <code>!</code> (<code>sort!</code>, <code>upcase!</code>) verändern das Objekt, Methoden ohne geben ein neues zurück. Gib Objekte, die andere nicht verändern sollen, eingefroren oder als Kopie heraus. Und wenn sich etwas „von selbst“ ändert: Frag dich, wer noch auf dieselbe Kiste zeigt.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-dup' target='_blank'>dup</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-freeze' target='_blank'>freeze</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-equal-3F' target='_blank'>equal?</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#class-Hash-label-Hash+Default' target='_blank'>Standardwerte von Hash</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Das <code>Stundenblatt</code> unten hat ein Leck: <code>eintraege</code> gibt die interne Liste heraus, und wer sie verändert, verändert das Stundenblatt mit. Repariere <code>eintraege</code> so, dass es eine <strong>eingefrorene Kopie</strong> zurückgibt. Dann kann niemand mehr von aussen Einträge hineinschmuggeln – <code>add</code> muss aber weiter funktionieren.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Stundenblatt\n  def initialize\n    @eintraege = []\n  end\n\n  def add(projekt, stunden)\n    @eintraege << { projekt: projekt, stunden: stunden }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def eintraege\n    @eintraege   # das Leck: die interne Liste selbst\n  end\nend\n\nblatt = Stundenblatt.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nblatt.eintraege.frozen?\n",
            "hints": [
              "Nur die Methode <code>eintraege</code> ändert sich: Statt der Liste selbst gibst du eine Kopie zurück, die du einfrierst. <code>add</code> und <code>@eintraege</code> bleiben, wie sie sind."
            ],
            "check": "b = Stundenblatt.new.add(\"A\", 1.0); liste = b.eintraege; liste.frozen? && (begin; liste << 1; false; rescue FrozenError; true; end) && b.eintraege.length == 1 && b.add(\"B\", 2.0).eintraege.length == 2",
            "hint": "Zwei Methoden, eine Zeile: <code>@eintraege.dup.freeze</code> – erst kopieren, dann die Kopie einfrieren. Friere nicht <code>@eintraege</code> selbst ein, sonst klappt <code>add</code> nicht mehr.",
            "solution": "class Stundenblatt\n  def initialize\n    @eintraege = []\n  end\n\n  def add(projekt, stunden)\n    @eintraege << { projekt: projekt, stunden: stunden }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def eintraege\n    @eintraege.dup.freeze\n  end\nend\n\nblatt = Stundenblatt.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nblatt.eintraege.frozen?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Sharing objects: dup & freeze",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Who points at what?</h2><p>In Ruby a variable doesn't contain the object itself – it only <strong>points</strong> at it, like a name tag on a box. Assign it to a second variable and the same box gets a second tag. That surprises almost everyone at some point:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "monday = [\"ProjectX\"]\ntuesday = monday\ntuesday << \"Intern\"\nmonday"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>monday</code> changed although we only worked on <code>tuesday</code> – it's one and the same array. <code>equal?</code> checks whether two names point at the <em>same</em> object; <code>==</code> only checks whether the contents are equal. And <code>object_id</code> is, so to speak, the number of the box:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "p tuesday.equal?(monday)\np tuesday == [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"]\np tuesday.equal?([\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"])\nmonday.object_id == tuesday.object_id"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>If you want a real copy, say so: <code>dup</code> builds a new object with the same content. Afterwards the two are independent of each other:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "wednesday = monday.dup\nwednesday << \"Support\"\n[monday, wednesday]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>But careful: <code>dup</code> only copies the outermost layer – a <em>shallow</em> copy. If the array holds hashes, original and copy share the very same hashes:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }]\ncopy = entries.dup\ncopy.first[:hours] = 99\nentries.first[:hours]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>For a deep copy you copy every level yourself, for instance with <code>entries.map(&amp;:dup)</code>.</p><p>The same trap hides in a classic: <code>Hash.new</code> with an array as the default value. Every missing key gets the <em>same</em> array:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new([])\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\n[by_project[\"C\"], by_project.keys]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Two surprises at once: the “empty” default value is no longer empty at all, and the hash has no keys whatsoever – <code>&lt;&lt;</code> only changed the shared default array and never stored anything in the hash. The fix is a block: it runs anew for every missing key, creates an array of its own and stores it:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\nby_project"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The safest protection against unwanted changes: <strong>freeze</strong> objects. <code>freeze</code> makes an object unchangeable. Anyone who tries anyway gets a <code>FrozenError</code> – at once and right where it happens, instead of as puzzlingly wrong data at some later point:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "projects = [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"].freeze\np projects.frozen?\nprojects << \"Secret\"",
            "raises": "FrozenError"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Frozen objects can be shared without danger. That's why numbers and symbols are always frozen, and <code>Data</code> (lesson {{nr:tl-mixins}}) freezes its objects by itself. Many Ruby files start with the magic comment <code># frozen_string_literal: true</code>: then every string written in quotes in that file's code is frozen.</p><p>Three rules of thumb to take along: methods with <code>!</code> (<code>sort!</code>, <code>upcase!</code>) change the object, methods without return a new one. Hand out objects that others must not change frozen or as a copy. And when something changes “all by itself”: ask yourself who else points at the same box.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-dup' target='_blank'>dup</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-freeze' target='_blank'>freeze</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-equal-3F' target='_blank'>equal?</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#class-Hash-label-Hash+Default' target='_blank'>Hash default values</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> The <code>Logbook</code> below has a leak: <code>entries</code> hands out the internal list, and whoever changes it changes the logbook too. Repair <code>entries</code> so that it returns a <strong>frozen copy</strong>. Then nobody can smuggle entries in from outside anymore – but <code>add</code> must keep working.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries   # the leak: the internal list itself\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?\n",
            "hints": [
              "Only the method <code>entries</code> changes: instead of the list itself, return a copy that you freeze. <code>add</code> and <code>@entries</code> stay as they are."
            ],
            "check": "b = Logbook.new.add(\"A\", 1.0); list = b.entries; list.frozen? && (begin; list << 1; false; rescue FrozenError; true; end) && b.entries.length == 1 && b.add(\"B\", 2.0).entries.length == 2",
            "hint": "Two methods, one line: <code>@entries.dup.freeze</code> – copy first, then freeze the copy. Don't freeze <code>@entries</code> itself, or <code>add</code> stops working.",
            "solution": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries.dup.freeze\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "オブジェクトの共有：dupとfreeze",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>だれが何を指している？</h2><p>Rubyの変数は、オブジェクトそのものを入れているわけではありません。箱につけた名札のように、オブジェクトを<strong>指している</strong>だけです。それを2つ目の変数に代入すると、同じ箱に2枚目の名札がつきます。これには、ほとんどだれもが一度は驚かされます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "monday = [\"ProjectX\"]\ntuesday = monday\ntuesday << \"Intern\"\nmonday"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>tuesday</code>しかいじっていないのに、<code>monday</code>が変わってしまいました。2つはまったく同じ配列なのです。<code>equal?</code>は2つの名前が<em>同じ</em>オブジェクトを指しているかを調べ、<code>==</code>は中身が等しいかどうかだけを調べます。そして<code>object_id</code>は、いわば箱の番号です：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "p tuesday.equal?(monday)\np tuesday == [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"]\np tuesday.equal?([\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"])\nmonday.object_id == tuesday.object_id"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>本当のコピーがほしいなら、そう伝えましょう。<code>dup</code>は、同じ中身を持つ新しいオブジェクトを作ります。そのあとは、2つはおたがいに無関係です：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "wednesday = monday.dup\nwednesday << \"Support\"\n[monday, wednesday]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ただし注意してください。<code>dup</code>がコピーするのはいちばん外側の層だけです。これを<em>浅い</em>コピーといいます。配列の中にハッシュが入っていると、もとの配列とコピーは同じハッシュを共有します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }]\ncopy = entries.dup\ncopy.first[:hours] = 99\nentries.first[:hours]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>深いコピーがほしいときは、<code>entries.map(&amp;:dup)</code>のように、それぞれの層を自分でコピーします。</p><p>同じ落とし穴が、定番の書き方にもひそんでいます。デフォルト値に配列を渡した<code>Hash.new</code>です。存在しないキーはどれも<em>同じ</em>配列を受け取ります：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new([])\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\n[by_project[\"C\"], by_project.keys]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>驚きが2つ同時にやってきました。「空」だったはずのデフォルト値はもう空ではなく、しかもハッシュにはキーが1つもありません。<code>&lt;&lt;</code>は共有されたデフォルトの配列を変えただけで、ハッシュには何も保存していないのです。解決策はブロックです。存在しないキーのたびに新しく実行され、そのキー専用の配列を作って保存します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\nby_project"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>意図しない変更を防ぐいちばん確実な方法は、オブジェクトを<strong>凍結</strong>することです。<code>freeze</code>はオブジェクトを変更できなくします。それでも変えようとすると<code>FrozenError</code>が発生します。あとになってから謎のおかしなデータとして気づくのではなく、その場ですぐにわかるのです：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "projects = [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"].freeze\np projects.frozen?\nprojects << \"Secret\"",
            "raises": "FrozenError"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>凍結されたオブジェクトなら、安心して共有できます。数やシンボルがいつも凍結されているのはそのためですし、<code>Data</code>（レッスン{{nr:tl-mixins}}）も自分のオブジェクトを自動で凍結します。多くのRubyファイルは<code># frozen_string_literal: true</code>というマジックコメントで始まります。こう書くと、そのファイルのコードで引用符に囲まれた文字列は、すべて凍結されます。</p><p>持ち帰ってほしい目安が3つあります。<code>!</code>のついたメソッド（<code>sort!</code>、<code>upcase!</code>）はオブジェクトそのものを変え、ついていないメソッドは新しいオブジェクトを返します。ほかの人に変えられては困るオブジェクトは、凍結するかコピーにして渡します。そして何かが「ひとりでに」変わったときは、同じ箱をほかにだれが指しているかを考えましょう。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Object/i/dup.html' target='_blank'>dup</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Object/i/freeze.html' target='_blank'>freeze</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/BasicObject/i/equal=3f.html' target='_blank'>equal?</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Hash/s/new.html' target='_blank'>Hash.newとデフォルト値</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>下の<code>Logbook</code>には漏れがあります。<code>entries</code>が内部のリストをそのまま渡してしまうので、それを変えた人は、ログブックまで変えてしまうのです。<code>entries</code>が<strong>凍結したコピー</strong>を返すように直してください。そうすれば、もうだれも外から記録をこっそり入れられません。ただし、<code>add</code>はこれまでどおり使えるようにしておきます。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries   # 漏れ：内部のリストそのもの\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?\n",
            "hints": [
              "変えるのは<code>entries</code>メソッドだけだよ。リストそのものではなく、コピーを返して、そのコピーをfreezeするんだ。<code>add</code>と<code>@entries</code>はそのままでね。"
            ],
            "check": "b = Logbook.new.add(\"A\", 1.0); list = b.entries; list.frozen? && (begin; list << 1; false; rescue FrozenError; true; end) && b.entries.length == 1 && b.add(\"B\", 2.0).entries.length == 2",
            "hint": "メソッド2つで1行だよ：<code>@entries.dup.freeze</code>。まずコピーして、そのコピーを凍結するんだ。<code>@entries</code>そのものを凍結しちゃだめだよ。<code>add</code>が動かなくなるからね。",
            "solution": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries.dup.freeze\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Partager des objets : dup et freeze",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Qui pointe vers quoi ?</h2><p>En Ruby, une variable ne contient pas l'objet lui-même – elle ne fait que <strong>pointer</strong> vers lui, comme une étiquette sur une boîte. Affectez-la à une deuxième variable, et la même boîte reçoit une deuxième étiquette. Cela surprend presque tout le monde un jour ou l'autre :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "monday = [\"ProjectX\"]\ntuesday = monday\ntuesday << \"Intern\"\nmonday"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>monday</code> a changé alors que nous n'avons travaillé que sur <code>tuesday</code> – c'est un seul et même array. <code>equal?</code> vérifie si deux noms pointent vers le <em>même</em> objet ; <code>==</code> vérifie seulement si les contenus sont égaux. Et <code>object_id</code> est, pour ainsi dire, le numéro de la boîte :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "p tuesday.equal?(monday)\np tuesday == [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"]\np tuesday.equal?([\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"])\nmonday.object_id == tuesday.object_id"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Si vous voulez une vraie copie, demandez-la : <code>dup</code> construit un nouvel objet avec le même contenu. Ensuite, les deux sont indépendants l'un de l'autre :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "wednesday = monday.dup\nwednesday << \"Support\"\n[monday, wednesday]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Mais attention : <code>dup</code> ne copie que la couche extérieure – une copie <em>superficielle</em>. Si l'array contient des hashes, l'original et la copie partagent exactement les mêmes hashes :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }]\ncopy = entries.dup\ncopy.first[:hours] = 99\nentries.first[:hours]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Pour une copie profonde, copiez vous-même chaque niveau, par exemple avec <code>entries.map(&amp;:dup)</code>.</p><p>Le même piège se cache dans un grand classique : <code>Hash.new</code> avec un array comme valeur par défaut. Chaque clé manquante reçoit le <em>même</em> array :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new([])\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\n[by_project[\"C\"], by_project.keys]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Deux surprises d'un coup : la valeur par défaut « vide » n'est plus vide du tout, et le hash n'a aucune clé – <code>&lt;&lt;</code> n'a fait que modifier l'array par défaut partagé et n'a jamais rien enregistré dans le hash. La solution est un bloc : il s'exécute à nouveau pour chaque clé manquante, crée son propre array et l'enregistre :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\nby_project"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>La protection la plus sûre contre les modifications indésirables : <strong>geler</strong> les objets. <code>freeze</code> rend un objet non modifiable. Quiconque essaie quand même obtient une <code>FrozenError</code> – immédiatement et à l'endroit exact où cela se produit, au lieu de données mystérieusement fausses plus tard :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "projects = [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"].freeze\np projects.frozen?\nprojects << \"Secret\"",
            "raises": "FrozenError"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Les objets gelés peuvent être partagés sans danger. C'est pourquoi les nombres et les symboles sont toujours gelés, et <code>Data</code> (leçon {{nr:tl-mixins}}) gèle ses objets de lui-même. Beaucoup de fichiers Ruby commencent par le commentaire magique <code># frozen_string_literal: true</code> : chaque chaîne écrite entre guillemets dans le code de ce fichier est alors gelée.</p><p>Trois règles pratiques à retenir : les méthodes avec <code>!</code> (<code>sort!</code>, <code>upcase!</code>) modifient l'objet, celles sans ce signe renvoient un nouvel objet. Les objets que d'autres ne doivent pas modifier, transmettez-les gelés ou sous forme de copie. Et quand quelque chose change « tout seul » : demandez-vous qui d'autre pointe vers la même boîte.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-dup' target='_blank'>dup</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-freeze' target='_blank'>freeze</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-equal-3F' target='_blank'>equal?</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#class-Hash-label-Hash+Default' target='_blank'>Valeurs par défaut des hashes</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Le <code>Logbook</code> ci-dessous a une fuite : <code>entries</code> transmet la liste interne, et quiconque la modifie modifie aussi le journal. Réparez <code>entries</code> pour qu'elle renvoie une <strong>copie gelée</strong>. Ainsi, plus personne ne pourra glisser des saisies de l'extérieur – mais <code>add</code> doit continuer à fonctionner.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries   # la fuite : la liste interne elle-même\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?\n",
            "hints": [
              "Seule la méthode <code>entries</code> change : au lieu de la liste elle-même, renvoyez une copie que vous gelez. <code>add</code> et <code>@entries</code> restent tels quels."
            ],
            "check": "b = Logbook.new.add(\"A\", 1.0); list = b.entries; list.frozen? && (begin; list << 1; false; rescue FrozenError; true; end) && b.entries.length == 1 && b.add(\"B\", 2.0).entries.length == 2",
            "hint": "Deux méthodes, une ligne : <code>@entries.dup.freeze</code> – d'abord copier, puis geler la copie. Ne gelez pas <code>@entries</code> lui-même, sinon <code>add</code> ne fonctionne plus.",
            "solution": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries.dup.freeze\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Condividere oggetti: dup e freeze",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Chi punta a cosa?</h2><p>In Ruby una variabile non contiene l'oggetto stesso – si limita a <strong>puntare</strong> a esso, come un'etichetta su una scatola. Assegnala a una seconda variabile e la stessa scatola riceve una seconda etichetta. Prima o poi questo sorprende quasi tutti:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "monday = [\"ProjectX\"]\ntuesday = monday\ntuesday << \"Intern\"\nmonday"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>monday</code> è cambiato anche se abbiamo lavorato solo su <code>tuesday</code> – è un unico e solo array. <code>equal?</code> verifica se due nomi puntano allo <em>stesso</em> oggetto; <code>==</code> verifica solo se i contenuti sono uguali. E <code>object_id</code> è, per così dire, il numero della scatola:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "p tuesday.equal?(monday)\np tuesday == [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"]\np tuesday.equal?([\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"])\nmonday.object_id == tuesday.object_id"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Se vuoi una copia vera, devi dirlo: <code>dup</code> costruisce un nuovo oggetto con lo stesso contenuto. Da quel momento i due sono indipendenti l'uno dall'altro:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "wednesday = monday.dup\nwednesday << \"Support\"\n[monday, wednesday]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Attenzione però: <code>dup</code> copia solo lo strato più esterno – una copia <em>superficiale</em>. Se l'array contiene degli hash, originale e copia condividono esattamente gli stessi hash:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entries = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }]\ncopy = entries.dup\ncopy.first[:hours] = 99\nentries.first[:hours]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Per una copia profonda copi tu ogni livello, per esempio con <code>entries.map(&amp;:dup)</code>.</p><p>La stessa trappola si nasconde in un classico: <code>Hash.new</code> con un array come valore predefinito. Ogni chiave mancante riceve lo <em>stesso</em> array:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new([])\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\n[by_project[\"C\"], by_project.keys]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Due sorprese in una volta: il valore predefinito “vuoto” non è affatto più vuoto, e l'hash non ha nessuna chiave – <code>&lt;&lt;</code> ha modificato solo l'array predefinito condiviso e non ha mai salvato nulla nell'hash. La soluzione è un blocco: viene eseguito di nuovo per ogni chiave mancante, crea un array tutto suo e lo salva:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "by_project = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }\nby_project[\"A\"] << 2.0\nby_project[\"B\"] << 1.5\nby_project"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>La protezione più sicura contro le modifiche indesiderate: <strong>congelare</strong> gli oggetti. <code>freeze</code> rende un oggetto immodificabile. Chi ci prova lo stesso riceve un <code>FrozenError</code> – subito e proprio nel punto in cui succede, invece che più tardi sotto forma di dati misteriosamente sbagliati:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "projects = [\"ProjectX\", \"Intern\"].freeze\np projects.frozen?\nprojects << \"Secret\"",
            "raises": "FrozenError"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Gli oggetti congelati si possono condividere senza pericolo. Per questo numeri e simboli sono sempre congelati, e <code>Data</code> (lezione {{nr:tl-mixins}}) congela da sé i propri oggetti. Molti file Ruby iniziano con il commento magico <code># frozen_string_literal: true</code>: allora ogni stringa scritta tra virgolette nel codice di quel file è congelata.</p><p>Tre regole pratiche da portare con sé: i metodi con <code>!</code> (<code>sort!</code>, <code>upcase!</code>) modificano l'oggetto, quelli senza ne restituiscono uno nuovo. Gli oggetti che altri non devono modificare consegnali congelati o come copia. E quando qualcosa cambia “da solo”: chiediti chi altro punta alla stessa scatola.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-dup' target='_blank'>dup</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-freeze' target='_blank'>freeze</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-equal-3F' target='_blank'>equal?</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Hash.html#class-Hash-label-Hash+Default' target='_blank'>Valori predefiniti di Hash</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Il <code>Logbook</code> qui sotto ha una falla: <code>entries</code> consegna la lista interna, e chi la modifica modifica anche il registro. Ripara <code>entries</code> in modo che restituisca una <strong>copia congelata</strong>. Così nessuno potrà più infilare voci dall'esterno – ma <code>add</code> deve continuare a funzionare.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries   # la falla: proprio la lista interna\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?\n",
            "hints": [
              "Cambia solo il metodo <code>entries</code>: invece della lista stessa, restituisci una copia che congeli. <code>add</code> e <code>@entries</code> restano come sono."
            ],
            "check": "b = Logbook.new.add(\"A\", 1.0); list = b.entries; list.frozen? && (begin; list << 1; false; rescue FrozenError; true; end) && b.entries.length == 1 && b.add(\"B\", 2.0).entries.length == 2",
            "hint": "Due metodi, una riga: <code>@entries.dup.freeze</code> – prima copia, poi congela la copia. Non congelare <code>@entries</code> stesso, altrimenti <code>add</code> smette di funzionare.",
            "solution": "class Logbook\n  def initialize\n    @entries = []\n  end\n\n  def add(project, hours)\n    @entries << { project: project, hours: hours }\n    self\n  end\n\n  def entries\n    @entries.dup.freeze\n  end\nend\n\nlog = Logbook.new.add(\"ProjectX\", 3.5)\nlog.entries.frozen?"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-minitest",
      "de": {
        "title": "Testen mit Minitest",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Tests – dein Sicherheitsnetz</h2><p>Ruby prüft Typen erst zur Laufzeit – ein Tippfehler fällt sonst erst auf, wenn der Code läuft. Deshalb gehören <strong>Tests</strong> in Ruby-Projekten fest dazu. Das Werkzeug ist schon an Bord: <strong>Minitest</strong> kommt mit Ruby mit.</p><p>Ein Test ist eine Klasse, die von <code>Minitest::Test</code> erbt; jede Methode, die mit <code>test_</code> beginnt, ist ein Testfall. <code>assert_equal erwartet, tatsaechlich</code> prüft Gleichheit. Hier im Notizbuch startet <code>run_tests</code> den Testlauf:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Dauer\n  attr_reader :minuten\n\n  def initialize(minuten)\n    @minuten = minuten\n  end\n\n  def in_stunden\n    minuten / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestDauer < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_stunden\n    assert_equal 1.5, Dauer.new(90).in_stunden\n  end\n\n  def test_null_minuten\n    assert_equal 0.0, Dauer.new(0).in_stunden\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Der Bericht liest sich so: <code>2 runs</code> (zwei Testmethoden), <code>2 assertions</code> (zwei Prüfungen), <code>0 failures, 0 errors</code> – alles grün. Und wenn etwas schiefgeht? Minitest zeigt dir genau, <em>was</em> erwartet wurde und <em>was</em> kam:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TestKaputt < Minitest::Test\n  def test_absichtlich_falsch\n    assert_equal 100, Dauer.new(90).minuten\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><p>Dort schreibst du Tests in eigene Dateien und startest sie direkt – <code>minitest/autorun</code> sorgt dafür, dass sie am Programmende automatisch laufen:</p><pre><code># test/test_dauer.rb\nrequire \"minitest/autorun\"\nrequire_relative \"../lib/dauer\"\n\nclass TestDauer < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_stunden\n    assert_equal 1.5, Dauer.new(90).in_stunden\n  end\nend</code></pre><pre><code>$ ruby test/test_dauer.rb\n2 runs, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skips</code></pre><p>Weitere Helfer: <code>assert</code>, <code>refute</code>, <code>assert_nil</code>, <code>assert_raises</code>, und <code>setup</code> für gemeinsame Vorbereitung. RSpec ist die bekannteste Alternative mit eigener Sprache (<code>expect(x).to eq(y)</code>).</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/' target='_blank'>Minitest</a> · <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/Minitest/Assertions.html' target='_blank'>alle assert-Methoden</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Unten steht die Klasse <code>Eintrag</code>. Schreibe <code>TestEintrag</code> mit <strong>mindestens zwei</strong> Tests: einer für einen gültigen Eintrag, einer für ungültige Fälle (negative Stunden oder leeres Projekt). Starte mit <code>run_tests</code> – alles muss grün sein. Ab jetzt gilt: <em>Keine Aufgabe ohne Tests!</em></div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Eintrag\n  attr_reader :projekt, :stunden\n\n  def initialize(projekt, stunden)\n    @projekt = projekt\n    @stunden = stunden\n  end\n\n  def gueltig?\n    stunden > 0 && !projekt.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\n# class TestEintrag < Minitest::Test\n#   def test_...\n#   end\n# end\n\n# run_tests\n",
            "hints": [
              "Entferne die <code>#</code> vor dem Klassengerüst. Eine Testmethode heisst <code>test_…</code>; mit <code>assert</code> prüfst du, dass ein guter Eintrag gültig ist, mit <code>refute</code>, dass ein schlechter es nicht ist."
            ],
            "check": "defined?(TestEintrag) && TestEintrag.instance_methods.grep(/\\Atest_/).length >= 2 && output.include?(\"0 failures\") && output.include?(\"0 errors\") && output.include?(\"runs,\")",
            "hint": "Zum Beispiel: <code>def test_gueltig; assert Eintrag.new(\"X\", 2.0).gueltig?; end</code> und <code>def test_negative_stunden; refute Eintrag.new(\"X\", -1).gueltig?; end</code> – dann <code>run_tests</code>.",
            "solution": "class Eintrag\n  attr_reader :projekt, :stunden\n  def initialize(projekt, stunden)\n    @projekt = projekt\n    @stunden = stunden\n  end\n  def gueltig?\n    stunden > 0 && !projekt.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestEintrag < Minitest::Test\n  def test_gueltig\n    assert Eintrag.new(\"X\", 2.0).gueltig?\n  end\n\n  def test_negative_stunden\n    refute Eintrag.new(\"X\", -1).gueltig?\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Testing with Minitest",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Tests – your safety net</h2><p>Ruby checks types only at runtime – a typo won't surface until the code actually runs. That's why <strong>tests</strong> are a fixture of Ruby projects. The tool is already on board: <strong>Minitest</strong> ships with Ruby.</p><p>A test is a class inheriting from <code>Minitest::Test</code>; every method starting with <code>test_</code> is a test case. <code>assert_equal expected, actual</code> checks equality. Here in the notebook, <code>run_tests</code> starts the test run:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Duration\n  attr_reader :minutes\n\n  def initialize(minutes)\n    @minutes = minutes\n  end\n\n  def in_hours\n    minutes / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\n\n  def test_zero_minutes\n    assert_equal 0.0, Duration.new(0).in_hours\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Reading the report: <code>2 runs</code> (two test methods), <code>2 assertions</code> (two checks), <code>0 failures, 0 errors</code> – all green. And when something breaks? Minitest shows you exactly <em>what</em> was expected and <em>what</em> arrived:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TestBroken < Minitest::Test\n  def test_deliberately_wrong\n    assert_equal 100, Duration.new(90).minutes\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><p>There you put tests into their own files and run them directly – <code>minitest/autorun</code> makes them run automatically when the program ends:</p><pre><code># test/test_duration.rb\nrequire \"minitest/autorun\"\nrequire_relative \"../lib/duration\"\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\nend</code></pre><pre><code>$ ruby test/test_duration.rb\n2 runs, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skips</code></pre><p>More helpers: <code>assert</code>, <code>refute</code>, <code>assert_nil</code>, <code>assert_raises</code>, and <code>setup</code> for shared preparation. RSpec is the best-known alternative with its own language (<code>expect(x).to eq(y)</code>).</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/' target='_blank'>Minitest</a> · <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/Minitest/Assertions.html' target='_blank'>all assert methods</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Below is the class <code>Entry</code>. Write <code>TestEntry</code> with <strong>at least two</strong> tests: one for a valid entry, one for invalid cases (negative hours or empty project). Finish with <code>run_tests</code> – everything must be green. From now on: <em>no exercise without tests!</em></div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\n# class TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n#   def test_...\n#   end\n# end\n\n# run_tests\n",
            "hints": [
              "Remove the <code>#</code> in front of the class skeleton. A test method is called <code>test_…</code>; with <code>assert</code> you check that a good entry is valid, with <code>refute</code> that a bad one is not."
            ],
            "check": "defined?(TestEntry) && TestEntry.instance_methods.grep(/\\Atest_/).length >= 2 && output.include?(\"0 failures\") && output.include?(\"0 errors\") && output.include?(\"runs,\")",
            "hint": "For example: <code>def test_valid; assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?; end</code> and <code>def test_negative_hours; refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?; end</code> – then <code>run_tests</code>.",
            "solution": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n  def test_valid\n    assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?\n  end\n\n  def test_negative_hours\n    refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "Minitestでテスト",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>テストは頼れる安全ネット</h2><p>Rubyが型をチェックするのは、実行するときだけです。タイプミスがあっても、そのコードが実際に動くまで気づけません。だからこそ、Rubyのプロジェクトに<strong>テスト</strong>は欠かせません。道具はもうそろっています。<strong>Minitest</strong>はRubyに最初から付いてきます。</p><p>テストは<code>Minitest::Test</code>を継承したクラスです。<code>test_</code>で始まるメソッドが、それぞれ1つのテストケースになります。<code>assert_equal expected, actual</code>は、期待する値と実際の値が等しいかどうかを確かめます。このノートブックでは、<code>run_tests</code>でテストを実行します。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Duration\n  attr_reader :minutes\n\n  def initialize(minutes)\n    @minutes = minutes\n  end\n\n  def in_hours\n    minutes / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\n\n  def test_zero_minutes\n    assert_equal 0.0, Duration.new(0).in_hours\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>結果の読み方：<code>2 runs</code>（テストメソッドが2つ）、<code>2 assertions</code>（チェックが2回）、<code>0 failures, 0 errors</code>。すべて成功、オールグリーンです。では、何かが壊れていたら？Minitestは、<em>何</em>が期待されていて、実際には<em>何</em>が返ってきたのかを正確に教えてくれます。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TestBroken < Minitest::Test\n  def test_deliberately_wrong\n    assert_equal 100, Duration.new(90).minutes\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><p>テストは専用のファイルに書いて、そのファイルを直接実行します。<code>minitest/autorun</code>を読み込んでおくと、プログラムの最後にテストが自動で実行されます。</p><pre><code># test/test_duration.rb\nrequire \"minitest/autorun\"\nrequire_relative \"../lib/duration\"\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\nend</code></pre><pre><code>$ ruby test/test_duration.rb\n2 runs, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skips</code></pre><p>ほかにも便利なメソッドがあります。<code>assert</code>、<code>refute</code>、<code>assert_nil</code>、<code>assert_raises</code>、そして共通の準備をまとめて書ける<code>setup</code>です。Minitestのほかにいちばんよく知られているのはRSpecで、独自の書き方（<code>expect(x).to eq(y)</code>）をします。</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/' target='_blank'>Minitest（英語）</a>・<a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/Minitest/Assertions.html' target='_blank'>assertメソッド一覧（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>下にあるのはクラス<code>Entry</code>です。<strong>2つ以上</strong>のテストを持つ<code>TestEntry</code>を書きましょう。1つは正しいエントリのテスト、もう1つは正しくないケース（時間がマイナス、またはプロジェクトが空）のテストです。最後に<code>run_tests</code>を実行して、すべて成功させてください。これからは<em>テストのない課題はなし！</em>です。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\n# class TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n#   def test_...\n#   end\n# end\n\n# run_tests\n",
            "hints": [
              "クラスの骨組みの<code>#</code>を外してね。テストメソッドの名前は<code>test_…</code>で始めるよ。良いエントリが有効なことは<code>assert</code>で、悪いエントリが無効なことは<code>refute</code>で確かめるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "defined?(TestEntry) && TestEntry.instance_methods.grep(/\\Atest_/).length >= 2 && output.include?(\"0 failures\") && output.include?(\"0 errors\") && output.include?(\"runs,\")",
            "hint": "たとえば<code>def test_valid; assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?; end</code>と<code>def test_negative_hours; refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?; end</code>を書いて、最後に<code>run_tests</code>を呼んでみて。",
            "solution": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n  def test_valid\n    assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?\n  end\n\n  def test_negative_hours\n    refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Tester avec Minitest",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Les tests – votre filet de sécurité</h2><p>Ruby ne vérifie les types qu'à l'exécution – une faute de frappe n'apparaît que lorsque le code s'exécute réellement. C'est pourquoi les <strong>tests</strong> font partie intégrante des projets Ruby. L'outil est déjà fourni : <strong>Minitest</strong> est livré avec Ruby.</p><p>Un test est une classe qui hérite de <code>Minitest::Test</code> ; chaque méthode qui commence par <code>test_</code> est un cas de test. <code>assert_equal expected, actual</code> vérifie l'égalité. Ici, dans le notebook, <code>run_tests</code> lance l'exécution des tests :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Duration\n  attr_reader :minutes\n\n  def initialize(minutes)\n    @minutes = minutes\n  end\n\n  def in_hours\n    minutes / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\n\n  def test_zero_minutes\n    assert_equal 0.0, Duration.new(0).in_hours\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Lire le rapport : <code>2 runs</code> (deux méthodes de test), <code>2 assertions</code> (deux vérifications), <code>0 failures, 0 errors</code> – tout est au vert. Et quand quelque chose casse ? Minitest vous montre exactement <em>ce qui</em> était attendu et <em>ce qui</em> est arrivé :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TestBroken < Minitest::Test\n  def test_deliberately_wrong\n    assert_equal 100, Duration.new(90).minutes\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><p>Là, vous placez les tests dans leurs propres fichiers et les exécutez directement – <code>minitest/autorun</code> les lance automatiquement à la fin du programme :</p><pre><code># test/test_duration.rb\nrequire \"minitest/autorun\"\nrequire_relative \"../lib/duration\"\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\nend</code></pre><pre><code>$ ruby test/test_duration.rb\n2 runs, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skips</code></pre><p>Autres outils : <code>assert</code>, <code>refute</code>, <code>assert_nil</code>, <code>assert_raises</code>, et <code>setup</code> pour une préparation commune. RSpec est l'alternative la plus connue, avec son propre langage (<code>expect(x).to eq(y)</code>).</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/' target='_blank'>Minitest</a> · <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/Minitest/Assertions.html' target='_blank'>toutes les méthodes assert</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Voici ci-dessous la classe <code>Entry</code>. Écrivez <code>TestEntry</code> avec <strong>au moins deux</strong> tests : un pour une saisie valide, un pour les cas invalides (heures négatives ou projet vide). Terminez par <code>run_tests</code> – tout doit être au vert. À partir de maintenant : <em>pas d'exercice sans tests !</em></div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\n# class TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n#   def test_...\n#   end\n# end\n\n# run_tests\n",
            "hints": [
              "Supprimez le <code>#</code> devant le squelette de la classe. Une méthode de test s'appelle <code>test_…</code> ; avec <code>assert</code>, vous vérifiez qu'une bonne saisie est valide, avec <code>refute</code> qu'une mauvaise ne l'est pas."
            ],
            "check": "defined?(TestEntry) && TestEntry.instance_methods.grep(/\\Atest_/).length >= 2 && output.include?(\"0 failures\") && output.include?(\"0 errors\") && output.include?(\"runs,\")",
            "hint": "Par exemple : <code>def test_valid; assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?; end</code> et <code>def test_negative_hours; refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?; end</code> – puis <code>run_tests</code>.",
            "solution": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n  def test_valid\n    assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?\n  end\n\n  def test_negative_hours\n    refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Testare con Minitest",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>I test – la tua rete di sicurezza</h2><p>Ruby controlla i tipi solo durante l'esecuzione – un errore di battitura emerge solo quando il codice viene davvero eseguito. Per questo i <strong>test</strong> sono una presenza fissa nei progetti Ruby. Lo strumento è già a bordo: <strong>Minitest</strong> è incluso in Ruby.</p><p>Un test è una classe che eredita da <code>Minitest::Test</code>; ogni metodo che inizia con <code>test_</code> è un caso di test. <code>assert_equal expected, actual</code> verifica l'uguaglianza. Qui nel notebook <code>run_tests</code> avvia l'esecuzione dei test:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Duration\n  attr_reader :minutes\n\n  def initialize(minutes)\n    @minutes = minutes\n  end\n\n  def in_hours\n    minutes / 60.0\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\n\n  def test_zero_minutes\n    assert_equal 0.0, Duration.new(0).in_hours\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Come leggere il resoconto: <code>2 runs</code> (due metodi di test), <code>2 assertions</code> (due verifiche), <code>0 failures, 0 errors</code> – tutto verde. E quando qualcosa si rompe? Minitest ti mostra esattamente <em>che cosa</em> ci si aspettava e <em>che cosa</em> è arrivato:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TestBroken < Minitest::Test\n  def test_deliberately_wrong\n    assert_equal 100, Duration.new(90).minutes\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><p>Lì metti i test in file a parte e li esegui direttamente – <code>minitest/autorun</code> li fa partire automaticamente alla fine del programma:</p><pre><code># test/test_duration.rb\nrequire \"minitest/autorun\"\nrequire_relative \"../lib/duration\"\n\nclass TestDuration < Minitest::Test\n  def test_in_hours\n    assert_equal 1.5, Duration.new(90).in_hours\n  end\nend</code></pre><pre><code>$ ruby test/test_duration.rb\n2 runs, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skips</code></pre><p>Altri aiuti: <code>assert</code>, <code>refute</code>, <code>assert_nil</code>, <code>assert_raises</code>, e <code>setup</code> per la preparazione comune. RSpec è l'alternativa più nota, con un linguaggio tutto suo (<code>expect(x).to eq(y)</code>).</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/' target='_blank'>Minitest</a> · <a href='https://docs.seattlerb.org/minitest/Minitest/Assertions.html' target='_blank'>tutti i metodi assert</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Qui sotto c'è la classe <code>Entry</code>. Scrivi <code>TestEntry</code> con <strong>almeno due</strong> test: uno per una voce valida, uno per i casi non validi (ore negative o progetto vuoto). Concludi con <code>run_tests</code> – tutto deve essere verde. Da ora in poi: <em>nessun esercizio senza test!</em></div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\n# class TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n#   def test_...\n#   end\n# end\n\n# run_tests\n",
            "hints": [
              "Togli il <code>#</code> davanti allo scheletro della classe. Un metodo di test si chiama <code>test_…</code>; con <code>assert</code> verifichi che una voce corretta sia valida, con <code>refute</code> che una sbagliata non lo sia."
            ],
            "check": "defined?(TestEntry) && TestEntry.instance_methods.grep(/\\Atest_/).length >= 2 && output.include?(\"0 failures\") && output.include?(\"0 errors\") && output.include?(\"runs,\")",
            "hint": "Per esempio: <code>def test_valid; assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?; end</code> e <code>def test_negative_hours; refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?; end</code> – poi <code>run_tests</code>.",
            "solution": "class Entry\n  attr_reader :project, :hours\n  def initialize(project, hours)\n    @project = project\n    @hours = hours\n  end\n  def valid?\n    hours > 0 && !project.to_s.empty?\n  end\nend\n\nclass TestEntry < Minitest::Test\n  def test_valid\n    assert Entry.new(\"X\", 2.0).valid?\n  end\n\n  def test_negative_hours\n    refute Entry.new(\"X\", -1).valid?\n  end\nend\n\nrun_tests"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-mixins",
      "de": {
        "title": "Enumerable & Data",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Zwei Superkräfte zum Einmischen</h2><p>In Lektion {{nr:module}} hat <code>Comparable</code> einem Paket sechs Vergleiche geschenkt – jetzt setzen wir die zwei berühmtesten Mixins für timelog ein. <strong>Comparable</strong>: Sobald deine Klasse <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> kann (den „Raumschiff-Operator“: -1, 0 oder 1), schenkt dir das Mixin <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>between?</code> und mehr.</p><p>Gleichzeitig lernst du <code>Data</code> kennen – Rubys Klasse für unveränderliche Wertobjekte:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "Dauer = Data.define(:minuten) do\n  include Comparable\n\n  def <=>(andere)\n    minuten <=> andere.minuten\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{minuten / 60}h #{minuten % 60}min\"\n  end\nend\n\npausen = [Dauer.new(minuten: 90), Dauer.new(minuten: 45), Dauer.new(minuten: 120)]\npausen.max.to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>Data.define</code> erzeugt eine Klasse mit fixen Feldern, Gleichheit und <code>inspect</code> geschenkt – und die Objekte sind eingefroren (kein versehentliches Ändern, siehe Lektion {{nr:tl-objects}}). Wer einen anderen Wert braucht, ändert darum nicht das Objekt, sondern lässt sich mit <code>with</code> ein neues geben:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "kurz = Dauer.new(minuten: 30)\nlang = kurz.with(minuten: 90)\n[kurz.to_s, lang.to_s, kurz.frozen?, kurz == Dauer.new(minuten: 30)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Zwei Data-Objekte mit gleichem Inhalt sind gleich (<code>==</code>) – darum passen sie für Werte wie eine Dauer, einen Geldbetrag oder einen Zeitraum. Für veränderliche Fälle gibt es das ältere <code>Struct</code>.</p><p>Die zweite Superkraft: <strong>Enumerable</strong>. Deine Klasse liefert nur <code>each</code> – und bekommt dafür die GESAMTE Collection-Werkzeugkiste: <code>map</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>sort_by</code>, <code>group_by</code> … genau die Methoden aus Lektion {{nr:tl-collections}}, jetzt auf deiner eigenen Klasse.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Data.html' target='_blank'>Data</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Comparable.html' target='_blank'>Comparable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html' target='_blank'>Enumerable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Struct.html' target='_blank'>Struct</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Mach <code>Timesheet</code> enumerable: <code>include Enumerable</code> plus eine Methode <code>each</code>, die den Block an <code>@eintraege.each</code> weiterreicht. Danach funktioniert die letzte Zeile.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  # include ...\n\n  def initialize(eintraege)\n    @eintraege = eintraege\n  end\n\n  # def each(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([\n  { projekt: \"A\", stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"B\", stunden: 1.0 }\n])\n\n# ts.sum { |e| e[:stunden] }\n",
            "hints": [
              "Zwei auskommentierte Stellen: <code>include Enumerable</code> oben in der Klasse und eine Methode <code>each</code>, die den Block einfach an <code>@eintraege.each</code> weitergibt."
            ],
            "check": "Timesheet.include?(Enumerable) && ts.map { |e| e[:projekt] } == [\"A\", \"B\"] && ts.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } == 3.0 && code.include?(\"include Enumerable\") && code.include?(\"def each\")",
            "hint": "<code>include Enumerable</code> in die Klasse, dazu <code>def each(&block); @eintraege.each(&block); end</code>.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  include Enumerable\n\n  def initialize(eintraege)\n    @eintraege = eintraege\n  end\n\n  def each(&block)\n    @eintraege.each(&block)\n  end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([{ projekt: \"A\", stunden: 2.0 }, { projekt: \"B\", stunden: 1.0 }])\nts.sum { |e| e[:stunden] }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Enumerable & Data",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Two superpowers to mix in</h2><p>In lesson {{nr:module}}, <code>Comparable</code> gave a parcel six comparisons – now the two most famous mixins go to work for timelog. <strong>Comparable</strong>: as soon as your class can <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (the “spaceship operator”: -1, 0 or 1), the mixin gives you <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>between?</code> and more.</p><p>At the same time, meet <code>Data</code> – Ruby's class for immutable value objects:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "Duration = Data.define(:minutes) do\n  include Comparable\n\n  def <=>(other)\n    minutes <=> other.minutes\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{minutes / 60}h #{minutes % 60}min\"\n  end\nend\n\nbreaks = [Duration.new(minutes: 90), Duration.new(minutes: 45), Duration.new(minutes: 120)]\nbreaks.max.to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>Data.define</code> creates a class with fixed fields, equality and <code>inspect</code> for free – and the objects are frozen (no accidental mutation, see lesson {{nr:tl-objects}}). So whoever needs a different value doesn't change the object but asks <code>with</code> for a new one:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "short = Duration.new(minutes: 30)\nlong = short.with(minutes: 90)\n[short.to_s, long.to_s, short.frozen?, short == Duration.new(minutes: 30)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Two Data objects with the same content are equal (<code>==</code>) – which makes them a good fit for values such as a duration, an amount of money or a period of time. For mutable cases there's the older <code>Struct</code>.</p><p>The second superpower: <strong>Enumerable</strong>. Your class provides just <code>each</code> – and receives the ENTIRE collection toolbox in return: <code>map</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>sort_by</code>, <code>group_by</code> … exactly the methods from lesson {{nr:tl-collections}}, now on your own class.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Data.html' target='_blank'>Data</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Comparable.html' target='_blank'>Comparable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html' target='_blank'>Enumerable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Struct.html' target='_blank'>Struct</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Make <code>Timesheet</code> enumerable: <code>include Enumerable</code> plus an <code>each</code> method that forwards the block to <code>@entries.each</code>. Then the last line works.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  # include ...\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  # def each(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }\n])\n\n# ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }\n",
            "hints": [
              "Two commented spots: <code>include Enumerable</code> at the top of the class and an <code>each</code> method that simply passes the block on to <code>@entries.each</code>."
            ],
            "check": "Timesheet.include?(Enumerable) && ts.map { |e| e[:project] } == [\"A\", \"B\"] && ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] } == 3.0 && code.include?(\"include Enumerable\") && code.include?(\"def each\")",
            "hint": "<code>include Enumerable</code> into the class, plus <code>def each(&block); @entries.each(&block); end</code>.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  include Enumerable\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  def each(&block)\n    @entries.each(&block)\n  end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }])\nts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "EnumerableとData",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Mix-inで手に入る2つの超能力</h2><p>レッスン{{nr:module}}では、<code>Comparable</code>が小包に比較を6つも与えてくれました。今回は、とりわけ有名な2つのMix-inをtimelogで活躍させます。まずは<strong>Comparable</strong>。クラスに<code>&lt;=&gt;</code>（「宇宙船演算子」。-1、0、1のどれかを返す）を用意するだけで、このMix-inが<code>&lt;</code>、<code>&gt;</code>、<code>==</code>、<code>between?</code>などを使えるようにしてくれます。</p><p>あわせて<code>Data</code>も紹介します。変更できない値オブジェクトを作るための、Rubyのクラスです。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "Duration = Data.define(:minutes) do\n  include Comparable\n\n  def <=>(other)\n    minutes <=> other.minutes\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{minutes / 60}h #{minutes % 60}min\"\n  end\nend\n\nbreaks = [Duration.new(minutes: 90), Duration.new(minutes: 45), Duration.new(minutes: 120)]\nbreaks.max.to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>Data.define</code>は、決まったフィールドを持つクラスを作ります。等しいかどうかの比較や<code>inspect</code>も自動で付いてきます。しかも、できたオブジェクトは凍結（freeze）されているので、うっかり書き換えてしまう心配もありません（レッスン{{nr:tl-objects}}）。別の値がほしいときは、オブジェクトを変えるのではなく、<code>with</code>で新しいオブジェクトをもらいます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "short = Duration.new(minutes: 30)\nlong = short.with(minutes: 90)\n[short.to_s, long.to_s, short.frozen?, short == Duration.new(minutes: 30)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>中身が同じDataオブジェクトどうしは等しい（<code>==</code>）ので、時間の長さや金額、期間のような「値」を表すのにぴったりです。値を書き換えたいときは、昔からある<code>Struct</code>を使います。</p><p>2つ目の超能力は<strong>Enumerable</strong>です。クラスが用意するのは<code>each</code>だけ。それだけで、コレクションの道具箱がまるごと手に入ります。<code>map</code>、<code>select</code>、<code>sum</code>、<code>sort_by</code>、<code>group_by</code>……レッスン{{nr:tl-collections}}で使ったメソッドが、今度は自分のクラスで使えるのです。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Data.html' target='_blank'>Data</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Comparable.html' target='_blank'>Comparable</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Enumerable.html' target='_blank'>Enumerable</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Struct.html' target='_blank'>Struct</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>Timesheet</code>でEnumerableのメソッドを使えるようにしましょう。<code>include Enumerable</code>を書き、受け取ったブロックを<code>@entries.each</code>にそのまま渡す<code>each</code>メソッドを定義します。そうすれば、最後の行が動くようになります。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  # include ...\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  # def each(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }\n])\n\n# ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }\n",
            "hints": [
              "コメントになっている2か所だよ。クラスの上のほうに<code>include Enumerable</code>、それとブロックをそのまま<code>@entries.each</code>に渡すだけの<code>each</code>メソッドだよ。"
            ],
            "check": "Timesheet.include?(Enumerable) && ts.map { |e| e[:project] } == [\"A\", \"B\"] && ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] } == 3.0 && code.include?(\"include Enumerable\") && code.include?(\"def each\")",
            "hint": "クラスの中に<code>include Enumerable</code>を書いて、<code>def each(&block); @entries.each(&block); end</code>も足してみて。",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  include Enumerable\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  def each(&block)\n    @entries.each(&block)\n  end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }])\nts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Enumerable et Data",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Deux super-pouvoirs à intégrer</h2><p>Dans la leçon {{nr:module}}, <code>Comparable</code> a offert six comparaisons à un colis – maintenant, les deux mixins les plus célèbres se mettent au service de timelog. <strong>Comparable</strong> : dès que votre classe sait faire <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (l'« opérateur vaisseau spatial » : -1, 0 ou 1), le mixin vous donne <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>between?</code> et d'autres encore.</p><p>Faites en même temps connaissance avec <code>Data</code> – la classe de Ruby pour les objets valeur immuables :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "Duration = Data.define(:minutes) do\n  include Comparable\n\n  def <=>(other)\n    minutes <=> other.minutes\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{minutes / 60}h #{minutes % 60}min\"\n  end\nend\n\nbreaks = [Duration.new(minutes: 90), Duration.new(minutes: 45), Duration.new(minutes: 120)]\nbreaks.max.to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>Data.define</code> crée une classe avec des champs fixes, l'égalité et <code>inspect</code> offerts – et les objets sont gelés (pas de modification accidentelle, voir la leçon {{nr:tl-objects}}). Qui a besoin d'une autre valeur ne modifie donc pas l'objet, mais en demande un nouveau à <code>with</code> :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "short = Duration.new(minutes: 30)\nlong = short.with(minutes: 90)\n[short.to_s, long.to_s, short.frozen?, short == Duration.new(minutes: 30)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Deux objets Data de même contenu sont égaux (<code>==</code>) – ce qui les rend parfaits pour des valeurs comme une durée, une somme d'argent ou une période. Pour les cas modifiables, il existe le plus ancien <code>Struct</code>.</p><p>Le deuxième super-pouvoir : <strong>Enumerable</strong>. Votre classe fournit simplement <code>each</code> – et reçoit en échange TOUTE la boîte à outils des collections : <code>map</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>sort_by</code>, <code>group_by</code> … exactement les méthodes de la leçon {{nr:tl-collections}}, désormais sur votre propre classe.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Data.html' target='_blank'>Data</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Comparable.html' target='_blank'>Comparable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html' target='_blank'>Enumerable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Struct.html' target='_blank'>Struct</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Rendez <code>Timesheet</code> énumérable : <code>include Enumerable</code> plus une méthode <code>each</code> qui transmet le bloc à <code>@entries.each</code>. La dernière ligne fonctionnera alors.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  # include ...\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  # def each(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }\n])\n\n# ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }\n",
            "hints": [
              "Deux endroits en commentaire : <code>include Enumerable</code> en haut de la classe et une méthode <code>each</code> qui se contente de transmettre le bloc à <code>@entries.each</code>."
            ],
            "check": "Timesheet.include?(Enumerable) && ts.map { |e| e[:project] } == [\"A\", \"B\"] && ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] } == 3.0 && code.include?(\"include Enumerable\") && code.include?(\"def each\")",
            "hint": "<code>include Enumerable</code> dans la classe, plus <code>def each(&block); @entries.each(&block); end</code>.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  include Enumerable\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  def each(&block)\n    @entries.each(&block)\n  end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }])\nts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Enumerable e Data",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Due superpoteri da includere</h2><p>Nella lezione {{nr:module}} <code>Comparable</code> ha dato a un pacco sei confronti – ora i due mixin più famosi si mettono al lavoro per timelog. <strong>Comparable</strong>: appena la tua classe sa fare <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (l'“operatore astronave”: -1, 0 o 1), il mixin ti regala <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>between?</code> e altro ancora.</p><p>Nello stesso tempo, ecco <code>Data</code> – la classe di Ruby per gli oggetti valore immutabili:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "Duration = Data.define(:minutes) do\n  include Comparable\n\n  def <=>(other)\n    minutes <=> other.minutes\n  end\n\n  def to_s\n    \"#{minutes / 60}h #{minutes % 60}min\"\n  end\nend\n\nbreaks = [Duration.new(minutes: 90), Duration.new(minutes: 45), Duration.new(minutes: 120)]\nbreaks.max.to_s"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>Data.define</code> crea una classe con campi fissi, uguaglianza e <code>inspect</code> in omaggio – e gli oggetti sono congelati (niente modifiche accidentali, vedi lezione {{nr:tl-objects}}). Chi ha bisogno di un valore diverso, quindi, non modifica l'oggetto ma ne chiede uno nuovo a <code>with</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "short = Duration.new(minutes: 30)\nlong = short.with(minutes: 90)\n[short.to_s, long.to_s, short.frozen?, short == Duration.new(minutes: 30)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Due oggetti Data con lo stesso contenuto sono uguali (<code>==</code>) – il che li rende adatti a valori come una durata, un importo di denaro o un periodo di tempo. Per i casi modificabili c'è il più vecchio <code>Struct</code>.</p><p>Il secondo superpotere: <strong>Enumerable</strong>. La tua classe fornisce soltanto <code>each</code> – e in cambio riceve TUTTA la cassetta degli attrezzi delle collezioni: <code>map</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>sort_by</code>, <code>group_by</code> … esattamente i metodi della lezione {{nr:tl-collections}}, ora sulla tua classe.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Data.html' target='_blank'>Data</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Comparable.html' target='_blank'>Comparable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Enumerable.html' target='_blank'>Enumerable</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Struct.html' target='_blank'>Struct</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Rendi <code>Timesheet</code> enumerabile: <code>include Enumerable</code> più un metodo <code>each</code> che inoltra il blocco a <code>@entries.each</code>. Allora l'ultima riga funziona.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Timesheet\n  # include ...\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  # def each(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }\n])\n\n# ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }\n",
            "hints": [
              "Due punti commentati: <code>include Enumerable</code> in cima alla classe e un metodo <code>each</code> che passa semplicemente il blocco a <code>@entries.each</code>."
            ],
            "check": "Timesheet.include?(Enumerable) && ts.map { |e| e[:project] } == [\"A\", \"B\"] && ts.sum { |e| e[:hours] } == 3.0 && code.include?(\"include Enumerable\") && code.include?(\"def each\")",
            "hint": "<code>include Enumerable</code> nella classe, più <code>def each(&block); @entries.each(&block); end</code>.",
            "solution": "class Timesheet\n  include Enumerable\n\n  def initialize(entries)\n    @entries = entries\n  end\n\n  def each(&block)\n    @entries.each(&block)\n  end\nend\n\nts = Timesheet.new([{ project: \"A\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 1.0 }])\nts.sum { |e| e[:hours] }"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-blocks",
      "de": {
        "title": "Blocks, Procs & Lambdas",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Blocks – Code als Geschenk</h2><p>Du benutzt Blocks seit Lektion {{nr:schleifen}} – jetzt schauen wir hinter den Vorhang. Eine Methode nimmt einen Block entgegen und führt ihn mit <code>yield</code> aus; <code>block_given?</code> verrät, ob einer da ist. Damit baust du „Rahmen“-Methoden wie diese Stoppuhr:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def mit_zeitmessung(name)\n  start = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)\n  ergebnis = yield\n  ms = (Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - start) * 1000\n  puts \"#{name}: #{ms.round(1)} ms\"\n  ergebnis\nend\n\nmit_zeitmessung(\"Summe\") { (1..100_000).sum }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Ein Block ist kein Objekt – aber du kannst ihn zu einem machen. <code>proc</code> und <code>lambda</code> verpacken Code in Variablen. Die Unterschiede: Ein Lambda prüft die Argumentzahl streng und <code>return</code> verlässt nur das Lambda; ein Proc ist bei beidem locker. Und <code>&amp;:to_s</code> ist die Kurzform „mach aus dem Symbol einen Block“:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "verdopple = ->(x) { x * 2 }\n\n[verdopple.call(21), verdopple.(5), [1, 2, 3].map(&:to_s)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Lambdas sind <strong>Closures</strong>: Sie nehmen ihre Umgebung mit. Perfekt für timelog-Berichtsformate – jedes Format ist ein kleines verpacktes Programm:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "formate = {\n  text: ->(e) { \"#{e[:projekt].ljust(10)} #{e[:stunden]}h\" },\n  csv:  ->(e) { \"#{e[:projekt]};#{e[:stunden]}\" }\n}\n\neintrag = { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 }\nformate[:csv].call(eintrag)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Block+Argument' target='_blank'>Blöcke als Argument</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Proc.html' target='_blank'>Proc</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Kernel.html#method-i-lambda' target='_blank'>lambda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>each_projekt(eintraege)</code>: Die Methode gruppiert die Einträge nach Projekt und übergibt <strong>per <code>yield</code></strong> jedes Paar <code>(projekt, liste)</code> an den Block – wie <code>each</code>, nur eine Etage höher.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def each_projekt(eintraege)\n#   ... group_by ... yield ...\n# end\n\n# Test:\n# each_projekt([{ projekt: \"A\", stunden: 1.0 }]) do |projekt, liste|\n#   puts \"#{projekt}: #{liste.length} Eintraege\"\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Gruppiere die Einträge mit <code>group_by</code> nach dem Projekt und geh die Gruppen mit <code>each</code> durch. Für jede rufst du <code>yield</code> mit Projekt und Liste auf."
            ],
            "check": "gesammelt = []; each_projekt([{ projekt: \"A\", stunden: 1.0 }, { projekt: \"B\", stunden: 2.0 }, { projekt: \"A\", stunden: 0.5 }]) { |p, liste| gesammelt << [p, liste.length] }; gesammelt == [[\"A\", 2], [\"B\", 1]] && code.include?(\"yield\")",
            "hint": "<code>def each_projekt(eintraege); eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.each { |projekt, liste| yield(projekt, liste) }; end</code>",
            "solution": "def each_projekt(eintraege)\n  eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.each { |projekt, liste| yield(projekt, liste) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Blocks, procs & lambdas",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Blocks – code as a gift</h2><p>You've been using blocks since lesson {{nr:schleifen}} – now let's look behind the curtain. A method receives a block and runs it with <code>yield</code>; <code>block_given?</code> tells you whether one arrived. That's how you build “wrapper” methods like this stopwatch:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def with_timing(name)\n  start = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)\n  result = yield\n  ms = (Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - start) * 1000\n  puts \"#{name}: #{ms.round(1)} ms\"\n  result\nend\n\nwith_timing(\"sum\") { (1..100_000).sum }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>A block is not an object – but you can make it one. <code>proc</code> and <code>lambda</code> wrap code into variables. The differences: a lambda checks its argument count strictly and <code>return</code> leaves only the lambda; a proc is relaxed about both. And <code>&amp;:to_s</code> is the shorthand for “turn this symbol into a block”:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "double = ->(x) { x * 2 }\n\n[double.call(21), double.(5), [1, 2, 3].map(&:to_s)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Lambdas are <strong>closures</strong>: they carry their environment with them. Perfect for timelog report formats – each format is a small packaged program:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "formats = {\n  text: ->(e) { \"#{e[:project].ljust(10)} #{e[:hours]}h\" },\n  csv:  ->(e) { \"#{e[:project]};#{e[:hours]}\" }\n}\n\nentry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\nformats[:csv].call(entry)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Block+Argument' target='_blank'>Block arguments</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Proc.html' target='_blank'>Proc</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Kernel.html#method-i-lambda' target='_blank'>lambda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>each_project(entries)</code>: the method groups the entries by project and hands <strong>via <code>yield</code></strong> each pair <code>(project, list)</code> to the block – like <code>each</code>, one floor up.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def each_project(entries)\n#   ... group_by ... yield ...\n# end\n\n# Test:\n# each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }]) do |project, list|\n#   puts \"#{project}: #{list.length} entries\"\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Group the entries by project with <code>group_by</code> and walk the groups with <code>each</code>. For each one, call <code>yield</code> with the project and its list."
            ],
            "check": "collected = []; each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"A\", hours: 0.5 }]) { |p, list| collected << [p, list.length] }; collected == [[\"A\", 2], [\"B\", 1]] && code.include?(\"yield\")",
            "hint": "<code>def each_project(entries); entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }; end</code>",
            "solution": "def each_project(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "ブロック、Proc、lambda",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>ブロックは、コードの贈り物</h2><p>ブロックはレッスン{{nr:schleifen}}からずっと使ってきました。今回はその舞台裏をのぞいてみましょう。メソッドはブロックを受け取り、<code>yield</code>でそれを実行します。ブロックが渡されたかどうかは<code>block_given?</code>でわかります。これを使うと、次のストップウォッチのように、処理を包み込む「ラッパー」メソッドが作れます。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def with_timing(name)\n  start = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)\n  result = yield\n  ms = (Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - start) * 1000\n  puts \"#{name}: #{ms.round(1)} ms\"\n  result\nend\n\nwith_timing(\"sum\") { (1..100_000).sum }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ブロックそのものはオブジェクトではありません。でも、オブジェクトにすることはできます。<code>proc</code>や<code>lambda</code>を使うと、コードを包んで変数に入れておけます。違いは2つあります。lambdaは引数の数を厳しくチェックし、<code>return</code>してもlambdaから抜けるだけです。procはどちらについてもゆるやかです。また、<code>&amp;:to_s</code>は「このシンボルをブロックにして」という意味の省略記法です。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "double = ->(x) { x * 2 }\n\n[double.call(21), double.(5), [1, 2, 3].map(&:to_s)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>lambdaは<strong>クロージャ</strong>です。作られた場所の環境（変数など）をいっしょに持ち歩きます。timelogのレポートの出力形式にぴったりです。形式のひとつひとつが、小さくまとめられたプログラムになります。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "formats = {\n  text: ->(e) { \"#{e[:project].ljust(10)} #{e[:hours]}h\" },\n  csv:  ->(e) { \"#{e[:project]};#{e[:hours]}\" }\n}\n\nentry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\nformats[:csv].call(entry)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fcall.html' target='_blank'>ブロック引数</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Proc.html' target='_blank'>Proc</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Kernel/m/lambda.html' target='_blank'>lambda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>each_project(entries)</code>を書きましょう。このメソッドはエントリをプロジェクトごとにグループ分けし、<code>(project, list)</code>の組をひとつずつ<strong><code>yield</code>で</strong>ブロックに渡します。いわば、1段上のレベルの<code>each</code>です。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def each_project(entries)\n#   ... group_by ... yield ...\n# end\n\n# テスト:\n# each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }]) do |project, list|\n#   puts \"#{project}: #{list.length} entries\"\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "エントリを<code>group_by</code>でプロジェクトごとにまとめて、そのグループを<code>each</code>でたどってね。グループごとに、プロジェクトとリストを渡して<code>yield</code>を呼ぶんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "collected = []; each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"A\", hours: 0.5 }]) { |p, list| collected << [p, list.length] }; collected == [[\"A\", 2], [\"B\", 1]] && code.include?(\"yield\")",
            "hint": "たとえばこう書けるよ：<code>def each_project(entries); entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }; end</code>",
            "solution": "def each_project(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Blocs, procs et lambdas",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Les blocs – du code en cadeau</h2><p>Vous utilisez des blocs depuis la leçon {{nr:schleifen}} – jetons maintenant un œil dans les coulisses. Une méthode reçoit un bloc et l'exécute avec <code>yield</code> ; <code>block_given?</code> vous indique si un bloc a été transmis. C'est ainsi que l'on construit des méthodes « enveloppes » comme ce chronomètre :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def with_timing(name)\n  start = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)\n  result = yield\n  ms = (Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - start) * 1000\n  puts \"#{name}: #{ms.round(1)} ms\"\n  result\nend\n\nwith_timing(\"sum\") { (1..100_000).sum }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Un bloc n'est pas un objet – mais vous pouvez en faire un. <code>proc</code> et <code>lambda</code> enveloppent du code dans des variables. Les différences : une lambda vérifie strictement son nombre d'arguments et <code>return</code> ne quitte que la lambda ; une proc est plus souple sur ces deux points. Et <code>&amp;:to_s</code> est le raccourci pour « transforme ce symbole en bloc » :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "double = ->(x) { x * 2 }\n\n[double.call(21), double.(5), [1, 2, 3].map(&:to_s)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Les lambdas sont des <strong>closures</strong> (fermetures) : elles emportent leur environnement avec elles. Parfait pour les formats de rapport de timelog – chaque format est un petit programme empaqueté :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "formats = {\n  text: ->(e) { \"#{e[:project].ljust(10)} #{e[:hours]}h\" },\n  csv:  ->(e) { \"#{e[:project]};#{e[:hours]}\" }\n}\n\nentry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\nformats[:csv].call(entry)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Block+Argument' target='_blank'>Arguments de bloc</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Proc.html' target='_blank'>Proc</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Kernel.html#method-i-lambda' target='_blank'>lambda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>each_project(entries)</code> : la méthode regroupe les saisies par projet et transmet <strong>via <code>yield</code></strong> chaque paire <code>(project, list)</code> au bloc – comme <code>each</code>, un étage plus haut.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def each_project(entries)\n#   ... group_by ... yield ...\n# end\n\n# Test :\n# each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }]) do |project, list|\n#   puts \"#{project}: #{list.length} entries\"\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Regroupez les saisies par projet avec <code>group_by</code> et parcourez les groupes avec <code>each</code>. Pour chacun, appelez <code>yield</code> avec le projet et sa liste."
            ],
            "check": "collected = []; each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"A\", hours: 0.5 }]) { |p, list| collected << [p, list.length] }; collected == [[\"A\", 2], [\"B\", 1]] && code.include?(\"yield\")",
            "hint": "<code>def each_project(entries); entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }; end</code>",
            "solution": "def each_project(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Blocchi, proc e lambda",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Blocchi – codice come regalo</h2><p>Usi i blocchi dalla lezione {{nr:schleifen}} – ora guardiamo dietro le quinte. Un metodo riceve un blocco e lo esegue con <code>yield</code>; <code>block_given?</code> ti dice se ne è arrivato uno. Così si costruiscono metodi “involucro” come questo cronometro:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "def with_timing(name)\n  start = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)\n  result = yield\n  ms = (Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - start) * 1000\n  puts \"#{name}: #{ms.round(1)} ms\"\n  result\nend\n\nwith_timing(\"sum\") { (1..100_000).sum }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Un blocco non è un oggetto – ma puoi farlo diventare tale. <code>proc</code> e <code>lambda</code> racchiudono del codice in variabili. Le differenze: una lambda controlla rigorosamente il numero di argomenti e <code>return</code> esce solo dalla lambda; una proc è tollerante su entrambi i punti. E <code>&amp;:to_s</code> è la forma abbreviata di “trasforma questo simbolo in un blocco”:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "double = ->(x) { x * 2 }\n\n[double.call(21), double.(5), [1, 2, 3].map(&:to_s)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le lambda sono <strong>closure</strong>: portano con sé il loro ambiente. Perfette per i formati dei report di timelog – ogni formato è un piccolo programma impacchettato:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "formats = {\n  text: ->(e) { \"#{e[:project].ljust(10)} #{e[:hours]}h\" },\n  csv:  ->(e) { \"#{e[:project]};#{e[:hours]}\" }\n}\n\nentry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\nformats[:csv].call(entry)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/methods_rdoc.html#label-Block+Argument' target='_blank'>Argomenti blocco</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Proc.html' target='_blank'>Proc</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Kernel.html#method-i-lambda' target='_blank'>lambda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>each_project(entries)</code>: il metodo raggruppa le voci per progetto e passa al blocco, <strong>tramite <code>yield</code></strong>, ogni coppia <code>(project, list)</code> – come <code>each</code>, ma un piano più su.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def each_project(entries)\n#   ... group_by ... yield ...\n# end\n\n# Test:\n# each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }]) do |project, list|\n#   puts \"#{project}: #{list.length} entries\"\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Raggruppa le voci per progetto con <code>group_by</code> e percorri i gruppi con <code>each</code>. Per ciascuno chiama <code>yield</code> con il progetto e la sua lista."
            ],
            "check": "collected = []; each_project([{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.0 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }, { project: \"A\", hours: 0.5 }]) { |p, list| collected << [p, list.length] }; collected == [[\"A\", 2], [\"B\", 1]] && code.include?(\"yield\")",
            "hint": "<code>def each_project(entries); entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }; end</code>",
            "solution": "def each_project(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.each { |project, list| yield(project, list) }\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-errors",
      "de": {
        "title": "Fehler behandeln",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Wenn etwas schiefgeht</h2><p>Mit <code>raise</code> wirft Ruby eine Exception, mit <code>rescue</code> fängst du sie, <code>ensure</code> läuft <em>immer</em> (aufräumen!). Gute Programme definieren eine eigene <strong>Fehler-Familie</strong> – dann können Aufrufer gezielt „alle timelog-Fehler“ fangen, ohne fremde Fehler zu verschlucken:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Error        < StandardError; end\n  class ParseError   < Error; end\n  class OverlapError < Error; end\nend\n\nbegin\n  raise Timelog::ParseError, \"Zeile 7 ist kein Zeiteintrag\"\nrescue Timelog::Error => e\n  \"gefangen: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Wann Exception, wann Rückgabewert? Faustregel: <code>nil</code> für „kann normal vorkommen“ (Zeile passt nicht ins Muster), Exception für „hier stimmt etwas grundsätzlich nicht“ (Eintrag endet vor seinem Beginn). Erbe immer von <code>StandardError</code>, nie von <code>Exception</code> direkt – sonst fängst du auch Strg-C. Und pack die Familie in dein Modul (<code>Timelog::ParseError</code>), damit sie niemandem in die Quere kommt.</p><p>Bei <em>vorübergehenden</em> Fehlern (Netzwerk!) hilft <code>retry</code>: Es springt zurück an den Anfang des <code>begin</code>-Blocks:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\nversuche = 0\nwackliger_dienst = lambda do\n  versuche += 1\n  raise TimelogError, \"Netzwerkfehler\" if versuche < 3\n  \"Daten empfangen (Versuch #{versuche})\"\nend\n\nbegin\n  wackliger_dienst.call\nrescue TimelogError\n  retry if versuche < 5\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><p>Echter Retry-Code wartet zwischen den Versuchen immer länger (<em>Backoff</em>) – <code>sleep</code> gibt es im Browser nicht:</p><pre><code>rescue TimelogError\n  wartezeit = 2 ** versuch   # 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s ...\n  sleep(wartezeit)\n  retry if versuch < 5</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/exceptions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Exceptions: begin, rescue, ensure</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Exception.html' target='_blank'>Exception</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>sync_mit_retry(dienst, max:)</code>: Sie ruft <code>dienst.call</code> auf. Wirft der Dienst einen <code>TimelogError</code>, wird bis zu <code>max</code>-mal insgesamt versucht – danach fliegt der Fehler weiter (einfach nicht mehr <code>retry</code> aufrufen). Bei Erfolg gibt sie das Ergebnis zurück.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\n# def sync_mit_retry(dienst, max:)\n#   versuche = 0\n#   begin\n#     ...\n#   rescue TimelogError\n#     ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Zähl im <code>begin</code>-Block die Versuche hoch, bevor du <code>dienst.call</code> aufrufst. Im <code>rescue</code> entscheidest du: unter <code>max</code> noch einmal <code>retry</code>, sonst den Fehler mit <code>raise</code> weiterwerfen."
            ],
            "check": "z1 = 0; ok_dienst = lambda { z1 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"kaputt\" if z1 < 3; \"ok\" }; erg = sync_mit_retry(ok_dienst, max: 5); kaputt = begin; z2 = 0; immer_kaputt = lambda { z2 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"kaputt\" }; sync_mit_retry(immer_kaputt, max: 2); false; rescue TimelogError; z2 == 2; end; erg == \"ok\" && z1 == 3 && kaputt && code.include?(\"retry\")",
            "hint": "<code>versuche += 1</code> im begin-Block vor <code>dienst.call</code>; im rescue: <code>retry if versuche < max</code>, sonst <code>raise</code>.",
            "solution": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\ndef sync_mit_retry(dienst, max:)\n  versuche = 0\n  begin\n    versuche += 1\n    dienst.call\n  rescue TimelogError\n    retry if versuche < max\n    raise\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Handling errors",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>When things go wrong</h2><p><code>raise</code> throws an exception, <code>rescue</code> catches it, <code>ensure</code> runs <em>always</em> (cleanup!). Good programs define their own <strong>error family</strong> – then callers can catch “all timelog errors” without swallowing unrelated ones:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Error        < StandardError; end\n  class ParseError   < Error; end\n  class OverlapError < Error; end\nend\n\nbegin\n  raise Timelog::ParseError, \"line 7 is not a time entry\"\nrescue Timelog::Error => e\n  \"caught: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Exception or return value? Rule of thumb: <code>nil</code> for “can happen normally” (a line doesn't match the pattern), exception for “something is fundamentally wrong here” (an entry ends before it starts). Always inherit from <code>StandardError</code>, never from <code>Exception</code> directly – or you'll catch Ctrl-C too. And put the family into your module (<code>Timelog::ParseError</code>) so it never clashes with anyone else's.</p><p>For <em>transient</em> errors (networks!) there's <code>retry</code>: it jumps back to the start of the <code>begin</code> block:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\nattempts = 0\nflaky_service = lambda do\n  attempts += 1\n  raise TimelogError, \"network error\" if attempts < 3\n  \"data received (attempt #{attempts})\"\nend\n\nbegin\n  flaky_service.call\nrescue TimelogError\n  retry if attempts < 5\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><p>Real retry code waits longer between attempts (<em>backoff</em>) – there is no <code>sleep</code> in the browser:</p><pre><code>rescue TimelogError\n  wait = 2 ** attempt   # 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s ...\n  sleep(wait)\n  retry if attempt < 5</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/exceptions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Exceptions: begin, rescue, ensure</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Exception.html' target='_blank'>Exception</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>sync_with_retry(service, max:)</code>: it calls <code>service.call</code>. If the service raises a <code>TimelogError</code>, it tries up to <code>max</code> times in total – after that the error propagates (simply don't <code>retry</code> anymore). On success it returns the result.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\n# def sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n#   attempts = 0\n#   begin\n#     ...\n#   rescue TimelogError\n#     ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Count the attempts up in the <code>begin</code> block before calling <code>service.call</code>. In the <code>rescue</code> you decide: below <code>max</code>, <code>retry</code> once more, otherwise throw the error on with <code>raise</code>."
            ],
            "check": "c1 = 0; ok_service = lambda { c1 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" if c1 < 3; \"ok\" }; res = sync_with_retry(ok_service, max: 5); broke = begin; c2 = 0; always_broken = lambda { c2 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" }; sync_with_retry(always_broken, max: 2); false; rescue TimelogError; c2 == 2; end; res == \"ok\" && c1 == 3 && broke && code.include?(\"retry\")",
            "hint": "<code>attempts += 1</code> in the begin block before <code>service.call</code>; in the rescue: <code>retry if attempts < max</code>, otherwise <code>raise</code>.",
            "solution": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\ndef sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n  attempts = 0\n  begin\n    attempts += 1\n    service.call\n  rescue TimelogError\n    retry if attempts < max\n    raise\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "エラー処理",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>うまくいかないときは</h2><p><code>raise</code>で例外を発生させ、<code>rescue</code>でそれを捕まえます。<code>ensure</code>の中身は<em>どんなときでも</em>実行されます（後片付けに便利！）。よくできたプログラムは、自分専用の<strong>エラークラスの一族</strong>を定義します。そうすれば呼び出す側は、関係のないエラーまで握りつぶすことなく、「timelogのエラー全部」をまとめて捕まえられます。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Error        < StandardError; end\n  class ParseError   < Error; end\n  class OverlapError < Error; end\nend\n\nbegin\n  raise Timelog::ParseError, \"line 7 is not a time entry\"\nrescue Timelog::Error => e\n  \"caught: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>例外にするか、戻り値で知らせるか？目安はこうです。「ふつうに起こりうること」（ある行がパターンに合わない）なら<code>nil</code>、「何かが根本的におかしい」（エントリの終わりが始まりより前になっている）なら例外です。継承元はかならず<code>StandardError</code>にして、<code>Exception</code>を直接継承してはいけません。そうしないと、Ctrl-Cまで捕まえてしまいます。それから、一族は自分のモジュールの中に入れておきましょう（<code>Timelog::ParseError</code>）。そうすれば、ほかの誰かのクラスと名前がぶつかることはありません。</p><p><em>一時的な</em>エラー（ネットワークなど！）には<code>retry</code>が使えます。<code>begin</code>ブロックの先頭に戻って、もう一度やり直してくれます。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\nattempts = 0\nflaky_service = lambda do\n  attempts += 1\n  raise TimelogError, \"network error\" if attempts < 3\n  \"data received (attempt #{attempts})\"\nend\n\nbegin\n  flaky_service.call\nrescue TimelogError\n  retry if attempts < 5\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><p>実際のリトライ処理では、試すたびに待ち時間を長くしていきます（<em>バックオフ</em>）。ブラウザの中には<code>sleep</code>がないため、上の例では待ち時間を入れていません。</p><pre><code>rescue TimelogError\n  wait = 2 ** attempt   # 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s ...\n  sleep(wait)\n  retry if attempt < 5</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fcontrol.html#begin' target='_blank'>例外処理：begin、rescue、ensure</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Exception.html' target='_blank'>Exception</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>sync_with_retry(service, max:)</code>を書きましょう。このメソッドは<code>service.call</code>を呼び出します。サービスが<code>TimelogError</code>を発生させたら、合計で<code>max</code>回まで試します。それでもだめなら、エラーをそのまま外に伝えます（もう<code>retry</code>しなければいいだけです）。成功したら、その結果を返します。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\n# def sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n#   attempts = 0\n#   begin\n#     ...\n#   rescue TimelogError\n#     ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>begin</code>ブロックの中で、<code>service.call</code>を呼ぶ前に試行回数を1ふやしてね。<code>rescue</code>では、<code>max</code>より少なければ<code>retry</code>、そうでなければ<code>raise</code>でエラーをそのまま投げるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "c1 = 0; ok_service = lambda { c1 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" if c1 < 3; \"ok\" }; res = sync_with_retry(ok_service, max: 5); broke = begin; c2 = 0; always_broken = lambda { c2 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" }; sync_with_retry(always_broken, max: 2); false; rescue TimelogError; c2 == 2; end; res == \"ok\" && c1 == 3 && broke && code.include?(\"retry\")",
            "hint": "beginブロックの中で、<code>service.call</code>の前に<code>attempts += 1</code>しよう。rescueの中では<code>retry if attempts < max</code>、そうでなければ<code>raise</code>だよ。",
            "solution": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\ndef sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n  attempts = 0\n  begin\n    attempts += 1\n    service.call\n  rescue TimelogError\n    retry if attempts < max\n    raise\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Gérer les erreurs",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Quand les choses tournent mal</h2><p><code>raise</code> lève une exception, <code>rescue</code> l'intercepte, <code>ensure</code> s'exécute <em>toujours</em> (pour le nettoyage !). Les bons programmes définissent leur propre <strong>famille d'erreurs</strong> – ainsi, l'appelant peut intercepter « toutes les erreurs de timelog » sans avaler celles qui n'ont rien à voir :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Error        < StandardError; end\n  class ParseError   < Error; end\n  class OverlapError < Error; end\nend\n\nbegin\n  raise Timelog::ParseError, \"line 7 is not a time entry\"\nrescue Timelog::Error => e\n  \"caught: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Exception ou valeur de retour ? Règle pratique : <code>nil</code> pour « peut arriver normalement » (une ligne ne correspond pas au motif), une exception pour « quelque chose ne va fondamentalement pas » (une saisie se termine avant de commencer). Héritez toujours de <code>StandardError</code>, jamais directement de <code>Exception</code> – sinon vous intercepterez aussi Ctrl-C. Et placez la famille dans votre module (<code>Timelog::ParseError</code>) pour qu'elle n'entre jamais en conflit avec celle de quelqu'un d'autre.</p><p>Pour les erreurs <em>passagères</em> (le réseau !), il existe <code>retry</code> : il revient au début du bloc <code>begin</code> :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\nattempts = 0\nflaky_service = lambda do\n  attempts += 1\n  raise TimelogError, \"network error\" if attempts < 3\n  \"data received (attempt #{attempts})\"\nend\n\nbegin\n  flaky_service.call\nrescue TimelogError\n  retry if attempts < 5\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><p>Un vrai code de nouvelle tentative attend de plus en plus longtemps entre les essais (<em>backoff</em>) – il n'y a pas de <code>sleep</code> dans le navigateur :</p><pre><code>rescue TimelogError\n  wait = 2 ** attempt   # 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s ...\n  sleep(wait)\n  retry if attempt < 5</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/exceptions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Exceptions : begin, rescue, ensure</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Exception.html' target='_blank'>Exception</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>sync_with_retry(service, max:)</code> : elle appelle <code>service.call</code>. Si le service lève une <code>TimelogError</code>, elle réessaie jusqu'à <code>max</code> fois au total – au-delà, l'erreur se propage (il suffit de ne plus faire de <code>retry</code>). En cas de succès, elle renvoie le résultat.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\n# def sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n#   attempts = 0\n#   begin\n#     ...\n#   rescue TimelogError\n#     ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Incrémentez le nombre de tentatives dans le bloc <code>begin</code> avant d'appeler <code>service.call</code>. Dans le <code>rescue</code>, vous décidez : en dessous de <code>max</code>, un <code>retry</code> de plus, sinon relancez l'erreur avec <code>raise</code>."
            ],
            "check": "c1 = 0; ok_service = lambda { c1 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" if c1 < 3; \"ok\" }; res = sync_with_retry(ok_service, max: 5); broke = begin; c2 = 0; always_broken = lambda { c2 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" }; sync_with_retry(always_broken, max: 2); false; rescue TimelogError; c2 == 2; end; res == \"ok\" && c1 == 3 && broke && code.include?(\"retry\")",
            "hint": "<code>attempts += 1</code> dans le bloc begin, avant <code>service.call</code> ; dans le rescue : <code>retry if attempts < max</code>, sinon <code>raise</code>.",
            "solution": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\ndef sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n  attempts = 0\n  begin\n    attempts += 1\n    service.call\n  rescue TimelogError\n    retry if attempts < max\n    raise\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Gestire gli errori",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Quando le cose vanno storte</h2><p><code>raise</code> solleva un'eccezione, <code>rescue</code> la cattura, <code>ensure</code> viene eseguito <em>sempre</em> (pulizia!). I buoni programmi definiscono una propria <strong>famiglia di errori</strong> – così chi chiama può catturare “tutti gli errori di timelog” senza inghiottire quelli che non c'entrano:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Error        < StandardError; end\n  class ParseError   < Error; end\n  class OverlapError < Error; end\nend\n\nbegin\n  raise Timelog::ParseError, \"line 7 is not a time entry\"\nrescue Timelog::Error => e\n  \"caught: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Eccezione o valore di ritorno? Regola pratica: <code>nil</code> per ciò che “può succedere normalmente” (una riga non corrisponde allo schema), eccezione per “qui c'è qualcosa di fondamentalmente sbagliato” (una voce finisce prima di iniziare). Eredita sempre da <code>StandardError</code>, mai direttamente da <code>Exception</code> – altrimenti catturi anche Ctrl-C. E metti la famiglia nel tuo modulo (<code>Timelog::ParseError</code>), così non entra mai in conflitto con quella di qualcun altro.</p><p>Per gli errori <em>temporanei</em> (le reti!) c'è <code>retry</code>: salta di nuovo all'inizio del blocco <code>begin</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\nattempts = 0\nflaky_service = lambda do\n  attempts += 1\n  raise TimelogError, \"network error\" if attempts < 3\n  \"data received (attempt #{attempts})\"\nend\n\nbegin\n  flaky_service.call\nrescue TimelogError\n  retry if attempts < 5\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><p>Il vero codice di retry attende sempre più a lungo tra un tentativo e l'altro (<em>backoff</em>) – nel browser non c'è <code>sleep</code>:</p><pre><code>rescue TimelogError\n  wait = 2 ** attempt   # 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s ...\n  sleep(wait)\n  retry if attempt < 5</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/exceptions_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Eccezioni: begin, rescue, ensure</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Exception.html' target='_blank'>Exception</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>sync_with_retry(service, max:)</code>: chiama <code>service.call</code>. Se il servizio solleva un <code>TimelogError</code>, ritenta fino a <code>max</code> volte in tutto – dopodiché l'errore si propaga (semplicemente non fare più <code>retry</code>). In caso di successo restituisce il risultato.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\n# def sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n#   attempts = 0\n#   begin\n#     ...\n#   rescue TimelogError\n#     ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Incrementa il numero di tentativi nel blocco <code>begin</code> prima di chiamare <code>service.call</code>. Nel <code>rescue</code> decidi tu: sotto <code>max</code>, ancora un <code>retry</code>, altrimenti rilancia l'errore con <code>raise</code>."
            ],
            "check": "c1 = 0; ok_service = lambda { c1 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" if c1 < 3; \"ok\" }; res = sync_with_retry(ok_service, max: 5); broke = begin; c2 = 0; always_broken = lambda { c2 += 1; raise TimelogError, \"broken\" }; sync_with_retry(always_broken, max: 2); false; rescue TimelogError; c2 == 2; end; res == \"ok\" && c1 == 3 && broke && code.include?(\"retry\")",
            "hint": "<code>attempts += 1</code> nel blocco begin prima di <code>service.call</code>; nel rescue: <code>retry if attempts < max</code>, altrimenti <code>raise</code>.",
            "solution": "class TimelogError < StandardError; end\n\ndef sync_with_retry(service, max:)\n  attempts = 0\n  begin\n    attempts += 1\n    service.call\n  rescue TimelogError\n    retry if attempts < max\n    raise\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-formats",
      "de": {
        "title": "Daten speichern: Formate",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog wird dauerhaft</h2><p>Bisher leben unsere Einträge nur im Speicher. Zeit für <strong>Serialisierung</strong>: Daten in Text verwandeln und zurück. Ruby bringt die drei wichtigsten Formate mit. <strong>JSON</strong> ist die Sprache der Web-APIs:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"json\"\n\neintraege = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 }\n]\n\ntext = JSON.pretty_generate(eintraege)\nputs text\nJSON.parse(text, symbolize_names: true) == eintraege"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Beachte <code>symbolize_names: true</code> – JSON kennt keine Symbole, beim Einlesen wären die Schlüssel sonst Strings. <strong>CSV</strong> ist das Format für Tabellen (Excel!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ncsv_text = CSV.generate do |csv|\n  csv << [\"projekt\", \"stunden\"]\n  eintraege.each { |e| csv << [e[:projekt], e[:stunden]] }\nend\nputs csv_text\n\nCSV.parse(csv_text, headers: true).map { |zeile| zeile[\"projekt\"] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Und <strong>YAML</strong> ist das Lieblingsformat für Konfigurationsdateien – Menschen können es gut lesen und schreiben:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"yaml\"\n\nkonfig = YAML.safe_load(\"stundensatz: 120\\nrunden_auf: 15\\n\")\nkonfig[\"stundensatz\"]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Und jetzt: <strong>Dateien</strong>! Auf deinem Rechner speichert Ruby mit <code>File.write</code> und liest mit <code>File.read</code>. Hier im Browser simulieren wir dafür ein kleines Dateisystem – mit eigenem Datei-Fenster (<code>show_files</code>):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "File.write(\"notizen.txt\", \"Speck kaufen!\\nTimelog testen.\")\nFile.write(\"projekte/plan.txt\", \"Q4: timelog fertigstellen\")\n\nshow_files"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Klick eine Datei an, um hineinzuschauen – wie im Explorer oder Finder. Und alles Übrige geht wie gewohnt: lesen, suchen, prüfen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "[File.read(\"notizen.txt\"),\n Dir.glob(\"**/*.txt\"),\n File.exist?(\"beispiel.csv\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><p>Unser Dateifenster ist eine Simulation – bei dir sind es echte Dateien auf der Platte, mit denselben Befehlen. Dort gibt es noch mehr Komfort:</p><pre><code># Block-Form schliesst die Datei automatisch:\nFile.open(\"log.txt\", \"a\") { |f| f.puts \"neuer Eintrag\" }\n\nPathname.new(\"a/b.json\")    # Pfade als Objekte\nTempfile.create(\"test\")     # Wegwerf-Dateien fuer Tests\nStringIO.new(\"...\")         # \"Datei\" im Speicher</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/JSON.html' target='_blank'>JSON</a> · <a href='https://ruby.github.io/csv/' target='_blank'>CSV</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Psych.html' target='_blank'>YAML (Psych)</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/File.html' target='_blank'>File</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>nach_csv(eintraege)</code> (CSV-Text mit Kopfzeile <code>projekt,stunden</code>) und <code>aus_csv(text)</code> (zurück zu Hashes mit Symbol-Schlüsseln und <code>Float</code>-Stunden). Speichere dann <code>daten</code> mit <code>File.write</code> in <code>eintraege.csv</code> und lies sie mit <code>aus_csv(File.read(…))</code> zurück – verlustfrei. Wirf danach einen Blick ins Datei-Fenster oben (⟳)!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ndaten = [\n  { projekt: \"A\", stunden: 1.5 },\n  { projekt: \"B\", stunden: 2.0 }\n]\n\n# def nach_csv(eintraege)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# def aus_csv(text)\n#   ...  # Tipp: zeile[\"stunden\"].to_f\n# end\n\n# File.write(\"eintraege.csv\", nach_csv(daten))\n# aus_csv(File.read(\"eintraege.csv\"))\n",
            "hints": [
              "Die Demos oben zeigen <code>CSV.generate</code> und <code>CSV.parse</code> mit <code>headers: true</code> – daraus baust du die beiden Methoden. Danach schreiben, zurücklesen und als letzte Zeile das Ergebnis von <code>aus_csv</code> stehen lassen."
            ],
            "check": "File.exist?(\"eintraege.csv\") && aus_csv(File.read(\"eintraege.csv\")) == daten && nach_csv(daten).lines.first.strip == \"projekt,stunden\"",
            "hint": "<code>nach_csv</code>/<code>aus_csv</code> wie in den Demos, dann <code>File.write(\"eintraege.csv\", nach_csv(daten))</code> und als letzte Zeile <code>aus_csv(File.read(\"eintraege.csv\"))</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"csv\"\n\ndef nach_csv(eintraege)\n  CSV.generate do |csv|\n    csv << [\"projekt\", \"stunden\"]\n    eintraege.each { |e| csv << [e[:projekt], e[:stunden]] }\n  end\nend\n\ndef aus_csv(text)\n  CSV.parse(text, headers: true).map { |z| { projekt: z[\"projekt\"], stunden: z[\"stunden\"].to_f } }\nend\n\ndaten = [{ projekt: \"A\", stunden: 1.5 }, { projekt: \"B\", stunden: 2.0 }]\nFile.write(\"eintraege.csv\", nach_csv(daten))\naus_csv(File.read(\"eintraege.csv\"))"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Saving data: formats",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog becomes persistent</h2><p>So far our entries live only in memory. Time for <strong>serialization</strong>: turning data into text and back. Ruby ships the three most important formats. <strong>JSON</strong> is the language of web APIs:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"json\"\n\nentries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\ntext = JSON.pretty_generate(entries)\nputs text\nJSON.parse(text, symbolize_names: true) == entries"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Note <code>symbolize_names: true</code> – JSON has no symbols, so keys would come back as strings otherwise. <strong>CSV</strong> is the format for tables (Excel!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ncsv_text = CSV.generate do |csv|\n  csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n  entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\nend\nputs csv_text\n\nCSV.parse(csv_text, headers: true).map { |row| row[\"project\"] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>And <strong>YAML</strong> is the favorite for configuration files – pleasant for humans to read and write:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"yaml\"\n\nconfig = YAML.safe_load(\"rate: 120\\nround_to: 15\\n\")\nconfig[\"rate\"]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>And now: <strong>files</strong>! On your machine Ruby saves with <code>File.write</code> and reads with <code>File.read</code>. Here in the browser we simulate a little filesystem for that – with its own file window (<code>show_files</code>):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "File.write(\"notizen.txt\", \"buy bacon!\\ntest timelog.\")\nFile.write(\"projekte/plan.txt\", \"Q4: finish timelog\")\n\nshow_files"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Click a file to peek inside – just like Explorer or Finder. And everything else works as usual: reading, searching, checking:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "[File.read(\"notizen.txt\"),\n Dir.glob(\"**/*.txt\"),\n File.exist?(\"beispiel.csv\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><p>Our file window is a simulation – on your machine these are real files on disk, with the same commands. There you also get extra comfort:</p><pre><code># Block form closes the file automatically:\nFile.open(\"log.txt\", \"a\") { |f| f.puts \"new entry\" }\n\nPathname.new(\"a/b.json\")    # paths as objects\nTempfile.create(\"test\")     # throwaway files for tests\nStringIO.new(\"...\")         # in-memory \"file\"</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/JSON.html' target='_blank'>JSON</a> · <a href='https://ruby.github.io/csv/' target='_blank'>CSV</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Psych.html' target='_blank'>YAML (Psych)</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/File.html' target='_blank'>File</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>to_csv(entries)</code> (CSV text with header <code>project,hours</code>) and <code>from_csv(text)</code> (back to hashes with symbol keys and <code>Float</code> hours). Then save <code>data</code> with <code>File.write</code> into <code>entries.csv</code> and read it back with <code>from_csv(File.read(…))</code> – losslessly. Afterwards take a look at the file window above (⟳)!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ndata = [\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\n# def to_csv(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# def from_csv(text)\n#   ...  # hint: row[\"hours\"].to_f\n# end\n\n# File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\n# from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))\n",
            "hints": [
              "The demos above show <code>CSV.generate</code> and <code>CSV.parse</code> with <code>headers: true</code> – build the two methods from them. Then write, read back, and leave the result of <code>from_csv</code> as the last line."
            ],
            "check": "File.exist?(\"entries.csv\") && from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\")) == data && to_csv(data).lines.first.strip == \"project,hours\"",
            "hint": "<code>to_csv</code>/<code>from_csv</code> like the demos, then <code>File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))</code> and as the last line <code>from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"csv\"\n\ndef to_csv(entries)\n  CSV.generate do |csv|\n    csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n    entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\n  end\nend\n\ndef from_csv(text)\n  CSV.parse(text, headers: true).map { |r| { project: r[\"project\"], hours: r[\"hours\"].to_f } }\nend\n\ndata = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }]\nFile.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\nfrom_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "データの保存：フォーマット",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelogのデータを永続化する</h2><p>これまで、エントリはメモリの中にしかありませんでした。そこで<strong>シリアライズ</strong>の出番です。データをテキストに変換し、またデータに戻すことをいいます。Rubyには、特に大事な3つのフォーマットが最初から付いています。まずは<strong>JSON</strong>。Web APIの共通語です。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"json\"\n\nentries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\ntext = JSON.pretty_generate(entries)\nputs text\nJSON.parse(text, symbolize_names: true) == entries"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>symbolize_names: true</code>に注目してください。JSONにはシンボルがないので、これを付けないとキーが文字列で返ってきます。<strong>CSV</strong>は表のためのフォーマットです（Excelでおなじみ！）。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ncsv_text = CSV.generate do |csv|\n  csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n  entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\nend\nputs csv_text\n\nCSV.parse(csv_text, headers: true).map { |row| row[\"project\"] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>そして<strong>YAML</strong>は、設定ファイルの定番です。人間が読むのも書くのも楽なフォーマットです。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"yaml\"\n\nconfig = YAML.safe_load(\"rate: 120\\nround_to: 15\\n\")\nconfig[\"rate\"]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>いよいよ<strong>ファイル</strong>です！自分のコンピューターでは、Rubyは<code>File.write</code>で保存し、<code>File.read</code>で読み込みます。ここブラウザの中では、そのために小さなファイルシステムをシミュレーションしています。専用のファイルウィンドウ（<code>show_files</code>）付きです。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "File.write(\"notizen.txt\", \"buy bacon!\\ntest timelog.\")\nFile.write(\"projekte/plan.txt\", \"Q4: finish timelog\")\n\nshow_files"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ファイルをクリックすると、中身をのぞけます。エクスプローラーやFinderと同じですね。ほかの操作も、いつもどおりに使えます。読み込んだり、探したり、あるかどうか確かめたり。</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "[File.read(\"notizen.txt\"),\n Dir.glob(\"**/*.txt\"),\n File.exist?(\"beispiel.csv\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><p>このファイルウィンドウはシミュレーションです。自分のコンピューターでは、同じコードのままディスク上の本物のファイルを扱えます。さらに、便利な道具も使えます。</p><pre><code># Block form closes the file automatically:\nFile.open(\"log.txt\", \"a\") { |f| f.puts \"new entry\" }\n\nPathname.new(\"a/b.json\")    # paths as objects\nTempfile.create(\"test\")     # throwaway files for tests\nStringIO.new(\"...\")         # in-memory \"file\"</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/JSON.html' target='_blank'>JSON</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/CSV.html' target='_blank'>CSV</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Psych.html' target='_blank'>YAML（Psych）</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/File.html' target='_blank'>File</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>to_csv(entries)</code>（ヘッダー行が<code>project,hours</code>のCSVテキストを返す）と、<code>from_csv(text)</code>（キーがシンボルで、時間の値が<code>Float</code>のハッシュに戻す）を書きましょう。次に、<code>data</code>を<code>File.write</code>で<code>entries.csv</code>に保存し、<code>from_csv(File.read(…))</code>で読み戻します。データが1つも失われずに元どおりになれば成功です。終わったら、上のファイルウィンドウ（⟳）ものぞいてみてください！</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ndata = [\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\n# def to_csv(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# def from_csv(text)\n#   ...  # ヒント: row[\"hours\"].to_f\n# end\n\n# File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\n# from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))\n",
            "hints": [
              "上のデモに<code>CSV.generate</code>と<code>headers: true</code>つきの<code>CSV.parse</code>があるよ。それをもとに2つのメソッドを作ってね。書き出して、読み戻して、最後の行は<code>from_csv</code>の結果にしておくんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "File.exist?(\"entries.csv\") && from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\")) == data && to_csv(data).lines.first.strip == \"project,hours\"",
            "hint": "<code>to_csv</code>と<code>from_csv</code>はデモと同じ要領で書けるよ。そのあと<code>File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))</code>で保存して、最後の行を<code>from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))</code>にしてね。",
            "solution": "require \"csv\"\n\ndef to_csv(entries)\n  CSV.generate do |csv|\n    csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n    entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\n  end\nend\n\ndef from_csv(text)\n  CSV.parse(text, headers: true).map { |r| { project: r[\"project\"], hours: r[\"hours\"].to_f } }\nend\n\ndata = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }]\nFile.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\nfrom_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Enregistrer des données : les formats",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog devient persistant</h2><p>Jusqu'ici, nos saisies ne vivent qu'en mémoire. Place à la <strong>sérialisation</strong> : transformer des données en texte, et inversement. Ruby fournit les trois formats les plus importants. <strong>JSON</strong> est la langue des API web :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"json\"\n\nentries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\ntext = JSON.pretty_generate(entries)\nputs text\nJSON.parse(text, symbolize_names: true) == entries"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Remarquez <code>symbolize_names: true</code> – JSON n'a pas de symboles, les clés reviendraient donc sinon sous forme de chaînes. <strong>CSV</strong> est le format des tableaux (Excel !) :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ncsv_text = CSV.generate do |csv|\n  csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n  entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\nend\nputs csv_text\n\nCSV.parse(csv_text, headers: true).map { |row| row[\"project\"] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Et <strong>YAML</strong> est le favori des fichiers de configuration – agréable à lire et à écrire pour un humain :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"yaml\"\n\nconfig = YAML.safe_load(\"rate: 120\\nround_to: 15\\n\")\nconfig[\"rate\"]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Et maintenant : les <strong>fichiers</strong> ! Sur votre machine, Ruby enregistre avec <code>File.write</code> et lit avec <code>File.read</code>. Ici, dans le navigateur, nous simulons pour cela un petit système de fichiers – avec sa propre fenêtre de fichiers (<code>show_files</code>) :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "File.write(\"notizen.txt\", \"buy bacon!\\ntest timelog.\")\nFile.write(\"projekte/plan.txt\", \"Q4: finish timelog\")\n\nshow_files"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Cliquez sur un fichier pour voir son contenu – exactement comme dans l'Explorateur ou le Finder. Et tout le reste fonctionne comme d'habitude : lire, chercher, vérifier :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "[File.read(\"notizen.txt\"),\n Dir.glob(\"**/*.txt\"),\n File.exist?(\"beispiel.csv\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><p>Notre fenêtre de fichiers est une simulation – sur votre machine, ce sont de vrais fichiers sur le disque, avec les mêmes commandes. Vous y disposez en plus de quelques commodités :</p><pre><code># Block form closes the file automatically:\nFile.open(\"log.txt\", \"a\") { |f| f.puts \"new entry\" }\n\nPathname.new(\"a/b.json\")    # paths as objects\nTempfile.create(\"test\")     # throwaway files for tests\nStringIO.new(\"...\")         # in-memory \"file\"</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/JSON.html' target='_blank'>JSON</a> · <a href='https://ruby.github.io/csv/' target='_blank'>CSV</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Psych.html' target='_blank'>YAML (Psych)</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/File.html' target='_blank'>File</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>to_csv(entries)</code> (un texte CSV avec l'en-tête <code>project,hours</code>) et <code>from_csv(text)</code> (retour à des hashes avec des clés symboles et des heures en <code>Float</code>). Enregistrez ensuite <code>data</code> avec <code>File.write</code> dans <code>entries.csv</code> et relisez-le avec <code>from_csv(File.read(…))</code> – sans perte. Jetez ensuite un œil à la fenêtre de fichiers ci-dessus (⟳) !</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ndata = [\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\n# def to_csv(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# def from_csv(text)\n#   ...  # indice : row[\"hours\"].to_f\n# end\n\n# File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\n# from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))\n",
            "hints": [
              "Les démos ci-dessus montrent <code>CSV.generate</code> et <code>CSV.parse</code> avec <code>headers: true</code> – construisez les deux méthodes à partir de là. Écrivez ensuite, relisez, et laissez le résultat de <code>from_csv</code> en dernière ligne."
            ],
            "check": "File.exist?(\"entries.csv\") && from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\")) == data && to_csv(data).lines.first.strip == \"project,hours\"",
            "hint": "<code>to_csv</code>/<code>from_csv</code> comme dans les démos, puis <code>File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))</code> et en dernière ligne <code>from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"csv\"\n\ndef to_csv(entries)\n  CSV.generate do |csv|\n    csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n    entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\n  end\nend\n\ndef from_csv(text)\n  CSV.parse(text, headers: true).map { |r| { project: r[\"project\"], hours: r[\"hours\"].to_f } }\nend\n\ndata = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }]\nFile.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\nfrom_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Salvare i dati: formati",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog diventa persistente</h2><p>Finora le nostre voci vivono solo in memoria. È il momento della <strong>serializzazione</strong>: trasformare i dati in testo e viceversa. Ruby include i tre formati più importanti. <strong>JSON</strong> è la lingua delle API web:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"json\"\n\nentries = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\ntext = JSON.pretty_generate(entries)\nputs text\nJSON.parse(text, symbolize_names: true) == entries"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Nota <code>symbolize_names: true</code> – JSON non ha simboli, quindi altrimenti le chiavi tornerebbero come stringhe. <strong>CSV</strong> è il formato per le tabelle (Excel!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ncsv_text = CSV.generate do |csv|\n  csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n  entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\nend\nputs csv_text\n\nCSV.parse(csv_text, headers: true).map { |row| row[\"project\"] }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>E <strong>YAML</strong> è il preferito per i file di configurazione – piacevole da leggere e scrivere per gli esseri umani:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"yaml\"\n\nconfig = YAML.safe_load(\"rate: 120\\nround_to: 15\\n\")\nconfig[\"rate\"]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>E ora: i <strong>file</strong>! Sul tuo computer Ruby salva con <code>File.write</code> e legge con <code>File.read</code>. Qui nel browser simuliamo a questo scopo un piccolo file system – con una sua finestra dei file (<code>show_files</code>):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "File.write(\"notizen.txt\", \"buy bacon!\\ntest timelog.\")\nFile.write(\"projekte/plan.txt\", \"Q4: finish timelog\")\n\nshow_files"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Clicca su un file per sbirciarci dentro – proprio come in Esplora risorse o nel Finder. E tutto il resto funziona come al solito: leggere, cercare, verificare:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "[File.read(\"notizen.txt\"),\n Dir.glob(\"**/*.txt\"),\n File.exist?(\"beispiel.csv\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><p>La nostra finestra dei file è una simulazione – sul tuo computer sono file veri sul disco, con gli stessi comandi. Lì hai anche qualche comodità in più:</p><pre><code># Block form closes the file automatically:\nFile.open(\"log.txt\", \"a\") { |f| f.puts \"new entry\" }\n\nPathname.new(\"a/b.json\")    # paths as objects\nTempfile.create(\"test\")     # throwaway files for tests\nStringIO.new(\"...\")         # in-memory \"file\"</code></pre></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/JSON.html' target='_blank'>JSON</a> · <a href='https://ruby.github.io/csv/' target='_blank'>CSV</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Psych.html' target='_blank'>YAML (Psych)</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/File.html' target='_blank'>File</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>to_csv(entries)</code> (testo CSV con l'intestazione <code>project,hours</code>) e <code>from_csv(text)</code> (di nuovo in hash con chiavi simbolo e ore come <code>Float</code>). Poi salva <code>data</code> con <code>File.write</code> in <code>entries.csv</code> e rileggilo con <code>from_csv(File.read(…))</code> – senza perdite. Dopo dai un'occhiata alla finestra dei file qui sopra (⟳)!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"csv\"\n\ndata = [\n  { project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 },\n  { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }\n]\n\n# def to_csv(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# def from_csv(text)\n#   ...  # suggerimento: row[\"hours\"].to_f\n# end\n\n# File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\n# from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))\n",
            "hints": [
              "Le dimostrazioni qui sopra mostrano <code>CSV.generate</code> e <code>CSV.parse</code> con <code>headers: true</code> – costruisci i due metodi a partire da lì. Poi scrivi, rileggi e lascia come ultima riga il risultato di <code>from_csv</code>."
            ],
            "check": "File.exist?(\"entries.csv\") && from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\")) == data && to_csv(data).lines.first.strip == \"project,hours\"",
            "hint": "<code>to_csv</code>/<code>from_csv</code> come nelle dimostrazioni, poi <code>File.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))</code> e come ultima riga <code>from_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))</code>.",
            "solution": "require \"csv\"\n\ndef to_csv(entries)\n  CSV.generate do |csv|\n    csv << [\"project\", \"hours\"]\n    entries.each { |e| csv << [e[:project], e[:hours]] }\n  end\nend\n\ndef from_csv(text)\n  CSV.parse(text, headers: true).map { |r| { project: r[\"project\"], hours: r[\"hours\"].to_f } }\nend\n\ndata = [{ project: \"A\", hours: 1.5 }, { project: \"B\", hours: 2.0 }]\nFile.write(\"entries.csv\", to_csv(data))\nfrom_csv(File.read(\"entries.csv\"))"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-cli",
      "de": {
        "title": "Kommandozeile & Gems",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog als richtiges Werkzeug</h2><p>Auf deinem Rechner startet man Programme im Terminal: <code>timelog add \"ProjectX\" --from 08:30</code>. Alles hinter dem Programmnamen landet als String-Array in <code>ARGV</code>. Für das saubere Zerlegen gibt es <strong>OptionParser</strong> aus der Standardbibliothek – hier üben wir mit selbstgebauten Arrays:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\nargv = [\"report\", \"--week\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]\n\noptionen = { format: \"text\", woche: false }\nparser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n  p.banner = \"Aufruf: timelog KOMMANDO [Optionen]\"\n  p.on(\"--week\", \"nur diese Woche\")        { optionen[:woche] = true }\n  p.on(\"--format FORMAT\", \"text oder csv\") { |f| optionen[:format] = f }\nend\n\nrest = parser.parse(argv)\n[optionen, rest]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>parse</code> pflückt die Optionen heraus und gibt zurück, was übrig bleibt – hier das Kommando <code>\"report\"</code>. Gratis dazu gibt es eine Hilfe, gebaut aus den Beschreibungstexten: Ruft jemand <code>timelog --help</code> auf, zeigt OptionParser genau das hier an und beendet das Programm:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts parser.help"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer: vom Skript zur Gem'><p>Ein installierbares Werkzeug hat eine feste Struktur und eine <code>.gemspec</code>-Datei:</p><pre><code>timelog/\n├── lib/timelog.rb        # der Code\n├── bin/timelog           # das Kommando (#!/usr/bin/env ruby)\n├── test/test_timelog.rb\n├── timelog.gemspec       # Name, Version, Autor, Dateien\n├── Gemfile               # Abhaengigkeiten (Bundler)\n└── Rakefile              # Aufgaben: rake test</code></pre><pre><code>$ bundle install          # holt Abhaengigkeiten, schreibt Gemfile.lock\n$ rake test               # laesst die Tests laufen\n$ gem build timelog.gemspec\n$ gem install timelog-0.1.0.gem\n$ timelog report --week   # dein Werkzeug, ueberall!</code></pre><p>Exit-Codes nicht vergessen: <code>exit 1</code> bei Fehlern, damit Skripte deine Fehlschläge bemerken.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/OptionParser.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser-Tutorial</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>parse_argv(argv)</code> mit OptionParser: Unterstützt werden <code>--week</code> und <code>--format FORMAT</code> (Default <code>\"text\"</code>). Zurück kommt ein Hash <code>{ befehl:, woche:, format: }</code>, wobei <code>befehl</code> das erste übrige Argument ist.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\n# def parse_argv(argv)\n#   optionen = { woche: false, format: \"text\" }\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Lege wie in der Demo einen <code>OptionParser</code> an, mit einem <code>on</code> pro Option, das in <code>optionen</code> schreibt. <code>parse</code> gibt dir die übrigen Argumente – das erste davon ist der Befehl."
            ],
            "check": "a = parse_argv([\"report\", \"--week\"]); b = parse_argv([\"export\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]); c2 = parse_argv([\"add\"]); a == { befehl: \"report\", woche: true, format: \"text\" } && b == { befehl: \"export\", woche: false, format: \"csv\" } && c2 == { befehl: \"add\", woche: false, format: \"text\" }",
            "hint": "Wie in der Demo – am Ende: <code>rest = parser.parse(argv); { befehl: rest.first, woche: optionen[:woche], format: optionen[:format] }</code>",
            "solution": "require \"optparse\"\n\ndef parse_argv(argv)\n  optionen = { woche: false, format: \"text\" }\n  parser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n    p.on(\"--week\") { optionen[:woche] = true }\n    p.on(\"--format FORMAT\") { |f| optionen[:format] = f }\n  end\n  rest = parser.parse(argv)\n  { befehl: rest.first, woche: optionen[:woche], format: optionen[:format] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Command line & gems",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog as a real tool</h2><p>On your machine, programs start in the terminal: <code>timelog add \"ProjectX\" --from 08:30</code>. Everything after the program name arrives as a string array in <code>ARGV</code>. For clean parsing there's <strong>OptionParser</strong> from the standard library – here we practice with hand-built arrays:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\nargv = [\"report\", \"--week\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]\n\noptions = { format: \"text\", week: false }\nparser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n  p.banner = \"Usage: timelog COMMAND [options]\"\n  p.on(\"--week\", \"this week only\")        { options[:week] = true }\n  p.on(\"--format FORMAT\", \"text or csv\")  { |f| options[:format] = f }\nend\n\nrest = parser.parse(argv)\n[options, rest]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>parse</code> plucks out the options and returns what remains – here the command <code>\"report\"</code>. On top of that you get help for free, built from the descriptions: if someone runs <code>timelog --help</code>, OptionParser prints exactly this and ends the program:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts parser.help"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine: from script to gem'><p>An installable tool has a fixed structure and a <code>.gemspec</code> file:</p><pre><code>timelog/\n├── lib/timelog.rb        # the code\n├── bin/timelog           # the command (#!/usr/bin/env ruby)\n├── test/test_timelog.rb\n├── timelog.gemspec       # name, version, author, files\n├── Gemfile               # dependencies (Bundler)\n└── Rakefile              # tasks: rake test</code></pre><pre><code>$ bundle install          # fetches deps, writes Gemfile.lock\n$ rake test               # runs the tests\n$ gem build timelog.gemspec\n$ gem install timelog-0.1.0.gem\n$ timelog report --week   # your tool, everywhere!</code></pre><p>Don't forget exit codes: <code>exit 1</code> on failure, so scripts notice your errors.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/OptionParser.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser tutorial</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>parse_argv(argv)</code> with OptionParser: it supports <code>--week</code> and <code>--format FORMAT</code> (default <code>\"text\"</code>). It returns a hash <code>{ command:, week:, format: }</code>, where <code>command</code> is the first remaining argument.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\n# def parse_argv(argv)\n#   options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Create an <code>OptionParser</code> as in the demo, with one <code>on</code> per option that writes into <code>options</code>. <code>parse</code> gives you the remaining arguments – the first of them is the command."
            ],
            "check": "a = parse_argv([\"report\", \"--week\"]); b = parse_argv([\"export\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]); c2 = parse_argv([\"add\"]); a == { command: \"report\", week: true, format: \"text\" } && b == { command: \"export\", week: false, format: \"csv\" } && c2 == { command: \"add\", week: false, format: \"text\" }",
            "hint": "Like the demo – at the end: <code>rest = parser.parse(argv); { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }</code>",
            "solution": "require \"optparse\"\n\ndef parse_argv(argv)\n  options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n  parser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n    p.on(\"--week\") { options[:week] = true }\n    p.on(\"--format FORMAT\") { |f| options[:format] = f }\n  end\n  rest = parser.parse(argv)\n  { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "コマンドラインとgem",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelogを本物のツールに</h2><p>自分のコンピューターでは、プログラムはターミナルから起動します：<code>timelog add \"ProjectX\" --from 08:30</code>。プログラム名のあとに書いたものは、すべて文字列の配列として<code>ARGV</code>に入ります。これをきれいに解析するには、標準ライブラリの<strong>OptionParser</strong>が便利です。ここでは、自分で用意した配列で練習しましょう：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\nargv = [\"report\", \"--week\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]\n\noptions = { format: \"text\", week: false }\nparser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n  p.banner = \"Usage: timelog COMMAND [options]\"\n  p.on(\"--week\", \"this week only\")        { options[:week] = true }\n  p.on(\"--format FORMAT\", \"text or csv\")  { |f| options[:format] = f }\nend\n\nrest = parser.parse(argv)\n[options, rest]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>parse</code>はオプションを抜き出して、残ったものを返します。ここではコマンドの<code>\"report\"</code>です。おまけに、説明文からヘルプも自動で作られます。だれかが<code>timelog --help</code>と実行すると、OptionParserはちょうど次のように表示して、プログラムを終了します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts parser.help"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは：スクリプトからgemへ'><p>インストールできるツールには、決まったフォルダー構成と<code>.gemspec</code>ファイルがあります：</p><pre><code>timelog/\n├── lib/timelog.rb        # the code\n├── bin/timelog           # the command (#!/usr/bin/env ruby)\n├── test/test_timelog.rb\n├── timelog.gemspec       # name, version, author, files\n├── Gemfile               # dependencies (Bundler)\n└── Rakefile              # tasks: rake test</code></pre><pre><code>$ bundle install          # fetches deps, writes Gemfile.lock\n$ rake test               # runs the tests\n$ gem build timelog.gemspec\n$ gem install timelog-0.1.0.gem\n$ timelog report --week   # your tool, everywhere!</code></pre><p>終了コードも忘れずに。失敗したときは<code>exit 1</code>で終わらせれば、ツールを呼び出したスクリプトがエラーに気づけます。</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/OptionParser.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>OptionParserチュートリアル（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>OptionParserを使って<code>parse_argv(argv)</code>を書きましょう。対応するのは<code>--week</code>と<code>--format FORMAT</code>（デフォルトは<code>\"text\"</code>）です。戻り値はハッシュ<code>{ command:, week:, format: }</code>で、<code>command</code>には残った引数の最初のものが入ります。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\n# def parse_argv(argv)\n#   options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "デモみたいに<code>OptionParser</code>を作って、オプションごとに<code>on</code>を1つ書いて<code>options</code>に入れてね。<code>parse</code>は残りの引数を返すから、その最初がコマンドだよ。"
            ],
            "check": "a = parse_argv([\"report\", \"--week\"]); b = parse_argv([\"export\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]); c2 = parse_argv([\"add\"]); a == { command: \"report\", week: true, format: \"text\" } && b == { command: \"export\", week: false, format: \"csv\" } && c2 == { command: \"add\", week: false, format: \"text\" }",
            "hint": "デモと同じやり方でいいよ。最後はこうしよう：<code>rest = parser.parse(argv); { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }</code>",
            "solution": "require \"optparse\"\n\ndef parse_argv(argv)\n  options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n  parser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n    p.on(\"--week\") { options[:week] = true }\n    p.on(\"--format FORMAT\") { |f| options[:format] = f }\n  end\n  rest = parser.parse(argv)\n  { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Ligne de commande et gems",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog, un vrai outil</h2><p>Sur votre machine, les programmes se lancent dans le terminal : <code>timelog add \"ProjectX\" --from 08:30</code>. Tout ce qui suit le nom du programme arrive sous forme d'array de chaînes dans <code>ARGV</code>. Pour une analyse propre, il y a <strong>OptionParser</strong> dans la bibliothèque standard – ici, nous nous exerçons avec des arrays construits à la main :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\nargv = [\"report\", \"--week\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]\n\noptions = { format: \"text\", week: false }\nparser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n  p.banner = \"Usage: timelog COMMAND [options]\"\n  p.on(\"--week\", \"this week only\")        { options[:week] = true }\n  p.on(\"--format FORMAT\", \"text or csv\")  { |f| options[:format] = f }\nend\n\nrest = parser.parse(argv)\n[options, rest]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>parse</code> extrait les options et renvoie ce qui reste – ici la commande <code>\"report\"</code>. En prime, vous obtenez gratuitement une aide, construite à partir des descriptions : si quelqu'un lance <code>timelog --help</code>, OptionParser affiche exactement ceci et termine le programme :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts parser.help"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine : du script à la gem'><p>Un outil installable a une structure fixe et un fichier <code>.gemspec</code> :</p><pre><code>timelog/\n├── lib/timelog.rb        # the code\n├── bin/timelog           # the command (#!/usr/bin/env ruby)\n├── test/test_timelog.rb\n├── timelog.gemspec       # name, version, author, files\n├── Gemfile               # dependencies (Bundler)\n└── Rakefile              # tasks: rake test</code></pre><pre><code>$ bundle install          # fetches deps, writes Gemfile.lock\n$ rake test               # runs the tests\n$ gem build timelog.gemspec\n$ gem install timelog-0.1.0.gem\n$ timelog report --week   # your tool, everywhere!</code></pre><p>N'oubliez pas les codes de sortie : <code>exit 1</code> en cas d'échec, pour que les scripts remarquent vos erreurs.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/OptionParser.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Tutoriel OptionParser</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>parse_argv(argv)</code> avec OptionParser : elle prend en charge <code>--week</code> et <code>--format FORMAT</code> (par défaut <code>\"text\"</code>). Elle renvoie un hash <code>{ command:, week:, format: }</code>, où <code>command</code> est le premier argument restant.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\n# def parse_argv(argv)\n#   options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Créez un <code>OptionParser</code> comme dans la démo, avec un <code>on</code> par option, qui écrit dans <code>options</code>. <code>parse</code> vous renvoie les arguments restants – le premier d'entre eux est la commande."
            ],
            "check": "a = parse_argv([\"report\", \"--week\"]); b = parse_argv([\"export\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]); c2 = parse_argv([\"add\"]); a == { command: \"report\", week: true, format: \"text\" } && b == { command: \"export\", week: false, format: \"csv\" } && c2 == { command: \"add\", week: false, format: \"text\" }",
            "hint": "Comme dans la démo – à la fin : <code>rest = parser.parse(argv); { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }</code>",
            "solution": "require \"optparse\"\n\ndef parse_argv(argv)\n  options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n  parser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n    p.on(\"--week\") { options[:week] = true }\n    p.on(\"--format FORMAT\") { |f| options[:format] = f }\n  end\n  rest = parser.parse(argv)\n  { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Riga di comando e gem",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>timelog come strumento vero</h2><p>Sul tuo computer i programmi si avviano nel terminale: <code>timelog add \"ProjectX\" --from 08:30</code>. Tutto ciò che segue il nome del programma arriva come array di stringhe in <code>ARGV</code>. Per un'analisi pulita c'è <strong>OptionParser</strong> della libreria standard – qui ci esercitiamo con array costruiti a mano:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\nargv = [\"report\", \"--week\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]\n\noptions = { format: \"text\", week: false }\nparser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n  p.banner = \"Usage: timelog COMMAND [options]\"\n  p.on(\"--week\", \"this week only\")        { options[:week] = true }\n  p.on(\"--format FORMAT\", \"text or csv\")  { |f| options[:format] = f }\nend\n\nrest = parser.parse(argv)\n[options, rest]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>parse</code> estrae le opzioni e restituisce ciò che resta – qui il comando <code>\"report\"</code>. In più ottieni gratis un aiuto, costruito dalle descrizioni: se qualcuno esegue <code>timelog --help</code>, OptionParser stampa esattamente questo e termina il programma:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "puts parser.help"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer: dallo script alla gem'><p>Uno strumento installabile ha una struttura fissa e un file <code>.gemspec</code>:</p><pre><code>timelog/\n├── lib/timelog.rb        # the code\n├── bin/timelog           # the command (#!/usr/bin/env ruby)\n├── test/test_timelog.rb\n├── timelog.gemspec       # name, version, author, files\n├── Gemfile               # dependencies (Bundler)\n└── Rakefile              # tasks: rake test</code></pre><pre><code>$ bundle install          # fetches deps, writes Gemfile.lock\n$ rake test               # runs the tests\n$ gem build timelog.gemspec\n$ gem install timelog-0.1.0.gem\n$ timelog report --week   # your tool, everywhere!</code></pre><p>Non dimenticare i codici di uscita: <code>exit 1</code> in caso di errore, così gli script si accorgono dei tuoi errori.</p></div><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/OptionParser.html' target='_blank'>OptionParser</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/optparse/tutorial_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Tutorial di OptionParser</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>parse_argv(argv)</code> con OptionParser: supporta <code>--week</code> e <code>--format FORMAT</code> (predefinito <code>\"text\"</code>). Restituisce un hash <code>{ command:, week:, format: }</code>, dove <code>command</code> è il primo argomento rimasto.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "require \"optparse\"\n\n# def parse_argv(argv)\n#   options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n#   ...\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Crea un <code>OptionParser</code> come nella dimostrazione, con un <code>on</code> per ogni opzione che scrive in <code>options</code>. <code>parse</code> ti dà gli argomenti rimasti – il primo è il comando."
            ],
            "check": "a = parse_argv([\"report\", \"--week\"]); b = parse_argv([\"export\", \"--format\", \"csv\"]); c2 = parse_argv([\"add\"]); a == { command: \"report\", week: true, format: \"text\" } && b == { command: \"export\", week: false, format: \"csv\" } && c2 == { command: \"add\", week: false, format: \"text\" }",
            "hint": "Come nella dimostrazione – alla fine: <code>rest = parser.parse(argv); { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }</code>",
            "solution": "require \"optparse\"\n\ndef parse_argv(argv)\n  options = { week: false, format: \"text\" }\n  parser = OptionParser.new do |p|\n    p.on(\"--week\") { options[:week] = true }\n    p.on(\"--format FORMAT\") { |f| options[:format] = f }\n  end\n  rest = parser.parse(argv)\n  { command: rest.first, week: options[:week], format: options[:format] }\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-pattern",
      "de": {
        "title": "Pattern Matching",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Modernes Ruby: case/in</h2><p>Seit Ruby 3 gibt es neben <code>case/when</code> das mächtigere <strong>Pattern Matching</strong> mit <code>case/in</code>: Es prüft die <em>Form</em> von Daten und zerlegt sie im selben Schritt. Perfekt für unser Kommando-Array aus der CLI-Lektion:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "befehl = [\"add\", \"ProjectX\", 3.5]\n\ncase befehl\nin [\"add\", projekt, stunden]\n  \"Neuer Eintrag: #{projekt} (#{stunden}h)\"\nin [\"report\"]\n  \"Bericht wird erstellt\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Das Muster <code>[\"add\", projekt, stunden]</code> passt nur auf dreielementige Arrays mit <code>\"add\"</code> vorn – und bindet die restlichen Werte gleich an Variablen. Auch Hashes lassen sich zerlegen, mit Typ-Prüfung und Wächter-Bedingung (<code>if</code>) obendrauf:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintrag = { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 }\n\ncase eintrag\nin { projekt: String => p, stunden: Float => s } if s > 0\n  \"#{p}: #{s}h - sieht gut aus\"\nin { stunden: }\n  \"Ungueltige Stunden: #{stunden.inspect}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Ein Muster kann auch einfach Ja oder Nein sagen: <code>wert in muster</code> ergibt <code>true</code> oder <code>false</code> – praktisch in einem <code>if</code>. Mit <code>[*, \"meeting\", *]</code>, dem <em>Find-Pattern</em>, suchst du ein Element irgendwo in einem Array. Und <code>=&gt;</code> zerlegt einen Wert ganz ohne <code>case</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "eintrag = { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5, tags: [\"planung\", \"meeting\"] }\n\nif eintrag in { stunden: Float => stunden }\n  puts \"#{stunden} Stunden, als Float\"\nend\nputs \"ein Meeting!\" if eintrag in { tags: [*, \"meeting\", *] }\n\neintrag => { projekt:, tags: [erster_tag, *] }\n[projekt, erster_tag]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dazu passt der Rest des modernen Rubys: <em>endless methods</em> für Einzeiler (<code>def quadrat(x) = x * x</code>) und die Hash-Kurzform <code>{ projekt:, stunden: }</code>, wenn Variable und Schlüssel gleich heissen.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/pattern_matching_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Pattern Matching</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Schreibe <code>dispatch(befehl)</code> mit <code>case/in</code>: <code>[\"add\", projekt, stunden]</code> → <code>\"Eintrag: &lt;projekt&gt; (&lt;stunden&gt;h)\"</code>, <code>[\"report\"]</code> → <code>\"Bericht\"</code>, <code>[\"export\", format]</code> → <code>\"Export als &lt;format&gt;\"</code>, alles andere (<code>else</code>) → <code>\"Unbekanntes Kommando\"</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def dispatch(befehl)\n#   case befehl\n#   in ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Ein <code>case befehl</code> mit vier Zweigen: drei <code>in</code>-Muster für Arrays – die Variablen darin fängst du gleich im Muster ein – und ein <code>else</code> für alles andere."
            ],
            "check": "dispatch([\"add\", \"X\", 2.5]) == \"Eintrag: X (2.5h)\" && dispatch([\"report\"]) == \"Bericht\" && dispatch([\"export\", \"csv\"]) == \"Export als csv\" && dispatch([\"tanzen\"]) == \"Unbekanntes Kommando\" && code.include?(\"in [\")",
            "hint": "Vier Zweige: <code>in [\"add\", projekt, stunden]</code>, <code>in [\"report\"]</code>, <code>in [\"export\", format]</code>, <code>else</code>.",
            "solution": "def dispatch(befehl)\n  case befehl\n  in [\"add\", projekt, stunden]\n    \"Eintrag: #{projekt} (#{stunden}h)\"\n  in [\"report\"]\n    \"Bericht\"\n  in [\"export\", format]\n    \"Export als #{format}\"\n  else\n    \"Unbekanntes Kommando\"\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Pattern matching",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Modern Ruby: case/in</h2><p>Since Ruby 3 there is, next to <code>case/when</code>, the more powerful <strong>pattern matching</strong> with <code>case/in</code>: it checks the <em>shape</em> of data and takes it apart in the same step. Perfect for our command array from the CLI lesson:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "command = [\"add\", \"ProjectX\", 3.5]\n\ncase command\nin [\"add\", project, hours]\n  \"New entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\nin [\"report\"]\n  \"Generating report\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The pattern <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> only matches three-element arrays starting with <code>\"add\"</code> – and binds the remaining values to variables right away. Hashes deconstruct too, with type checks and a guard condition (<code>if</code>) on top:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\n\ncase entry\nin { project: String => p, hours: Float => h } if h > 0\n  \"#{p}: #{h}h - looks good\"\nin { hours: }\n  \"Invalid hours: #{hours.inspect}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>A pattern can also just say yes or no: <code>value in pattern</code> gives <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> – handy inside an <code>if</code>. With <code>[*, \"meeting\", *]</code>, the <em>find pattern</em>, you look for an element anywhere in an array. And <code>=&gt;</code> takes a value apart without any <code>case</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5, tags: [\"planning\", \"meeting\"] }\n\nif entry in { hours: Float => hours }\n  puts \"#{hours} hours, as a Float\"\nend\nputs \"a meeting!\" if entry in { tags: [*, \"meeting\", *] }\n\nentry => { project:, tags: [first_tag, *] }\n[project, first_tag]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>The rest of modern Ruby fits right in: <em>endless methods</em> for one-liners (<code>def square(x) = x * x</code>) and the hash shorthand <code>{ project:, hours: }</code> when variable and key share a name.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/pattern_matching_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Pattern matching</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Write <code>dispatch(command)</code> with <code>case/in</code>: <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> → <code>\"Entry: &lt;project&gt; (&lt;hours&gt;h)\"</code>, <code>[\"report\"]</code> → <code>\"Report\"</code>, <code>[\"export\", format]</code> → <code>\"Export as &lt;format&gt;\"</code>, anything else (<code>else</code>) → <code>\"Unknown command\"</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def dispatch(command)\n#   case command\n#   in ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "A <code>case command</code> with four branches: three <code>in</code> patterns for arrays – you capture the variables right inside the pattern – and an <code>else</code> for everything else."
            ],
            "check": "dispatch([\"add\", \"X\", 2.5]) == \"Entry: X (2.5h)\" && dispatch([\"report\"]) == \"Report\" && dispatch([\"export\", \"csv\"]) == \"Export as csv\" && dispatch([\"dance\"]) == \"Unknown command\" && code.include?(\"in [\")",
            "hint": "Four branches: <code>in [\"add\", project, hours]</code>, <code>in [\"report\"]</code>, <code>in [\"export\", format]</code>, <code>else</code>.",
            "solution": "def dispatch(command)\n  case command\n  in [\"add\", project, hours]\n    \"Entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\n  in [\"report\"]\n    \"Report\"\n  in [\"export\", format]\n    \"Export as #{format}\"\n  else\n    \"Unknown command\"\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "パターンマッチ",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>モダンなRuby：case/in</h2><p>Ruby 3からは、<code>case/when</code>に加えて、もっと強力な<strong>パターンマッチ</strong>が<code>case/in</code>で使えます。データの<em>形</em>を調べて、同時にそれを分解してくれる仕組みです。コマンドラインのレッスンで出てきたコマンドの配列にぴったりです：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "command = [\"add\", \"ProjectX\", 3.5]\n\ncase command\nin [\"add\", project, hours]\n  \"New entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\nin [\"report\"]\n  \"Generating report\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>パターン<code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code>は、<code>\"add\"</code>で始まる要素3つの配列にだけマッチし、残りの値をその場で変数に入れてくれます。ハッシュも同じように分解できます。型のチェックやガード条件（<code>if</code>）を付けることもできます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\n\ncase entry\nin { project: String => p, hours: Float => h } if h > 0\n  \"#{p}: #{h}h - looks good\"\nin { hours: }\n  \"Invalid hours: #{hours.inspect}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>パターンは、はい・いいえを答えるだけにも使えます。<code>値 in パターン</code>は<code>true</code>か<code>false</code>になるので、<code>if</code>の中で便利です。<code>[*, \"meeting\", *]</code>という<em>findパターン</em>を使えば、配列のどこかにある要素を探せます。そして<code>=&gt;</code>を使えば、<code>case</code>なしで値を分解できます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5, tags: [\"planning\", \"meeting\"] }\n\nif entry in { hours: Float => hours }\n  puts \"#{hours} hours, as a Float\"\nend\nputs \"a meeting!\" if entry in { tags: [*, \"meeting\", *] }\n\nentry => { project:, tags: [first_tag, *] }\n[project, first_tag]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>モダンなRubyのほかの機能も、これとよくなじみます。1行で書ける<em>エンドレスメソッド</em>（<code>def square(x) = x * x</code>）、そして変数とキーが同じ名前のときのハッシュの省略記法<code>{ project:, hours: }</code>です。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/doc/spec=2fpattern_matching.html' target='_blank'>パターンマッチ</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong><code>case/in</code>を使って、次のように値を返す<code>dispatch(command)</code>を書きましょう：<code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> → <code>\"Entry: &lt;project&gt; (&lt;hours&gt;h)\"</code>、<code>[\"report\"]</code> → <code>\"Report\"</code>、<code>[\"export\", format]</code> → <code>\"Export as &lt;format&gt;\"</code>、それ以外（<code>else</code>）→ <code>\"Unknown command\"</code>。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def dispatch(command)\n#   case command\n#   in ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>case command</code>に4つの分岐だよ。配列の<code>in</code>パターンが3つで、変数はパターンの中でそのまま受け取れるよ。あとは<code>else</code>でそれ以外を受けるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "dispatch([\"add\", \"X\", 2.5]) == \"Entry: X (2.5h)\" && dispatch([\"report\"]) == \"Report\" && dispatch([\"export\", \"csv\"]) == \"Export as csv\" && dispatch([\"dance\"]) == \"Unknown command\" && code.include?(\"in [\")",
            "hint": "分岐は4つだよ：<code>in [\"add\", project, hours]</code>、<code>in [\"report\"]</code>、<code>in [\"export\", format]</code>、そして<code>else</code>。",
            "solution": "def dispatch(command)\n  case command\n  in [\"add\", project, hours]\n    \"Entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\n  in [\"report\"]\n    \"Report\"\n  in [\"export\", format]\n    \"Export as #{format}\"\n  else\n    \"Unknown command\"\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Le pattern matching",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Ruby moderne : case/in</h2><p>Depuis Ruby 3, il existe, à côté de <code>case/when</code>, le <strong>pattern matching</strong> (filtrage par motif), plus puissant, avec <code>case/in</code> : il vérifie la <em>forme</em> des données et les décompose dans la même étape. Parfait pour notre array de commandes de la leçon sur la ligne de commande :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "command = [\"add\", \"ProjectX\", 3.5]\n\ncase command\nin [\"add\", project, hours]\n  \"New entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\nin [\"report\"]\n  \"Generating report\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le motif <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> ne correspond qu'aux arrays de trois éléments commençant par <code>\"add\"</code> – et lie aussitôt les valeurs restantes à des variables. Les hashes se décomposent aussi, avec en prime des vérifications de type et une condition de garde (<code>if</code>) :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\n\ncase entry\nin { project: String => p, hours: Float => h } if h > 0\n  \"#{p}: #{h}h - looks good\"\nin { hours: }\n  \"Invalid hours: #{hours.inspect}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Un motif peut aussi se contenter de répondre oui ou non : <code>value in pattern</code> donne <code>true</code> ou <code>false</code> – pratique dans un <code>if</code>. Avec <code>[*, \"meeting\", *]</code>, le <em>find pattern</em>, vous cherchez un élément n'importe où dans un array. Et <code>=&gt;</code> décompose une valeur sans aucun <code>case</code> :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5, tags: [\"planning\", \"meeting\"] }\n\nif entry in { hours: Float => hours }\n  puts \"#{hours} hours, as a Float\"\nend\nputs \"a meeting!\" if entry in { tags: [*, \"meeting\", *] }\n\nentry => { project:, tags: [first_tag, *] }\n[project, first_tag]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Le reste du Ruby moderne s'intègre parfaitement : les <em>endless methods</em> (méthodes sans le mot-clé end) pour les one-liners (<code>def square(x) = x * x</code>) et le raccourci de hash <code>{ project:, hours: }</code> quand la variable et la clé portent le même nom.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/pattern_matching_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Pattern matching</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Écrivez <code>dispatch(command)</code> avec <code>case/in</code> : <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> → <code>\"Entry: &lt;project&gt; (&lt;hours&gt;h)\"</code>, <code>[\"report\"]</code> → <code>\"Report\"</code>, <code>[\"export\", format]</code> → <code>\"Export as &lt;format&gt;\"</code>, tout le reste (<code>else</code>) → <code>\"Unknown command\"</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def dispatch(command)\n#   case command\n#   in ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Un <code>case command</code> avec quatre branches : trois motifs <code>in</code> pour des arrays – vous capturez les variables directement dans le motif – et un <code>else</code> pour tout le reste."
            ],
            "check": "dispatch([\"add\", \"X\", 2.5]) == \"Entry: X (2.5h)\" && dispatch([\"report\"]) == \"Report\" && dispatch([\"export\", \"csv\"]) == \"Export as csv\" && dispatch([\"dance\"]) == \"Unknown command\" && code.include?(\"in [\")",
            "hint": "Quatre branches : <code>in [\"add\", project, hours]</code>, <code>in [\"report\"]</code>, <code>in [\"export\", format]</code>, <code>else</code>.",
            "solution": "def dispatch(command)\n  case command\n  in [\"add\", project, hours]\n    \"Entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\n  in [\"report\"]\n    \"Report\"\n  in [\"export\", format]\n    \"Export as #{format}\"\n  else\n    \"Unknown command\"\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Pattern matching",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Ruby moderno: case/in</h2><p>Da Ruby 3, accanto a <code>case/when</code>, c'è il più potente <strong>pattern matching</strong> con <code>case/in</code>: verifica la <em>forma</em> dei dati e nello stesso passo li scompone. Perfetto per il nostro array di comandi della lezione sulla riga di comando:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "command = [\"add\", \"ProjectX\", 3.5]\n\ncase command\nin [\"add\", project, hours]\n  \"New entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\nin [\"report\"]\n  \"Generating report\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Lo schema <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> corrisponde solo ad array di tre elementi che iniziano con <code>\"add\"</code> – e lega subito i valori restanti a delle variabili. Anche gli hash si possono scomporre, con controlli di tipo e in più una condizione di guardia (<code>if</code>):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 }\n\ncase entry\nin { project: String => p, hours: Float => h } if h > 0\n  \"#{p}: #{h}h - looks good\"\nin { hours: }\n  \"Invalid hours: #{hours.inspect}\"\nend"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Uno schema può anche limitarsi a dire sì o no: <code>value in pattern</code> dà <code>true</code> o <code>false</code> – comodo dentro un <code>if</code>. Con <code>[*, \"meeting\", *]</code>, il <em>find pattern</em>, cerchi un elemento in qualunque punto di un array. E <code>=&gt;</code> scompone un valore senza alcun <code>case</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "entry = { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5, tags: [\"planning\", \"meeting\"] }\n\nif entry in { hours: Float => hours }\n  puts \"#{hours} hours, as a Float\"\nend\nputs \"a meeting!\" if entry in { tags: [*, \"meeting\", *] }\n\nentry => { project:, tags: [first_tag, *] }\n[project, first_tag]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Il resto del Ruby moderno si inserisce alla perfezione: gli <em>endless method</em> per le funzioni di una riga (<code>def square(x) = x * x</code>) e la forma abbreviata degli hash <code>{ project:, hours: }</code> quando variabile e chiave hanno lo stesso nome.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/syntax/pattern_matching_rdoc.html' target='_blank'>Pattern matching</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Scrivi <code>dispatch(command)</code> con <code>case/in</code>: <code>[\"add\", project, hours]</code> → <code>\"Entry: &lt;project&gt; (&lt;hours&gt;h)\"</code>, <code>[\"report\"]</code> → <code>\"Report\"</code>, <code>[\"export\", format]</code> → <code>\"Export as &lt;format&gt;\"</code>, tutto il resto (<code>else</code>) → <code>\"Unknown command\"</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# def dispatch(command)\n#   case command\n#   in ...\n#   end\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Un <code>case command</code> con quattro rami: tre schemi <code>in</code> per gli array – le variabili le catturi direttamente nello schema – e un <code>else</code> per tutto il resto."
            ],
            "check": "dispatch([\"add\", \"X\", 2.5]) == \"Entry: X (2.5h)\" && dispatch([\"report\"]) == \"Report\" && dispatch([\"export\", \"csv\"]) == \"Export as csv\" && dispatch([\"dance\"]) == \"Unknown command\" && code.include?(\"in [\")",
            "hint": "Quattro rami: <code>in [\"add\", project, hours]</code>, <code>in [\"report\"]</code>, <code>in [\"export\", format]</code>, <code>else</code>.",
            "solution": "def dispatch(command)\n  case command\n  in [\"add\", project, hours]\n    \"Entry: #{project} (#{hours}h)\"\n  in [\"report\"]\n    \"Report\"\n  in [\"export\", format]\n    \"Export as #{format}\"\n  else\n    \"Unknown command\"\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-meta",
      "de": {
        "title": "Objektmodell & Metaprogrammierung",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Wie Ruby Methoden findet</h2><p>Wenn du <code>objekt.methode</code> aufrufst, klettert Ruby eine Kette hinauf: erst die Singleton-Klasse des Objekts (dort landen Methoden, die nur <em>dieses</em> Objekt hat), dann seine Klasse, dann die Mixins, dann die Oberklassen. Die Kette kannst du dir anzeigen lassen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "sonderling = \"normaler String\"\n\ndef sonderling.schrei\n  upcase + \"!!!\"\nend\n\n[sonderling.schrei, String.ancestors.first(4)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Weil Klassen selbst Objekte sind, kann Code <em>Code erzeugen</em> – das ist Metaprogrammierung. <code>define_method</code> definiert Methoden zur Laufzeit, <code>send</code> ruft eine Methode auf, deren Name in einer Variablen steckt:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Konfig\n  def initialize(werte)\n    @werte = werte\n  end\n\n  %w[host port sprache].each do |feld|\n    define_method(feld) { @werte[feld] }\n  end\nend\n\nk = Konfig.new({ \"host\" => \"idogawa.com\", \"port\" => 8011 })\n[k.host, k.send(\"port\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Genau so bauen Rails &amp; Co. ihre Makros wie <code>has_many</code> oder <code>validates</code>. Wichtige Warnung eines weisen Fuchses: <em>Der cleverste Code ist oft das Erste, was man später löscht.</em> Es gibt noch einen zweiten Weg: <code>method_missing</code> fängt jeden Aufruf ab, für den Ruby keine Methode findet. Wer <code>method_missing</code> benutzt, schreibt immer auch <code>respond_to_missing?</code> – sonst behauptet <code>respond_to?</code> etwas Falsches – und reicht alles, was die Klasse nicht kennt, mit <code>super</code> weiter:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Einstellungen\n  def initialize(werte)\n    @werte = werte\n  end\n\n  def method_missing(name, *args)\n    schluessel = name.to_s\n    return @werte[schluessel] if @werte.key?(schluessel)\n\n    super\n  end\n\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    @werte.key?(name.to_s) || super\n  end\nend\n\ne = Einstellungen.new(\"thema\" => \"Fuchs\", \"sprache\" => \"de\")\n[e.thema, e.respond_to?(:thema), e.respond_to?(:farbe)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Probier <code>e.farbe</code>: <code>super</code> macht daraus einen ganz normalen <code>NoMethodError</code>. Das funktioniert – aber solche Methoden sieht niemand in der Klasse stehen, keine Doku kennt sie, und ein Tippfehler landet stillschweigend in <code>method_missing</code>. Ein explizites <code>define_method</code> ist darum fast immer die klarere Wahl.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-define_method' target='_blank'>define_method</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-send' target='_blank'>send</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-ancestors' target='_blank'>ancestors</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Baue dein eigenes Rails-Makro: <code>Modell.validates_presence_of(*felder)</code> soll per <code>define_method</code> eine Methode <code>valid?</code> erzeugen, die prüft, dass keines der Felder <code>nil</code> oder <code>\"\"</code> ist. Die Klasse <code>Buchung</code> unten benutzt es dann wie in Rails.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class Modell\n  # def self.validates_presence_of(*felder)\n  #   define_method(:valid?) do\n  #     ...\n  #   end\n  # end\nend\n\nclass Buchung < Modell\n  attr_accessor :projekt, :stunden\n  # validates_presence_of :projekt\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Entferne die <code>#</code> in <code>Modell</code> und <code>Buchung</code>. Im <code>define_method</code>-Block prüfst du mit <code>all?</code> jedes Feld – seinen Wert holst du mit <code>send</code>."
            ],
            "check": "b = Buchung.new; b.projekt = \"X\"; b2 = Buchung.new; b2.projekt = \"\"; b3 = Buchung.new; b.valid? && !b2.valid? && !b3.valid? && code.include?(\"define_method\")",
            "hint": "<code>felder.all? { |f| wert = send(f); !wert.nil? && wert != \"\" }</code> – im define_method-Block. Danach in <code>Buchung</code> die Zeile <code>validates_presence_of :projekt</code> aktivieren.",
            "solution": "class Modell\n  def self.validates_presence_of(*felder)\n    define_method(:valid?) do\n      felder.all? { |f| wert = send(f); !wert.nil? && wert != \"\" }\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nclass Buchung < Modell\n  attr_accessor :projekt, :stunden\n  validates_presence_of :projekt\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Object model & metaprogramming",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>How Ruby finds methods</h2><p>When you call <code>object.method</code>, Ruby climbs a chain: first the object's singleton class (home of methods only <em>this</em> object has), then its class, then the mixins, then the superclasses. You can display the chain:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "oddball = \"a normal string\"\n\ndef oddball.shout\n  upcase + \"!!!\"\nend\n\n[oddball.shout, String.ancestors.first(4)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Because classes are objects themselves, code can <em>create code</em> – that's metaprogramming. <code>define_method</code> defines methods at runtime, <code>send</code> calls a method whose name sits in a variable:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Config\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  %w[host port language].each do |field|\n    define_method(field) { @values[field] }\n  end\nend\n\nc = Config.new({ \"host\" => \"idogawa.com\", \"port\" => 8011 })\n[c.host, c.send(\"port\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>That's exactly how Rails &amp; co. build their macros like <code>has_many</code> or <code>validates</code>. An important warning from a wise fox: <em>the cleverest code is often the first thing you delete later.</em> There is a second way, too: <code>method_missing</code> catches every call for which Ruby finds no method. Whoever uses <code>method_missing</code> always writes <code>respond_to_missing?</code> as well – otherwise <code>respond_to?</code> tells lies – and passes everything the class doesn't know on with <code>super</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Settings\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  def method_missing(name, *args)\n    key = name.to_s\n    return @values[key] if @values.key?(key)\n\n    super\n  end\n\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    @values.key?(name.to_s) || super\n  end\nend\n\ns = Settings.new(\"theme\" => \"fox\", \"language\" => \"en\")\n[s.theme, s.respond_to?(:theme), s.respond_to?(:color)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Try <code>s.color</code>: <code>super</code> turns it into a perfectly ordinary <code>NoMethodError</code>. It works – but nobody sees such methods in the class, no documentation knows them, and a typo lands silently in <code>method_missing</code>. That's why an explicit <code>define_method</code> is almost always the clearer choice.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-define_method' target='_blank'>define_method</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-send' target='_blank'>send</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-ancestors' target='_blank'>ancestors</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Build your own Rails macro: <code>BaseModel.validates_presence_of(*fields)</code> should use <code>define_method</code> to create a <code>valid?</code> method checking that none of the fields is <code>nil</code> or <code>\"\"</code>. The class <code>Booking</code> below then uses it Rails-style.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class BaseModel\n  # def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n  #   define_method(:valid?) do\n  #     ...\n  #   end\n  # end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  # validates_presence_of :project\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Remove the <code>#</code> in <code>BaseModel</code> and <code>Booking</code>. Inside the <code>define_method</code> block, check every field with <code>all?</code> – fetch its value with <code>send</code>."
            ],
            "check": "b = Booking.new; b.project = \"X\"; b2 = Booking.new; b2.project = \"\"; b3 = Booking.new; b.valid? && !b2.valid? && !b3.valid? && code.include?(\"define_method\")",
            "hint": "<code>fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }</code> – inside the define_method block. Then activate the line <code>validates_presence_of :project</code> in <code>Booking</code>.",
            "solution": "class BaseModel\n  def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n    define_method(:valid?) do\n      fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  validates_presence_of :project\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "オブジェクトモデルとメタプログラミング",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Rubyはどうやってメソッドを見つけるのか</h2><p><code>object.method</code>を呼び出すと、Rubyは決まった順番でメソッドを探していきます。まずそのオブジェクトの特異クラス（<em>このオブジェクトだけ</em>が持つメソッドの置き場所）、次にそのクラス、次にMix-inしたモジュール、そしてスーパークラスへと上っていきます。この順番は表示させることもできます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "oddball = \"a normal string\"\n\ndef oddball.shout\n  upcase + \"!!!\"\nend\n\n[oddball.shout, String.ancestors.first(4)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>クラスそのものもオブジェクトなので、コードが<em>コードを作る</em>こともできます。これがメタプログラミングです。<code>define_method</code>は実行時にメソッドを定義し、<code>send</code>は名前が変数に入っているメソッドを呼び出します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Config\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  %w[host port language].each do |field|\n    define_method(field) { @values[field] }\n  end\nend\n\nc = Config.new({ \"host\" => \"idogawa.com\", \"port\" => 8011 })\n[c.host, c.send(\"port\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Railsなどのフレームワークも、<code>has_many</code>や<code>validates</code>のようなマクロをまさにこうやって作っています。ただし、賢いキツネから大事な忠告をひとつ：<em>凝りに凝ったコードほど、あとで真っ先に消すことになりがちです。</em>もう1つの方法もあります。<code>method_missing</code>は、Rubyがメソッドを見つけられなかった呼び出しをすべて受け止めます。使うときは、必ず<code>respond_to_missing?</code>も書きます（そうしないと<code>respond_to?</code>がまちがった答えを返します）。そして知らない呼び出しは<code>super</code>で先へ回します：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Settings\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  def method_missing(name, *args)\n    key = name.to_s\n    return @values[key] if @values.key?(key)\n\n    super\n  end\n\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    @values.key?(name.to_s) || super\n  end\nend\n\ns = Settings.new(\"theme\" => \"fox\", \"language\" => \"en\")\n[s.theme, s.respond_to?(:theme), s.respond_to?(:color)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>s.color</code>も試してみましょう。<code>super</code>のおかげで、ごくふつうの<code>NoMethodError</code>になります。これでも動きます。でも、こうしたメソッドはクラスのどこにも書かれていないので、だれの目にも見えず、ドキュメントにも載らず、打ち間違いはこっそり<code>method_missing</code>に吸いこまれてしまいます。だからこそ、はっきり書いた<code>define_method</code>のほうが、ほとんどの場合わかりやすいのです。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Module/i/define_method.html' target='_blank'>define_method</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Object/i/send.html' target='_blank'>send</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/Module/i/ancestors.html' target='_blank'>ancestors</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>自分だけのRailsマクロを作りましょう。<code>BaseModel.validates_presence_of(*fields)</code>は、<code>define_method</code>で<code>valid?</code>メソッドを作ります。このメソッドは、指定したフィールドのどれも<code>nil</code>や<code>\"\"</code>でないことを確かめます。下の<code>Booking</code>クラスでは、それをRails風に使います。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class BaseModel\n  # def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n  #   define_method(:valid?) do\n  #     ...\n  #   end\n  # end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  # validates_presence_of :project\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>BaseModel</code>と<code>Booking</code>の<code>#</code>を外してね。<code>define_method</code>のブロックの中では、<code>all?</code>で各フィールドを確かめるよ。値は<code>send</code>で取り出すんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "b = Booking.new; b.project = \"X\"; b2 = Booking.new; b2.project = \"\"; b3 = Booking.new; b.valid? && !b2.valid? && !b3.valid? && code.include?(\"define_method\")",
            "hint": "define_methodのブロックの中で<code>fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }</code>を使ってみて。それから<code>Booking</code>の中の<code>validates_presence_of :project</code>の行を有効にしよう（先頭の#を消すだけだよ）。",
            "solution": "class BaseModel\n  def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n    define_method(:valid?) do\n      fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  validates_presence_of :project\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Modèle objet et métaprogrammation",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Comment Ruby trouve les méthodes</h2><p>Quand vous appelez <code>object.method</code>, Ruby remonte une chaîne : d'abord la classe singleton de l'objet (où vivent les méthodes que seul <em>cet</em> objet possède), puis sa classe, puis les mixins, puis les superclasses. Vous pouvez afficher cette chaîne :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "oddball = \"a normal string\"\n\ndef oddball.shout\n  upcase + \"!!!\"\nend\n\n[oddball.shout, String.ancestors.first(4)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Comme les classes sont elles-mêmes des objets, du code peut <em>créer du code</em> – c'est la métaprogrammation. <code>define_method</code> définit des méthodes à l'exécution, <code>send</code> appelle une méthode dont le nom se trouve dans une variable :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Config\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  %w[host port language].each do |field|\n    define_method(field) { @values[field] }\n  end\nend\n\nc = Config.new({ \"host\" => \"idogawa.com\", \"port\" => 8011 })\n[c.host, c.send(\"port\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>C'est exactement ainsi que Rails et consorts construisent leurs macros comme <code>has_many</code> ou <code>validates</code>. Un avertissement important d'un renard plein de sagesse : <em>le code le plus astucieux est souvent le premier que l'on supprime plus tard.</em> Il existe aussi une deuxième voie : <code>method_missing</code> intercepte chaque appel pour lequel Ruby ne trouve aucune méthode. Qui utilise <code>method_missing</code> écrit toujours aussi <code>respond_to_missing?</code> – sinon <code>respond_to?</code> raconte des mensonges – et transmet tout ce que la classe ne connaît pas avec <code>super</code> :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Settings\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  def method_missing(name, *args)\n    key = name.to_s\n    return @values[key] if @values.key?(key)\n\n    super\n  end\n\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    @values.key?(name.to_s) || super\n  end\nend\n\ns = Settings.new(\"theme\" => \"fox\", \"language\" => \"en\")\n[s.theme, s.respond_to?(:theme), s.respond_to?(:color)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Essayez <code>s.color</code> : <code>super</code> le transforme en une <code>NoMethodError</code> tout à fait ordinaire. Ça fonctionne – mais personne ne voit de telles méthodes dans la classe, aucune documentation ne les connaît, et une faute de frappe atterrit silencieusement dans <code>method_missing</code>. C'est pourquoi un <code>define_method</code> explicite est presque toujours le choix le plus clair.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-define_method' target='_blank'>define_method</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-send' target='_blank'>send</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-ancestors' target='_blank'>ancestors</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Construisez votre propre macro à la Rails : <code>BaseModel.validates_presence_of(*fields)</code> doit utiliser <code>define_method</code> pour créer une méthode <code>valid?</code> qui vérifie qu'aucun des champs n'est <code>nil</code> ou <code>\"\"</code>. La classe <code>Booking</code> ci-dessous l'utilise ensuite à la manière de Rails.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class BaseModel\n  # def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n  #   define_method(:valid?) do\n  #     ...\n  #   end\n  # end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  # validates_presence_of :project\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Supprimez les <code>#</code> dans <code>BaseModel</code> et <code>Booking</code>. Dans le bloc de <code>define_method</code>, vérifiez chaque champ avec <code>all?</code> – récupérez sa valeur avec <code>send</code>."
            ],
            "check": "b = Booking.new; b.project = \"X\"; b2 = Booking.new; b2.project = \"\"; b3 = Booking.new; b.valid? && !b2.valid? && !b3.valid? && code.include?(\"define_method\")",
            "hint": "<code>fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }</code> – dans le bloc de define_method. Activez ensuite la ligne <code>validates_presence_of :project</code> dans <code>Booking</code>.",
            "solution": "class BaseModel\n  def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n    define_method(:valid?) do\n      fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  validates_presence_of :project\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Modello a oggetti e metaprogrammazione",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Come Ruby trova i metodi</h2><p>Quando chiami <code>object.method</code>, Ruby risale una catena: prima la classe singleton dell'oggetto (la casa dei metodi che ha solo <em>questo</em> oggetto), poi la sua classe, poi i mixin, poi le superclassi. Puoi visualizzare la catena:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "oddball = \"a normal string\"\n\ndef oddball.shout\n  upcase + \"!!!\"\nend\n\n[oddball.shout, String.ancestors.first(4)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dato che le classi sono a loro volta oggetti, il codice può <em>creare codice</em> – questa è la metaprogrammazione. <code>define_method</code> definisce metodi durante l'esecuzione, <code>send</code> chiama un metodo il cui nome si trova in una variabile:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Config\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  %w[host port language].each do |field|\n    define_method(field) { @values[field] }\n  end\nend\n\nc = Config.new({ \"host\" => \"idogawa.com\", \"port\" => 8011 })\n[c.host, c.send(\"port\")]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>È esattamente così che Rails e simili costruiscono le loro macro come <code>has_many</code> o <code>validates</code>. Un avvertimento importante da una volpe saggia: <em>il codice più astuto è spesso la prima cosa che poi cancelli.</em> C'è anche una seconda strada: <code>method_missing</code> intercetta ogni chiamata per cui Ruby non trova un metodo. Chi usa <code>method_missing</code> scrive sempre anche <code>respond_to_missing?</code> – altrimenti <code>respond_to?</code> dice bugie – e passa oltre con <code>super</code> tutto ciò che la classe non conosce:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Settings\n  def initialize(values)\n    @values = values\n  end\n\n  def method_missing(name, *args)\n    key = name.to_s\n    return @values[key] if @values.key?(key)\n\n    super\n  end\n\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    @values.key?(name.to_s) || super\n  end\nend\n\ns = Settings.new(\"theme\" => \"fox\", \"language\" => \"en\")\n[s.theme, s.respond_to?(:theme), s.respond_to?(:color)]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Prova <code>s.color</code>: <code>super</code> lo trasforma in un <code>NoMethodError</code> del tutto normale. Funziona – ma nessuno vede questi metodi nella classe, nessuna documentazione li conosce, e un errore di battitura finisce in silenzio in <code>method_missing</code>. Per questo un <code>define_method</code> esplicito è quasi sempre la scelta più chiara.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-define_method' target='_blank'>define_method</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Object.html#method-i-send' target='_blank'>send</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Module.html#method-i-ancestors' target='_blank'>ancestors</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Costruisci la tua macro alla Rails: <code>BaseModel.validates_presence_of(*fields)</code> deve usare <code>define_method</code> per creare un metodo <code>valid?</code> che verifica che nessuno dei campi sia <code>nil</code> o <code>\"\"</code>. La classe <code>Booking</code> qui sotto la usa poi in stile Rails.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "class BaseModel\n  # def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n  #   define_method(:valid?) do\n  #     ...\n  #   end\n  # end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  # validates_presence_of :project\nend\n",
            "hints": [
              "Togli il <code>#</code> in <code>BaseModel</code> e in <code>Booking</code>. Dentro il blocco di <code>define_method</code> verifica ogni campo con <code>all?</code> – il suo valore lo ottieni con <code>send</code>."
            ],
            "check": "b = Booking.new; b.project = \"X\"; b2 = Booking.new; b2.project = \"\"; b3 = Booking.new; b.valid? && !b2.valid? && !b3.valid? && code.include?(\"define_method\")",
            "hint": "<code>fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }</code> – dentro il blocco di define_method. Poi attiva la riga <code>validates_presence_of :project</code> in <code>Booking</code>.",
            "solution": "class BaseModel\n  def self.validates_presence_of(*fields)\n    define_method(:valid?) do\n      fields.all? { |f| value = send(f); !value.nil? && value != \"\" }\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nclass Booking < BaseModel\n  attr_accessor :project, :hours\n  validates_presence_of :project\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-dsl",
      "de": {
        "title": "Eine eigene DSL",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Konfiguration wie die Grossen</h2><p>Viele Ruby-Werkzeuge konfiguriert man mit eleganten Blöcken – das nennt man eine <strong>DSL</strong> (domain-specific language). Der Trick dahinter ist eine einzige Methode: <code>instance_eval</code> führt einen Block so aus, als stünde er <em>im Objekt drin</em> – <code>self</code> wechselt:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Speisekarte\n  attr_reader :gerichte\n\n  def initialize\n    @gerichte = {}\n  end\n\n  def gericht(name, preis:)\n    @gerichte[name] = preis\n  end\nend\n\ndef speisekarte(&block)\n  karte = Speisekarte.new\n  karte.instance_eval(&block)\n  karte\nend\n\nkarte = speisekarte do\n  gericht \"Speck\", preis: 8\n  gericht \"Ei\",    preis: 3\nend\n\nkarte.gerichte"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Im Block ruft <code>gericht \"Speck\", preis: 8</code> in Wahrheit eine Methode der <code>Speisekarte</code> auf – ganz ohne Empfänger davor. Das liest sich wie eine Mini-Sprache. (<code>instance_exec</code> ist die Schwester, die zusätzlich Argumente in den Block reicht.)</p><p><strong>Ehrliche Warnung:</strong> Eine DSL lohnt sich nur, wenn viele Menschen sie oft lesen – sonst tut es ein schlichter Hash genauso gut und ist leichter zu debuggen. Verwandte Bausteine aus der Werkzeugkiste: unsere Formatter-Lambdas aus Lektion {{nr:tl-blocks}} waren das <em>Strategy</em>-Muster, und ein <em>Null-Objekt</em> (z. B. ein GastNutzer statt <code>nil</code>) erspart tausend <code>if</code>-Abfragen.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_eval' target='_blank'>instance_eval</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_exec' target='_blank'>instance_exec</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Baue die timelog-Konfiguration: <code>Timelog.configure { … }</code> führt den Block per <code>instance_eval</code> auf einer neuen <code>Konfiguration</code> aus, <code>Timelog.config</code> gibt sie zurück. Im Block sollen <code>projekt \"Name\", satz: 120</code> und <code>runde_auf 15</code> funktionieren.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Konfiguration\n    attr_reader :projekte, :raster\n\n    def initialize\n      @projekte = {}\n      @raster = 60\n    end\n\n    # def projekt(name, satz:)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n\n    # def runde_auf(minuten)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n  end\n\n  # def self.configure(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\n\n  # def self.config\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\n# Timelog.configure do\n#   projekt \"ProjectX\", satz: 120\n#   runde_auf 15\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Drei Teile: die beiden DSL-Methoden in <code>Konfiguration</code> schreiben nur in <code>@projekte</code> und <code>@raster</code>; <code>Timelog.configure</code> legt eine neue Konfiguration an und führt den Block mit <code>instance_eval</code> darauf aus; <code>Timelog.config</code> gibt sie zurück."
            ],
            "check": "Timelog.configure { projekt \"A\", satz: 100\n runde_auf 30 }; Timelog.config.projekte[\"A\"] == 100 && Timelog.config.raster == 30 && code.include?(\"instance_eval\")",
            "hint": "<code>def self.configure(&block); @config = Konfiguration.new; @config.instance_eval(&block); @config; end</code> und <code>def self.config; @config; end</code>. Die DSL-Methoden schreiben einfach in <code>@projekte</code> bzw. <code>@raster</code>.",
            "solution": "module Timelog\n  class Konfiguration\n    attr_reader :projekte, :raster\n\n    def initialize\n      @projekte = {}\n      @raster = 60\n    end\n\n    def projekt(name, satz:)\n      @projekte[name] = satz\n    end\n\n    def runde_auf(minuten)\n      @raster = minuten\n    end\n  end\n\n  def self.configure(&block)\n    @config = Konfiguration.new\n    @config.instance_eval(&block)\n    @config\n  end\n\n  def self.config\n    @config\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Your own DSL",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Configuration like the big ones</h2><p>Many Ruby tools are configured with elegant blocks – that's called a <strong>DSL</strong> (domain-specific language). The trick behind it is a single method: <code>instance_eval</code> runs a block as if it were written <em>inside the object</em> – <code>self</code> switches:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Menu\n  attr_reader :dishes\n\n  def initialize\n    @dishes = {}\n  end\n\n  def dish(name, price:)\n    @dishes[name] = price\n  end\nend\n\ndef menu(&block)\n  m = Menu.new\n  m.instance_eval(&block)\n  m\nend\n\ncard = menu do\n  dish \"Bacon\", price: 8\n  dish \"Egg\",   price: 3\nend\n\ncard.dishes"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Inside the block, <code>dish \"Bacon\", price: 8</code> really calls a method of the <code>Menu</code> – with no receiver in front. It reads like a mini language. (<code>instance_exec</code> is the sibling that additionally passes arguments into the block.)</p><p><strong>Honest warning:</strong> a DSL only pays off when many people read it often – otherwise a plain hash does the job and is easier to debug. Related building blocks: our formatter lambdas from lesson {{nr:tl-blocks}} were the <em>Strategy</em> pattern, and a <em>null object</em> (e.g. a GuestUser instead of <code>nil</code>) saves a thousand <code>if</code> checks.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_eval' target='_blank'>instance_eval</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_exec' target='_blank'>instance_exec</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> Build the timelog configuration: <code>Timelog.configure { … }</code> runs the block via <code>instance_eval</code> on a fresh <code>Configuration</code>, <code>Timelog.config</code> returns it. Inside the block, <code>project \"Name\", rate: 120</code> and <code>round_to 15</code> must work.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    # def project(name, rate:)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n\n    # def round_to(minutes)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n  end\n\n  # def self.configure(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\n\n  # def self.config\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\n# Timelog.configure do\n#   project \"ProjectX\", rate: 120\n#   round_to 15\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Three parts: the two DSL methods in <code>Configuration</code> just write into <code>@projects</code> and <code>@grid</code>; <code>Timelog.configure</code> creates a new configuration and runs the block on it with <code>instance_eval</code>; <code>Timelog.config</code> returns it."
            ],
            "check": "Timelog.configure { project \"A\", rate: 100\n round_to 30 }; Timelog.config.projects[\"A\"] == 100 && Timelog.config.grid == 30 && code.include?(\"instance_eval\")",
            "hint": "<code>def self.configure(&block); @config = Configuration.new; @config.instance_eval(&block); @config; end</code> and <code>def self.config; @config; end</code>. The DSL methods simply write into <code>@projects</code> / <code>@grid</code>.",
            "solution": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    def project(name, rate:)\n      @projects[name] = rate\n    end\n\n    def round_to(minutes)\n      @grid = minutes\n    end\n  end\n\n  def self.configure(&block)\n    @config = Configuration.new\n    @config.instance_eval(&block)\n    @config\n  end\n\n  def self.config\n    @config\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "自分だけのDSL",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>人気ツールのような設定の書き方</h2><p>Rubyのツールの多くは、エレガントなブロックで設定を書けるようになっています。これを<strong>DSL</strong>（ドメイン固有言語、domain-specific language）と呼びます。その裏にある仕掛けは、たった1つのメソッドです。<code>instance_eval</code>は、ブロックをまるで<em>そのオブジェクトの中に</em>書いたかのように実行します。つまり<code>self</code>が切り替わるのです：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Menu\n  attr_reader :dishes\n\n  def initialize\n    @dishes = {}\n  end\n\n  def dish(name, price:)\n    @dishes[name] = price\n  end\nend\n\ndef menu(&block)\n  m = Menu.new\n  m.instance_eval(&block)\n  m\nend\n\ncard = menu do\n  dish \"Bacon\", price: 8\n  dish \"Egg\",   price: 3\nend\n\ncard.dishes"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ブロックの中の<code>dish \"Bacon\", price: 8</code>は、前にレシーバーを書いていないのに、実は<code>Menu</code>のメソッドを呼び出しています。まるで小さな言語のように読めますね。（<code>instance_exec</code>はその兄弟分で、ブロックに引数も渡せます。）</p><p><strong>正直に言っておくと：</strong>DSLが割に合うのは、たくさんの人が何度も読む場合だけです。そうでなければ、ただのハッシュで十分ですし、そのほうがデバッグも簡単です。関連する道具もあります。レッスン{{nr:tl-blocks}}のフォーマッター用ラムダは、じつは<em>Strategy</em>パターンでした。また<em>ヌルオブジェクト</em>（たとえば<code>nil</code>の代わりにGuestUserを使う）を使えば、山ほどの<code>if</code>チェックを書かずに済みます。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/BasicObject/i/instance_eval.html' target='_blank'>instance_eval</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/method/BasicObject/i/instance_exec.html' target='_blank'>instance_exec</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>timelogの設定のしくみを作りましょう。<code>Timelog.configure { … }</code>は、新しく作った<code>Configuration</code>の上で<code>instance_eval</code>を使ってブロックを実行し、<code>Timelog.config</code>はその設定を返します。ブロックの中では<code>project \"Name\", rate: 120</code>と<code>round_to 15</code>が使えるようにします。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    # def project(name, rate:)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n\n    # def round_to(minutes)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n  end\n\n  # def self.configure(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\n\n  # def self.config\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\n# Timelog.configure do\n#   project \"ProjectX\", rate: 120\n#   round_to 15\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "3つのパーツだよ。<code>Configuration</code>の2つのDSLメソッドは<code>@projects</code>と<code>@grid</code>に書き込むだけ。<code>Timelog.configure</code>は新しい設定を作って、<code>instance_eval</code>でその上でブロックを実行する。<code>Timelog.config</code>はそれを返すんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "Timelog.configure { project \"A\", rate: 100\n round_to 30 }; Timelog.config.projects[\"A\"] == 100 && Timelog.config.grid == 30 && code.include?(\"instance_eval\")",
            "hint": "<code>def self.configure(&block); @config = Configuration.new; @config.instance_eval(&block); @config; end</code>と<code>def self.config; @config; end</code>だよ。DSLのメソッドは、<code>@projects</code>や<code>@grid</code>に書き込むだけでいいんだ。",
            "solution": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    def project(name, rate:)\n      @projects[name] = rate\n    end\n\n    def round_to(minutes)\n      @grid = minutes\n    end\n  end\n\n  def self.configure(&block)\n    @config = Configuration.new\n    @config.instance_eval(&block)\n    @config\n  end\n\n  def self.config\n    @config\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Votre propre DSL",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Une configuration comme chez les grands</h2><p>Beaucoup d'outils Ruby se configurent avec d'élégants blocs – on appelle cela un <strong>DSL</strong> (domain-specific language, langage dédié). L'astuce repose sur une seule méthode : <code>instance_eval</code> exécute un bloc comme s'il était écrit <em>à l'intérieur de l'objet</em> – <code>self</code> change :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Menu\n  attr_reader :dishes\n\n  def initialize\n    @dishes = {}\n  end\n\n  def dish(name, price:)\n    @dishes[name] = price\n  end\nend\n\ndef menu(&block)\n  m = Menu.new\n  m.instance_eval(&block)\n  m\nend\n\ncard = menu do\n  dish \"Bacon\", price: 8\n  dish \"Egg\",   price: 3\nend\n\ncard.dishes"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dans le bloc, <code>dish \"Bacon\", price: 8</code> appelle vraiment une méthode du <code>Menu</code> – sans receveur devant. On dirait un mini-langage. (<code>instance_exec</code> est sa sœur, qui transmet en plus des arguments au bloc.)</p><p><strong>Avertissement honnête :</strong> un DSL ne vaut la peine que si beaucoup de personnes le lisent souvent – sinon, un simple hash fait l'affaire et se débogue plus facilement. Briques apparentées : nos lambdas de formatage de la leçon {{nr:tl-blocks}} étaient le patron <em>Strategy</em>, et un <em>null object</em> (par exemple un GuestUser au lieu de <code>nil</code>) épargne un millier de tests <code>if</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_eval' target='_blank'>instance_eval</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_exec' target='_blank'>instance_exec</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Construisez la configuration de timelog : <code>Timelog.configure { … }</code> exécute le bloc via <code>instance_eval</code> sur une nouvelle <code>Configuration</code>, <code>Timelog.config</code> la renvoie. Dans le bloc, <code>project \"Name\", rate: 120</code> et <code>round_to 15</code> doivent fonctionner.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    # def project(name, rate:)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n\n    # def round_to(minutes)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n  end\n\n  # def self.configure(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\n\n  # def self.config\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\n# Timelog.configure do\n#   project \"ProjectX\", rate: 120\n#   round_to 15\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Trois parties : les deux méthodes du DSL dans <code>Configuration</code> écrivent simplement dans <code>@projects</code> et <code>@grid</code> ; <code>Timelog.configure</code> crée une nouvelle configuration et y exécute le bloc avec <code>instance_eval</code> ; <code>Timelog.config</code> la renvoie."
            ],
            "check": "Timelog.configure { project \"A\", rate: 100\n round_to 30 }; Timelog.config.projects[\"A\"] == 100 && Timelog.config.grid == 30 && code.include?(\"instance_eval\")",
            "hint": "<code>def self.configure(&block); @config = Configuration.new; @config.instance_eval(&block); @config; end</code> et <code>def self.config; @config; end</code>. Les méthodes du DSL se contentent d'écrire dans <code>@projects</code> / <code>@grid</code>.",
            "solution": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    def project(name, rate:)\n      @projects[name] = rate\n    end\n\n    def round_to(minutes)\n      @grid = minutes\n    end\n  end\n\n  def self.configure(&block)\n    @config = Configuration.new\n    @config.instance_eval(&block)\n    @config\n  end\n\n  def self.config\n    @config\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Il tuo DSL",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Configurazione come fanno i grandi</h2><p>Molti strumenti Ruby si configurano con blocchi eleganti – si chiama <strong>DSL</strong> (domain-specific language, linguaggio specifico di dominio). Il trucco che c'è dietro è un solo metodo: <code>instance_eval</code> esegue un blocco come se fosse scritto <em>dentro l'oggetto</em> – <code>self</code> cambia:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "class Menu\n  attr_reader :dishes\n\n  def initialize\n    @dishes = {}\n  end\n\n  def dish(name, price:)\n    @dishes[name] = price\n  end\nend\n\ndef menu(&block)\n  m = Menu.new\n  m.instance_eval(&block)\n  m\nend\n\ncard = menu do\n  dish \"Bacon\", price: 8\n  dish \"Egg\",   price: 3\nend\n\ncard.dishes"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dentro il blocco, <code>dish \"Bacon\", price: 8</code> chiama davvero un metodo del <code>Menu</code> – senza nessun destinatario davanti. Si legge come un mini linguaggio. (<code>instance_exec</code> è il fratello che in più passa argomenti al blocco.)</p><p><strong>Avvertenza onesta:</strong> un DSL conviene solo quando molte persone lo leggono spesso – altrimenti un semplice hash fa lo stesso lavoro ed è più facile da debuggare. Mattoni affini: le nostre lambda di formattazione della lezione {{nr:tl-blocks}} erano il pattern <em>Strategy</em>, e un <em>null object</em> (per es. un GuestUser al posto di <code>nil</code>) risparmia mille controlli <code>if</code>.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_eval' target='_blank'>instance_eval</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/BasicObject.html#method-i-instance_exec' target='_blank'>instance_exec</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> Costruisci la configurazione di timelog: <code>Timelog.configure { … }</code> esegue il blocco tramite <code>instance_eval</code> su una <code>Configuration</code> nuova, <code>Timelog.config</code> la restituisce. Dentro il blocco devono funzionare <code>project \"Name\", rate: 120</code> e <code>round_to 15</code>.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    # def project(name, rate:)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n\n    # def round_to(minutes)\n    #   ...\n    # end\n  end\n\n  # def self.configure(&block)\n  #   ...\n  # end\n\n  # def self.config\n  #   ...\n  # end\nend\n\n# Timelog.configure do\n#   project \"ProjectX\", rate: 120\n#   round_to 15\n# end\n",
            "hints": [
              "Tre parti: i due metodi DSL in <code>Configuration</code> scrivono semplicemente in <code>@projects</code> e <code>@grid</code>; <code>Timelog.configure</code> crea una nuova configurazione ed esegue su di essa il blocco con <code>instance_eval</code>; <code>Timelog.config</code> la restituisce."
            ],
            "check": "Timelog.configure { project \"A\", rate: 100\n round_to 30 }; Timelog.config.projects[\"A\"] == 100 && Timelog.config.grid == 30 && code.include?(\"instance_eval\")",
            "hint": "<code>def self.configure(&block); @config = Configuration.new; @config.instance_eval(&block); @config; end</code> e <code>def self.config; @config; end</code>. I metodi DSL scrivono semplicemente in <code>@projects</code> / <code>@grid</code>.",
            "solution": "module Timelog\n  class Configuration\n    attr_reader :projects, :grid\n\n    def initialize\n      @projects = {}\n      @grid = 60\n    end\n\n    def project(name, rate:)\n      @projects[name] = rate\n    end\n\n    def round_to(minutes)\n      @grid = minutes\n    end\n  end\n\n  def self.configure(&block)\n    @config = Configuration.new\n    @config.instance_eval(&block)\n    @config\n  end\n\n  def self.config\n    @config\n  end\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-quality",
      "de": {
        "title": "Codequalität & Debugging",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Werkzeuge für sauberen Code</h2><p>In Teams sorgt ein <strong>Linter</strong> für einheitlichen Stil: <em>RuboCop</em> ist der grosse Konfigurierbare mit hunderten Regeln, <em>Standard</em> die Null-Diskussionen-Variante („eine Konfiguration für alle“). Wichtiger als jede einzelne Regel: Das Team streitet nicht mehr über Kommas. Dazu kommen <em>YARD</em> für Doku-Kommentare und – wer mag – Typsignaturen mit <em>RBS</em> oder <em>Sorbet</em>.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer'><pre><code>$ gem install standard\n$ standardrb            # prueft den Stil\n$ standardrb --fix      # repariert vieles gleich selbst\n\n# Und zum Debuggen mit Haltepunkt mitten im Code:\nrequire \"debug\"\nbinding.break           # oder binding.irb - oeffnet eine Konsole GENAU HIER</code></pre></div><p>Debuggen geht aber überall – auch hier. Das Universalwerkzeug heisst <code>p</code>: Es druckt sein Argument <em>und gibt es zurück</em>, du kannst es also mitten in jede Kette einschleusen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "werte = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\n\nwerte.select { |x| p(x).odd? }.sum"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Warum <code>p</code> und nicht <code>puts</code>? Weil <code>puts</code> nicht alles zeigt: <code>nil</code> und ein leerer String werden zu leeren Zeilen, und ein Array zerfällt in einzelne Zeilen. <code>p</code> zeigt die ehrliche Darstellung, die von <code>inspect</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "werte = [\"Speck\", nil, \"\", 42]\nputs werte\np werte"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Ist ein Wert seltsam leer, frag also <code>p</code> statt <code>puts</code>. Verwandte Helfer: <code>pp</code> bricht verschachtelte Daten hübsch um, und <code>caller</code> zeigt, wer die aktuelle Methode aufgerufen hat.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/' target='_blank'>RuboCop</a> · <a href='https://github.com/standardrb/standard' target='_blank'>Standard</a> · <a href='https://github.com/ruby/debug' target='_blank'>debug</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> In der Zelle unten steckt ein klassischer Bug: <code>runde</code> soll Minuten auf das <strong>nächste</strong> Raster runden (<code>runde(38, 15)</code> → <code>45</code>), schneidet aber immer ab. Finde den Fehler (probier <code>p</code>!) und repariere die Methode.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Diese Methode soll auf das NAECHSTE Raster runden -\n# aber sie liefert runde(38, 15) => 30 statt 45. Warum?\n\ndef runde(minuten, raster)\n  (minuten / raster) * raster\nend\n\nrunde(38, 15)\n",
            "hints": [
              "Probier <code>p 38 / 15</code> aus – was kommt heraus, und warum? Dann überlege, wie du aus der Division eine Kommazahl machst und sie mit <code>round</code> rundest, bevor du mit dem Raster multiplizierst."
            ],
            "check": "runde(38, 15) == 45 && runde(8, 15) == 15 && runde(7, 15) == 0 && runde(22, 15) == 15 && runde(60, 60) == 60",
            "hint": "<code>38 / 15</code> ist Ganzzahl-Division (ergibt 2, Rest weg!). Erst zu Float machen, dann runden: <code>(minuten.to_f / raster).round * raster</code>.",
            "solution": "def runde(minuten, raster)\n  (minuten.to_f / raster).round * raster\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Code quality & debugging",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Tools for clean code</h2><p>In teams, a <strong>linter</strong> keeps the style uniform: <em>RuboCop</em> is the big configurable one with hundreds of rules, <em>Standard</em> the zero-discussion variant (“one config for everyone”). More important than any single rule: the team stops arguing about commas. Add <em>YARD</em> for doc comments and – if you like – type signatures with <em>RBS</em> or <em>Sorbet</em>.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine'><pre><code>$ gem install standard\n$ standardrb            # checks the style\n$ standardrb --fix      # repairs a lot by itself\n\n# And for breakpoint debugging right in your code:\nrequire \"debug\"\nbinding.break           # or binding.irb - opens a console RIGHT HERE</code></pre></div><p>But debugging works everywhere – including here. The universal tool is <code>p</code>: it prints its argument <em>and returns it</em>, so you can sneak it into the middle of any chain:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\n\nvalues.select { |x| p(x).odd? }.sum"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Why <code>p</code> and not <code>puts</code>? Because <code>puts</code> doesn't show everything: <code>nil</code> and an empty string turn into empty lines, and an array falls apart into single lines. <code>p</code> shows the honest representation, the one from <code>inspect</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [\"bacon\", nil, \"\", 42]\nputs values\np values"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>So when a value looks strangely empty, ask <code>p</code> rather than <code>puts</code>. Related helpers: <code>pp</code> wraps nested data nicely, and <code>caller</code> shows who called the current method.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/' target='_blank'>RuboCop</a> · <a href='https://github.com/standardrb/standard' target='_blank'>Standard</a> · <a href='https://github.com/ruby/debug' target='_blank'>debug</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> The cell below hides a classic bug: <code>round_to</code> should round minutes to the <strong>nearest</strong> grid (<code>round_to(38, 15)</code> → <code>45</code>), but it always truncates. Find the bug (try <code>p</code>!) and repair the method.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# This method should round to the NEAREST grid -\n# but it returns round_to(38, 15) => 30 instead of 45. Why?\n\ndef round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes / grid) * grid\nend\n\nround_to(38, 15)\n",
            "hints": [
              "Try <code>p 38 / 15</code> – what comes out, and why? Then think about how to make the division a decimal and round it with <code>round</code> before multiplying by the grid."
            ],
            "check": "round_to(38, 15) == 45 && round_to(8, 15) == 15 && round_to(7, 15) == 0 && round_to(22, 15) == 15 && round_to(60, 60) == 60",
            "hint": "<code>38 / 15</code> is integer division (gives 2, remainder gone!). Convert to Float first, then round: <code>(minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid</code>.",
            "solution": "def round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "コードの品質とデバッグ",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>きれいなコードのための道具</h2><p>チームで開発するときは、<strong>リンター</strong>がコードのスタイルをそろえてくれます。<em>RuboCop</em>は何百ものルールを細かく設定できる大物、<em>Standard</em>は議論いらずの選択肢です（「全員に同じ設定を」）。どのルールよりも大切なのは、チームがカンマの位置で言い争わなくなることです。さらに、ドキュメントコメントには<em>YARD</em>、お好みで<em>RBS</em>や<em>Sorbet</em>による型シグネチャも使えます。</p><div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは'><pre><code>$ gem install standard\n$ standardrb            # checks the style\n$ standardrb --fix      # repairs a lot by itself\n\n# And for breakpoint debugging right in your code:\nrequire \"debug\"\nbinding.break           # or binding.irb - opens a console RIGHT HERE</code></pre></div><p>でも、デバッグはどこでもできます。もちろんここでも。万能の道具は<code>p</code>です。引数を出力して、<em>しかもそれをそのまま返す</em>ので、どんなメソッドチェーンの途中にもこっそり差し込めます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\n\nvalues.select { |x| p(x).odd? }.sum"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>なぜ<code>puts</code>ではなく<code>p</code>なのでしょう？ <code>puts</code>はすべてを見せてくれるわけではないからです。<code>nil</code>や空の文字列は空行になってしまい、配列はばらばらの行に分かれてしまいます。<code>p</code>は、<code>inspect</code>が返すありのままの姿を見せてくれます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [\"bacon\", nil, \"\", 42]\nputs values\np values"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>値がなぜか空っぽに見えるときは、<code>puts</code>ではなく<code>p</code>に聞きましょう。ほかにも便利な道具があります。入れ子になったデータをきれいに折り返して表示する<code>pp</code>、そして今のメソッドを誰が呼び出したかを教えてくれる<code>caller</code>です。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/' target='_blank'>RuboCop（英語）</a>・<a href='https://github.com/standardrb/standard' target='_blank'>Standard（英語）</a>・<a href='https://github.com/ruby/debug' target='_blank'>debug（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>下のセルには、よくあるバグが隠れています。<code>round_to</code>は分を<strong>いちばん近い</strong>刻みに丸めるはずなのに（<code>round_to(38, 15)</code> → <code>45</code>）、いつも切り捨ててしまいます。バグを見つけて（<code>p</code>を使ってみましょう！）、メソッドを直してください。</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# このメソッドは「いちばん近い」刻みに丸めるはず。\n# なのにround_to(38, 15)が45ではなく30を返す。なぜ？\n\ndef round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes / grid) * grid\nend\n\nround_to(38, 15)\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>p 38 / 15</code>を試してみて。何が出る？それはなぜかな？それから、割り算を小数にして<code>round</code>で丸めてからグリッドをかける方法を考えてみよう。"
            ],
            "check": "round_to(38, 15) == 45 && round_to(8, 15) == 15 && round_to(7, 15) == 0 && round_to(22, 15) == 15 && round_to(60, 60) == 60",
            "hint": "<code>38 / 15</code>は整数どうしの割り算だよ（答えは2で、余りは消えちゃう！）。先にFloatに変換してから丸めよう：<code>(minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid</code>",
            "solution": "def round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Qualité du code et débogage",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Des outils pour un code propre</h2><p>En équipe, un <strong>linter</strong> garde un style uniforme : <em>RuboCop</em> est le grand outil configurable avec des centaines de règles, <em>Standard</em> la variante « sans discussion » (« une configuration pour tout le monde »). Plus important que n'importe quelle règle : l'équipe cesse de se disputer à propos des virgules. Ajoutez-y <em>YARD</em> pour les commentaires de documentation et – si vous le souhaitez – des signatures de types avec <em>RBS</em> ou <em>Sorbet</em>.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine'><pre><code>$ gem install standard\n$ standardrb            # checks the style\n$ standardrb --fix      # repairs a lot by itself\n\n# And for breakpoint debugging right in your code:\nrequire \"debug\"\nbinding.break           # or binding.irb - opens a console RIGHT HERE</code></pre></div><p>Mais le débogage fonctionne partout – y compris ici. L'outil universel, c'est <code>p</code> : il affiche son argument <em>et le renvoie</em>, vous pouvez donc le glisser au milieu de n'importe quelle chaîne d'appels :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\n\nvalues.select { |x| p(x).odd? }.sum"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Pourquoi <code>p</code> et pas <code>puts</code> ? Parce que <code>puts</code> ne montre pas tout : <code>nil</code> et une chaîne vide deviennent des lignes vides, et un array se décompose en lignes séparées. <code>p</code> montre la représentation fidèle, celle de <code>inspect</code> :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [\"bacon\", nil, \"\", 42]\nputs values\np values"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Quand une valeur semble étrangement vide, interrogez donc <code>p</code> plutôt que <code>puts</code>. Outils apparentés : <code>pp</code> met joliment en forme les données imbriquées, et <code>caller</code> montre qui a appelé la méthode en cours.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/' target='_blank'>RuboCop</a> · <a href='https://github.com/standardrb/standard' target='_blank'>Standard</a> · <a href='https://github.com/ruby/debug' target='_blank'>debug</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> La cellule ci-dessous cache un bug classique : <code>round_to</code> devrait arrondir les minutes à la graduation la <strong>plus proche</strong> (<code>round_to(38, 15)</code> → <code>45</code>), mais elle tronque toujours. Trouvez le bug (essayez <code>p</code> !) et réparez la méthode.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Cette méthode devrait arrondir à la graduation la PLUS PROCHE -\n# mais round_to(38, 15) renvoie 30 au lieu de 45. Pourquoi ?\n\ndef round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes / grid) * grid\nend\n\nround_to(38, 15)\n",
            "hints": [
              "Essayez <code>p 38 / 15</code> – qu'obtenez-vous, et pourquoi ? Réfléchissez ensuite à la manière d'obtenir une division décimale et de l'arrondir avec <code>round</code> avant de multiplier par la graduation."
            ],
            "check": "round_to(38, 15) == 45 && round_to(8, 15) == 15 && round_to(7, 15) == 0 && round_to(22, 15) == 15 && round_to(60, 60) == 60",
            "hint": "<code>38 / 15</code> est une division entière (elle donne 2, le reste disparaît !). Convertissez d'abord en Float, puis arrondissez : <code>(minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid</code>.",
            "solution": "def round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Qualità del codice e debugging",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Strumenti per un codice pulito</h2><p>Nei team un <strong>linter</strong> mantiene uniforme lo stile: <em>RuboCop</em> è quello grande e configurabile con centinaia di regole, <em>Standard</em> la variante senza discussioni (“una configurazione per tutti”). Più importante di ogni singola regola: il team smette di litigare sulle virgole. Aggiungi <em>YARD</em> per i commenti di documentazione e – se ti va – le firme di tipo con <em>RBS</em> o <em>Sorbet</em>.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer'><pre><code>$ gem install standard\n$ standardrb            # checks the style\n$ standardrb --fix      # repairs a lot by itself\n\n# And for breakpoint debugging right in your code:\nrequire \"debug\"\nbinding.break           # or binding.irb - opens a console RIGHT HERE</code></pre></div><p>Ma il debugging funziona ovunque – anche qui. Lo strumento universale è <code>p</code>: stampa il suo argomento <em>e lo restituisce</em>, quindi puoi infilarlo in mezzo a qualsiasi catena:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\n\nvalues.select { |x| p(x).odd? }.sum"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Perché <code>p</code> e non <code>puts</code>? Perché <code>puts</code> non mostra tutto: <code>nil</code> e una stringa vuota diventano righe vuote, e un array si sfalda in righe singole. <code>p</code> mostra la rappresentazione onesta, quella di <code>inspect</code>:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "values = [\"bacon\", nil, \"\", 42]\nputs values\np values"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Quindi, quando un valore sembra stranamente vuoto, chiedi a <code>p</code> anziché a <code>puts</code>. Aiuti affini: <code>pp</code> impagina bene i dati annidati, e <code>caller</code> mostra chi ha chiamato il metodo corrente.</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/' target='_blank'>RuboCop</a> · <a href='https://github.com/standardrb/standard' target='_blank'>Standard</a> · <a href='https://github.com/ruby/debug' target='_blank'>debug</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> La cella qui sotto nasconde un bug classico: <code>round_to</code> dovrebbe arrotondare i minuti al multiplo <strong>più vicino</strong> della griglia (<code>round_to(38, 15)</code> → <code>45</code>), ma tronca sempre. Trova il bug (prova con <code>p</code>!) e ripara il metodo.</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "# Questo metodo dovrebbe arrotondare al multiplo PIÙ VICINO della griglia -\n# ma restituisce round_to(38, 15) => 30 invece di 45. Perché?\n\ndef round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes / grid) * grid\nend\n\nround_to(38, 15)\n",
            "hints": [
              "Prova <code>p 38 / 15</code> – che cosa esce, e perché? Poi pensa a come rendere la divisione un numero decimale e ad arrotondarla con <code>round</code> prima di moltiplicare per la griglia."
            ],
            "check": "round_to(38, 15) == 45 && round_to(8, 15) == 15 && round_to(7, 15) == 0 && round_to(22, 15) == 15 && round_to(60, 60) == 60",
            "hint": "<code>38 / 15</code> è una divisione intera (dà 2, il resto sparisce!). Prima converti in Float, poi arrotonda: <code>(minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid</code>.",
            "solution": "def round_to(minutes, grid)\n  (minutes.to_f / grid).round * grid\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-performance",
      "de": {
        "title": "Performance & Nebenläufigkeit",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Erst messen, dann optimieren</h2><p>Die goldene Regel: <strong>niemals raten</strong>. Rubys <code>Benchmark</code> misst, was wirklich langsam ist – oft ist es nicht das, was man denkt. Klassiker: <code>+=</code> auf Strings erzeugt jedes Mal einen <em>neuen</em> String, die Schaufel <code>&lt;&lt;</code> erweitert denselben:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"benchmark\"\n\nn = 5_000\nplus = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s += \"x\" }\nend\nschaufel = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s << \"x\" }\nend\n\n{ plus: plus.round(4), schaufel: schaufel.round(4) }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dahinter steckt das grosse Thema <strong>Allokationen</strong>: Jedes unnötige Zwischenobjekt kostet Zeit und Speicher. Und die schnellste Optimierung überhaupt: Code, der gar nicht erst läuft.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Auf deinem Computer: Profiler'><pre><code>$ gem install benchmark-ips stackprof\n# benchmark-ips: wie oft pro Sekunde? (aussagekraeftiger als einmal messen)\n# stackprof:     WO verbringt das Programm seine Zeit?</code></pre></div><p><strong>Nebenläufigkeit:</strong> Mit <code>Thread</code> erledigt Ruby mehrere Dinge „gleichzeitig“. In MRI teilen sich alle Threads einen Interpreter (die <em>GVL</em>) – echter Gewinn entsteht darum vor allem beim <em>Warten</em> (Netzwerk, Dateien); für CPU-Parallelität gibt es <em>Ractors</em>. Im Browser existieren keine echten Threads, deshalb sind <code>Thread</code> und <code>sleep</code> hier <strong>simuliert</strong> (kooperativ, mit virtueller Zeit) – aber die API ist die echte:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "abrufe = [\"kunden\", \"projekte\", \"zeiten\"].map do |name|\n  Thread.new do\n    sleep 1   # simulierter Netzwerk-Download\n    \"#{name}: geladen\"\n  end\nend\n\nabrufe.map(&:value)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Drei „Downloads“, gestartet mit <code>Thread.new</code>, eingesammelt mit <code>value</code> – auf deinem Rechner dauert das die Zeit von <em>einem</em> Download statt dreien. Und weil unsere simulierten Threads sich bei jedem <code>sleep</code> abwechseln, kannst du das Verschachteln sogar in der Ausgabe beobachten:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "faeden = 2.times.map do |i|\n  Thread.new do\n    3.times do |n|\n      puts \"Faden #{i}: Schritt #{n}\"\n      sleep 0.1\n    end\n  end\nend\nfaeden.each(&:join)\n\"fertig\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Unter der Haube unserer Simulation stecken übrigens <strong>Fibers</strong>: kooperative Mini-Programme, die sich die Kontrolle explizit zuwerfen – das Fundament vieler async-Bibliotheken. Die kannst du auch direkt benutzen:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "erzaehler = Fiber.new do\n  Fiber.yield \"Kapitel 1: Speck\"\n  Fiber.yield \"Kapitel 2: Mehr Speck\"\n  \"Ende\"\nend\n\n[erzaehler.resume, erzaehler.resume, erzaehler.resume]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://github.com/ruby/benchmark' target='_blank'>Benchmark</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Thread.html' target='_blank'>Thread</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Fiber.html' target='_blank'>Fiber</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Ractor.html' target='_blank'>Ractor</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> <code>langsamer_bericht</code> unten durchsucht die Einträge <strong>einmal pro Projekt</strong> – bei vielen Projekten wird das quadratisch langsam. Schreibe <code>schneller_bericht</code>, der mit <strong>einem einzigen</strong> <code>group_by</code>-Durchlauf dasselbe Ergebnis liefert. Miss den Unterschied mit Benchmark!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "eintraege = 500.times.map { |i| { projekt: \"P#{i % 5}\", stunden: 1.0 } }\n\ndef langsamer_bericht(eintraege)\n  eintraege.map { |e| e[:projekt] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, eintraege.select { |e| e[:projekt] == p }.sum { |e| e[:stunden] }]\n  end\nend\n\n# def schneller_bericht(eintraege)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# require \"benchmark\"\n# { langsam: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { langsamer_bericht(eintraege) } }.round(3),\n#   schnell: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { schneller_bericht(eintraege) } }.round(3) }\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>schneller_bericht</code> braucht nur einen Durchlauf: <code>group_by</code> nach dem Projekt und dann <code>transform_values</code> für die Stundensummen – wie in der Collections-Lektion. Vergleich beide danach mit <code>Benchmark</code>."
            ],
            "check": "schneller_bericht(eintraege) == langsamer_bericht(eintraege) && code.include?(\"group_by\")",
            "hint": "<code>eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } }</code> – ein Durchlauf statt einer pro Projekt.",
            "solution": "eintraege = 500.times.map { |i| { projekt: \"P#{i % 5}\", stunden: 1.0 } }\n\ndef langsamer_bericht(eintraege)\n  eintraege.map { |e| e[:projekt] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, eintraege.select { |e| e[:projekt] == p }.sum { |e| e[:stunden] }]\n  end\nend\n\ndef schneller_bericht(eintraege)\n  eintraege.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Performance & concurrency",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Measure first, then optimize</h2><p>The golden rule: <strong>never guess</strong>. Ruby's <code>Benchmark</code> measures what is actually slow – often it's not what you think. A classic: <code>+=</code> on strings creates a <em>new</em> string every time, the shovel <code>&lt;&lt;</code> extends the same one:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"benchmark\"\n\nn = 5_000\nplus = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s += \"x\" }\nend\nshovel = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s << \"x\" }\nend\n\n{ plus: plus.round(4), shovel: shovel.round(4) }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Behind this sits the big topic of <strong>allocations</strong>: every unnecessary intermediate object costs time and memory. And the fastest optimization of all: code that never runs in the first place.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='On your machine: profilers'><pre><code>$ gem install benchmark-ips stackprof\n# benchmark-ips: how many times per second? (more telling than one run)\n# stackprof:     WHERE does the program spend its time?</code></pre></div><p><strong>Concurrency:</strong> with <code>Thread</code>, Ruby does several things “at once”. In MRI all threads share one interpreter (the <em>GVL</em>) – real gains therefore appear mostly while <em>waiting</em> (network, files); for CPU parallelism there are <em>Ractors</em>. The browser has no real threads, so <code>Thread</code> and <code>sleep</code> are <strong>simulated</strong> here (cooperatively, with virtual time) – but the API is the real one:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "fetches = [\"clients\", \"projects\", \"times\"].map do |name|\n  Thread.new do\n    sleep 1   # simulated network download\n    \"#{name}: loaded\"\n  end\nend\n\nfetches.map(&:value)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Three “downloads”, started with <code>Thread.new</code>, collected with <code>value</code> – on your machine this takes the time of <em>one</em> download instead of three. And because our simulated threads take turns at every <code>sleep</code>, you can even watch the interleaving in the output:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "threads = 2.times.map do |i|\n  Thread.new do\n    3.times do |n|\n      puts \"thread #{i}: step #{n}\"\n      sleep 0.1\n    end\n  end\nend\nthreads.each(&:join)\n\"done\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>By the way, under the hood of our simulation sit <strong>Fibers</strong>: cooperative mini-programs that pass control explicitly – the foundation of many async libraries. You can use them directly too:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "narrator = Fiber.new do\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 1: Bacon\"\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 2: More bacon\"\n  \"The end\"\nend\n\n[narrator.resume, narrator.resume, narrator.resume]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/ruby/benchmark' target='_blank'>Benchmark</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Thread.html' target='_blank'>Thread</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Fiber.html' target='_blank'>Fiber</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Ractor.html' target='_blank'>Ractor</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> <code>slow_report</code> below scans the entries <strong>once per project</strong> – with many projects that turns quadratic. Write <code>fast_report</code> producing the same result with a <strong>single</strong> <code>group_by</code> pass. Measure the difference with Benchmark!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\n# def fast_report(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# require \"benchmark\"\n# { slow: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { slow_report(entries) } }.round(3),\n#   fast: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { fast_report(entries) } }.round(3) }\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>fast_report</code> needs a single pass: <code>group_by</code> the project, then <code>transform_values</code> for the sums of hours – as in the collections lesson. Afterwards compare both with <code>Benchmark</code>."
            ],
            "check": "fast_report(entries) == slow_report(entries) && code.include?(\"group_by\")",
            "hint": "<code>entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code> – one pass instead of one per project.",
            "solution": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\ndef fast_report(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "パフォーマンスと並行処理",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>まず測って、それから最適化</h2><p>黄金律は<strong>決して推測しないこと</strong>です。Rubyの<code>Benchmark</code>を使えば、本当に遅いのはどこかを測れます。たいてい、思っていたのとは別の場所です。定番の例を見てみましょう。文字列に<code>+=</code>を使うと毎回<em>新しい</em>文字列が作られますが、シャベル演算子<code>&lt;&lt;</code>は同じ文字列を伸ばしていきます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"benchmark\"\n\nn = 5_000\nplus = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s += \"x\" }\nend\nshovel = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s << \"x\" }\nend\n\n{ plus: plus.round(4), shovel: shovel.round(4) }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>この背景には、<strong>アロケーション</strong>（メモリの割り当て）という大きなテーマがあります。不要な中間オブジェクトは、ひとつひとつが時間とメモリを消費します。そして何より速い最適化は、そもそも実行されないコードです。</p><div class='offweb' data-title='自分のコンピューターでは：プロファイラー'><pre><code>$ gem install benchmark-ips stackprof\n# benchmark-ips: how many times per second? (more telling than one run)\n# stackprof:     WHERE does the program spend its time?</code></pre></div><p><strong>並行処理：</strong><code>Thread</code>を使うと、Rubyは複数のことを「同時に」こなせます。MRIではすべてのスレッドが1つのインタープリターを共有している（<em>GVL</em>）ので、効果が出るのはおもに<em>待ち時間</em>のある処理（ネットワーク、ファイル）です。CPUを使う処理を並列に動かしたいときは<em>Ractor</em>があります。ブラウザには本物のスレッドがないため、ここでの<code>Thread</code>と<code>sleep</code>は<strong>シミュレーション</strong>です（協調的に、仮想の時間で動きます）。でもAPIは本物と同じです：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "fetches = [\"clients\", \"projects\", \"times\"].map do |name|\n  Thread.new do\n    sleep 1   # ネットワークからのダウンロードのシミュレーション\n    \"#{name}: loaded\"\n  end\nend\n\nfetches.map(&:value)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>3つの「ダウンロード」を<code>Thread.new</code>で始めて、<code>value</code>で結果を集めました。自分のコンピューターなら、かかる時間は3回ぶんではなく<em>1回</em>ぶんで済みます。しかも、ここでシミュレーションしているスレッドは<code>sleep</code>のたびに順番を譲り合うので、処理が交互に進む様子を出力で確かめることもできます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "threads = 2.times.map do |i|\n  Thread.new do\n    3.times do |n|\n      puts \"thread #{i}: step #{n}\"\n      sleep 0.1\n    end\n  end\nend\nthreads.each(&:join)\n\"done\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>ちなみに、このシミュレーションの中身は<strong>Fiber</strong>でできています。Fiberは、制御を明示的に受け渡しながら協調して動くミニプログラムで、多くの非同期ライブラリの土台になっています。Fiberを直接使うこともできます：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "narrator = Fiber.new do\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 1: Bacon\"\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 2: More bacon\"\n  \"The end\"\nend\n\n[narrator.resume, narrator.resume, narrator.resume]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/library/benchmark.html' target='_blank'>Benchmark</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Thread.html' target='_blank'>Thread</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Fiber.html' target='_blank'>Fiber</a>・<a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/Ractor.html' target='_blank'>Ractor</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>下の<code>slow_report</code>は、エントリを<strong>プロジェクトごとに1回ずつ</strong>調べ直しています。プロジェクトが多いと、処理量は2乗のペースで増えてしまいます。<code>group_by</code>で<strong>1回だけ</strong>走査して同じ結果を返す<code>fast_report</code>を書きましょう。違いをBenchmarkで測ってみてください！</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\n# def fast_report(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# require \"benchmark\"\n# { slow: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { slow_report(entries) } }.round(3),\n#   fast: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { fast_report(entries) } }.round(3) }\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>fast_report</code>は1回たどるだけでいいよ。プロジェクトで<code>group_by</code>して、<code>transform_values</code>で時間の合計にするんだ。コレクションのレッスンと同じだね。そのあと<code>Benchmark</code>で両方を比べてみて。"
            ],
            "check": "fast_report(entries) == slow_report(entries) && code.include?(\"group_by\")",
            "hint": "<code>entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code>を使ってみて。プロジェクトごとに1回ずつじゃなくて、全体を1回たどるだけで済むよ。",
            "solution": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\ndef fast_report(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Performance et concurrence",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Mesurer d'abord, optimiser ensuite</h2><p>La règle d'or : <strong>ne jamais deviner</strong>. <code>Benchmark</code> de Ruby mesure ce qui est réellement lent – et ce n'est souvent pas ce que l'on croit. Un classique : <code>+=</code> sur des chaînes crée à chaque fois une <em>nouvelle</em> chaîne, tandis que l'opérateur <code>&lt;&lt;</code> (la « pelle ») prolonge la même :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"benchmark\"\n\nn = 5_000\nplus = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s += \"x\" }\nend\nshovel = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s << \"x\" }\nend\n\n{ plus: plus.round(4), shovel: shovel.round(4) }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Derrière cela se cache le grand sujet des <strong>allocations</strong> : chaque objet intermédiaire inutile coûte du temps et de la mémoire. Et l'optimisation la plus rapide de toutes : le code qui ne s'exécute jamais.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sur votre machine : les profileurs'><pre><code>$ gem install benchmark-ips stackprof\n# benchmark-ips: how many times per second? (more telling than one run)\n# stackprof:     WHERE does the program spend its time?</code></pre></div><p><strong>Concurrence :</strong> avec <code>Thread</code>, Ruby fait plusieurs choses « en même temps ». Dans MRI, tous les threads partagent un seul interpréteur (le <em>GVL</em>) – les vrais gains apparaissent donc surtout pendant les <em>attentes</em> (réseau, fichiers) ; pour le parallélisme de calcul, il existe les <em>Ractors</em>. Le navigateur n'a pas de vrais threads, <code>Thread</code> et <code>sleep</code> sont donc <strong>simulés</strong> ici (de manière coopérative, avec un temps virtuel) – mais l'API est bien la vraie :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "fetches = [\"clients\", \"projects\", \"times\"].map do |name|\n  Thread.new do\n    sleep 1   # téléchargement réseau simulé\n    \"#{name}: loaded\"\n  end\nend\n\nfetches.map(&:value)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Trois « téléchargements », lancés avec <code>Thread.new</code>, récupérés avec <code>value</code> – sur votre machine, cela prend le temps d'<em>un seul</em> téléchargement au lieu de trois. Et comme nos threads simulés passent la main à chaque <code>sleep</code>, vous pouvez même observer l'entrelacement dans la sortie :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "threads = 2.times.map do |i|\n  Thread.new do\n    3.times do |n|\n      puts \"thread #{i}: step #{n}\"\n      sleep 0.1\n    end\n  end\nend\nthreads.each(&:join)\n\"done\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Au passage, sous le capot de notre simulation se trouvent des <strong>Fibers</strong> : des mini-programmes coopératifs qui se passent explicitement le contrôle – la base de nombreuses bibliothèques asynchrones. Vous pouvez aussi les utiliser directement :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "narrator = Fiber.new do\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 1: Bacon\"\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 2: More bacon\"\n  \"The end\"\nend\n\n[narrator.resume, narrator.resume, narrator.resume]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://github.com/ruby/benchmark' target='_blank'>Benchmark</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Thread.html' target='_blank'>Thread</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Fiber.html' target='_blank'>Fiber</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Ractor.html' target='_blank'>Ractor</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> <code>slow_report</code> ci-dessous parcourt les saisies <strong>une fois par projet</strong> – avec beaucoup de projets, cela devient quadratique. Écrivez <code>fast_report</code>, qui produit le même résultat en un <strong>seul</strong> passage avec <code>group_by</code>. Mesurez la différence avec Benchmark !</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\n# def fast_report(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# require \"benchmark\"\n# { slow: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { slow_report(entries) } }.round(3),\n#   fast: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { fast_report(entries) } }.round(3) }\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>fast_report</code> n'a besoin que d'un seul passage : <code>group_by</code> sur le projet, puis <code>transform_values</code> pour les sommes d'heures – comme dans la leçon sur les collections. Comparez ensuite les deux avec <code>Benchmark</code>."
            ],
            "check": "fast_report(entries) == slow_report(entries) && code.include?(\"group_by\")",
            "hint": "<code>entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code> – un seul passage au lieu d'un par projet.",
            "solution": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\ndef fast_report(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\nend"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Prestazioni e concorrenza",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Prima misura, poi ottimizza</h2><p>La regola d'oro: <strong>mai tirare a indovinare</strong>. <code>Benchmark</code> di Ruby misura che cosa è davvero lento – spesso non è ciò che pensi. Un classico: <code>+=</code> sulle stringhe crea ogni volta una stringa <em>nuova</em>, la “paletta” <code>&lt;&lt;</code> estende quella esistente:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "require \"benchmark\"\n\nn = 5_000\nplus = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s += \"x\" }\nend\nshovel = Benchmark.realtime do\n  s = \"\"\n  n.times { s << \"x\" }\nend\n\n{ plus: plus.round(4), shovel: shovel.round(4) }"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Dietro c'è il grande tema delle <strong>allocazioni</strong>: ogni oggetto intermedio superfluo costa tempo e memoria. E l'ottimizzazione più rapida di tutte: il codice che non viene eseguito affatto.</p><div class='offweb' data-title='Sul tuo computer: profiler'><pre><code>$ gem install benchmark-ips stackprof\n# benchmark-ips: how many times per second? (more telling than one run)\n# stackprof:     WHERE does the program spend its time?</code></pre></div><p><strong>Concorrenza:</strong> con <code>Thread</code> Ruby fa più cose “contemporaneamente”. In MRI tutti i thread condividono un unico interprete (il <em>GVL</em>) – i guadagni reali si vedono quindi soprattutto durante le <em>attese</em> (rete, file); per il parallelismo sulla CPU ci sono i <em>Ractor</em>. Il browser non ha thread veri, perciò qui <code>Thread</code> e <code>sleep</code> sono <strong>simulati</strong> (in modo cooperativo, con un tempo virtuale) – ma l'API è quella vera:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "fetches = [\"clients\", \"projects\", \"times\"].map do |name|\n  Thread.new do\n    sleep 1   # download di rete simulato\n    \"#{name}: loaded\"\n  end\nend\n\nfetches.map(&:value)"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>Tre “download”, avviati con <code>Thread.new</code>, raccolti con <code>value</code> – sul tuo computer questo richiede il tempo di <em>un</em> download invece di tre. E dato che i nostri thread simulati si danno il turno a ogni <code>sleep</code>, puoi persino osservare l'alternanza nell'output:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "threads = 2.times.map do |i|\n  Thread.new do\n    3.times do |n|\n      puts \"thread #{i}: step #{n}\"\n      sleep 0.1\n    end\n  end\nend\nthreads.each(&:join)\n\"done\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p>A proposito, sotto il cofano della nostra simulazione ci sono le <strong>Fiber</strong>: mini-programmi cooperativi che si passano il controllo in modo esplicito – la base di molte librerie asincrone. Puoi usarle anche direttamente:</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "narrator = Fiber.new do\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 1: Bacon\"\n  Fiber.yield \"Chapter 2: More bacon\"\n  \"The end\"\nend\n\n[narrator.resume, narrator.resume, narrator.resume]"
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://github.com/ruby/benchmark' target='_blank'>Benchmark</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Thread.html' target='_blank'>Thread</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Fiber.html' target='_blank'>Fiber</a> · <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/Ractor.html' target='_blank'>Ractor</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> <code>slow_report</code> qui sotto scorre le voci <strong>una volta per ogni progetto</strong> – con molti progetti diventa quadratico. Scrivi <code>fast_report</code>, che produce lo stesso risultato con un <strong>unico</strong> passaggio di <code>group_by</code>. Misura la differenza con Benchmark!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\n# def fast_report(entries)\n#   ...\n# end\n\n# require \"benchmark\"\n# { slow: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { slow_report(entries) } }.round(3),\n#   fast: Benchmark.realtime { 50.times { fast_report(entries) } }.round(3) }\n",
            "hints": [
              "<code>fast_report</code> ha bisogno di un solo passaggio: <code>group_by</code> per progetto, poi <code>transform_values</code> per le somme delle ore – come nella lezione sulle collezioni. Dopo confronta i due con <code>Benchmark</code>."
            ],
            "check": "fast_report(entries) == slow_report(entries) && code.include?(\"group_by\")",
            "hint": "<code>entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }</code> – un solo passaggio invece di uno per progetto.",
            "solution": "entries = 500.times.map { |i| { project: \"P#{i % 5}\", hours: 1.0 } }\n\ndef slow_report(entries)\n  entries.map { |e| e[:project] }.uniq.to_h do |p|\n    [p, entries.select { |e| e[:project] == p }.sum { |e| e[:hours] }]\n  end\nend\n\ndef fast_report(entries)\n  entries.group_by { |e| e[:project] }.transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\nend"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tl-capstone",
      "de": {
        "title": "Finale: timelog im Web",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Alles zusammen: die Weboberfläche</h2><p>Zum Abschluss verbinden wir <em>alles</em>: Collections für den Bericht, Roda fürs Routing, <strong>ERB</strong> als Vorlagensprache und den Mini-Browser als Bühne. ERB ist HTML mit eingebettetem Ruby: <code>&lt;%= … %&gt;</code> fügt einen Wert ein, <code>&lt;% … %&gt;</code> führt Code aus (Schleifen!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nEINTRAEGE = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }\n]\n\nVORLAGE = ERB.new(<<~HTML)\n  <h1>timelog</h1>\n  <table border='1' cellpadding='6'>\n    <tr><th>Projekt</th><th>Stunden</th></tr>\n    <% bericht.each do |projekt, stunden| %>\n      <tr>\n        <td><a href='/projekt/<%= projekt %>'><%= projekt %></a></td>\n        <td><%= stunden %></td>\n      </tr>\n    <% end %>\n  </table>\nHTML\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      bericht = EINTRAEGE.group_by { |e| e[:projekt] }\n                         .transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:stunden] } }\n      VORLAGE.result(binding)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>VORLAGE.result(binding)</code> gibt der Vorlage Zugriff auf die lokalen Variablen der Route – so kommt <code>bericht</code> ins HTML. Genau so funktionieren die Views in Rails, Sinatra und Roda (dort mit Komfort-Helfern drumherum).</p><div class='docs'><strong>Nachschlagen</strong> (englisch): <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/ERB.html' target='_blank'>ERB</a> · <a href='https://roda.jeremyevans.net/documentation.html' target='_blank'>Roda-Dokumentation</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Aufgabe:</strong> Die Projektnamen sind schon Links! Ergänze die Route <code>r.get \"projekt\", String do |name| … end</code>: Sie zeigt eine Detailseite mit dem Projektnamen als Überschrift und allen Stunden-Werten des Projekts (z.&nbsp;B. per <code>map</code>/<code>join</code>). Unbekannte Pfade sollen 404 bleiben. Damit ist timelog komplett – klick dich durch!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nEINTRAEGE = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/projekt/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a> <a href='/projekt/Intern'>Intern</a>\"\n    end\n\n    # r.get \"projekt\", String do |name|\n    #   passende = EINTRAEGE.select { ... }\n    #   \"<h2>...</h2>...\"\n    # end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\"\n",
            "hints": [
              "Die neue Route kommt in den <code>route</code>-Block neben <code>r.root</code>. Darin wählst du mit <code>select</code> die Einträge des Projekts aus <code>EINTRAEGE</code> und baust aus Überschrift und Stunden einen HTML-String."
            ],
            "check": "s1, b1 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/\"); s2, b2 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/projekt/ProjectX\"); s3, _ = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/quatsch\"); s1 == 200 && b1.include?(\"ProjectX\") && s2 == 200 && b2.include?(\"ProjectX\") && b2.include?(\"3.5\") && s3 == 404",
            "hint": "<code>r.get \"projekt\", String do |name|; passende = EINTRAEGE.select { |e| e[:projekt] == name }; \"&lt;h2&gt;#{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\" + passende.map { |e| \"#{e[:stunden]}h\" }.join(\", \"); end</code>",
            "solution": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nEINTRAEGE = [\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.5 },\n  { projekt: \"Intern\",   stunden: 2.0 },\n  { projekt: \"ProjectX\", stunden: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/projekt/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a>\"\n    end\n\n    r.get \"projekt\", String do |name|\n      passende = EINTRAEGE.select { |e| e[:projekt] == name }\n      \"<h2>#{name}</h2>\" + passende.map { |e| \"#{e[:stunden]}h\" }.join(\", \")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>🎓 Geschafft!</h2><p>Du hast timelog von der ersten Collection bis zur Weboberfläche gebaut – mit Tests, Fehlerbehandlung, eigener DSL und Metaprogrammierung. Das ist kein Spielzeug-Wissen: Genau diese Bausteine stecken in jedem echten Ruby-Projekt.</p><p><strong>Wie weiter?</strong> Übe mit den <a href='https://koans.idogawa.com'>Ruby Koans</a>, bau timelog auf deinem eigenen Rechner als richtige Gem nach (Lektion {{nr:tl-cli}} zeigt die Struktur) – und wenn du tiefer graben willst: Die Bücher <em>Programming Ruby</em> („Pickaxe“) und <em>Polished Ruby Programming</em> begleiten dich vom Handwerk zur Meisterschaft. Und was du unterwegs vergisst, steht auf dem <button type='button' class='cheat-link'>📋 Spickzettel</button> und in der Ruby-Doku (Lektion {{nr:doku}}). CHUNKY BACON! 🦊🥓</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "en": {
        "title": "Finale: timelog on the web",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Everything together: the web interface</h2><p>To finish, we connect <em>everything</em>: collections for the report, Roda for routing, <strong>ERB</strong> as the template language and the mini browser as the stage. ERB is HTML with embedded Ruby: <code>&lt;%= … %&gt;</code> inserts a value, <code>&lt;% … %&gt;</code> runs code (loops!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nTEMPLATE = ERB.new(<<~HTML)\n  <h1>timelog</h1>\n  <table border='1' cellpadding='6'>\n    <tr><th>Project</th><th>Hours</th></tr>\n    <% report.each do |project, hours| %>\n      <tr>\n        <td><a href='/project/<%= project %>'><%= project %></a></td>\n        <td><%= hours %></td>\n      </tr>\n    <% end %>\n  </table>\nHTML\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      report = ENTRIES.group_by { |e| e[:project] }\n                      .transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\n      TEMPLATE.result(binding)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>TEMPLATE.result(binding)</code> gives the template access to the route's local variables – that's how <code>report</code> reaches the HTML. Views in Rails, Sinatra and Roda work exactly like this (with comfort helpers wrapped around).</p><div class='docs'><strong>Look it up:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/ERB.html' target='_blank'>ERB</a> · <a href='https://roda.jeremyevans.net/documentation.html' target='_blank'>Roda documentation</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Task:</strong> The project names are already links! Add the route <code>r.get \"project\", String do |name| … end</code>: it shows a detail page with the project name as heading and all the project's hour values (e.g. via <code>map</code>/<code>join</code>). Unknown paths stay 404. With that, timelog is complete – click around!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a> <a href='/project/Intern'>Intern</a>\"\n    end\n\n    # r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n    #   matching = ENTRIES.select { ... }\n    #   \"<h2>...</h2>...\"\n    # end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\"\n",
            "hints": [
              "The new route goes into the <code>route</code> block next to <code>r.root</code>. Inside it, pick the project's entries from <code>ENTRIES</code> with <code>select</code> and build an HTML string from the heading and the hours."
            ],
            "check": "s1, b1 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/\"); s2, b2 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/project/ProjectX\"); s3, _ = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/nonsense\"); s1 == 200 && b1.include?(\"ProjectX\") && s2 == 200 && b2.include?(\"ProjectX\") && b2.include?(\"3.5\") && s3 == 404",
            "hint": "<code>r.get \"project\", String do |name|; matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }; \"&lt;h2&gt;#{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \"); end</code>",
            "solution": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a>\"\n    end\n\n    r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n      matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }\n      \"<h2>#{name}</h2>\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>🎓 You made it!</h2><p>You built timelog from the first collection to a web interface – with tests, error handling, your own DSL and metaprogramming. That's not toy knowledge: exactly these building blocks sit inside every real Ruby project.</p><p><strong>Where next?</strong> Practice with the <a href='https://koans.idogawa.com'>Ruby Koans</a>, rebuild timelog on your own machine as a proper gem (lesson {{nr:tl-cli}} shows the structure) – and if you want to dig deeper: the books <em>Programming Ruby</em> (“the Pickaxe”) and <em>Polished Ruby Programming</em> take you from craft to mastery. And whatever you forget along the way is on the <button type='button' class='cheat-link'>📋 Cheat sheet</button> and in the Ruby docs (lesson {{nr:doku}}). CHUNKY BACON! 🦊🥓</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "ja": {
        "title": "フィナーレ：Webで動くtimelog",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>すべてを組み合わせる：Webインターフェース</h2><p>最後に、これまで学んだ<em>すべて</em>をつなげましょう。集計にはコレクション、ルーティングにはRoda、テンプレート言語には<strong>ERB</strong>、そして舞台はミニブラウザです。ERBは、Rubyを埋め込んだHTMLです。<code>&lt;%= … %&gt;</code>は値を差し込み、<code>&lt;% … %&gt;</code>はコードを実行します（ループも書けます！）：</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nTEMPLATE = ERB.new(<<~HTML)\n  <h1>timelog</h1>\n  <table border='1' cellpadding='6'>\n    <tr><th>Project</th><th>Hours</th></tr>\n    <% report.each do |project, hours| %>\n      <tr>\n        <td><a href='/project/<%= project %>'><%= project %></a></td>\n        <td><%= hours %></td>\n      </tr>\n    <% end %>\n  </table>\nHTML\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      report = ENTRIES.group_by { |e| e[:project] }\n                      .transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\n      TEMPLATE.result(binding)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>TEMPLATE.result(binding)</code>は、テンプレートからルートのローカル変数を使えるようにします。こうして<code>report</code>がHTMLまで届くのです。Rails、Sinatra、Rodaのビューも、まさにこの仕組みで動いています（実際には、便利なヘルパーがいろいろ用意されていますが）。</p><div class='docs'><strong>調べてみよう：</strong><a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/ja/latest/class/ERB.html' target='_blank'>ERB</a>・<a href='https://roda.jeremyevans.net/documentation.html' target='_blank'>Rodaのドキュメント（英語）</a></div><div class='task'><strong>課題：</strong>プロジェクト名はもうリンクになっています！ルート<code>r.get \"project\", String do |name| … end</code>を追加しましょう。プロジェクト名を見出しにして、そのプロジェクトの時間をすべて並べた詳細ページを表示します（たとえば<code>map</code>/<code>join</code>を使って）。知らないパスは404のままにしておきます。これでtimelogは完成です。あちこちクリックしてみてください！</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a> <a href='/project/Intern'>Intern</a>\"\n    end\n\n    # r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n    #   matching = ENTRIES.select { ... }\n    #   \"<h2>...</h2>...\"\n    # end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\"\n",
            "hints": [
              "新しいルートは<code>route</code>ブロックの中、<code>r.root</code>のとなりに書くよ。その中で<code>ENTRIES</code>からそのプロジェクトのエントリを<code>select</code>で選んで、見出しと時間からHTMLの文字列を組み立てるんだ。"
            ],
            "check": "s1, b1 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/\"); s2, b2 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/project/ProjectX\"); s3, _ = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/nonsense\"); s1 == 200 && b1.include?(\"ProjectX\") && s2 == 200 && b2.include?(\"ProjectX\") && b2.include?(\"3.5\") && s3 == 404",
            "hint": "たとえばこう書けるよ：<code>r.get \"project\", String do |name|; matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }; \"&lt;h2&gt;#{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \"); end</code>",
            "solution": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a>\"\n    end\n\n    r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n      matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }\n      \"<h2>#{name}</h2>\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>🎓 完走、おめでとうございます！</h2><p>最初のコレクションからWebインターフェースまで、timelogを自分の手で作り上げました。テストも、エラー処理も、自分だけのDSLも、メタプログラミングも使いこなしました。これはおもちゃの知識ではありません。本物のRubyプロジェクトの中には、どれもまさにこの部品が詰まっています。</p><p><strong>次はどこへ？</strong><a href='https://koans.idogawa.com'>Ruby Koans</a>で腕を磨いたり、自分のコンピューターでtimelogをちゃんとしたgemとして作り直したり（構成はレッスン{{nr:tl-cli}}で紹介しました）してみてください。もっと深く学びたくなったら、『<em>Programming Ruby</em>』（通称「Pickaxe（つるはし）本」）や『<em>Polished Ruby Programming</em>』といった本が、職人の技から達人の域へと導いてくれるでしょう。ここまで一緒に歩いてくれて、本当にありがとうございました。あなたのRubyの旅は、ここからが本番です。楽しいコードを、たくさん書いてくださいね。途中で忘れたことは、<button type='button' class='cheat-link'>📋 チートシート</button>とRubyのドキュメント（レッスン{{nr:doku}}）で確かめられます。CHUNKY BACON! 🦊🥓</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "fr": {
        "title": "Finale : timelog sur le web",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Tout ensemble : l'interface web</h2><p>Pour terminer, nous relions <em>tout</em> : les collections pour le rapport, Roda pour le routage, <strong>ERB</strong> comme langage de templates et le mini-navigateur comme scène. ERB, c'est du HTML avec du Ruby intégré : <code>&lt;%= … %&gt;</code> insère une valeur, <code>&lt;% … %&gt;</code> exécute du code (des boucles !) :</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nTEMPLATE = ERB.new(<<~HTML)\n  <h1>timelog</h1>\n  <table border='1' cellpadding='6'>\n    <tr><th>Project</th><th>Hours</th></tr>\n    <% report.each do |project, hours| %>\n      <tr>\n        <td><a href='/project/<%= project %>'><%= project %></a></td>\n        <td><%= hours %></td>\n      </tr>\n    <% end %>\n  </table>\nHTML\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      report = ENTRIES.group_by { |e| e[:project] }\n                      .transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\n      TEMPLATE.result(binding)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>TEMPLATE.result(binding)</code> donne au template accès aux variables locales de la route – c'est ainsi que <code>report</code> parvient jusqu'au HTML. Les vues de Rails, Sinatra et Roda fonctionnent exactement ainsi (avec des outils de confort autour).</p><div class='docs'><strong>Pour aller plus loin :</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/ERB.html' target='_blank'>ERB</a> · <a href='https://roda.jeremyevans.net/documentation.html' target='_blank'>Documentation de Roda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Exercice :</strong> Les noms de projets sont déjà des liens ! Ajoutez la route <code>r.get \"project\", String do |name| … end</code> : elle affiche une page de détail avec le nom du projet en titre et toutes les valeurs d'heures du projet (par exemple via <code>map</code>/<code>join</code>). Les chemins inconnus restent en 404. Avec cela, timelog est complet – cliquez partout !</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a> <a href='/project/Intern'>Intern</a>\"\n    end\n\n    # r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n    #   matching = ENTRIES.select { ... }\n    #   \"<h2>...</h2>...\"\n    # end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\"\n",
            "hints": [
              "La nouvelle route va dans le bloc <code>route</code>, à côté de <code>r.root</code>. À l'intérieur, sélectionnez les saisies du projet dans <code>ENTRIES</code> avec <code>select</code> et construisez une chaîne HTML à partir du titre et des heures."
            ],
            "check": "s1, b1 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/\"); s2, b2 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/project/ProjectX\"); s3, _ = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/nonsense\"); s1 == 200 && b1.include?(\"ProjectX\") && s2 == 200 && b2.include?(\"ProjectX\") && b2.include?(\"3.5\") && s3 == 404",
            "hint": "<code>r.get \"project\", String do |name|; matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }; \"&lt;h2&gt;#{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \"); end</code>",
            "solution": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a>\"\n    end\n\n    r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n      matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }\n      \"<h2>#{name}</h2>\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>🎓 Vous y êtes arrivé !</h2><p>Vous avez construit timelog, de la première collection jusqu'à une interface web – avec des tests, la gestion des erreurs, votre propre DSL et de la métaprogrammation. Ce ne sont pas des connaissances de jouet : ce sont exactement ces briques qui se trouvent dans tout vrai projet Ruby.</p><p><strong>Et ensuite ?</strong> Exercez-vous avec les <a href='https://koans.idogawa.com'>Ruby Koans</a>, reconstruisez timelog sur votre propre machine sous forme de vraie gem (la leçon {{nr:tl-cli}} en montre la structure) – et si vous voulez approfondir : les livres <em>Programming Ruby</em> (« the Pickaxe ») et <em>Polished Ruby Programming</em> vous mèneront du savoir-faire à la maîtrise. Et tout ce que vous oublierez en chemin se trouve dans l'<button type='button' class='cheat-link'>📋 Aide-mémoire</button> et dans la documentation Ruby (leçon {{nr:doku}}). CHUNKY BACON ! 🦊🥓</p>"
          }
        ]
      },
      "it": {
        "title": "Gran finale: timelog sul web",
        "cells": [
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>Tutto insieme: l'interfaccia web</h2><p>Per concludere colleghiamo <em>tutto</em>: le collezioni per il report, Roda per il routing, <strong>ERB</strong> come linguaggio di template e il mini browser come palcoscenico. ERB è HTML con Ruby incorporato: <code>&lt;%= … %&gt;</code> inserisce un valore, <code>&lt;% … %&gt;</code> esegue codice (cicli!):</p>"
          },
          {
            "t": "c",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nTEMPLATE = ERB.new(<<~HTML)\n  <h1>timelog</h1>\n  <table border='1' cellpadding='6'>\n    <tr><th>Project</th><th>Hours</th></tr>\n    <% report.each do |project, hours| %>\n      <tr>\n        <td><a href='/project/<%= project %>'><%= project %></a></td>\n        <td><%= hours %></td>\n      </tr>\n    <% end %>\n  </table>\nHTML\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      report = ENTRIES.group_by { |e| e[:project] }\n                      .transform_values { |l| l.sum { |e| e[:hours] } }\n      TEMPLATE.result(binding)\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<p><code>TEMPLATE.result(binding)</code> dà al template accesso alle variabili locali della route – è così che <code>report</code> arriva nell'HTML. Le view in Rails, Sinatra e Roda funzionano esattamente così (con in più qualche comodo helper attorno).</p><div class='docs'><strong>Approfondimenti:</strong> <a href='https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/4.0/ERB.html' target='_blank'>ERB</a> · <a href='https://roda.jeremyevans.net/documentation.html' target='_blank'>Documentazione di Roda</a></div><div class='task'><strong>Esercizio:</strong> I nomi dei progetti sono già dei link! Aggiungi la route <code>r.get \"project\", String do |name| … end</code>: mostra una pagina di dettaglio con il nome del progetto come titolo e tutti i valori di ore del progetto (per es. con <code>map</code>/<code>join</code>). I percorsi sconosciuti restano 404. Con questo timelog è completo – fai un giro tra le pagine!</div>"
          },
          {
            "t": "x",
            "code": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a> <a href='/project/Intern'>Intern</a>\"\n    end\n\n    # r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n    #   matching = ENTRIES.select { ... }\n    #   \"<h2>...</h2>...\"\n    # end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\"\n",
            "hints": [
              "La nuova route va nel blocco <code>route</code>, accanto a <code>r.root</code>. Lì dentro scegli da <code>ENTRIES</code> con <code>select</code> le voci del progetto e costruisci una stringa HTML con il titolo e le ore."
            ],
            "check": "s1, b1 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/\"); s2, b2 = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/project/ProjectX\"); s3, _ = mock_get(TimelogWeb, \"/nonsense\"); s1 == 200 && b1.include?(\"ProjectX\") && s2 == 200 && b2.include?(\"ProjectX\") && b2.include?(\"3.5\") && s3 == 404",
            "hint": "<code>r.get \"project\", String do |name|; matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }; \"&lt;h2&gt;#{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \"); end</code>",
            "solution": "install_gem \"roda\"\nrequire \"roda\"\nrequire \"erb\"\n\nENTRIES = [\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.5 },\n  { project: \"Intern\",   hours: 2.0 },\n  { project: \"ProjectX\", hours: 3.0 }\n]\n\nclass TimelogWeb < Roda\n  route do |r|\n    r.root do\n      \"<h1>timelog</h1><a href='/project/ProjectX'>ProjectX</a>\"\n    end\n\n    r.get \"project\", String do |name|\n      matching = ENTRIES.select { |e| e[:project] == name }\n      \"<h2>#{name}</h2>\" + matching.map { |e| \"#{e[:hours]}h\" }.join(\", \")\n    end\n  end\nend\n\nshow_browser TimelogWeb, \"/\""
          },
          {
            "t": "h",
            "html": "<h2>🎓 Ce l'hai fatta!</h2><p>Hai costruito timelog dalla prima collezione fino a un'interfaccia web – con test, gestione degli errori, il tuo DSL e la metaprogrammazione. Non sono conoscenze da giocattolo: proprio questi mattoni si trovano in ogni vero progetto Ruby.</p><p><strong>E adesso?</strong> Esercitati con i <a href='https://koans.idogawa.com'>Ruby Koans</a>, ricostruisci timelog sul tuo computer come gem vera e propria (la lezione {{nr:tl-cli}} ne mostra la struttura) – e se vuoi approfondire: i libri <em>Programming Ruby</em> (“il Pickaxe”) e <em>Polished Ruby Programming</em> ti portano dal mestiere alla maestria. E tutto ciò che dimentichi strada facendo lo trovi nel <button type='button' class='cheat-link'>📋 Promemoria</button> e nella documentazione di Ruby (lezione {{nr:doku}}). CHUNKY BACON! 🦊🥓</p>"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
